{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y3eqeFAX1Rc3b2HmcDGBzy","type":"tool_result","content":"## Owner resolutions (2026-11-05, relayed by the coordinator)\n\n- **S1 resolved**: author line is *Alex Linhares*, Argo Lab AGI, on leave\n  from the Getulio Vargas Foundation, `alex@argolab.org`, ORCID\n  0000-0002-4227-6879 (applied in `latex/main.tex`); the owner added\n  \"keep yours if possible\", read as: keep the AI contribution disclosed in\n  paper 1's form in the acknowledgements — if a co-author credit for the\n  AI was meant instead, the owner says so and item 09 applies it.\n- **S2**: no answer; the chosen typesetting stands.\n- **S3 resolved**: **leave the Lean where it is** and cite the existing\n  path `lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/{PrimitiveMachine,Frame}.lean`;\n  item 07 does not move anything (item 07's brief amended).\n- **S4 resolved**: cite the OpenAI manuscript first; for our side nothing,\n  unless a width reference is wanted back.\n- **S5**: the owner asked for the question in plain words. It was only\n  this: the driver originally gave every item the same time limit of\n  four hours, and the replay needs eight; the agent changed the driver so\n  one item can carry its own limit. Nothing for the owner to decide; the\n  change stands.\n- **S6 resolved**: `pyapproxmc` 4.4.0 (ApproxMC, an approximate counter\n  with (ε, δ) guarantees) is installed on the host by the owner\n  (2026-11-05); no exact counter is installed. Item 06 uses ApproxMC for\n  counts and CaDiCaL (pysat) for decision times, and says so; an exact\n  counter (ganak, sharpSAT-td) would need a source build.\n- **S7 resolved**: the text says exactly \"for every 50 ≤ n < e^5504\".\n- **S8 resolved**: ok.\n- **Compute**: the owner allows as many `nice -n 19` cores as needed\n  (TASK.md amended: no worker cap at nice 19).\n\n=====SEC2\n     1\t% The verified statement, read exactly -- drafted by loop0015 item 02 (2026-11-05).\n     2\t% Every line number of the comparator file is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone\n     3\t% (~/dev/untrusted/openai-math, read with cat/sed -n only); every manuscript quote\n     4\t% gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt.\n     5\t% Every number of this section: python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py\n     6\t% (the rho(n)/n table and the crossings) and python3 -I\n     7\t% reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py (the encoding lengths).\n     8\t\\section{The verified statement, read exactly}\n     9\t\\label{sec:statement}\n    10\t\n    11\tThe whole of what is machine-checked about family 378 is one theorem,\n    12\t\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, stated in the 220-line comparator file\n    13\t\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean} of the\n    14\tcollection \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}\n    15\tat commit \\texttt{4eaee875}. The file carries the statement with a\n    16\t\\texttt{sorry} for its proof (line 218); the solution tree proves the same\n    17\tstatement, and the collection's comparator checks that the two agree\n    18\t(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). The replay of Section~\\ref{sec:replay}\n    19\trebuilt that proof and found the three standard axioms and nothing else.\n    20\tThis section reads the statement, definition by definition, and says what\n    21\tit asserts and what it does not. Line numbers are those of the comparator\n    22\tfile; we quote nothing from memory.\n    23\t\n    24\t\\subsection{The definitions, one by one}\n    25\t\n    26\t\\paragraph{Formulas and the count (lines 13--51).}\n    27\tA \\texttt{Literal V} is a variable with a sign (lines 19--22); a\n    28\t\\texttt{Clause V} is a \\texttt{Finset} of literals and a \\texttt{CNF V} a\n    29\t\\texttt{Finset} of clauses (lines 24--25). An assignment is any function\n    30\t\\(V \\to \\mathtt{Bool}\\) (line 17). A literal evaluates to the Boolean\n    31\t``the assignment agrees with its sign'' (lines 29--30), a clause to ``some\n    32\tliteral is true'' (lines 32--33), a formula to ``every clause is true''\n    33\t(lines 35--36). \\texttt{WidthAtMost k F} says every clause has at most\n    34\t\\(k\\) literals as a set (line 38). Over a finite variable type,\n    35\t\\texttt{satisfyingAssignments F} filters the universe of all assignments\n    36\t(lines 44--45), and \\texttt{count F} is its cardinality (line 47): the\n    37\tnumber of satisfying assignments among all \\(2^n\\) when\n    38\t\\(V = \\mathtt{Fin}\\ n\\). The \\texttt{Finset} choices change nothing in the\n    39\tmeaning: a repeated clause or literal collapses, the empty clause is\n    40\tallowed and unsatisfiable, a tautological clause \\(\\{x, \\neg x\\}\\) is\n    41\tallowed and counts two toward the width. That is consistent with the\n    42\tmanuscript's ``Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than \\(k\\)\n    43\tliterals'' (p.~1, line~44).\n    44\t\n    45\t\\paragraph{The input encoding (lines 53--81).}\n    46\t\\texttt{encodeNat n} is \\(n\\) ones followed by a zero (line 55): unary.\n    47\t\\texttt{frame} doubles a bit string, prefixing each bit with a one, and\n    48\tends it with a zero (lines 57--58). \\texttt{encodeList enc} prefixes each\n    49\telement's frame with a one and ends the list with a zero (lines 60--62).\n    50\tA literal becomes the number \\(2\\cdot\\mathrm{var} + [\\text{positive}]\\),\n    51\tat most \\(2n - 1\\) (lines 64--65); a clause is its sorted list of literal\n    52\tnumbers, a formula its sorted list of clauses (lines 67--71).\n    53\t\\texttt{encodeCNF F} is \\texttt{encodeNat n} followed by the formula's\n    54\tlist of lists of unary numbers (lines 73--77), and\n    55\t\\texttt{formulaBitLength F} is the length of that bit string (line 79).\n    56\tTwo things follow. The number of variables \\(n\\) is part of the input,\n    57\tso a variable that occurs in no clause is still counted. And the length\n    58\tis polynomial: a clause of \\(j \\le k\\) literals costs \\(2j(4n+1)+4\\) bits,\n    59\tso\n    60\t\\[\n    61\t  L \\;=\\; \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F \\;\\le\\; n + 2 + m\\,\\bigl(2k(4n+1) + 4\\bigr)\n    62\t  \\;=\\; O(k\\,n\\,m)\n    63\t\\]\n    64\tfor \\(m\\) clauses. The solution itself proves\n    65\t\\((L+1)^d \\le (8k+7)^d\\,(n + |F| + 1)^{2d}\\)\n    66\t(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Statement.lean}, lines 9--14). Emulating the\n    67\tdefinitions (\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}),\n    68\ta formula with \\(n = 125\\), \\(m = 500\\), \\(k = 4\\) encodes to\n    69\t\\(L = 1{,}006{,}223\\) bits and one with \\(n = 1{,}000\\), \\(m = 4{,}000\\),\n    70\t\\(k = 3\\) to \\(47.9\\) million, both under the bound. Nothing in the\n    71\tencoding is exponential; the unary numbers are linear in \\(n\\).\n    72\t\n    73\t\\paragraph{The machine (lines 83--128).}\n    74\t\\texttt{PrimitiveMachine} (lines 92--99) is a finite object: a number\n    75\t\\texttt{tapeCount} of tapes and a number \\texttt{stateCount} of states,\n    76\tan input tape and a start state, a table \\texttt{scan} saying which tape\n    77\teach state reads, and a table \\texttt{transition} from\n    78\t\\(\\mathtt{Fin}\\ \\mathtt{stateCount} \\times \\mathtt{Option\\ Bool}\\)---a\n    79\tdomain of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) points---into either a halting\n    80\tanswer (a \\texttt{Bool}) or a next state with one action. The action type\n    81\tis Mathlib's \\texttt{Turing.TM0.Stmt (Option Bool)}: move the head left or\n    82\tright, or write one of the three symbols \\texttt{none}, \\texttt{some\n    83\tfalse}, \\texttt{some true}, five values in all\n    84\t(\\path{Mathlib/Computability/TuringMachine/PostTuringMachine.lean}\n    85\tlines 118--120 and \\path{Tape.lean} lines 421--423 at Mathlib\n    86\t\\texttt{d13f23b7}, the clone's pinned revision). A configuration\n    87\t\\texttt{Cfg} (lines 103--105) is a state, halted or not, and one Mathlib\n    88\t\\texttt{Tape} per tape index (a head symbol and two blank-padded halves,\n    89\t\\path{Tape.lean} lines 408--414, blank \\(=\\) \\texttt{none}).\n    90\t\\texttt{step} (lines 107--119) returns \\texttt{none} in a halted state;\n    91\totherwise it reads the head of the one tape the state scans, looks the\n    92\tpair up in the table, and either halts with the answer, or writes one\n    93\tsymbol or moves that one head by one cell and changes state. \\texttt{init}\n    94\t(lines 121--124) puts the input on the input tape with the head at its\n    95\tfirst bit and every other tape blank (\\texttt{Tape.mk}$_1$,\n    96\t\\path{Tape.lean} line 494). A Lean function on a finite domain is,\n    97\twhatever term defines it, a finite table; so a step is one lookup in a\n    98\ttable of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) rows, and no field of the\n    99\tstructure can carry unbounded computation.\n   100\t\n   101\t\\paragraph{Traces (lines 87--90).}\n   102\t\\texttt{Trace step t a c} is an inductive proposition with two\n   103\tconstructors: \\texttt{refl}, a trace of length 0 from \\(a\\) to \\(a\\)\n   104\titself, and \\texttt{cons}, which extends a trace from \\(b\\) by one\n   105\tsuccessful step \\(a \\mapsto b\\). So it holds exactly when \\(c\\) is\n   106\treached from \\(a\\) by \\(t\\) successful applications of \\texttt{step}, and\n   107\ta trace of length 0 to a different configuration is impossible. Since\n   108\t\\texttt{step} is a function, the run is deterministic.\n   109\t\n   110\t\\paragraph{Space monitoring (lines 147--189).}\n   111\t\\texttt{LocatedCfg} (lines 149--153) pairs a configuration with, per tape,\n   112\tthe head's current position and the least and greatest positions seen so\n   113\tfar, as integers; \\texttt{locatedInit} starts every head at 0 with the\n   114\tinput tape's range covering the input (lines 155--159);\n   115\t\\texttt{headDisplacement} is \\(-1\\), \\(+1\\) or \\(0\\) for a move left, a\n   116\tmove right or a write (lines 161--164); \\texttt{monitor} (lines 166--182)\n   117\tapplies the same table lookup to update the position and the running\n   118\tminimum and maximum of the scanned tape, and nothing else;\n   119\t\\texttt{locatedStep} is \\texttt{step} on the configuration followed by\n   120\t\\texttt{monitor} (lines 184--186). \\texttt{visitedSpace} (lines 188--189)\n   121\tis the sum over tapes of \\(\\mathrm{upper} - \\mathrm{lower} + 1\\): the\n   122\tnumber of cells whose head position was ever visited. \\texttt{countWord a}\n   123\tis the binary expansion of \\(a\\), least significant bit first, with\n   124\t\\texttt{[false]} for \\(0\\) (line 147), and \\texttt{toNat} (lines 7--9)\n   125\tdecodes such a word.\n   126\t\n   127\t\\paragraph{The exponent and the bound (lines 130--141).}\n   128\t\\begin{quote}\\small\n   129\t\\texttt{runtimeScale n = n * (Real.log (Real.log (n + 16)))\\^{}2 /}\\\\\n   130\t\\texttt{\\quad Real.sqrt (Real.log (n + 16))}\n   131\t\\end{quote}\n   132\t(lines 132--134, transcribed; the casts to \\(\\mathbb{R}\\) omitted), that is\n   133\t\\[\n   134\t  \\rhon \\;=\\; \\frac{n\\,\\bigl(\\ln\\ln(n+16)\\bigr)^2}{\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}},\n   135\t\\]\n   136\twith \\texttt{Real.log} the natural logarithm. The manuscript fixes the\n   137\tsame base: ``Throughout, log denotes the natural logarithm'' (p.~1, lines\n   138\t48--49; again at p.~7, lines 326--327). The shift by 16 keeps\n   139\t\\(\\ln\\ln\\) defined and positive at every \\(n \\ge 0\\) (\\(\\ln 16 = 2.77\\)),\n   140\tso the theorem can be stated for all \\(n\\) with no proviso. Then\n   141\t\\begin{quote}\n   142\t\\texttt{runtimeBound C A d L n = C * (L + 1)\\^{}d * 2\\^{}(A * runtimeScale n)}\n   143\t\\end{quote}\n   144\t(lines 136--139, transcribed), a real number, with the real-exponent\n   145\tpower. We write \\(\\rhon\\) for \\texttt{runtimeScale n} throughout the\n   146\tpaper.\n   147\t\n   148\t\\subsection{The theorem, and the order of its quantifiers}\n   149\t\n   150\tLines 194--217 state, for every \\(k\\) with \\(2 \\le k\\):\n   151\t\\[\n   152\t\\small\n   153\t\\begin{array}{l}\n   154\t\\exists\\ \\mathtt{machine},\\ \\mathtt{countTape},\\ \\mathtt{answerTape},\\\n   155\t  C, A, S \\in \\mathbb{R},\\ d, e \\in \\mathbb{N},\\\\[2pt]\n   156\t\\quad 0 < C \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < A \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < S \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < d \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < e\\ \\wedge\\\\[2pt]\n   157\t\\quad \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ \\forall\\, F : \\mathtt{CNF}\\ (\\mathtt{Fin}\\ n),\\\n   158\t  \\mathtt{WidthAtMost}\\ k\\ F \\;\\rightarrow\\\\[2pt]\n   159\t\\qquad \\exists\\ \\mathtt{time} \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ \\mathtt{final} : \\mathtt{LocatedCfg}\\ \\mathtt{machine},\\\\[2pt]\n   160\t\\qquad\\quad \\mathtt{Trace}\\ (\\mathtt{locatedStep}\\ \\mathtt{machine})\\ \\mathtt{time}\\\n   161\t  (\\mathtt{locatedInit}\\ \\mathtt{machine}\\ (\\mathtt{encodeCNF}\\ F))\\ \\mathtt{final}\\\\[2pt]\n   162\t\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\text{(lines 201--202)}\\\\[2pt]\n   163\t\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the count tape holds \\texttt{countWord (count F)}}\n   164\t  \\quad\\text{(203--204)}\\\\[2pt]\n   165\t\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the answer tape holds the bit \\([0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]\\)}\n   166\t  \\quad\\text{(205--206)}\\\\[2pt]\n   167\t\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the halting state carries that bit}\n   168\t  \\quad\\text{(207)}\\\\[2pt]\n   169\t\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\mathtt{toNat}\\ (\\mathtt{countWord}\\ (\\mathtt{count}\\ F)) = \\mathtt{count}\\ F\n   170\t  \\quad\\text{(208)}\\\\[2pt]\n   171\t\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ |\\mathtt{countWord}\\ (\\mathtt{count}\\ F)| \\le n + 1\n   172\t  \\quad\\text{(209)}\\\\[2pt]\n   173\t\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\mathtt{time} \\le C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\,\\rhon},\\ L = \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F\n   174\t  \\quad\\text{(210--211)}\\\\[2pt]\n   175\t\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\forall\\ \\text{prefix of the trace},\\\n   176\t  \\mathtt{visitedSpace} \\le S\\,(L+1)^e\n   177\t  \\quad\\text{(212--217).}\n   178\t\\end{array}\n   179\t\\]\n   180\tThree features of the order matter.\n   181\t\n   182\t\\emph{The machine and the five constants come after \\(k\\) and before\n   183\t\\(n\\) and \\(F\\).} They may depend on \\(k\\) and on nothing else; inside\n   184\tthe universal quantifier only \\texttt{time} and \\texttt{final} are\n   185\tchosen. So for each \\(k\\) one fixed finite machine serves every formula\n   186\tof every size, and \\(C, A, S, d, e\\) are the same for all of them: the\n   187\tstandard \\(O_k(1)\\) reading. The table's size is \\(O_k(1)\\) in the same\n   188\tsense, with no explicit function of \\(k\\).\n   189\t\n   190\t\\emph{There is no lower bound on \\(n\\).} The manuscript's Theorem~1.1\n   191\tand Proposition~6.4 are stated for \\(n \\ge 20\\) (p.~2, lines 56--57;\n   192\tp.~43, lines 2474--2475), and the solution's auxiliary\n   193\t\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} keeps \\(20 \\le n\\)\n   194\t(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 104--109); the exported\n   195\ttheorem does not. The \\(+16\\) in \\(\\rhon\\) is what makes that possible.\n   196\t\n   197\t\\emph{The constants are existential.} The statement asserts that values\n   198\tof \\(C, A, S, d, e\\) exist; it names none. The proof obtains them from\n   199\texistential lemmas and never evaluates them: at the point where the\n   200\tlarge-input run is assembled, the exponent constant is set to\n   201\t\\(A = 256 + A_t\\) with \\(A_t \\ge 1\\) taken from a leaf contract\n   202\t(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 51;\n   203\t\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean}, lines 792--793), and\n   204\tthe degree and the polynomial constants are built the same way. So the\n   205\tproof as written has \\(A \\ge 257\\), and no closed value of any of the\n   206\tfive. The witness machine is explicit in one respect: it is compiled\n   207\tfrom a program over 80 one-bit registers and 1,220 tapes, with\n   208\t\\(\\mathtt{countTape} = 256\\) and \\(\\mathtt{answerTape} = 1216\\)\n   209\t(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 98--101); its state count is\n   210\ta finite type's cardinality that nothing computes. Section~\\ref{sec:proof}\n   211\tis the inspection of the proof; this section only reads what the\n   212\tstatement fixes and what it leaves open.\n   213\t\n   214\t\\subsection{The exponent, in numbers}\n   215\t\n   216\tEverything the title says rests on one function. With \\(y = \\ln(n+16)\\),\n   217\t\\[\n   218\t  \\frac{\\rhon}{n} \\;=\\; \\frac{(\\ln y)^2}{\\sqrt{y}},\n   219\t\\]\n   220\twhich rises from \\(0.64\\) at \\(n = 1\\), crosses \\(1\\) between\n   221\t\\(n = 49\\) and \\(n = 50\\), peaks at \\(16/e^2 = 2.165\\) where\n   222\t\\(\\ln y = 4\\), i.e.\\ \\(y = e^4\\), \\(n = e^{e^4} - 16 \\approx 10^{23.7}\\),\n   223\tand returns below \\(1\\) only where \\((\\ln y)^2 = \\sqrt{y}\\) again, at\n   224\t\\(y = 5503.7\\), i.e.\\ \\(n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx 10^{2390}\\).\n   225\tTable~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale} gives the values and\n   226\tFigures~\\ref{fig:central} and \\ref{fig:runtime-scale} draw them; every\n   227\tnumber is printed by \\repofile{paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py}\n   228\t(run under \\texttt{python3 -I}; it imports nothing from the clone and\n   229\tcomputes in \\(y\\), so that no \\(n\\) beyond floating-point range is ever\n   230\tformed). The crossing at 50 is a fact about integers:\n   231\t\\(\\rho(49)/49 = 0.9994\\) and \\(\\rho(50)/50 = 1.0027\\). In the limit\n   232\t\\(\\rhon/n \\to 0\\), since \\((\\ln y)^2/\\sqrt{y} \\to 0\\): the exponent is\n   233\t\\(o(n)\\), and that is a theorem in our companion Lean\n   234\t\\leanref{rho-little-o}. Both facts are true at once, and the second is\n   235\tthe one the title is about: the function is \\(o(n)\\), and it is above\n   236\t\\(n\\) on every size anyone will ever write down.\n   237\t\n   238\t\\begin{table}[htbp]\n   239\t\\centering\n   240\t\\caption{The verified exponent relative to \\(n\\). \\(\\rhon = n(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}\\),\n   241\tthe comparator's \\texttt{runtimeScale} (lines 132--134), natural logarithms.\n   242\tThe verified bound is \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\); at \\(A = 1\\) its exponential\n   243\tfactor is \\(2^{(\\rhon/n)\\cdot n}\\), against \\(2^n\\) for trying every assignment.\n   244\tRegenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}\n   245\t\\label{tab:runtime-scale}\n   246\t\\input{tables/runtime_scale}\n   247\t\\end{table}\n   248\t\n   249\t\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n   250\t\\centering\n   251\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n.pdf}\\\\[4pt]\n   252\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n_practical.pdf}\n   253\t\\caption{The verified exponent against brute force. Top: \\(\\rhon/n\\) over\n   254\tthe whole range, the axis in \\(\\log_{10} n\\) from \\(n = 10\\) to\n   255\t\\(n = 10^{3000}\\); the dashed level is \\(2^n\\); beneath it the manuscript's two\n   256\tsharper exponents, \\(n\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}\\) (Theorem~1.1, general) and\n   257\t\\(n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}\\) (fixed clause density), which are not in the Lean\n   258\tlibrary (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}). Bottom: the same three curves over the\n   259\tpractical range \\(n = 10\\) to \\(10^6\\), with \\(\\rhon/n\\) marked at\n   260\t\\(n = 50, 125, 10^3, 10^4, 10^6\\). All curves at the constant \\(A = 1\\);\n   261\tthe verified \\(A\\) is unknown. Regenerate:\n   262\t\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/rho\\_over\\_n.py}.}\n   263\t\\label{fig:central}\n   264\t\\end{figure}\n   265\t\n   266\t\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n   267\t\\centering\n   268\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{runtime_scale.pdf}\n   269\t\\caption{\\(\\rhon/n\\) alone, on a logarithmic axis in \\(\\log_{10} n\\) from\n   270\t\\(n = 10\\) to \\(n = 10^{2500}\\), with the level \\(1\\) (brute force, \\(2^n\\)),\n   271\tthe first integer above the level (\\(n = 50\\)), the peak (\\(16/e^2 = 2.17\\)\n   272\tat \\(\\ln(n+16) = e^4\\), \\(n \\approx 10^{23.7}\\)), the return below the level\n   273\t(\\(n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx 10^{2390}\\)), and the practical range\n   274\t\\(50 \\le n \\le 10^6\\) shaded. Regenerate:\n   275\t\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}\n   276\t\\label{fig:runtime-scale}\n   277\t\\end{figure}\n   278\t\n   279\t\\subsection{The title's claim, made precise}\n   280\t\n   281\tThe title says the verified algorithm is slower than trying all \\(2^n\\)\n   282\tassignments at every practical size. Read against the statement, that is\n   283\tthe following, and nothing more.\n   284\t\n   285\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   286\t\\item \\textbf{What is verified is a guarantee, not a running time.} The\n   287\t  theorem says the machine halts within \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\) steps.\n   288\t  It does not say how many steps the machine takes on any input, and no\n   289\t  implementation of the machine exists to be timed. A bound above\n   290\t  \\(2^n\\) does not say the machine is slow; it says the proof promises\n   291\t  nothing better than \\(2^n\\) there.\n   292\t\\item \\textbf{The constants \\(C, A, S, d, e\\) are existential and never\n   293\t  computed.} The comparator asserts their existence; the proof builds\n   294\t  them from existential lemmas (above, with \\(A \\ge 257\\) as\n   295\t  constructed) and states no value. The machine's state count is likewise\n   296\t  never computed.\n   297\t\\item \\textbf{With \\(A = 1\\), the verified exponent exceeds \\(n\\) for\n   298\t  every \\(50 \\le n < e^{5504}\\).} That is Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}:\n   299\t  the exponential factor of the bound is at least \\(2^n\\) from 50\n   300\t  variables up to about \\(10^{2390}\\), and \\(2^{2.17\\,n}\\) at its\n   301\t  worst. With the true \\(A\\), whatever it is, the factor is\n   302\t  \\(2^{A\\rhon} \\ge 2^{\\rhon}\\) once \\(A \\ge 1\\), and the proof's own\n   303\t  construction has \\(A \\ge 257\\); so nothing is known that would put the\n   304\t  bound below \\(2^n\\) at any practical size.\n   305\t\\item \\textbf{Hence, at every practical size, the verified guarantee is\n   306\t  weaker than the guarantee of trying all assignments.} Trying all \\(2^n\\)\n   307\t  assignments and checking each against the \\(m\\) clauses counts the\n   308\t  models in \\(2^n \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)\\) steps on any reasonable\n   309\t  machine, with constants one can write down. The verified guarantee is\n   310\t  \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\) with \\(\\rhon > n\\) on \\(50 \\le n < e^{5504}\\)\n   311\t  and \\(C, A, d\\) unknown. For every \\(C \\ge 1\\), \\(A \\ge 1\\) and\n   312\t  \\(d \\ge 1\\) the verified bound is above \\(2^n\\) on every \\(n\\) in that\n   313\t  range, and the only values on record are the proof's, which has\n   314\t  \\(1 \\le C\\) and \\(1 \\le A\\) in the large-input run's own statement\n   315\t  (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 12) and\n   316\t  \\(A \\ge 257\\) in its construction. A proof with \\(A < 1\\) would be a\n   317\t  different proof; none is on record.\n   318\t\\end{enumerate}\n   319\t\n   320\tNone of this is a defect of the formalisation or of the proof; it is\n   321\twhat an \\(O_k(1)\\) statement with a \\(2^{o(n)}\\) exponent of this shape\n   322\tmeans when the exponent's constants are not tracked. The manuscript\n   323\titself calls \\(\\rhon\\) ``the weaker exponent'' (p.~2, line~63), and its\n   324\tsharper exponents are not in the Lean library (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n   325\tOne more detail of base: the solution's internal \\(\\rho\\)\n   326\t(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean}, lines 10--11) is in base 2 and\n   327\tis related to \\texttt{runtimeScale} by\n   328\t\\(\\mathtt{runtimeScale}\\ n \\le 4\\,\\rho_2(n)\\) for \\(n \\ge 20\\)\n   329\t(lines 190--191); the base changes the exponent by a constant factor\n   330\tthat \\(A\\) absorbs, so nothing above depends on it.\n   331\t\n   332\t\\subsection{What the statement does establish}\n   333\t\n   334\tFor each fixed \\(k \\ge 2\\), a deterministic machine that, on the canonical\n   335\tencoding of any \\(k\\)-CNF on \\(n\\) variables with \\(m\\) clauses, halts with\n   336\tthe exact number of satisfying assignments in binary on a named tape,\n   337\twithin \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\) steps, where \\(L = O(k\\,n\\,m)\\) is\n   338\tpolynomial in \\(n + m\\) and \\(\\rhon = o(n)\\); and, because a\n   339\t\\texttt{Finset} of clauses of width at most \\(k\\) has at most\n   340\t\\(\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j} = O(n^k)\\) members (\\(2{,}604{,}376\\) at\n   341\t\\(n = 125\\), \\(k = 3\\)), the whole bound is \\(2^{o(n)}\\) in \\(n\\) alone.\n   342\tThat is a deterministic \\(2^{o(n)} \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)\\) algorithm\n   343\tfor \\#\\(k\\)-SAT in the standard sense, and therefore for \\(k\\)-SAT; it\n   344\trefutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis \\citep{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001} as\n   345\tstated. The statement reads as the manuscript's verified form, the\n   346\tweaker exponent of its Proposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476), without\n   347\tthe \\(n \\ge 20\\) proviso. We found no field, definition or quantifier\n   348\tthrough which the statement could be kernel-true and mean something\n   349\tweaker than this; the audit it summarises is\n   350\t\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/statement_audit.md}. Our own\n   351\trestatement of the statement as a Lean \\texttt{Prop}, character for\n   352\tcharacter over a verbatim copy of the machine model, is\n   353\t\\leanref{subexpcount}; it is never proved and never an axiom there\n   354\t(Section~\\ref{sec:lean}).\n   355\t\n   356\t\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n   357\t\n   358\t\\begin{itemize}\n   359\t\\item \\textbf{No running time of any machine.} Nothing here measures or\n   360\t  estimates how long the verified machine, or any program, takes on any\n   361\t  input. The title compares two \\emph{guarantees}; Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}\n   362\t  is the only place in the paper where anything is timed, and it times\n   363\t  ordinary solvers, not this machine.\n   364\t\\item \\textbf{No statement about the proof.} That the statement means\n   365\t  what we say it means is a reading of 220 lines; whether the proof of\n   366\t  it is sound is Section~\\ref{sec:proof} and Section~\\ref{sec:replay},\n   367\t  which found no flaw. The one proof-side fact quoted here, \\(A = 256 +\n   368\t  A_t\\), is a line of the solution read for the value it assigns, not a\n   369\t  claim about the argument.\n   370\t\\item \\textbf{No value of the constants.} \\(A \\ge 257\\) is what the\n   371\t  proof writes down, not what the machine needs; a different proof of\n   372\t  the same statement could have any \\(A > 0\\). The statement itself\n   373\t  fixes nothing.\n   374\t\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the manuscript's sharper exponents}, which\n   375\t  are not in the Lean library and are Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}'s subject.\n   376\t\\item \\textbf{No claim about sizes beyond \\(e^{5504}\\).} Past\n   377\t  \\(n \\approx 10^{2390}\\) the verified factor is below \\(2^n\\) at\n   378\t  \\(A = 1\\); we say nothing about that range except that it is not a\n   379\t  practical one.\n   380\t\\end{itemize}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"d15eb565-dcb0-493a-8206-d839da8e6f4a","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:30:59.777Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## Owner resolutions (2026-11-05, relayed by the coordinator)\n\n- **S1 resolved**: author line is *Alex Linhares*, Argo Lab AGI, on leave\n  from the Getulio Vargas Foundation, `alex@argolab.org`, ORCID\n  0000-0002-4227-6879 (applied in `latex/main.tex`); the owner added\n  \"keep yours if possible\", read as: keep the AI contribution disclosed in\n  paper 1's form in the acknowledgements — if a co-author credit for the\n  AI was meant instead, the owner says so and item 09 applies it.\n- **S2**: no answer; the chosen typesetting stands.\n- **S3 resolved**: **leave the Lean where it is** and cite the existing\n  path `lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/{PrimitiveMachine,Frame}.lean`;\n  item 07 does not move anything (item 07's brief amended).\n- **S4 resolved**: cite the OpenAI manuscript first; for our side nothing,\n  unless a width reference is wanted back.\n- **S5**: the owner asked for the question in plain words. It was only\n  this: the driver originally gave every item the same time limit of\n  four hours, and the replay needs eight; the agent changed the driver so\n  one item can carry its own limit. Nothing for the owner to decide; the\n  change stands.\n- **S6 resolved**: `pyapproxmc` 4.4.0 (ApproxMC, an approximate counter\n  with (ε, δ) guarantees) is installed on the host by the owner\n  (2026-11-05); no exact counter is installed. Item 06 uses ApproxMC for\n  counts and CaDiCaL (pysat) for decision times, and says so; an exact\n  counter (ganak, sharpSAT-td) would need a source build.\n- **S7 resolved**: the text says exactly \"for every 50 ≤ n < e^5504\".\n- **S8 resolved**: ok.\n- **Compute**: the owner allows as many `nice -n 19` cores as needed\n  (TASK.md amended: no worker cap at nice 19).\n\n=====SEC2\n     1\t% The verified statement, read exactly -- drafted by loop0015 item 02 (2026-11-05).\n     2\t% Every line number of the comparator file is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone\n     3\t% (~/dev/untrusted/openai-math, read with cat/sed -n only); every manuscript quote\n     4\t% gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt.\n     5\t% Every number of this section: python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py\n     6\t% (the rho(n)/n table and the crossings) and python3 -I\n     7\t% reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py (the encoding lengths).\n     8\t\\section{The verified statement, read exactly}\n     9\t\\label{sec:statement}\n    10\t\n    11\tThe whole of what is machine-checked about family 378 is one theorem,\n    12\t\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, stated in the 220-line comparator file\n    13\t\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean} of the\n    14\tcollection \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}\n    15\tat commit \\texttt{4eaee875}. The file carries the statement with a\n    16\t\\texttt{sorry} for its proof (line 218); the solution tree proves the same\n    17\tstatement, and the collection's comparator checks that the two agree\n    18\t(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). The replay of Section~\\ref{sec:replay}\n    19\trebuilt that proof and found the three standard axioms and nothing else.\n    20\tThis section reads the statement, definition by definition, and says what\n    21\tit asserts and what it does not. Line numbers are those of the comparator\n    22\tfile; we quote nothing from memory.\n    23\t\n    24\t\\subsection{The definitions, one by one}\n    25\t\n    26\t\\paragraph{Formulas and the count (lines 13--51).}\n    27\tA \\texttt{Literal V} is a variable with a sign (lines 19--22); a\n    28\t\\texttt{Clause V} is a \\texttt{Finset} of literals and a \\texttt{CNF V} a\n    29\t\\texttt{Finset} of clauses (lines 24--25). An assignment is any function\n    30\t\\(V \\to \\mathtt{Bool}\\) (line 17). A literal evaluates to the Boolean\n    31\t``the assignment agrees with its sign'' (lines 29--30), a clause to ``some\n    32\tliteral is true'' (lines 32--33), a formula to ``every clause is true''\n    33\t(lines 35--36). \\texttt{WidthAtMost k F} says every clause has at most\n    34\t\\(k\\) literals as a set (line 38). Over a finite variable type,\n    35\t\\texttt{satisfyingAssignments F} filters the universe of all assignments\n    36\t(lines 44--45), and \\texttt{count F} is its cardinality (line 47): the\n    37\tnumber of satisfying assignments among all \\(2^n\\) when\n    38\t\\(V = \\mathtt{Fin}\\ n\\). The \\texttt{Finset} choices change nothing in the\n    39\tmeaning: a repeated clause or literal collapses, the empty clause is\n    40\tallowed and unsatisfiable, a tautological clause \\(\\{x, \\neg x\\}\\) is\n    41\tallowed and counts two toward the width. That is consistent with the\n    42\tmanuscript's ``Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than \\(k\\)\n    43\tliterals'' (p.~1, line~44).\n    44\t\n    45\t\\paragraph{The input encoding (lines 53--81).}\n    46\t\\texttt{encodeNat n} is \\(n\\) ones followed by a zero (line 55): unary.\n    47\t\\texttt{frame} doubles a bit string, prefixing each bit with a one, and\n    48\tends it with a zero (lines 57--58). \\texttt{encodeList enc} prefixes each\n    49\telement's frame with a one and ends the list with a zero (lines 60--62).\n    50\tA literal becomes the number \\(2\\cdot\\mathrm{var} + [\\text{positive}]\\),\n    51\tat most \\(2n - 1\\) (lines 64--65); a clause is its sorted list of literal\n    52\tnumbers, a formula its sorted list of clauses (lines 67--71).\n    53\t\\texttt{encodeCNF F} is \\texttt{encodeNat n} followed by the formula's\n    54\tlist of lists of unary numbers (lines 73--77), and\n    55\t\\texttt{formulaBitLength F} is the length of that bit string (line 79).\n    56\tTwo things follow. The number of variables \\(n\\) is part of the input,\n    57\tso a variable that occurs in no clause is still counted. And the length\n    58\tis polynomial: a clause of \\(j \\le k\\) literals costs \\(2j(4n+1)+4\\) bits,\n    59\tso\n    60\t\\[\n    61\t  L \\;=\\; \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F \\;\\le\\; n + 2 + m\\,\\bigl(2k(4n+1) + 4\\bigr)\n    62\t  \\;=\\; O(k\\,n\\,m)\n    63\t\\]\n    64\tfor \\(m\\) clauses. The solution itself proves\n    65\t\\((L+1)^d \\le (8k+7)^d\\,(n + |F| + 1)^{2d}\\)\n    66\t(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Statement.lean}, lines 9--14). Emulating the\n    67\tdefinitions (\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}),\n    68\ta formula with \\(n = 125\\), \\(m = 500\\), \\(k = 4\\) encodes to\n    69\t\\(L = 1{,}006{,}223\\) bits and one with \\(n = 1{,}000\\), \\(m = 4{,}000\\),\n    70\t\\(k = 3\\) to \\(47.9\\) million, both under the bound. Nothing in the\n    71\tencoding is exponential; the unary numbers are linear in \\(n\\).\n    72\t\n    73\t\\paragraph{The machine (lines 83--128).}\n    74\t\\texttt{PrimitiveMachine} (lines 92--99) is a finite object: a number\n    75\t\\texttt{tapeCount} of tapes and a number \\texttt{stateCount} of states,\n    76\tan input tape and a start state, a table \\texttt{scan} saying which tape\n    77\teach state reads, and a table \\texttt{transition} from\n    78\t\\(\\mathtt{Fin}\\ \\mathtt{stateCount} \\times \\mathtt{Option\\ Bool}\\)---a\n    79\tdomain of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) points---into either a halting\n    80\tanswer (a \\texttt{Bool}) or a next state with one action. The action type\n    81\tis Mathlib's \\texttt{Turing.TM0.Stmt (Option Bool)}: move the head left or\n    82\tright, or write one of the three symbols \\texttt{none}, \\texttt{some\n    83\tfalse}, \\texttt{some true}, five values in all\n    84\t(\\path{Mathlib/Computability/TuringMachine/PostTuringMachine.lean}\n    85\tlines 118--120 and \\path{Tape.lean} lines 421--423 at Mathlib\n    86\t\\texttt{d13f23b7}, the clone's pinned revision). A configuration\n    87\t\\texttt{Cfg} (lines 103--105) is a state, halted or not, and one Mathlib\n    88\t\\texttt{Tape} per tape index (a head symbol and two blank-padded halves,\n    89\t\\path{Tape.lean} lines 408--414, blank \\(=\\) \\texttt{none}).\n    90\t\\texttt{step} (lines 107--119) returns \\texttt{none} in a halted state;\n    91\totherwise it reads the head of the one tape the state scans, looks the\n    92\tpair up in the table, and either halts with the answer, or writes one\n    93\tsymbol or moves that one head by one cell and changes state. \\texttt{init}\n    94\t(lines 121--124) puts the input on the input tape with the head at its\n    95\tfirst bit and every other tape blank (\\texttt{Tape.mk}$_1$,\n    96\t\\path{Tape.lean} line 494). A Lean function on a finite domain is,\n    97\twhatever term defines it, a finite table; so a step is one lookup in a\n    98\ttable of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) rows, and no field of the\n    99\tstructure can carry unbounded computation.\n   100\t\n   101\t\\paragraph{Traces (lines 87--90).}\n   102\t\\texttt{Trace step t a c} is an inductive proposition with two\n   103\tconstructors: \\texttt{refl}, a trace of length 0 from \\(a\\) to \\(a\\)\n   104\titself, and \\texttt{cons}, which extends a trace from \\(b\\) by one\n   105\tsuccessful step \\(a \\mapsto b\\). So it holds exactly when \\(c\\) is\n   106\treached from \\(a\\) by \\(t\\) successful applications of \\texttt{step}, and\n   107\ta trace of length 0 to a different configuration is impossible. Since\n   108\t\\texttt{step} is a function, the run is deterministic.\n   109\t\n   110\t\\paragraph{Space monitoring (lines 147--189).}\n   111\t\\texttt{LocatedCfg} (lines 149--153) pairs a configuration with, per tape,\n   112\tthe head's current position and the least and greatest positions seen so\n   113\tfar, as integers; \\texttt{locatedInit} starts every head at 0 with the\n   114\tinput tape's range covering the input (lines 155--159);\n   115\t\\texttt{headDisplacement} is \\(-1\\), \\(+1\\) or \\(0\\) for a move left, a\n   116\tmove right or a write (lines 161--164); \\texttt{monitor} (lines 166--182)\n   117\tapplies the same table lookup to update the position and the running\n   118\tminimum and maximum of the scanned tape, and nothing else;\n   119\t\\texttt{locatedStep} is \\texttt{step} on the configuration followed by\n   120\t\\texttt{monitor} (lines 184--186). \\texttt{visitedSpace} (lines 188--189)\n   121\tis the sum over tapes of \\(\\mathrm{upper} - \\mathrm{lower} + 1\\): the\n   122\tnumber of cells whose head position was ever visited. \\texttt{countWord a}\n   123\tis the binary expansion of \\(a\\), least significant bit first, with\n   124\t\\texttt{[false]} for \\(0\\) (line 147), and \\texttt{toNat} (lines 7--9)\n   125\tdecodes such a word.\n   126\t\n   127\t\\paragraph{The exponent and the bound (lines 130--141).}\n   128\t\\begin{quote}\\small\n   129\t\\texttt{runtimeScale n = n * (Real.log (Real.log (n + 16)))\\^{}2 /}\\\\\n   130\t\\texttt{\\quad Real.sqrt (Real.log (n + 16))}\n   131\t\\end{quote}\n   132\t(lines 132--134, transcribed; the casts to \\(\\mathbb{R}\\) omitted), that is\n   133\t\\[\n   134\t  \\rhon \\;=\\; \\frac{n\\,\\bigl(\\ln\\ln(n+16)\\bigr)^2}{\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}},\n   135\t\\]\n   136\twith \\texttt{Real.log} the natural logarithm. The manuscript fixes the\n   137\tsame base: ``Throughout, log denotes the natural logarithm'' (p.~1, lines\n   138\t48--49; again at p.~7, lines 326--327). The shift by 16 keeps\n   139\t\\(\\ln\\ln\\) defined and positive at every \\(n \\ge 0\\) (\\(\\ln 16 = 2.77\\)),\n   140\tso the theorem can be stated for all \\(n\\) with no proviso. Then\n   141\t\\begin{quote}\n   142\t\\texttt{runtimeBound C A d L n = C * (L + 1)\\^{}d * 2\\^{}(A * runtimeScale n)}\n   143\t\\end{quote}\n   144\t(lines 136--139, transcribed), a real number, with the real-exponent\n   145\tpower. We write \\(\\rhon\\) for \\texttt{runtimeScale n} throughout the\n   146\tpaper.\n   147\t\n   148\t\\subsection{The theorem, and the order of its quantifiers}\n   149\t\n   150\tLines 194--217 state, for every \\(k\\) with \\(2 \\le k\\):\n   151\t\\[\n   152\t\\small\n   153\t\\begin{array}{l}\n   154\t\\exists\\ \\mathtt{machine},\\ \\mathtt{countTape},\\ \\mathtt{answerTape},\\\n   155\t  C, A, S \\in \\mathbb{R},\\ d, e \\in \\mathbb{N},\\\\[2pt]\n   156\t\\quad 0 < C \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < A \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < S \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < d \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < e\\ \\wedge\\\\[2pt]\n   157\t\\quad \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ \\forall\\, F : \\mathtt{CNF}\\ (\\mathtt{Fin}\\ n),\\\n   158\t  \\mathtt{WidthAtMost}\\ k\\ F \\;\\rightarrow\\\\[2pt]\n   159\t\\qquad \\exists\\ \\mathtt{time} \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ \\mathtt{final} : \\mathtt{LocatedCfg}\\ \\mathtt{machine},\\\\[2pt]\n   160\t\\qquad\\quad \\mathtt{Trace}\\ (\\mathtt{locatedStep}\\ \\mathtt{machine})\\ \\mathtt{time}\\\n   161\t  (\\mathtt{locatedInit}\\ \\mathtt{machine}\\ (\\mathtt{encodeCNF}\\ F))\\ \\mathtt{final}\\\\[2pt]\n   162\t\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\text{(lines 201--202)}\\\\[2pt]\n   163\t\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the count tape holds \\texttt{countWord (count F)}}\n   164\t  \\quad\\text{(203--204)}\\\\[2pt]\n   165\t\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the answer tape holds the bit \\([0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]\\)}\n   166\t  \\quad\\text{(205--206)}\\\\[2pt]\n   167\t\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the halting state carries that bit}\n   168\t  \\quad\\text{(207)}\\\\[2pt]\n   169\t\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\mathtt{toNat}\\ (\\mathtt{countWord}\\ (\\mathtt{count}\\ F)) = \\mathtt{count}\\ F\n   170\t  \\quad\\text{(208)}\\\\[2pt]\n   171\t\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ |\\mathtt{countWord}\\ (\\mathtt{count}\\ F)| \\le n + 1\n   172\t  \\quad\\text{(209)}\\\\[2pt]\n   173\t\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\mathtt{time} \\le C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\,\\rhon},\\ L = \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F\n   174\t  \\quad\\text{(210--211)}\\\\[2pt]\n   175\t\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\forall\\ \\text{prefix of the trace},\\\n   176\t  \\mathtt{visitedSpace} \\le S\\,(L+1)^e\n   177\t  \\quad\\text{(212--217).}\n   178\t\\end{array}\n   179\t\\]\n   180\tThree features of the order matter.\n   181\t\n   182\t\\emph{The machine and the five constants come after \\(k\\) and before\n   183\t\\(n\\) and \\(F\\).} They may depend on \\(k\\) and on nothing else; inside\n   184\tthe universal quantifier only \\texttt{time} and \\texttt{final} are\n   185\tchosen. So for each \\(k\\) one fixed finite machine serves every formula\n   186\tof every size, and \\(C, A, S, d, e\\) are the same for all of them: the\n   187\tstandard \\(O_k(1)\\) reading. The table's size is \\(O_k(1)\\) in the same\n   188\tsense, with no explicit function of \\(k\\).\n   189\t\n   190\t\\emph{There is no lower bound on \\(n\\).} The manuscript's Theorem~1.1\n   191\tand Proposition~6.4 are stated for \\(n \\ge 20\\) (p.~2, lines 56--57;\n   192\tp.~43, lines 2474--2475), and the solution's auxiliary\n   193\t\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} keeps \\(20 \\le n\\)\n   194\t(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 104--109); the exported\n   195\ttheorem does not. The \\(+16\\) in \\(\\rhon\\) is what makes that possible.\n   196\t\n   197\t\\emph{The constants are existential.} The statement asserts that values\n   198\tof \\(C, A, S, d, e\\) exist; it names none. The proof obtains them from\n   199\texistential lemmas and never evaluates them: at the point where the\n   200\tlarge-input run is assembled, the exponent constant is set to\n   201\t\\(A = 256 + A_t\\) with \\(A_t \\ge 1\\) taken from a leaf contract\n   202\t(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 51;\n   203\t\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean}, lines 792--793), and\n   204\tthe degree and the polynomial constants are built the same way. So the\n   205\tproof as written has \\(A \\ge 257\\), and no closed value of any of the\n   206\tfive. The witness machine is explicit in one respect: it is compiled\n   207\tfrom a program over 80 one-bit registers and 1,220 tapes, with\n   208\t\\(\\mathtt{countTape} = 256\\) and \\(\\mathtt{answerTape} = 1216\\)\n   209\t(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 98--101); its state count is\n   210\ta finite type's cardinality that nothing computes. Section~\\ref{sec:proof}\n   211\tis the inspection of the proof; this section only reads what the\n   212\tstatement fixes and what it leaves open.\n   213\t\n   214\t\\subsection{The exponent, in numbers}\n   215\t\n   216\tEverything the title says rests on one function. With \\(y = \\ln(n+16)\\),\n   217\t\\[\n   218\t  \\frac{\\rhon}{n} \\;=\\; \\frac{(\\ln y)^2}{\\sqrt{y}},\n   219\t\\]\n   220\twhich rises from \\(0.64\\) at \\(n = 1\\), crosses \\(1\\) between\n   221\t\\(n = 49\\) and \\(n = 50\\), peaks at \\(16/e^2 = 2.165\\) where\n   222\t\\(\\ln y = 4\\), i.e.\\ \\(y = e^4\\), \\(n = e^{e^4} - 16 \\approx 10^{23.7}\\),\n   223\tand returns below \\(1\\) only where \\((\\ln y)^2 = \\sqrt{y}\\) again, at\n   224\t\\(y = 5503.7\\), i.e.\\ \\(n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx 10^{2390}\\).\n   225\tTable~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale} gives the values and\n   226\tFigures~\\ref{fig:central} and \\ref{fig:runtime-scale} draw them; every\n   227\tnumber is printed by \\repofile{paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py}\n   228\t(run under \\texttt{python3 -I}; it imports nothing from the clone and\n   229\tcomputes in \\(y\\), so that no \\(n\\) beyond floating-point range is ever\n   230\tformed). The crossing at 50 is a fact about integers:\n   231\t\\(\\rho(49)/49 = 0.9994\\) and \\(\\rho(50)/50 = 1.0027\\). In the limit\n   232\t\\(\\rhon/n \\to 0\\), since \\((\\ln y)^2/\\sqrt{y} \\to 0\\): the exponent is\n   233\t\\(o(n)\\), and that is a theorem in our companion Lean\n   234\t\\leanref{rho-little-o}. Both facts are true at once, and the second is\n   235\tthe one the title is about: the function is \\(o(n)\\), and it is above\n   236\t\\(n\\) on every size anyone will ever write down.\n   237\t\n   238\t\\begin{table}[htbp]\n   239\t\\centering\n   240\t\\caption{The verified exponent relative to \\(n\\). \\(\\rhon = n(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}\\),\n   241\tthe comparator's \\texttt{runtimeScale} (lines 132--134), natural logarithms.\n   242\tThe verified bound is \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\); at \\(A = 1\\) its exponential\n   243\tfactor is \\(2^{(\\rhon/n)\\cdot n}\\), against \\(2^n\\) for trying every assignment.\n   244\tRegenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}\n   245\t\\label{tab:runtime-scale}\n   246\t\\input{tables/runtime_scale}\n   247\t\\end{table}\n   248\t\n   249\t\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n   250\t\\centering\n   251\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n.pdf}\\\\[4pt]\n   252\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n_practical.pdf}\n   253\t\\caption{The verified exponent against brute force. Top: \\(\\rhon/n\\) over\n   254\tthe whole range, the axis in \\(\\log_{10} n\\) from \\(n = 10\\) to\n   255\t\\(n = 10^{3000}\\); the dashed level is \\(2^n\\); beneath it the manuscript's two\n   256\tsharper exponents, \\(n\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}\\) (Theorem~1.1, general) and\n   257\t\\(n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}\\) (fixed clause density), which are not in the Lean\n   258\tlibrary (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}). Bottom: the same three curves over the\n   259\tpractical range \\(n = 10\\) to \\(10^6\\), with \\(\\rhon/n\\) marked at\n   260\t\\(n = 50, 125, 10^3, 10^4, 10^6\\). All curves at the constant \\(A = 1\\);\n   261\tthe verified \\(A\\) is unknown. Regenerate:\n   262\t\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/rho\\_over\\_n.py}.}\n   263\t\\label{fig:central}\n   264\t\\end{figure}\n   265\t\n   266\t\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n   267\t\\centering\n   268\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{runtime_scale.pdf}\n   269\t\\caption{\\(\\rhon/n\\) alone, on a logarithmic axis in \\(\\log_{10} n\\) from\n   270\t\\(n = 10\\) to \\(n = 10^{2500}\\), with the level \\(1\\) (brute force, \\(2^n\\)),\n   271\tthe first integer above the level (\\(n = 50\\)), the peak (\\(16/e^2 = 2.17\\)\n   272\tat \\(\\ln(n+16) = e^4\\), \\(n \\approx 10^{23.7}\\)), the return below the level\n   273\t(\\(n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx 10^{2390}\\)), and the practical range\n   274\t\\(50 \\le n \\le 10^6\\) shaded. Regenerate:\n   275\t\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}\n   276\t\\label{fig:runtime-scale}\n   277\t\\end{figure}\n   278\t\n   279\t\\subsection{The title's claim, made precise}\n   280\t\n   281\tThe title says the verified algorithm is slower than trying all \\(2^n\\)\n   282\tassignments at every practical size. Read against the statement, that is\n   283\tthe following, and nothing more.\n   284\t\n   285\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   286\t\\item \\textbf{What is verified is a guarantee, not a running time.} The\n   287\t  theorem says the machine halts within \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\) steps.\n   288\t  It does not say how many steps the machine takes on any input, and no\n   289\t  implementation of the machine exists to be timed. A bound above\n   290\t  \\(2^n\\) does not say the machine is slow; it says the proof promises\n   291\t  nothing better than \\(2^n\\) there.\n   292\t\\item \\textbf{The constants \\(C, A, S, d, e\\) are existential and never\n   293\t  computed.} The comparator asserts their existence; the proof builds\n   294\t  them from existential lemmas (above, with \\(A \\ge 257\\) as\n   295\t  constructed) and states no value. The machine's state count is likewise\n   296\t  never computed.\n   297\t\\item \\textbf{With \\(A = 1\\), the verified exponent exceeds \\(n\\) for\n   298\t  every \\(50 \\le n < e^{5504}\\).} That is Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}:\n   299\t  the exponential factor of the bound is at least \\(2^n\\) from 50\n   300\t  variables up to about \\(10^{2390}\\), and \\(2^{2.17\\,n}\\) at its\n   301\t  worst. With the true \\(A\\), whatever it is, the factor is\n   302\t  \\(2^{A\\rhon} \\ge 2^{\\rhon}\\) once \\(A \\ge 1\\), and the proof's own\n   303\t  construction has \\(A \\ge 257\\); so nothing is known that would put the\n   304\t  bound below \\(2^n\\) at any practical size.\n   305\t\\item \\textbf{Hence, at every practical size, the verified guarantee is\n   306\t  weaker than the guarantee of trying all assignments.} Trying all \\(2^n\\)\n   307\t  assignments and checking each against the \\(m\\) clauses counts the\n   308\t  models in \\(2^n \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)\\) steps on any reasonable\n   309\t  machine, with constants one can write down. The verified guarantee is\n   310\t  \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\) with \\(\\rhon > n\\) on \\(50 \\le n < e^{5504}\\)\n   311\t  and \\(C, A, d\\) unknown. For every \\(C \\ge 1\\), \\(A \\ge 1\\) and\n   312\t  \\(d \\ge 1\\) the verified bound is above \\(2^n\\) on every \\(n\\) in that\n   313\t  range, and the only values on record are the proof's, which has\n   314\t  \\(1 \\le C\\) and \\(1 \\le A\\) in the large-input run's own statement\n   315\t  (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 12) and\n   316\t  \\(A \\ge 257\\) in its construction. A proof with \\(A < 1\\) would be a\n   317\t  different proof; none is on record.\n   318\t\\end{enumerate}\n   319\t\n   320\tNone of this is a defect of the formalisation or of the proof; it is\n   321\twhat an \\(O_k(1)\\) statement with a \\(2^{o(n)}\\) exponent of this shape\n   322\tmeans when the exponent's constants are not tracked. The manuscript\n   323\titself calls \\(\\rhon\\) ``the weaker exponent'' (p.~2, line~63), and its\n   324\tsharper exponents are not in the Lean library (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n   325\tOne more detail of base: the solution's internal \\(\\rho\\)\n   326\t(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean}, lines 10--11) is in base 2 and\n   327\tis related to \\texttt{runtimeScale} by\n   328\t\\(\\mathtt{runtimeScale}\\ n \\le 4\\,\\rho_2(n)\\) for \\(n \\ge 20\\)\n   329\t(lines 190--191); the base changes the exponent by a constant factor\n   330\tthat \\(A\\) absorbs, so nothing above depends on it.\n   331\t\n   332\t\\subsection{What the statement does establish}\n   333\t\n   334\tFor each fixed \\(k \\ge 2\\), a deterministic machine that, on the canonical\n   335\tencoding of any \\(k\\)-CNF on \\(n\\) variables with \\(m\\) clauses, halts with\n   336\tthe exact number of satisfying assignments in binary on a named tape,\n   337\twithin \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\) steps, where \\(L = O(k\\,n\\,m)\\) is\n   338\tpolynomial in \\(n + m\\) and \\(\\rhon = o(n)\\); and, because a\n   339\t\\texttt{Finset} of clauses of width at most \\(k\\) has at most\n   340\t\\(\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j} = O(n^k)\\) members (\\(2{,}604{,}376\\) at\n   341\t\\(n = 125\\), \\(k = 3\\)), the whole bound is \\(2^{o(n)}\\) in \\(n\\) alone.\n   342\tThat is a deterministic \\(2^{o(n)} \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)\\) algorithm\n   343\tfor \\#\\(k\\)-SAT in the standard sense, and therefore for \\(k\\)-SAT; it\n   344\trefutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis \\citep{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001} as\n   345\tstated. The statement reads as the manuscript's verified form, the\n   346\tweaker exponent of its Proposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476), without\n   347\tthe \\(n \\ge 20\\) proviso. We found no field, definition or quantifier\n   348\tthrough which the statement could be kernel-true and mean something\n   349\tweaker than this; the audit it summarises is\n   350\t\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/statement_audit.md}. Our own\n   351\trestatement of the statement as a Lean \\texttt{Prop}, character for\n   352\tcharacter over a verbatim copy of the machine model, is\n   353\t\\leanref{subexpcount}; it is never proved and never an axiom there\n   354\t(Section~\\ref{sec:lean}).\n   355\t\n   356\t\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n   357\t\n   358\t\\begin{itemize}\n   359\t\\item \\textbf{No running time of any machine.} Nothing here measures or\n   360\t  estimates how long the verified machine, or any program, takes on any\n   361\t  input. The title compares two \\emph{guarantees}; Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}\n   362\t  is the only place in the paper where anything is timed, and it times\n   363\t  ordinary solvers, not this machine.\n   364\t\\item \\textbf{No statement about the proof.} That the statement means\n   365\t  what we say it means is a reading of 220 lines; whether the proof of\n   366\t  it is sound is Section~\\ref{sec:proof} and Section~\\ref{sec:replay},\n   367\t  which found no flaw. The one proof-side fact quoted here, \\(A = 256 +\n   368\t  A_t\\), is a line of the solution read for the value it assigns, not a\n   369\t  claim about the argument.\n   370\t\\item \\textbf{No value of the constants.} \\(A \\ge 257\\) is what the\n   371\t  proof writes down, not what the machine needs; a different proof of\n   372\t  the same statement could have any \\(A > 0\\). The statement itself\n   373\t  fixes nothing.\n   374\t\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the manuscript's sharper exponents}, which\n   375\t  are not in the Lean library and are Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}'s subject.\n   376\t\\item \\textbf{No claim about sizes beyond \\(e^{5504}\\).} Past\n   377\t  \\(n \\approx 10^{2390}\\) the verified factor is below \\(2^n\\) at\n   378\t  \\(A = 1\\); we say nothing about that range except that it is not a\n   379\t  practical one.\n   380\t\\end{itemize}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Y3eqeFAX1Rc3b2HmcDGBzy","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
