{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ShV6Lk3nShYs1JHqJ3b1kV","type":"tool_result","content":"     1\t% Independent replay -- drafted by loop0015 item 01 (2026-11-05).\n     2\t% Every number here is read from paper3/replay/logs/ (regenerate: re-run\n     3\t% ~/dev/untrusted/replay/run_sandbox.sh as paper3/replay/README.md records;\n     4\t% the counts below come from `grep -c` on the copied logs, see\n     5\t% paper3/replay/logs/run2/counts.txt).\n     6\t\\section{Independent replay}\n     7\t\\label{sec:replay}\n     8\t\n     9\tThe repository holds no recorded \\texttt{\\#print axioms} output for the\n    10\tfamily, no continuous integration that builds its Lean, and\n    11\t\\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false} in the comparator's JSON\n    12\t(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). We therefore built the solution's import\n    13\tclosure ourselves, in isolation, at the toolchain the repository pins, and\n    14\trecorded what the kernel reports. This section says exactly what was run,\n    15\twhere, and what came out; the scripts are quoted verbatim in\n    16\t\\repofile{paper3/replay/README.md}, and the logs are under\n    17\t\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/}.\n    18\t\n    19\t\\subsection{What was run, and where}\n    20\t\n    21\tA fresh copy of the clone (commit \\texttt{4eaee875}) was built inside a\n    22\t\\texttt{bwrap} (bubblewrap) sandbox on the project's machine: the system\n    23\tdirectories bound read-only, a private \\texttt{tmpfs} home with nothing of\n    24\tthe real home or of this repository visible, separate PID, IPC and UTS\n    25\tnamespaces, the whole sandbox confined to eight cores\n    26\t(\\texttt{taskset -c 0-7}) at the lowest scheduling priority, and the\n    27\tnetwork on, because the repository's \\texttt{lakefile} clones its 41\n    28\tdependencies at build time and the Mathlib object files come from the\n    29\tMathlib cache. Inside, \\texttt{elan} was installed into the private home\n    30\tand fetched the pinned toolchain from the repository's own\n    31\t\\texttt{lean-toolchain} file; then, in the clone's \\texttt{lean/}\n    32\tdirectory, \\texttt{lake exe cache get}, \\texttt{lake build\n    33\tOAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main} (the solution's closure, not the\n    34\tcollection's default target, which builds all 721 families), and\n    35\t\\texttt{\\#print axioms} of the one theorem the comparator names,\n    36\t\\texttt{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat},\n    37\tthrough \\texttt{lake env lean}. Only text left the sandbox; nothing built\n    38\tinside it was executed outside it.\n    39\t\n    40\tTwo runs were needed. The first, started at 01:03 on 2026-11-05, failed\n    41\tat once at the build step because its command line carried \\texttt{-j 8},\n    42\tan option this version of Lake does not have (the whole of its build log\n    43\tis the one line \\texttt{error: unknown short option '-j'}); nothing of\n    44\tthe family was compiled. The second, started at 01:16 with that option\n    45\tremoved and the core cap moved to \\texttt{taskset}, is the run reported\n    46\tbelow. Both scripts, both versions, and the launch command are in the\n    47\tREADME; the first run's logs are kept beside the second's.\n    48\t\n    49\t\\subsection{What came out}\n    50\t\n    51\tThe build finished with exit code~0 in 33~minutes of wall clock on\n    52\teight cores (01:16:25 to 01:49:31), compiling \\textbf{3,091} modules\n    53\twith the prefix \\texttt{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT}---exactly the\n    54\timport closure the inspection of Section~\\ref{sec:proof} enumerated, the\n    55\tsame 3,091 names with an empty set difference both ways\n    56\t(\\repofile{paper3/replay/counts.sh}; Lake's own job total,\n    57\t\\texttt{12014}, counts the cached Mathlib and dependency modules, none of\n    58\twhich was compiled). The log has no line containing \\texttt{error} and\n    59\tnone containing \\texttt{sorry}; its eleven \\texttt{warning} lines are\n    60\tLake noting that the lakefile's compatibility patches leave eleven\n    61\tdependencies with ``local changes'', and none of the eleven is Mathlib.\n    62\tEight source files of the closure themselves carry nineteen\n    63\t\\texttt{\\#print axioms} commands (seventeen files of the family's\n    64\tdirectory do, but nine of those are never built), so the build log\n    65\trecords nineteen axiom lines as it goes, every one\n    66\t\\texttt{[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]}; among them\n    67\t\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 197--201, prints the axioms of\n    68\t\\texttt{compiled\\_exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat},\n    69\t\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth}, \\texttt{sat\\_polynomial\\_space} and\n    70\t\\texttt{original\\_sat\\_main}. The separate \\texttt{\\#print axioms} run\n    71\tafter the build gives, for the comparator's theorem,\n    72\t\\begin{quote}\n    73\t\\texttt{'OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat'\n    74\tdepends on axioms: [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]}\n    75\t\\end{quote}\n    76\t(\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/run2/axioms.txt}). The versions are\n    77\t\\texttt{Lean (version 4.34.1, commit 5045d005…, Release)},\n    78\t\\texttt{Lake version 5.0.0-src+5045d00}, and Mathlib at\n    79\t\\texttt{d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612} from the clone's own\n    80\tmanifest, with its object files from the Mathlib cache (8,908 files).\n    81\tThe replay copy is at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}, the same as the pristine\n    82\tclone, and the comparator file and the solution's files hash identically\n    83\tin both (\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/run2/hashes.txt}; the comparator\n    84\tstatement's SHA-256 begins \\texttt{4183b205}). The full build log is\n    85\tkept out of the repository by size; its SHA-256\n    86\t(\\texttt{d313f594…}) and its last 200 lines are committed.\n    87\t\n    88\tSo the sentence every later section relies on reads: \\emph{replayed}---the\n    89\tsolution's closure builds from source at the pinned toolchain with\n    90\tupstream Mathlib, and the kernel reports the three standard axioms and\n    91\tnothing else for the theorem the comparator names. This moves the first\n    92\tthree items of the inspection's trust boundary (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}):\n    93\tthe build is no longer OpenAI's unpublished one, the \\texttt{\\#print axioms}\n    94\toutput is now on record, and the Mathlib revision was fetched from\n    95\tupstream and not patched.\n    96\t\n    97\t\\subsection{The second kernel}\n    98\t\n    99\tThe comparator's JSON for the family has \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false}:\n   100\tOpenAI's check of this family ran the Lean kernel only. We ran a second\n   101\tkernel, in the same sandbox, after the build\n   102\t(\\repofile{paper3/replay/nanoda.sh}, recorded in the README before it\n   103\tran): \\texttt{lean4export} (tag \\texttt{v4.34.0}, commit\n   104\t\\texttt{076e8e57}, built with the pinned \\texttt{v4.34.1} toolchain so\n   105\tthat it reads the object files the build produced) exported\n   106\t\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} and its transitive dependencies from the\n   107\tbuilt environment---2,355,666,449 bytes of NDJSON, 43,633,742 lines,\n   108\tformat version 3.1.0, Lean githash \\texttt{5045d005…}, in 68~seconds---and\n   109\t\\texttt{nanoda\\_lib} \\citep{Nanoda} (version 0.4.31, commit\n   110\t\\texttt{8de7e0c4}, 2026-10-29; an external type checker for Lean~4\n   111\twritten in Rust, which shares no code with the Lean kernel) checked that\n   112\texport with \\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Classical.choice} and\n   113\t\\texttt{Quot.sound} as the permitted axioms and the \\texttt{Nat} and\n   114\t\\texttt{String} literal extensions on. Its output\n   115\t(\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/nanoda/nanoda_check.txt}):\n   116\t\\begin{quote}\n   117\t\\texttt{Checked 95176 declarations with no errors}\n   118\t\\end{quote}\n   119\tin 110~seconds on eight cores, exit code~0, after printing back the\n   120\ttheorem's statement and the three axioms from its own parse of the\n   121\texport. The export contains exactly three \\texttt{axiom} declarations\n   122\t(\\texttt{grep -c '\"axiom\"'} on the file), so nothing else could have been\n   123\tpermitted. The export file (SHA-256 \\texttt{bdbc9afe…},\n   124\t\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/nanoda/export_sha256.txt}) stays in the\n   125\tsandbox directory by size; the configuration, the build logs and the\n   126\tchecker's output are committed.\n   127\t\n   128\t\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n   129\t\n   130\t\\begin{itemize}\n   131\t\\item The replay trusts the Lean kernel of the pinned toolchain\n   132\t  (\\texttt{v4.34.1}) and upstream Mathlib at the pinned revision; it\n   133\t  moves the proof's trust from ``OpenAI's unpublished build'' to ``a\n   134\t  build we ran'', not from the Lean kernel to anything else.\n   135\t  The second kernel removes the Lean kernel from the list for the\n   136\t  theorem's dependencies as exported, but adds \\texttt{lean4export}\n   137\t  (that it exports what the environment holds) and \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib}\n   138\t  (that it checks what it parses) to it; two independent checkers\n   139\t  agreeing is evidence, not a proof that either is correct.\n   140\t\\item It is not the comparator run. The comparator checks that the\n   141\t  solution's exported statement matches the challenge's by\n   142\t  \\texttt{lean4export}; we did not run that tool chain, and the\n   143\t  statement-level match the paper relies on is the textual one of\n   144\t  Section~\\ref{sec:proof} (the 35 definitions and the theorem statement,\n   145\t  identical character for character after whitespace normalisation, two\n   146\t  of them up to the \\texttt{noncomputable} keyword).\n   147\t\\item The counts above are counts of lines in one build's log on one\n   148\t  machine with the named versions; a different Lake would print different\n   149\t  job totals (the 12,014 includes the cached Mathlib modules), and a\n   150\t  different machine a different time.\n   151\t\\item Nothing here bears on what the theorem \\emph{says}\n   152\t  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}) or on the manuscript's unverified\n   153\t  exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}); a replayed proof of a weak bound\n   154\t  is a replayed proof of a weak bound.\n   155\t\\end{itemize}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"3df9f286-7272-4f18-8375-54d3ca67b2fe","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:31:18.948Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"     1\t% Independent replay -- drafted by loop0015 item 01 (2026-11-05).\n     2\t% Every number here is read from paper3/replay/logs/ (regenerate: re-run\n     3\t% ~/dev/untrusted/replay/run_sandbox.sh as paper3/replay/README.md records;\n     4\t% the counts below come from `grep -c` on the copied logs, see\n     5\t% paper3/replay/logs/run2/counts.txt).\n     6\t\\section{Independent replay}\n     7\t\\label{sec:replay}\n     8\t\n     9\tThe repository holds no recorded \\texttt{\\#print axioms} output for the\n    10\tfamily, no continuous integration that builds its Lean, and\n    11\t\\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false} in the comparator's JSON\n    12\t(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). We therefore built the solution's import\n    13\tclosure ourselves, in isolation, at the toolchain the repository pins, and\n    14\trecorded what the kernel reports. This section says exactly what was run,\n    15\twhere, and what came out; the scripts are quoted verbatim in\n    16\t\\repofile{paper3/replay/README.md}, and the logs are under\n    17\t\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/}.\n    18\t\n    19\t\\subsection{What was run, and where}\n    20\t\n    21\tA fresh copy of the clone (commit \\texttt{4eaee875}) was built inside a\n    22\t\\texttt{bwra