{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D5eiuEXxaqWyQhX7cSv4LG","type":"tool_result","content":"     1\t% What follows, in general terms -- drafted by loop0015 item 05 (2026-11-05).\n     2\t% Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of\n     3\t% reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the line + 1);\n     4\t% every comparator line is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone, read with cat/sed -n\n     5\t% only. Every page, line, fragment and the one table of this section are checked\n     6\t% and written by\n     7\t%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py\n     8\t% which exits non-zero if anything has moved. The section is in general terms\n     9\t% by the owner's rule (2026-11-05): no example from any width parameter, no\n    10\t% problem named beyond what the manuscript's §1.1.1 and §1.1.3 name.\n    11\t\\section{What follows, in general terms}\n    12\t\\label{sec:consequences}\n    13\t\n    14\tSections~\\ref{sec:statement}--\\ref{sec:replay} read the verified statement,\n    15\tdrew the line between it and the manuscript, inspected the proof and\n    16\treplayed it. This section asks what follows from the verified statement\n    17\talone: which hypotheses it refutes and by what step, and what a lower\n    18\tbound that was derived from one of those hypotheses has lost. The\n    19\tmanuscript answers both questions itself, in \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 (p.~4--5)\n    20\tand \\S1.1.3 (p.~5), and we quote it; what we add is the quantifier form\n    21\tof each hypothesis, the one line of the comparator statement that negates\n    22\tit, and the distinction, stated in general terms, between a theorem whose\n    23\tpremise has become false and a statement that has been refuted. Nothing\n    24\there names a problem the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 do not name, and\n    25\tnothing here is about any problem's complexity beyond what those two\n    26\tsections state.\n    27\t\n    28\t\\subsection{The one property every refutation uses}\n    29\t\\label{sec:consequences-property}\n    30\t\n    31\tEvery refutation below rests on the same conjuncts of\n    32\t\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (comparator file lines 194--217,\n    33\tSection~\\ref{sec:statement}): for each fixed $k \\ge 2$ there is one\n    34\tdeterministic machine which, on the canonical encoding of any\n    35\t\\texttt{WidthAtMost}~$k$ formula $F$ on $n$ variables (line 38), halts\n    36\twith \\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape (lines 203--204, 147),\n    37\tthe bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ on its answer tape and in its halting\n    38\tstate (lines 205--207), within $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps (lines\n    39\t210--211). Four features of that property do the work.\n    40\t\n    41\t\\begin{enumerate}\n    42\t\\item \\textbf{It is deterministic.} \\texttt{step} is a function and\n    43\t  \\texttt{Trace} is its iteration (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there\n    44\t  is no random tape and no nondeterministic choice. A deterministic\n    45\t  algorithm is also a bounded-error randomised algorithm, with error\n    46\t  $0$, and also a nondeterministic algorithm, with no guess; so each\n    47\t  refutation of a deterministic hypothesis is a refutation of its\n    48\t  randomised and nondeterministic forms by the same machine.\n    49\t\\item \\textbf{The bound is $2^{o(n)}$ in $n$ alone.} A \\texttt{Finset} of\n    50\t  clauses of width at most $k$ over $n$ variables has at most\n    51\t  $\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j}$ members ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$,\n    52\t  $k = 3$), so $m = O(n^k)$ and $L = O(k\\,n\\,m) = O(n^{k+1})$\n    53\t  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); hence the bound is\n    54\t  $n^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, and since $\\rhon/n \\to 0$\n    55\t  \\leanref{rho-little-o}, for every $\\delta > 0$ there is an\n    56\t  $n_0(k, \\delta)$ beyond which it is below $2^{\\delta n}$. Below\n    57\t  $n_0$ the bound is a constant depending on $k$ and $\\delta$. So the\n    58\t  running time is $O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every $\\delta > 0$,\n    59\t  and $2^{\\delta n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ with the polynomial written out. This\n    60\t  is an asymptotic fact and it is the only kind of fact the hypotheses\n    61\t  are about; it coexists with Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s observation\n    62\t  that $\\rhon > n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, which is about where\n    63\t  the asymptotics begin.\n    64\t\\item \\textbf{One machine per $k$, for every $n$.} The machine and the\n    65\t  constants are chosen after $k$ and before $n$ and $F$\n    66\t  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there is no $n \\ge 20$ proviso in the\n    67\t  comparator statement, though the manuscript's Theorem~1.1 carries one\n    68\t  (p.~2, lines 56--57). So the verified form is the strong, uniform\n    69\t  negation: a single algorithm that is $O(2^{\\delta n})$ for every\n    70\t  $\\delta$ at once, where a hypothesis of the form ``for every $\\delta$\n    71\t  there is an algorithm'' would have allowed a different algorithm for\n    72\t  each $\\delta$.\n    73\t\\item \\textbf{The model is the conservative one.} The comparator's\n    74\t  machine is a multitape Turing machine with one head per tape, moving\n    75\t  one cell or writing one symbol per step. Every step of such a machine\n    76\t  is a bounded amount of work on any of the standard models in which the\n    77\t  hypotheses are stated, so the bound transfers to those models with at\n    78\t  most a constant factor per step, which the polynomial absorbs. The\n    79\t  input is the manuscript's canonical encoding, of length polynomial in\n    80\t  $n + m$; converting from any other reasonable encoding is polynomial\n    81\t  too.\n    82\t\\end{enumerate}\n    83\t\n    84\t\\subsection{The hypotheses, one by one}\n    85\t\\label{sec:consequences-hypotheses}\n    86\t\n    87\tThe manuscript's own sentence is (p.~4, lines 187--191):\n    88\t\\begin{quote}\\small\n    89\t``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing\n    90\twhether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability\n    91\tand unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for\n    92\tevery fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise\n    93\tproblem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\n    94\trandomized versions of ETH [DHM$^+$14, Section 1] and SETH [CDL$^+$16,\n    95\tDefinition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH.''\n    96\t\\end{quote}\n    97\tand, for counting (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1)\n    98\tsolves \\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time\n    99\tand therefore refutes \\#ETH.'' We take each hypothesis in the form the\n   100\tmanuscript states it, with the manuscript's own source for that form,\n   101\tand say which conjunct negates it. Table~\\ref{tab:hypotheses} is the\n   102\tsummary. In every case the only thing used is the property of\n   103\tSection~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}; nothing is used from the\n   104\tmanuscript's sharper exponents, which are not verified\n   105\t(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n   106\t\n   107\t\\paragraph{ETH.}\n   108\tAs stated (p.~4, lines 165--166): ``The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH)\n   109\tasserted that 3-SAT requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time in the\n   110\tworst case, and hence so does $k$-SAT for every fixed $k \\ge 3$ [IP01,\n   111\tTheorem~1],'' citing \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}. In quantifiers:\n   112\tthere is a $\\delta > 0$ such that no deterministic algorithm decides\n   113\t3-SAT within $2^{\\delta n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps on all inputs.\n   114\tIts negation is: for every $\\delta > 0$ there is one. The verified\n   115\tstatement at $k = 3$ supplies, for every $\\delta > 0$, the same machine,\n   116\twhose answer bit is $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$, that is, the decision,\n   117\twithin $O_{\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps\n   118\t(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}, item 2). This holds on every\n   119\tinput of every size, so it refutes the hypothesis under either reading\n   120\tof ``$2^{\\Omega(n)}$'' (a lower bound at infinitely many $n$, or at all\n   121\tlarge $n$). In the vocabulary of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, where\n   122\t$s_k$ is the infimum of the $\\delta$ for which $k$-SAT has an\n   123\t$O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm and ETH is $s_3 > 0$: the verified statement\n   124\tgives $s_k = 0$ for every $k \\ge 2$, by one algorithm per $k$.\n   125\t\n   126\t\\paragraph{SETH.}\n   127\tAs stated (p.~4, lines 166--169): ``The Strong Exponential Time\n   128\tHypothesis (SETH) made the sharper prediction that $k$-SAT requires\n   129\t$2^{n(1-o_k(1))}$ time as $k$ grows. Precisely, for every\n   130\t$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$, there is a fixed $k$ for which no deterministic\n   131\talgorithm solves $k$-SAT in $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ time,''\n   132\twith the motivation attributed to \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}'s open\n   133\tproblems (lines 169--171). The negation is: there is an\n   134\t$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ such that for every fixed $k$ there is a\n   135\tdeterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm. The verified\n   136\tstatement gives more: for \\emph{every} $\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ and every\n   137\t$k \\ge 2$, the machine for $k$ runs within\n   138\t$C_k(L+1)^{d_k}\\,2^{A_k\\rhon}$ steps, and $A_k\\rhon \\le (1-\\varepsilon)n$\n   139\tfor all $n \\ge n_0(k, \\varepsilon)$ because $\\rhon = o(n)$; below $n_0$\n   140\tthe exponential factor is a constant. So each $k$ has a\n   141\t$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ algorithm, and the polynomial's\n   142\tdegree depending on $k$ is exactly what the hypothesis's ``for a fixed\n   143\t$k$'' permits. The refutation does not pass through ETH; it reads the\n   144\tstatement at each $k$. It is also standard that SETH implies ETH: if\n   145\t3-SAT had a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm then, by the Sparsification Lemma of\n   146\t\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}, which reduces $k$-SAT on $n$ variables\n   147\tto $2^{\\varepsilon n}$ instances with $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ clauses and\n   148\tso, after the clause-splitting reduction, to 3-SAT on $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$\n   149\tvariables, so would $k$-SAT for every fixed $k$; so the refutation of\n   150\tETH alone would already refute SETH. We note the implication for the\n   151\trecord; the direct reading above is what the paper rests on.\n   152\t\n   153\t\\paragraph{Gap-ETH, and its randomised form.}\n   154\tAs stated (p.~4, lines 172--174): ``The Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis\n   155\t(Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants $\\delta, \\gamma > 0$\n   156\tfor which no $O(2^{\\gamma n})$-time algorithm can distinguish a\n   157\tsatisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment violates at least a\n   158\t$\\delta$ fraction of the clauses, even when $m = O(n)$ [Din16, MR17],''\n   159\tciting \\citet{Dinur2016} and \\citet{ManurangsiRaghavendra2017}; and\n   160\t(lines 175--176) ``The randomized version rules out an algorithm with\n   161\tthe same running time that distinguishes the two cases with probability\n   162\tat least $2/3$ on every promised input [CCK$^+$20, Conjecture 2.5],''\n   163\tciting \\citet{Chalermsook2020}. The negation is: for every\n   164\t$\\delta, \\gamma > 0$ there is an $O(2^{\\gamma n})$ algorithm that\n   165\tseparates the two cases, already at $m = O(n)$. Exact decision separates\n   166\tthem, since a formula in which every assignment violates a positive\n   167\tfraction of the clauses is unsatisfiable; and the verified machine at\n   168\t$k = 3$ decides within $n^{O(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon} = O_{\\gamma}(2^{\\gamma n})$\n   169\tsteps for every $\\gamma > 0$, at every $m$ (item 2 above; at $m = O(n)$\n   170\tthe polynomial is $O(n^{2d})$). The promise is never used: the machine\n   171\tanswers correctly on every input, promised or not, with probability $1$,\n   172\twhich refutes the randomised form by item 1.\n   173\t\n   174\t\\paragraph{NSETH.}\n   175\tAs stated (p.~4, lines 181--183): ``The Nondeterministic Strong\n   176\tExponential Time Hypothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every\n   177\t$\\varepsilon > 0$, some fixed $k$ admits no nondeterministic\n   178\t$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}\\allowbreak(n+m)^{O(1)}$-time algorithm for deciding whether a\n   179\t$k$-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a $k$-DNF is a\n   180\ttautology [CGI$^+$16],'' citing \\citet{Carmosino2016}. The negation is:\n   181\tthere is an $\\varepsilon > 0$ such that every fixed $k$ has a\n   182\tnondeterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm for\n   183\tunsatisfiability. The verified machine's answer bit is \\texttt{false}\n   184\texactly when $\\mathtt{count}\\ F = 0$ (lines 205--207), so it decides\n   185\tunsatisfiability deterministically within the SETH bound of the previous\n   186\tparagraph for every $\\varepsilon$ and every $k$; and a deterministic\n   187\tmachine is a nondeterministic one whose only computation path is the\n   188\tdeterministic run, so every acceptance convention for nondeterministic\n   189\ttime is met. The manuscript's ``decides both satisfiability and\n   190\tunsatisfiability'' (line 188) is this.\n   191\t\n   192\t\\paragraph{\\#ETH.}\n   193\tAs stated (p.~4, lines 204--205): ``The counting Exponential Time\n   194\tHypothesis (\\#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying\n   195\tassignments of a 3-CNF requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time\n   196\t[DHM$^+$14, Section 1],'' citing \\citet{Dell2014}. The negation is: for\n   197\tevery $\\delta > 0$ there is a deterministic $O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm\n   198\tthat outputs the exact count. The verified machine at $k = 3$ halts with\n   199\t\\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape, a binary word of length\n   200\tat most $n + 1$ that decodes to $\\mathtt{count}\\ F$ (lines 203--204,\n   201\t208--209), within the same bound. This is the one hypothesis for which\n   202\tthe decision bit is not enough and the count tape is what refutes it;\n   203\tit is also the hypothesis the comparator statement was written for, the\n   204\tcount being the theorem's first conclusion and the decision its\n   205\tcorollary.\n   206\t\n   207\t\\paragraph{The bounded-error randomised ETH and SETH.}\n   208\tThe manuscript names them by citation only (p.~4, lines 190--191):\n   209\t``[DHM$^+$14, Section 1]'' for ETH \\citep{Dell2014} and ``[CDL$^+$16,\n   210\tDefinition 2.1]'' for SETH \\citep{Cygan2016}. In each, the algorithm\n   211\tmay be randomised and must be correct with probability bounded away\n   212\tfrom $1/2$ on every input. A deterministic algorithm is such an\n   213\talgorithm with error $0$ (item 1), so the refutations of ETH and SETH\n   214\tabove refute these forms by the same machine, with no change to the\n   215\tbound. We have not re-read the two sources' definitions; we state the\n   216\tforms as the manuscript cites them, and a reader who needs the exact\n   217\terror threshold or the exact model of the source should go to the\n   218\tcited section and definition.\n   219\t\n   220\t\\begin{table}[htbp]\n   221\t\\caption{The hypotheses the manuscript's \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 list, each in\n   222\tthe form the manuscript states it (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of\n   223\tthe committed extract), with the manuscript's own source for that form,\n   224\tthe quantifier form, and the conjunct of the comparator statement that\n   225\tnegates it (lines 194--217 of the comparator file at \\texttt{4eaee875}).\n   226\t$A$, $C_k$, $d_k$ are the existential constants of\n   227\tSection~\\ref{sec:statement}. Generated, with every page and fragment\n   228\tchecked against the extract and the clone, by\n   229\t\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py}.}\n   230\t\\label{tab:hypotheses}\n   231\t\\scriptsize\n   232\t\\input{tables/hypotheses}\n   233\t\\end{table}\n   234\t\n   235\t\\paragraph{What the manuscript places beside the list.}\n   236\tTwo sentences follow the refutation paragraph (p.~4, lines 191--194):\n   237\tthe result ``does not conflict with unconditional exponential lower\n   238\tbounds for restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13],''\n   239\tand ``In 2016, Ryan Williams refuted the Merlin--Arthur version of SETH\n   240\t(MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies $k$-CNF unsatisfiability\n   241\tin $2^{n/2}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3]''\n   242\t\\citep{Williams2016}. Both are context. The first says that a lower\n   243\tbound proved unconditionally for a restricted proof system is not a\n   244\tstatement about algorithms in general and is not touched; the second\n   245\tthat one member of the family was already false before this result.\n   246\tNeither is used below.\n   247\t\n   248\t\\subsection{What a derived lower bound loses}\n   249\t\\label{sec:consequences-lower-bounds}\n   250\t\n   251\tThe manuscript's \\S1.1.3 opens with a list of lower bounds that were\n   252\tproved from ETH or from SETH (p.~5, lines 213--225), for problems with\n   253\texponential algorithms and for problems in polynomial time, and then\n   254\tsays what its result does to them (lines 226--227):\n   255\t\\begin{quote}\\small\n   256\t``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness\n   257\tclaims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from weaker\n   258\thypotheses that may still hold.''\n   259\t\\end{quote}\n   260\tWe state what that means in general terms, for a theorem of either of\n   261\tthe two standard shapes,\n   262\t\\begin{align*}\n   263\t  \\text{(E)}&\\quad \\text{ETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } 2^{o(n)} \\text{ algorithm for } X,\\\\\n   264\t  \\text{(S)}&\\quad \\text{SETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } (c - \\varepsilon)^{k} \\text{ algorithm for } Y \\text{ for any } \\varepsilon > 0,\n   265\t\\end{align*}\n   266\twhere $n$ and $k$ are whatever parameter of $X$ and $Y$ the theorem\n   267\tnames and $c$ is its constant. Nothing below depends on what $X$ and $Y$\n   268\tare.\n   269\t\n   270\t\\paragraph{The theorem stands; its premise is false; it derives nothing.}\n   271\tA theorem of shape (E) or (S) is an implication, proved by a reduction\n   272\tfrom $k$-SAT to $X$ or $Y$ that turns a fast algorithm for the target\n   273\tinto a fast algorithm for $k$-SAT. The reduction is unaffected by\n   274\tanything in the verified statement; the implication remains a theorem.\n   275\tWhat has changed is that its premise is now known to be false\n   276\t(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-hypotheses}), so the implication can no\n   277\tlonger be discharged: it derives nothing about $X$ or $Y$. That is the\n   278\twhole of what ``removes one basis'' means.\n   279\t\n   280\t\\paragraph{The conclusion is not refuted.}\n   281\tA false premise does not make a conclusion false. The statement ``no\n   282\t$2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' and the statement ``no\n   283\t$(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$'' are untouched by anything in the\n   284\tcomparator statement, which speaks about $k$-CNF formulas and nothing\n   285\telse. To refute either, one would need an algorithm for $X$ or $Y$, and\n   286\tthe verified statement supplies one only through a reduction in the\n   287\topposite direction to the one the lower bound used. The manuscript says\n   288\texactly this (p.~5, lines 237--238): ``To obtain a faster algorithm for\n   289\ta target problem from our SAT algorithm, we need a reduction in the\n   290\tother direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT, that controls the\n   291\tnumber of variables.'' And it places the general caveat on itself at\n   292\tp.~2, lines 95--99:\n   293\t\\begin{quote}\\small\n   294\t``Note that runtime bounds of the form $2^{o(n)}$ do not transfer under\n   295\tgeneral polynomial-time reductions ($2^{n^{o(1)}}$ would be needed for\n   296\tthis), so our result does not imply that general NP problems admit\n   297\tsubexponential time algorithms.''\n   298\t\\end{quote}\n   299\tSo for shape (E): a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$ follows only from a\n   300\treduction of $X$ to fixed-width SAT in which the number of SAT variables\n   301\tis $O(n)$ in $X$'s parameter, and the manuscript gives one such transfer\n   302\t(below). For shape (S): a $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$ would\n   303\tneed the exponent of the SAT instance to be below $k \\log_2 c$ by a\n   304\tconstant factor, and the verified statement's exponent is\n   305\t$A\\rhon'$ in the variable count $n'$ of the SAT instance with $A$ unknown\n   306\t(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); no value of $A$ is on record below $257$,\n   307\tand no base $c$ is derived from the verified statement for any $Y$. No\n   308\tstatement of shape ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is refuted by\n   309\tanything in this paper or, so far as we can read, in the manuscript.\n   310\t\n   311\t\\paragraph{Hardness as a classification.}\n   312\tA statement of the form ``$Y$ is SETH-hard'' (or ETH-hard) records that\n   313\ta reduction exists from $k$-SAT to $Y$ with the stated parameter\n   314\tcontrol. The reduction still exists, and the statement is still true as\n   315\ta statement about reductions. What it used to convey, that a fast\n   316\talgorithm for $Y$ is unlikely because it would refute SETH, it no longer\n   317\tconveys: a fast algorithm for $Y$ would refute SETH, and SETH is\n   318\trefuted. The classification is now a fact about reductions and no\n   319\tlonger a fact about $Y$'s running time.\n   320\t\n   321\t\\paragraph{The weaker hypotheses the manuscript names are untouched.}\n   322\tThe manuscript gives two (p.~5, lines 229--236). The first is the\n   323\tOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture, which it states and then relates to SETH:\n   324\t``The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal Vectors shows that SETH implies\n   325\tOVC [Wil05, VW19]'' (lines 231--232). That implication is a theorem with\n   326\ta false premise, like (E) and (S): it says nothing about OVC either way,\n   327\tand nothing in the verified statement is about orthogonal vectors. The\n   328\tsecond is the width-unrestricted form of SETH, which we quote with the\n   329\tmanuscript's list of the problems it covers elided (lines 233--236):\n   330\t\\begin{quote}\\small\n   331\t``The lower bounds for [\\ldots] also follow from the hypothesis that\n   332\tunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$-time\n   333\talgorithm for any constant $\\delta > 0$ [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem\n   334\t1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each fixed clause width $k$,\n   335\twhile this hypothesis allows the width to grow with $n$.''\n   336\t\\end{quote}\n   337\tThe verified statement cannot reach this hypothesis, and the reason is\n   338\tvisible in its quantifiers (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}): the machine\n   339\tand the constants $C, A, S, d, e$ are chosen after $k$, and nothing in\n   340\tthe statement bounds how they grow with $k$. For a formula whose width\n   341\tgrows with $n$ there is no single machine and no single bound in the\n   342\tstatement at all; even the trivial observation that every $k$-CNF is a\n   343\tCNF runs the wrong way, since the hypothesis quantifies over all widths\n   344\tat once. So the unrestricted-width hypothesis, and every lower bound\n   345\tthat follows from it, is exactly where it was.\n   346\t\n   347\t\\paragraph{What a transferred algorithm inherits.}\n   348\tThe manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of\n   349\tsize at most $k$ (p.~5, lines 239--243): one variable per universe\n   350\telement and two clauses per set give a $k$-CNF with ``one variable per\n   351\tuniverse element, so for fixed $k$ Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential\n   352\ttime in the universe size, up to a polynomial input-size factor,''\n   353\tciting \\citet{Cygan2016}'s Observation~3.6. Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}\n   354\ttags this supported: the encoding is on paper, the counter is the\n   355\tverified one. What such a transfer inherits is the verified guarantee\n   356\ton the SAT instance it builds, $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rho(n')}$ with $n'$ the\n   357\tnumber of SAT variables, and therefore the title's limitation with it:\n   358\tat $A = 1$ the exponential factor is above $2^{n'}$ for every\n   359\t$50 \\le n' < e^{5504}$ (Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}), with $C, A, d$\n   360\tunknown. In general terms: an algorithm obtained from the verified\n   361\tstatement through a variable-controlling reduction is a $2^{o(n')}$\n   362\talgorithm whose verified guarantee is weaker, at every practical size,\n   363\tthan trying every assignment of the SAT instance it constructs. That is\n   364\tnot a defect of the transfer; it is the same sentence as the title,\n   365\tread through a reduction.\n   366\t\n   367\t\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n   368\t\\label{sec:consequences-not}\n   369\t\n   370\t\\begin{itemize}\n   371\t\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is\n   372\t  refuted}, for any problem, any parameter or any constant $c$. Such a\n   373\t  statement, proved from SETH, has lost its proof and kept its truth\n   374\t  value, which is unknown.\n   375\t\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' is\n   376\t  refuted} either, unless $X$ has a reduction to fixed-width SAT that\n   377\t  controls the number of variables; the manuscript names one such\n   378\t  problem (Set Splitting) and we add none.\n   379\t\\item \\textbf{The unrestricted-width hypothesis is untouched}, and so is\n   380\t  the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, and so is every lower bound proved\n   381\t  from either; the verified statement is for each fixed $k$ and says\n   382\t  nothing about widths that grow with $n$.\n   383\t\\item \\textbf{Nothing about any specific problem's complexity} beyond\n   384\t  what the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 state and we quote. The\n   385\t  problems the manuscript lists at lines 213--225 and 233--235 are not\n   386\t  discussed here, and no example from any other parameterisation is\n   387\t  given, by the scope of this paper.\n   388\t\\item \\textbf{No re-reading of the sources.} Each hypothesis is stated\n   389\t  in the form the manuscript gives it, with the manuscript's citation;\n   390\t  we did not check that the cited sections and definitions state it in\n   391\t  the same words. If a source's form is stronger than the manuscript's,\n   392\t  the refutation of the manuscript's form may not reach it, and the\n   393\t  reader should check the source.\n   394\t\\item \\textbf{Asymptotic only.} Every refutation here is the negation of\n   395\t  an asymptotic statement and uses only $\\rhon = o(n)$. None says that\n   396\t  any machine decides or counts anything fast at any size; the constants\n   397\t  are as unknown here as in Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, and the\n   398\t  verified bound is above $2^n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$.\n   399\t\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the verified statement.} The manuscript's\n   400\t  sharper exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) are not used; where the\n   401\t  manuscript derives a consequence from them, it is not a consequence\n   402\t  of what is verified and is not claimed here.\n   403\t\\end{itemize}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"2accaf10-b634-4f74-8f81-97d2b5fe86ca","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:31:21.225Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"     1\t% What follows, in general terms -- drafted by loop0015 item 05 (2026-11-05).\n     2\t% Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of\n     3\t% reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the line + 1);\n     4\t% every comparator line is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone, read with cat/sed -n\n     5\t% only. Every page, line, fragment and the one table of this section are checked\n     6\t% and written by\n     7\t%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py\n     8\t% which exits non-zero if anything has moved. The section is in general terms\n     9\t% by the owner's rule (2026-11-05): no example from any width parameter, no\n    10\t% problem named beyond what the manuscript's §1.1.1 and §1.1.3 name.\n    11\t\\section{What follows, in general terms}\n    12\t\\label{sec:consequences}\n    13\t\n    14\tSections~\\ref{sec:statement}--\\ref{sec:replay} read the verified statement,\n    15\tdrew the line between it and the manuscript, inspected the proof and\n    16\treplayed it. This section asks what follows from the verified statement\n    17\talone: which hypotheses it refutes and by what step, and what a lower\n    18\tbound that was derived from one of those hypotheses has lost. The\n    19\tmanuscript answers both questions itself, in \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 (p.~4--5)\n    20\tand \\S1.1.3 (p.~5), and we quote it; what we add is the quantifier form\n    21\tof each hypothesis, the one line of the comparator statement that negates\n    22\tit, and the distinction, stated in general terms, between a theorem whose\n    23\tpremise has become false and a statement that has been refuted. Nothing\n    24\there names a problem the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 do not name, and\n    25\tnothing here is about any problem's complexity beyond what those two\n    26\tsections state.\n    27\t\n    28\t\\subsection{The one property every refutation uses}\n    29\t\\label{sec:consequences-property}\n    30\t\n    31\tEvery refutation below rests on the same conjuncts of\n    32\t\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (comparator file lines 194--217,\n    33\tSection~\\ref{sec:statement}): for each fixed $k \\ge 2$ there is one\n    34\tdeterministic machine which, on the canonical encoding of any\n    35\t\\texttt{WidthAtMost}~$k$ formula $F$ on $n$ variables (line 38), halts\n    36\twith \\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape (lines 203--204, 147),\n    37\tthe bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ on its answer tape and in its halting\n    38\tstate (lines 205--207), within $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps (lines\n    39\t210--211). Four features of that property do the work.\n    40\t\n    41\t\\begin{enumerate}\n    42\t\\item \\textbf{It is deterministic.} \\texttt{step} is a function and\n    43\t  \\texttt{Trace} is its iteration (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there\n    44\t  is no random tape and no nondeterministic choice. A deterministic\n    45\t  algorithm is also a bounded-error randomised algorithm, with error\n    46\t  $0$, and also a nondeterministic algorithm, with no guess; so each\n    47\t  refutation of a deterministic hypothesis is a refutation of its\n    48\t  randomised and nondeterministic forms by the same machine.\n    49\t\\item \\textbf{The bound is $2^{o(n)}$ in $n$ alone.} A \\texttt{Finset} of\n    50\t  clauses of width at most $k$ over $n$ variables has at most\n    51\t  $\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j}$ members ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$,\n    52\t  $k = 3$), so $m = O(n^k)$ and $L = O(k\\,n\\,m) = O(n^{k+1})$\n    53\t  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); hence the bound is\n    54\t  $n^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, and since $\\rhon/n \\to 0$\n    55\t  \\leanref{rho-little-o}, for every $\\delta > 0$ there is an\n    56\t  $n_0(k, \\delta)$ beyond which it is below $2^{\\delta n}$. Below\n    57\t  $n_0$ the bound is a constant depending on $k$ and $\\delta$. So the\n    58\t  running time is $O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every $\\delta > 0$,\n    59\t  and $2^{\\delta n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ with the polynomial written out. This\n    60\t  is an asymptotic fact and it is the only kind of fact the hypotheses\n    61\t  are about; it coexists with Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s observation\n    62\t  that $\\rhon > n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, which is about where\n    63\t  the asymptotics begin.\n    64\t\\item \\textbf{One machine per $k$, for every $n$.} The machine and the\n    65\t  constants are chosen after $k$ and before $n$ and $F$\n    66\t  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there is no $n \\ge 20$ proviso in the\n    67\t  comparator statement, though the manuscript's Theorem~1.1 carries one\n    68\t  (p.~2, lines 56--57). So the verified form is the strong, uniform\n    69\t  negation: a single algorithm that is $O(2^{\\delta n})$ for every\n    70\t  $\\delta$ at once, where a hypothesis of the form ``for every $\\delta$\n    71\t  there is an algorithm'' would have allowed a different algorithm for\n    72\t  each $\\delta$.\n    73\t\\item \\textbf{The model is the conservative one.} The comparator's\n    74\t  machine is a multitape Turing machine with one head per tape, moving\n    75\t  one cell or writing one symbol per step. Every step of such a machine\n    76\t  is a bounded amount of work on any of the standard models in which the\n    77\t  hypotheses are stated, so the bound transfers to those models with at\n    78\t  most a constant factor per step, which the polynomial absorbs. The\n    79\t  input is the manuscript's canonical encoding, of length polynomial in\n    80\t  $n + m$; converting from any other reasonable encoding is polynomial\n    81\t  too.\n    82\t\\end{enumerate}\n    83\t\n    84\t\\subsection{The hypotheses, one by one}\n    85\t\\label{sec:consequences-hypotheses}\n    86\t\n    87\tThe manuscript's own sentence is (p.~4, lines 187--191):\n    88\t\\begin{quote}\\small\n    89\t``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing\n    90\twhether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability\n    91\tand unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for\n    92\tevery fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise\n    93\tproblem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\n    94\trandomized versions of ETH [DHM$^+$14, Section 1] and SETH [CDL$^+$16,\n    95\tDefinition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH.''\n    96\t\\end{quote}\n    97\tand, for counting (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1)\n    98\tsolves \\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time\n    99\tand therefore refutes \\#ETH.'' We take each hypothesis in the form the\n   100\tmanuscript states it, with the manuscript's own source for that form,\n   101\tand say which conjunct negates it. Table~\\ref{tab:hypotheses} is the\n   102\tsummary. In every case the only thing used is the property of\n   103\tSection~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}; nothing is used from the\n   104\tmanuscript's sharper exponents, which are not verified\n   105\t(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n   106\t\n   107\t\\paragraph{ETH.}\n   108\tAs stated (p.~4, lines 165--166): ``The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH)\n   109\tasserted that 3-SAT requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time in the\n   110\tworst case, and hence so does $k$-SAT for every fixed $k \\ge 3$ [IP01,\n   111\tTheorem~1],'' citing \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}. In quantifiers:\n   112\tthere is a $\\delta > 0$ such that no deterministic algorithm decides\n   113\t3-SAT within $2^{\\delta n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps on all inputs.\n   114\tIts negation is: for every $\\delta > 0$ there is one. The verified\n   115\tstatement at $k = 3$ supplies, for every $\\delta > 0$, the same machine,\n   116\twhose answer bit is $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$, that is, the decision,\n   117\twithin $O_{\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps\n   118\t(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}, item 2). This holds on every\n   119\tinput of every size, so it refutes the hypothesis under either reading\n   120\tof ``$2^{\\Omega(n)}$'' (a lower bound at infinitely many $n$, or at all\n   121\tlarge $n$). In the vocabulary of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, where\n   122\t$s_k$ is the infimum of the $\\delta$ for which $k$-SAT has an\n   123\t$O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm and ETH is $s_3 > 0$: the verified statement\n   124\tgives $s_k = 0$ for every $k \\ge 2$, by one algorithm per $k$.\n   125\t\n   126\t\\paragraph{SETH.}\n   127\tAs stated (p.~4, lines 166--169): ``The Strong Exponential Time\n   128\tHypothesis (SETH) made the sharper prediction that $k$-SAT requires\n   129\t$2^{n(1-o_k(1))}$ time as $k$ grows. Precisely, for every\n   130\t$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$, there is a fixed $k$ for which no deterministic\n   131\talgorithm solves $k$-SAT in $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ time,''\n   132\twith the motivation attributed to \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}'s open\n   133\tproblems (lines 169--171). The negation is: there is an\n   134\t$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ such that for every fixed $k$ there is a\n   135\tdeterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm. The verified\n   136\tstatement gives more: for \\emph{every} $\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ and every\n   137\t$k \\ge 2$, the machine for $k$ runs within\n   138\t$C_k(L+1)^{d_k}\\,2^{A_k\\rhon}$ steps, and $A_k\\rhon \\le (1-\\varepsilon)n$\n   139\tfor all $n \\ge n_0(k, \\varepsilon)$ because $\\rhon = o(n)$; below $n_0$\n   140\tthe exponential factor is a constant. So each $k$ has a\n   141\t$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ algorithm, and the polynomial's\n   142\tdegree depending on $k$ is exactly what the hypothesis's ``for a fixed\n   143\t$k$'' permits. The refutation does not pass through ETH; it reads the\n   144\tstatement at each $k$. It is also standard that SETH implies ETH: if\n   145\t3-SAT had a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm then, by the Sparsification Lemma of\n   146\t\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}, which reduces $k$-SAT on $n$ variables\n   147\tto $2^{\\varepsilon n}$ instances with $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ clauses and\n   148\tso, after the clause-splitting reduction, to 3-SAT on $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$\n   149\tvariables, so would $k$-SAT for every fixed $k$; so the refutation of\n   150\tETH alone would already refute SETH. We note the implication for the\n   151\trecord; the direct reading above is what the paper rests on.\n   152\t\n   153\t\\paragraph{Gap-ETH, and its randomised form.}\n   154\tAs stated (p.~4, lines 172--174): ``The Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis\n   155\t(Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants $\\delta, \\gamma > 0$\n   156\tfor which no $O(2^{\\gamma n})$-time algorithm can distinguish a\n   157\tsatisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment violates at least a\n   158\t$\\delta$ fraction of the clauses, even when $m = O(n)$ [Din16, MR17],''\n   159\tciting \\citet{Dinur2016} and \\citet{ManurangsiRaghavendra2017}; and\n   160\t(lines 175--176) ``The randomized version rules out an algorithm with\n   161\tthe same running time that distinguishes the two cases with probability\n   162\tat least $2/3$ on every promised input [CCK$^+$20, Conjecture 2.5],''\n   163\tciting \\citet{Chalermsook2020}. The negation is: for every\n   164\t$\\delta, \\gamma > 0$ there is an $O(2^{\\gamma n})$ algorithm that\n   165\tseparates the two cases, already at $m = O(n)$. Exact decision separates\n   166\tthem, since a formula in which every assignment violates a positive\n   167\tfraction of the clauses is unsatisfiable; and the verified machine at\n   168\t$k = 3$ decides within $n^{O(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon} = O_{\\gamma}(2^{\\gamma n})$\n   169\tsteps for every $\\gamma > 0$, at every $m$ (item 2 above; at $m = O(n)$\n   170\tthe polynomial is $O(n^{2d})$). The promise is never used: the machine\n   171\tanswers correctly on every input, promised or not, with probability $1$,\n   172\twhich refutes the randomised form by item 1.\n   173\t\n   174\t\\paragraph{NSETH.}\n   175\tAs stated (p.~4, lines 181--183): ``The Nondeterministic Strong\n   176\tExponential Time Hypothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every\n   177\t$\\varepsilon > 0$, some fixed $k$ admits no nondeterministic\n   178\t$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}\\allowbreak(n+m)^{O(1)}$-time algorithm for deciding whether a\n   179\t$k$-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a $k$-DNF is a\n   180\ttautology [CGI$^+$16],'' citing \\citet{Carmosino2016}. The negation is:\n   181\tthere is an $\\varepsilon > 0$ such that every fixed $k$ has a\n   182\tnondeterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm for\n   183\tunsatisfiability. The verified machine's answer bit is \\texttt{false}\n   184\texactly when $\\mathtt{count}\\ F = 0$ (lines 205--207), so it decides\n   185\tunsatisfiability deterministically within the SETH bound of the previous\n   186\tparagraph for every $\\varepsilon$ and every $k$; and a deterministic\n   187\tmachine is a nondeterministic one whose only computation path is the\n   188\tdeterministic run, so every acceptance convention for nondeterministic\n   189\ttime is met. The manuscript's ``decides both satisfiability and\n   190\tunsatisfiability'' (line 188) is this.\n   191\t\n   192\t\\paragraph{\\#ETH.}\n   193\tAs stated (p.~4, lines 204--205): ``The counting Exponential Time\n   194\tHypothesis (\\#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying\n   195\tassignments of a 3-CNF requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time\n   196\t[DHM$^+$14, Section 1],'' citing \\citet{Dell2014}. The negation is: for\n   197\tevery $\\delta > 0$ there is a deterministic $O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm\n   198\tthat outputs the exact count. The verified machine at $k = 3$ halts with\n   199\t\\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape, a binary word of length\n   200\tat most $n + 1$ that decodes to $\\mathtt{count}\\ F$ (lines 203--204,\n   201\t208--209), within the same bound. This is the one hypothesis for which\n   202\tthe decision bit is not enough and the count tape is what refutes it;\n   203\tit is also the hypothesis the comparator statement was written for, the\n   204\tcount being the theorem's first conclusion and the decision its\n   205\tcorollary.\n   206\t\n   207\t\\paragraph{The bounded-error randomised ETH and SETH.}\n   208\tThe manuscript names them by citation only (p.~4, lines 190--191):\n   209\t``[DHM$^+$14, Section 1]'' for ETH \\citep{Dell2014} and ``[CDL$^+$16,\n   210\tDefinition 2.1]'' for SETH \\citep{Cygan2016}. In each, the algorithm\n   211\tmay be randomised and must be correct with probability bounded away\n   212\tfrom $1/2$ on every input. A deterministic algorithm is such an\n   213\talgorithm with error $0$ (item 1), so the refutations of ETH and SETH\n   214\tabove refute these forms by the same machine, with no change to the\n   215\tbound. We have not re-read the two sources' definitions; we state the\n   216\tforms as the manuscript cites them, and a reader who needs the exact\n   217\terror threshold or the exact model of the source should go to the\n   218\tcited section and definition.\n   219\t\n   220\t\\begin{table}[htbp]\n   221\t\\caption{The hypotheses the manuscript's \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 list, each in\n   222\tthe form the manuscript states it (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of\n   223\tthe committed extract), with the manuscript's own source for that form,\n   224\tthe quantifier form, and the conjunct of the comparator statement that\n   225\tnegates it (lines 194--217 of the comparator file at \\texttt{4eaee875}).\n   226\t$A$, $C_k$, $d_k$ are the existential constants of\n   227\tSection~\\ref{sec:statement}. Generated, with every page and fragment\n   228\tchecked against the extract and the clone, by\n   229\t\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py}.}\n   230\t\\label{tab:hypotheses}\n   231\t\\scriptsize\n   232\t\\input{tables/hypotheses}\n   233\t\\end{table}\n   234\t\n   235\t\\paragraph{What the manuscript places beside the list.}\n   236\tTwo sentences follow the refutation paragraph (p.~4, lines 191--194):\n   237\tthe result ``does not conflict with unconditional exponential lower\n   238\tbounds for restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13],''\n   239\tand ``In 2016, Ryan Williams refuted the Merlin--Arthur version of SETH\n   240\t(MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies $k$-CNF unsatisfiability\n   241\tin $2^{n/2}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3]''\n   242\t\\citep{Williams2016}. Both are context. The first says that a lower\n   243\tbound proved unconditionally for a restricted proof system is not a\n   244\tstatement about algorithms in general and is not touched; the second\n   245\tthat one member of the family was already false before this result.\n   246\tNeither is used below.\n   247\t\n   248\t\\subsection{What a derived lower bound loses}\n   249\t\\label{sec:consequences-lower-bounds}\n   250\t\n   251\tThe manuscript's \\S1.1.3 opens with a list of lower bounds that were\n   252\tproved from ETH or from SETH (p.~5, lines 213--225), for problems with\n   253\texponential algorithms and for problems in polynomial time, and then\n   254\tsays what its result does to them (lines 226--227):\n   255\t\\begin{quote}\\small\n   256\t``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness\n   257\tclaims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from weaker\n   258\thypotheses that may still hold.''\n   259\t\\end{quote}\n   260\tWe state what that means in general terms, for a theorem of either of\n   261\tthe two standard shapes,\n   262\t\\begin{align*}\n   263\t  \\text{(E)}&\\quad \\text{ETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } 2^{o(n)} \\text{ algorithm for } X,\\\\\n   264\t  \\text{(S)}&\\quad \\text{SETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } (c - \\varepsilon)^{k} \\text{ algorithm for } Y \\text{ for any } \\varepsilon > 0,\n   265\t\\end{align*}\n   266\twhere $n$ and $k$ are whatever parameter of $X$ and $Y$ the theorem\n   267\tnames and $c$ is its constant. Nothing below depends on what $X$ and $Y$\n   268\tare.\n   269\t\n   270\t\\paragraph{The theorem stands; its premise is false; it derives nothing.}\n   271\tA theorem of shape (E) or (S) is an implication, proved by a reduction\n   272\tfrom $k$-SAT to $X$ or $Y$ that turns a fast algorithm for the target\n   273\tinto a fast algorithm for $k$-SAT. The reduction is unaffected by\n   274\tanything in the verified statement; the implication remains a theorem.\n   275\tWhat has changed is that its premise is now known to be false\n   276\t(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-hypotheses}), so the implication can no\n   277\tlonger be discharged: it derives nothing about $X$ or $Y$. That is the\n   278\twhole of what ``removes one basis'' means.\n   279\t\n   280\t\\paragraph{The conclusion is not refuted.}\n   281\tA false premise does not make a conclusion false. The statement ``no\n   282\t$2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' and the statement ``no\n   283\t$(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$'' are untouched by anything in the\n   284\tcomparator statement, which speaks about $k$-CNF formulas and nothing\n   285\telse. To refute either, one would need an algorithm for $X$ or $Y$, and\n   286\tthe verified statement supplies one only through a reduction in the\n   287\topposite direction to the one the lower bound used. The manuscript says\n   288\texactly this (p.~5, lines 237--238): ``To obtain a faster algorithm for\n   289\ta target problem from our SAT algorithm, we need a reduction in the\n   290\tother direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT, that controls the\n   291\tnumber of variables.'' And it places the general caveat on itself at\n   292\tp.~2, lines 95--99:\n   293\t\\begin{quote}\\small\n   294\t``Note that runtime bounds of the form $2^{o(n)}$ do not transfer under\n   295\tgeneral polynomial-time reductions ($2^{n^{o(1)}}$ would be needed for\n   296\tthis), so our result does not imply that general NP problems admit\n   297\tsubexponential time algorithms.''\n   298\t\\end{quote}\n   299\tSo for shape (E): a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$ follows only from a\n   300\treduction of $X$ to fixed-width SAT in which the number of SAT variables\n   301\tis $O(n)$ in $X$'s parameter, and the manuscript gives one such transfer\n   302\t(below). For shape (S): a $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$ would\n   303\tneed the exponent of the SAT instance to be below $k \\log_2 c$ by a\n   304\tconstant factor, and the verified statement's exponent is\n   305\t$A\\rhon'$ in the variable count $n'$ of the SAT instance with $A$ unknown\n   306\t(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); no value of $A$ is on record below $257$,\n   307\tand no base $c$ is derived from the verified statement for any $Y$. No\n   308\tstatement of shape ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is refuted by\n   309\tanything in this paper or, so far as we can read, in the manuscript.\n   310\t\n   311\t\\paragraph{Hardness as a classification.}\n   312\tA statement of the form ``$Y$ is SETH-hard'' (or ETH-hard) records that\n   313\ta reduction exists from $k$-SAT to $Y$ with the stated parameter\n   314\tcontrol. The reduction still exists, and the statement is still true as\n   315\ta statement about reductions. What it used to convey, that a fast\n   316\talgorithm for $Y$ is unlikely because it would refute SETH, it no longer\n   317\tconveys: a fast algorithm for $Y$ would refute SETH, and SETH is\n   318\trefuted. The classification is now a fact about reductions and no\n   319\tlonger a fact about $Y$'s running time.\n   320\t\n   321\t\\paragraph{The weaker hypotheses the manuscript names are untouched.}\n   322\tThe manuscript gives two (p.~5, lines 229--236). The first is the\n   323\tOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture, which it states and then relates to SETH:\n   324\t``The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal Vectors shows that SETH implies\n   325\tOVC [Wil05, VW19]'' (lines 231--232). That implication is a theorem with\n   326\ta false premise, like (E) and (S): it says nothing about OVC either way,\n   327\tand nothing in the verified statement is about orthogonal vectors. The\n   328\tsecond is the width-unrestricted form of SETH, which we quote with the\n   329\tmanuscript's list of the problems it covers elided (lines 233--236):\n   330\t\\begin{quote}\\small\n   331\t``The lower bounds for [\\ldots] also follow from the hypothesis that\n   332\tunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$-time\n   333\talgorithm for any constant $\\delta > 0$ [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem\n   334\t1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each fixed clause width $k$,\n   335\twhile this hypothesis allows the width to grow with $n$.''\n   336\t\\end{quote}\n   337\tThe verified statement cannot reach this hypothesis, and the reason is\n   338\tvisible in its quantifiers (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}): the machine\n   339\tand the constants $C, A, S, d, e$ are chosen after $k$, and nothing in\n   340\tthe statement bounds how they grow with $k$. For a formula whose width\n   341\tgrows with $n$ there is no single machine and no single bound in the\n   342\tstatement at all; even the trivial observation that every $k$-CNF is a\n   343\tCNF runs the wrong way, since the hypothesis quantifies over all widths\n   344\tat once. So the unrestricted-width hypothesis, and every lower bound\n   345\tthat follows from it, is exactly where it was.\n   346\t\n   347\t\\paragraph{What a transferred algorithm inherits.}\n   348\tThe manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of\n   349\tsize at most $k$ (p.~5, lines 239--243): one variable per universe\n   350\telement and two clauses per set give a $k$-CNF with ``one variable per\n   351\tuniverse element, so for fixed $k$ Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential\n   352\ttime in the universe size, up to a polynomial input-size factor,''\n   353\tciting \\citet{Cygan2016}'s Observation~3.6. Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}\n   354\ttags this supported: the encoding is on paper, the counter is the\n   355\tverified one. What such a transfer inherits is the verified guarantee\n   356\ton the SAT instance it builds, $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rho(n')}$ with $n'$ the\n   357\tnumber of SAT variables, and therefore the title's limitation with it:\n   358\tat $A = 1$ the exponential factor is above $2^{n'}$ for every\n   359\t$50 \\le n' < e^{5504}$ (Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}), with $C, A, d$\n   360\tunknown. In general terms: an algorithm obtained from the verified\n   361\tstatement through a variable-controlling reduction is a $2^{o(n')}$\n   362\talgorithm whose verified guarantee is weaker, at every practical size,\n   363\tthan trying every assignment of the SAT instance it constructs. That is\n   364\tnot a defect of the transfer; it is the same sentence as the title,\n   365\tread through a reduction.\n   366\t\n   367\t\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n   368\t\\label{sec:consequences-not}\n   369\t\n   370\t\\begin{itemize}\n   371\t\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is\n   372\t  refuted}, for any problem, any parameter or any constant $c$. Such a\n   373\t  statement, proved from SETH, has lost its proof and kept its truth\n   374\t  value, which is unknown.\n   375\t\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' is\n   376\t  refuted} either, unless $X$ has a reduction to fixed-width SAT that\n   377\t  controls the number of variables; the manuscript names one such\n   378\t  problem (Set Splitting) and we add none.\n   379\t\\item \\textbf{The unrestricted-width hypothesis is untouched}, and so is\n   380\t  the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, and so is every lower bound proved\n   381\t  from either; the verified statement is for each fixed $k$ and says\n   382\t  nothing about widths that grow with $n$.\n   383\t\\item \\textbf{Nothing about any specific problem's complexity} beyond\n   384\t  what the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 state and we quote. The\n   385\t  problems the manuscript lists at lines 213--225 and 233--235 are not\n   386\t  discussed here, and no example from any other parameterisation is\n   387\t  given, by the scope of this paper.\n   388\t\\item \\textbf{No re-reading of the sources.} Each hypothesis is stated\n   389\t  in the form the manuscript gives it, with the manuscript's citation;\n   390\t  we did not check that the cited sections and definitions state it in\n   391\t  the same words. If a source's form is stronger than the manuscript's,\n   392\t  the refutation of the manuscript's form may not reach it, and the\n   393\t  reader should check the source.\n   394\t\\item \\textbf{Asymptotic only.} Every refutation here is the negation of\n   395\t  an asymptotic statement and uses only $\\rhon = o(n)$. None says that\n   396\t  any machine decides or counts anything fast at any size; the constants\n   397\t  are as unknown here as in Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, and the\n   398\t  verified bound is above $2^n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$.\n   399\t\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the verified statement.} The manuscript's\n   400\t  sharper exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) are not used; where the\n   401\t  manuscript derives a consequence from them, it is not a consequence\n   402\t  of what is verified and is not claimed here.\n   403\t\\end{itemize}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01D5eiuEXxaqWyQhX7cSv4LG","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
