{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgjK36Dvw24oih3KoHL2x","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GrWnv3jUZhBMLtzg23utaM","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat -n paper3/latex/sec7_practitioner.tex","description":"Read section 7"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11135,"cache_read_input_tokens":147598,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11135,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"6f20ea41-ef43-41c5-bf79-cecf40ffdb0a","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:31:24.865Z","request_id":"req_011CgjK35s76aCmHnuFretba","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01GrWnv3jUZhBMLtzg23utaM":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP && cat -n paper3/latex/sec7_practitioner.tex","description":"Read section 7"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01GrWnv3jUZhBMLtzg23utaM":{"cwd":"/home/al/dev/MOSP"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GrWnv3jUZhBMLtzg23utaM","type":"tool_result","content":"     1\t% For the practitioner -- drafted by loop0015 item 06 (2026-11-05).\n     2\t% Every number of this section is produced by\n     3\t%   nice -n 19 python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py --regenerate   (the measurement)\n     4\t%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py                           (table, figure, stdout)\n     5\t% from random 3-CNF the script generates; no MOSP or pathwidth instance, nothing\n     6\t% under solutions/. Timings are from one machine on one day with named\n     7\t% versions (tables/practitioner_meta.json). The two guarantee curves are shapes\n     8\t% at constant 1, and nothing here is a running time of the verified machine.\n     9\t\\section{For the practitioner}\n    10\t\\label{sec:practitioner}\n    11\t\n    12\tThe title compares two guarantees: the verified one,\n    13\t$C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ with $C, A, d$ existential (comparator file lines\n    14\t136--139 and 210--211, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), and the trivial one,\n    15\t$2^n \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ for trying every assignment. Neither is what\n    16\ta practitioner runs. What runs on a machine, for the decision bit, is a\n    17\tconflict-driven clause-learning (CDCL) solver, and for the count an exact\n    18\tor approximate model counter; and no implementation of the verified\n    19\tmachine exists to run beside them. This section measures the tools a\n    20\tpractitioner has, on random 3-CNF, and draws the two guarantees next to\n    21\tthe measurements as shapes, so that the reader can see where each sits.\n    22\tThe point is small and the section says it once: the verified guarantee\n    23\tis not the binding one on any machine, because it is above $2^n$ for\n    24\tevery $n \\ge 50$ (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}) while the solvers are many\n    25\torders of magnitude below $2^n$ at every size they were run at.\n    26\t\n    27\t\\subsection{Why the comparison is between guarantees and measurements}\n    28\t\\label{sec:practitioner-why}\n    29\t\n    30\tThe verified statement is existential in the machine (comparator line\n    31\t195 opens with \\texttt{$\\exists$ (machine : \\ldots)}, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}) and\n    32\tthe solution's\n    33\t3,977 files contain no \\texttt{\\#eval}, no \\texttt{def main} and no\n    34\t\\texttt{IO} action (checked read-only by the script, which reads every\n    35\tfile of the family with \\texttt{open()} and counts those three patterns:\n    36\t0, 0, 0). The library proves that a machine with the stated bound\n    37\texists and exhibits one as a Lean term inside a development with 1,048\n    38\t\\texttt{noncomputable} declarations and no executable interface\n    39\t(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); nothing in the clone runs it on an input, and\n    40\twe found no implementation elsewhere. So the verified algorithm's actual\n    41\trunning time on any formula is unknown, in both directions: nothing says\n    42\tit is slow, and nothing says it is fast. What is known is its\n    43\t\\emph{guarantee}, and a guarantee is compared with other guarantees. The\n    44\tmeasurements below are of other programs entirely; they are in the\n    45\tsection because the question a practitioner asks is not ``which\n    46\tguarantee is smaller'' but ``what would I run'', and the answer to that\n    47\thas not changed.\n    48\t\n    49\t\\subsection{What was run}\n    50\t\\label{sec:practitioner-setup}\n    51\t\n    52\t\\paragraph{Instances.} Random 3-CNF on $n$ variables with\n    53\t$m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$ clauses, each clause three distinct variables\n    54\twith uniform signs, generated by the script from $(n, \\mathrm{seed})$;\n    55\ttwenty seeds per size; $n = 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250,\n    56\t300, 400, 500$. The ratio $4.26$ is the empirical satisfiability\n    57\tthreshold of random 3-SAT \\citep{Selman1996Hard}, where decision is\n    58\thardest for a given $n$ among random formulas (the cavity method puts the\n    59\tthreshold at $4.2667$, \\citealp{Mertens2006Threshold}); the sizes are\n    60\tchosen so that the smallest ones are where the title's crossing is\n    61\t($n = 50$) and the largest are where a CDCL solver on random formulas\n    62\tbegins to take seconds. These are random instances only; nothing is\n    63\tclaimed about structured formulas.\n    64\t\n    65\t\\paragraph{Tools.} CaDiCaL 1.9.5 \\citep{Biere2024CaDiCaL} through\n    66\tpython-sat 1.9.dev15 \\citep{Ignatiev2018PySAT}, one \\texttt{solve()} call,\n    67\tfor the decision bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$; Ganak 2.8.0\n    68\t\\citep{Sharma2019Ganak}, through the pyganak binding (which runs its Arjun\n    69\tpreprocessing and then the counter), for the exact count; ApproxMC 4.4.0\n    70\t\\citep{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC, Soos2019BIRD} at its defaults\n    71\t$\\varepsilon = 0.8$, $\\delta = 0.2$ with the projection set equal to all\n    72\tvariables, for an approximate count; and, at $n \\le 30$ only, exact\n    73\tcounting by enumeration over CaDiCaL with a blocking clause per model,\n    74\twhich is not a counter at any larger size (it is linear in the count,\n    75\tand the count is exponential in $n$) and is here as the independent check\n    76\tof the two counters where it can reach. Ganak and ApproxMC were installed\n    77\tfrom PyPI for this section; no \\texttt{sharpSAT}, \\texttt{d4} or other\n    78\tcounter binary is on the host.\n    79\t\n    80\t\\paragraph{Protocol.} Each call runs in its own interpreter under\n    81\t\\texttt{nice -n 19}, at most eight at a time on the thirty-two cores of\n    82\tan AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (Linux 7.0.0, Python 3.12.13; the machine was shared\n    83\twith other work, load average at the start in\n    84\t\\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}), with a wall-clock budget of\n    85\t60~s per call; the time recorded is \\texttt{perf\\_counter} around the\n    86\tconstruction of the solver object and the one call, inside the child,\n    87\twith the interpreter's start-up and the import outside it. A call over\n    88\tbudget is killed and recorded as censored; a size at which a tool finishes\n    89\tfewer than ten of its twenty instances ends that tool's escalation. The\n    90\twhole run took about twenty minutes (1,172~s) minutes.\n    91\t\n    92\t\\subsection{What came out}\n    93\t\\label{sec:practitioner-results}\n    94\t\n    95\tTable~\\ref{tab:practitioner} gives, per size, the number of satisfiable\n    96\tinstances, CaDiCaL's median and maximum over the twenty seeds, the number\n    97\tof instances each counter finished with its median and maximum (a median\n    98\tover all twenty with censored runs counted as above the budget, so a\n    99\tmedian is a number whenever more than ten finished), and the two\n   100\tguarantees in the common scale of the next subsection.\n   101\tFigure~\\ref{fig:practitioner} draws the same.\n   102\t\n   103\t\\begin{table}[htbp]\n   104\t\\centering\n   105\t\\caption{Random 3-CNF at $m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$, twenty seeds per\n   106\tsize, one machine, 60~s per call. ``sat'' is the number of satisfiable\n   107\tinstances among those CaDiCaL finished; ``fin'' the number a counter finished within budget; ``med''\n   108\tand ``max'' are seconds over all twenty seeds with a censored run counted\n   109\tas $> 60$. The last two columns are the brute-force and the verified\n   110\tguarantees at constant~1, $2^n$ and $2^{\\rhon}$, multiplied by the one\n   111\tcommon factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$ that puts $2^n$ through CaDiCaL's median at\n   112\t$n = 20$ ($t_{20} = 1.28 \\times 10^{-4}$~s); they are shapes, not running times.\n   113\tRegenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n   114\t\\label{tab:practitioner}\n   115\t\\footnotesize\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n   116\t\\input{tables/practitioner}\n   117\t\\end{table}\n   118\t\n   119\t\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n   120\t\\centering\n   121\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{practitioner.pdf}\n   122\t\\caption{The same data on a logarithmic time axis. Solid lines with\n   123\tmarkers are medians, dotted lines maxima, over the twenty seeds; a tool\n   124\twhose median is censored stops at the budget line. The two guarantees are\n   125\tdrawn at constant~1 with the common factor of Table~\\ref{tab:practitioner},\n   126\tso that $2^n$ passes through the $n = 20$ measurement and $2^{\\rhon}$\n   127\tcrosses it at $n = 50$, where $\\rhon \\ge n$ begins\n   128\t(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). From \\texttt{paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n   129\t\\label{fig:practitioner}\n   130\t\\end{figure}\n   131\t\n   132\t\\paragraph{The checks.} On every instance where both finished, Ganak's\n   133\tcount equals the enumeration's (40 instances at $n \\le 30$) and\n   134\tCaDiCaL's bit equals $[\\mathrm{count} > 0]$; ApproxMC's estimate is\n   135\twithin its factor $1 + \\varepsilon = 1.8$ of Ganak's on 178 of the\n   136\t206 instances where both finished (it promises that with\n   137\tprobability $1 - \\delta = 0.8$ per instance). The script exits non-zero\n   138\tif any of these fails.\n   139\t\n   140\t\\paragraph{What the solvers did.} CaDiCaL's median is under a\n   141\tmillisecond up to $n = 80$, 2.5~ms at 100, 58~ms at 200, 1.5~s at 250\n   142\tand 3.7~s at 300 (maximum 20.5~s); at 400 it finishes 10 of 20 within\n   143\tthe budget and at 500 8 of 20, every finished one satisfiable, which is\n   144\tthe known shape of CDCL on random formulas at the threshold (the\n   145\tunsatisfiable half is the expensive half). Ganak finishes all 20 up to\n   146\t$n = 250$ (median 17.6~s there, maximum 45.7~s), 10 of 20 at 300 and\n   147\tnone at 400; its median is flat at 8.3~s and 6.2~s at $n = 100$ and\n   148\t150 and lower, 2.4~s, at 200, while its maximum rises throughout ---\n   149\tthe plateau looks like a fixed budget inside the binding's\n   150\tpreprocessing rather than counting difficulty, and we report it as\n   151\tmeasured and tuned nothing (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06d). Where the\n   152\tcount is positive it is small for the size: at $n = 200$ the fifteen\n   153\tsatisfiable instances have between 372 and $2.5 \\times 10^{12}$ models\n   154\tout of $2^{200}$. ApproxMC finishes all 20 up to $n = 200$ (median\n   155\t0.51~s there), 18 at 250, 11 at 300 and none at 400. The sizes at which\n   156\tthe three tools leave the budget --- 300 for the counters, 400 for the\n   157\tdecision --- are where this measurement ends; nothing is claimed beyond\n   158\tthem.\n   159\t\n   160\t\\subsection{The two guarantees beside them}\n   161\t\\label{sec:practitioner-guarantees}\n   162\t\n   163\tThe guarantees are drawn as shapes. Both are taken at constant 1 --- the\n   164\tpolynomial factors, $C$, $A$ and $d$ are all set aside --- and both are\n   165\tmultiplied by the same factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$, where $t_{20}$ is\n   166\tCaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$. The factor is one number for both curves,\n   167\tso their ratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves,\n   168\t$2^{\\rhon - n}$: below 1 for $n < 50$, equal to 1 at $n = 50$ up to the\n   169\trounding of $\\rho(50) = 50.13$, and above 1 from there (it is $2.8 \\times 10^{3}$ at\n   170\t$n = 100$ and $4.0 \\times 10^{51}$ at $n = 500$). A reader who prefers each curve\n   171\tscaled to the $n = 20$ point on its own would multiply $2^{\\rhon}$ by a\n   172\tfurther $2^{20 - \\rho(20)} = 2^{2.79}$ and see the two curves touch at\n   173\t$n = 20$ and the verified one above the trivial one from there on; the\n   174\tcommon factor is the honest choice because it keeps the crossing where\n   175\tthe statement puts it (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a).\n   176\t\n   177\tIn that scale the brute-force guarantee reaches one second near\n   178\t$n = 33$ and the verified guarantee near $n = 35$; at $n = 60$,\n   179\tthe largest size of the title's practical range that this section's\n   180\tsmallest tools were run at, the measured CaDiCaL median is $3 \\times 10^{-4}$ s against\n   181\t$2^n$ at $1.4 \\times 10^{8}$ s and $2^{\\rhon}$ at $5.4 \\times 10^{8}$ s, 11.6 and 12.2 decades\n   182\trespectively; at $n = 500$ the decision median is above the budget (8 of 20 finished within 60~s), against\n   183\t$10^{140.6}$ s and $10^{192.2}$ s. Nothing in those decades is about the\n   184\tverified machine: the gap is between what a CDCL solver does on random\n   185\tformulas and what any $2^{\\Theta(n)}$ guarantee says, and it would be\n   186\tthe same gap for the brute-force guarantee alone. The one fact this\n   187\tsection adds to the title is the order of the three objects: at every\n   188\tsize in the table the measurement is far below $2^n$, and $2^{\\rhon}$ is\n   189\tabove $2^n$ from $n = 50$ on, so the verified guarantee is the largest\n   190\tof the three at every practical size and the measurement the smallest.\n   191\t\n   192\tOne remark on what the verified machine's \\emph{running time} would have\n   193\tto be, were it ever run. Nothing in this section bears on it: the\n   194\tconstants $C, A, d$ are unknown (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), the\n   195\tmachine is a Lean term, and an existential statement is not an\n   196\timplementation. The only values on record are the proof's own, $A \\ge\n   197\t257$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$ (Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and\n   198\t\\ref{sec:proof}); a guarantee of $2^{257\\,\\rhon}$ steps is a guarantee\n   199\tand not a measurement, and a different proof of the same statement could\n   200\tcarry any positive constants.\n   201\t\n   202\t\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n   203\t\\label{sec:practitioner-not}\n   204\t\n   205\t\\begin{itemize}\n   206\t\\item \\textbf{Timings on one machine, one day, named versions.} The\n   207\t  seconds are from one AMD Ryzen 9 9950X shared with other work, under\n   208\t  \\texttt{nice -n 19} with eight calls in flight, through Python\n   209\t  bindings; they are reproducible in shape and not in value, and the\n   210\t  versions are those named in Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner-setup} and\n   211\t  in \\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}.\n   212\t\\item \\textbf{Random instances only.} Random 3-CNF at the threshold ratio\n   213\t  is one distribution; structured formulas can be harder for CDCL at\n   214\t  far smaller $n$ and easier for counters. Nothing is claimed about any\n   215\t  other distribution, and nothing about worst-case behaviour.\n   216\t\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the verified machine's running time.} It is\n   217\t  unknown, in both directions; no implementation exists to run; the\n   218\t  guarantees drawn are at constant 1 and the constants are existential.\n   219\t\\item \\textbf{Nothing about $k > 3$.} The statement is for every fixed\n   220\t  $k$; the measurement is at $k = 3$.\n   221\t\\item \\textbf{The counters are reported, not tuned.} Ganak ran through\n   222\t  its binding at defaults, preprocessing included; ApproxMC at its\n   223\t  default tolerance; a different configuration gives different seconds.\n   224\t  The check against enumeration is at $n \\le 30$ only.\n   225\t\\item \\textbf{No statement about which tool a practitioner should use.}\n   226\t  The section shows that the verified guarantee is not binding; it does\n   227\t  not rank solvers.\n   228\t\\end{itemize}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"6db35931-9db8-410c-8bb7-0ebb03ab12d3","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:31:24.874Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"     1\t% For the practitioner -- drafted by loop0015 item 06 (2026-11-05).\n     2\t% Every number of this section is produced by\n     3\t%   nice -n 19 python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py --regenerate   (the measurement)\n     4\t%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py                           (table, figure, stdout)\n     5\t% from random 3-CNF the script generates; no MOSP or pathwidth instance, nothing\n     6\t% under solutions/. Timings are from one machine on one day with named\n     7\t% versions (tables/practitioner_meta.json). The two guarantee curves are shapes\n     8\t% at constant 1, and nothing here is a running time of the verified machine.\n     9\t\\section{For the practitioner}\n    10\t\\label{sec:practitioner}\n    11\t\n    12\tThe title compares two guarantees: the verified one,\n    13\t$C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ with $C, A, d$ existential (comparator file lines\n    14\t136--139 and 210--211, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), and the trivial one,\n    15\t$2^n \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ for trying every assignment. Neither is what\n    16\ta practitioner runs. What runs on a machine, for the decision bit, is a\n    17\tconflict-driven clause-learning (CDCL) solver, and for the count an exact\n    18\tor approximate model counter; and no implementation of the verified\n    19\tmachine exists to run beside them. This section measures the tools a\n    20\tpractitioner has, on random 3-CNF, and draws the two guarantees next to\n    21\tthe measurements as shapes, so that the reader can see where each sits.\n    22\tThe point is small and the section says it once: the verified guarantee\n    23\tis not the binding one on any machine, because it is above $2^n$ for\n    24\tevery $n \\ge 50$ (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}) while the solvers are many\n    25\torders of magnitude below $2^n$ at every size they were run at.\n    26\t\n    27\t\\subsection{Why the comparison is between guarantees and measurements}\n    28\t\\label{sec:practitioner-why}\n    29\t\n    30\tThe verified statement is existential in the machine (comparator line\n    31\t195 opens with \\texttt{$\\exists$ (machine : \\ldots)}, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}) and\n    32\tthe solution's\n    33\t3,977 files contain no \\texttt{\\#eval}, no \\texttt{def main} and no\n    34\t\\texttt{IO} action (checked read-only by the script, which reads every\n    35\tfile of the family with \\texttt{open()} and counts those three patterns:\n    36\t0, 0, 0). The library proves that a machine with the stated bound\n    37\texists and exhibits one as a Lean term inside a development with 1,048\n    38\t\\texttt{noncomputable} declarations and no executable interface\n    39\t(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); nothing in the clone runs it on an input, and\n    40\twe found no implementation elsewhere. So the verified algorithm's actual\n    41\trunning time on any formula is unknown, in both directions: nothing says\n    42\tit is slow, and nothing says it is fast. What is known is its\n    43\t\\emph{guarantee}, and a guarantee is compared with other guarantees. The\n    44\tmeasurements below are of other programs entirely; they are in the\n    45\tsection because the question a practitioner asks is not ``which\n    46\tguarantee is smaller'' but ``what would I run'', and the answer to that\n    47\thas not changed.\n    48\t\n    49\t\\subsection{What was run}\n    50\t\\label{sec:practitioner-setup}\n    51\t\n    52\t\\paragraph{Instances.} Random 3-CNF on $n$ variables with\n    53\t$m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$ clauses, each clause three distinct variables\n    54\twith uniform signs, generated by the script from $(n, \\mathrm{seed})$;\n    55\ttwenty seeds per size; $n = 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250,\n    56\t300, 400, 500$. The ratio $4.26$ is the empirical satisfiability\n    57\tthreshold of random 3-SAT \\citep{Selman1996Hard}, where decision is\n    58\thardest for a given $n$ among random formulas (the cavity method puts the\n    59\tthreshold at $4.2667$, \\citealp{Mertens2006Threshold}); the sizes are\n    60\tchosen so that the smallest ones are where the title's crossing is\n    61\t($n = 50$) and the largest are where a CDCL solver on random formulas\n    62\tbegins to take seconds. These are random instances only; nothing is\n    63\tclaimed about structured formulas.\n    64\t\n    65\t\\paragraph{Tools.} CaDiCaL 1.9.5 \\citep{Biere2024CaDiCaL} through\n    66\tpython-sat 1.9.dev15 \\citep{Ignatiev2018PySAT}, one \\texttt{solve()} call,\n    67\tfor the decision bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$; Ganak 2.8.0\n    68\t\\citep{Sharma2019Ganak}, through the pyganak binding (which runs its Arjun\n    69\tpreprocessing and then the counter), for the exact count; ApproxMC 4.4.0\n    70\t\\citep{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC, Soos2019BIRD} at its defaults\n    71\t$\\varepsilon = 0.8$, $\\delta = 0.2$ with the projection set equal to all\n    72\tvariables, for an approximate count; and, at $n \\le 30$ only, exact\n    73\tcounting by enumeration over CaDiCaL with a blocking clause per model,\n    74\twhich is not a counter at any larger size (it is linear in the count,\n    75\tand the count is exponential in $n$) and is here as the independent check\n    76\tof the two counters where it can reach. Ganak and ApproxMC were installed\n    77\tfrom PyPI for this section; no \\texttt{sharpSAT}, \\texttt{d4} or other\n    78\tcounter binary is on the host.\n    79\t\n    80\t\\paragraph{Protocol.} Each call runs in its own interpreter under\n    81\t\\texttt{nice -n 19}, at most eight at a time on the thirty-two cores of\n    82\tan AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (Linux 7.0.0, Python 3.12.13; the machine was shared\n    83\twith other work, load average at the start in\n    84\t\\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}), with a wall-clock budget of\n    85\t60~s per call; the time recorded is \\texttt{perf\\_counter} around the\n    86\tconstruction of the solver object and the one call, inside the child,\n    87\twith the interpreter's start-up and the import outside it. A call over\n    88\tbudget is killed and recorded as censored; a size at which a tool finishes\n    89\tfewer than ten of its twenty instances ends that tool's escalation. The\n    90\twhole run took about twenty minutes (1,172~s) minutes.\n    91\t\n    92\t\\subsection{What came out}\n    93\t\\label{sec:practitioner-results}\n    94\t\n    95\tTable~\\ref{tab:practitioner} gives, per size, the number of satisfiable\n    96\tinstances, CaDiCaL's median and maximum over the twenty seeds, the number\n    97\tof instances each counter finished with its median and maximum (a median\n    98\tover all twenty with censored runs counted as above the budget, so a\n    99\tmedian is a number whenever more than ten finished), and the two\n   100\tguarantees in the common scale of the next subsection.\n   101\tFigure~\\ref{fig:practitioner} draws the same.\n   102\t\n   103\t\\begin{table}[htbp]\n   104\t\\centering\n   105\t\\caption{Random 3-CNF at $m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$, twenty seeds per\n   106\tsize, one machine, 60~s per call. ``sat'' is the number of satisfiable\n   107\tinstances among those CaDiCaL finished; ``fin'' the number a counter finished within budget; ``med''\n   108\tand ``max'' are seconds over all twenty seeds with a censored run counted\n   109\tas $> 60$. The last two columns are the brute-force and the verified\n   110\tguarantees at constant~1, $2^n$ and $2^{\\rhon}$, multiplied by the one\n   111\tcommon factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$ that puts $2^n$ through CaDiCaL's median at\n   112\t$n = 20$ ($t_{20} = 1.28 \\times 10^{-4}$~s); they are shapes, not running times.\n   113\tRegenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n   114\t\\label{tab:practitioner}\n   115\t\\footnotesize\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n   116\t\\input{tables/practitioner}\n   117\t\\end{table}\n   118\t\n   119\t\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n   120\t\\centering\n   121\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{practitioner.pdf}\n   122\t\\caption{The same data on a logarithmic time axis. Solid lines with\n   123\tmarkers are medians, dotted lines maxima, over the twenty seeds; a tool\n   124\twhose median is censored stops at the budget line. The two guarantees are\n   125\tdrawn at constant~1 with the common factor of Table~\\ref{tab:practitioner},\n   126\tso that $2^n$ passes through the $n = 20$ measurement and $2^{\\rhon}$\n   127\tcrosses it at $n = 50$, where $\\rhon \\ge n$ begins\n   128\t(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). From \\texttt{paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n   129\t\\label{fig:practitioner}\n   130\t\\end{figure}\n   131\t\n   132\t\\paragraph{The checks.} On every instance where both finished, Ganak's\n   133\tcount equals the enumeration's (40 instances at $n \\le 30$) and\n   134\tCaDiCaL's bit equals $[\\mathrm{count} > 0]$; ApproxMC's estimate is\n   135\twithin its factor $1 + \\varepsilon = 1.8$ of Ganak's on 178 of the\n   136\t206 instances where both finished (it promises that with\n   137\tprobability $1 - \\delta = 0.8$ per instance). The script exits non-zero\n   138\tif any of these fails.\n   139\t\n   140\t\\paragraph{What the solvers did.} CaDiCaL's median is under a\n   141\tmillisecond up to $n = 80$, 2.5~ms at 100, 58~ms at 200, 1.5~s at 250\n   142\tand 3.7~s at 300 (maximum 20.5~s); at 400 it finishes 10 of 20 within\n   143\tthe budget and at 500 8 of 20, every finished one satisfiable, which is\n   144\tthe known shape of CDCL on random formulas at the threshold (the\n   145\tunsatisfiable half is the expensive half). Ganak finishes all 20 up to\n   146\t$n = 250$ (median 17.6~s there, maximum 45.7~s), 10 of 20 at 300 and\n   147\tnone at 400; its median is flat at 8.3~s and 6.2~s at $n = 100$ and\n   148\t150 and lower, 2.4~s, at 200, while its maximum rises throughout ---\n   149\tthe plateau looks like a fixed budget inside the binding's\n   150\tpreprocessing rather than counting difficulty, and we report it as\n   151\tmeasured and tuned nothing (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06d). Where the\n   152\tcount is positive it is small for the size: at $n = 200$ the fifteen\n   153\tsatisfiable instances have between 372 and $2.5 \\times 10^{12}$ models\n   154\tout of $2^{200}$. ApproxMC finishes all 20 up to $n = 200$ (median\n   155\t0.51~s there), 18 at 250, 11 at 300 and none at 400. The sizes at which\n   156\tthe three tools leave the budget --- 300 for the counters, 400 for the\n   157\tdecision --- are where this measurement ends; nothing is claimed beyond\n   158\tthem.\n   159\t\n   160\t\\subsection{The two guarantees beside them}\n   161\t\\label{sec:practitioner-guarantees}\n   162\t\n   163\tThe guarantees are drawn as shapes. Both are taken at constant 1 --- the\n   164\tpolynomial factors, $C$, $A$ and $d$ are all set aside --- and both are\n   165\tmultiplied by the same factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$, where $t_{20}$ is\n   166\tCaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$. The factor is one number for both curves,\n   167\tso their ratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves,\n   168\t$2^{\\rhon - n}$: below 1 for $n < 50$, equal to 1 at $n = 50$ up to the\n   169\trounding of $\\rho(50) = 50.13$, and above 1 from there (it is $2.8 \\times 10^{3}$ at\n   170\t$n = 100$ and $4.0 \\times 10^{51}$ at $n = 500$). A reader who prefers each curve\n   171\tscaled to the $n = 20$ point on its own would multiply $2^{\\rhon}$ by a\n   172\tfurther $2^{20 - \\rho(20)} = 2^{2.79}$ and see the two curves touch at\n   173\t$n = 20$ and the verified one above the trivial one from there on; the\n   174\tcommon factor is the honest choice because it keeps the crossing where\n   175\tthe statement puts it (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a).\n   176\t\n   177\tIn that scale the brute-force guarantee reaches one second near\n   178\t$n = 33$ and the verified guarantee near $n = 35$; at $n = 60$,\n   179\tthe largest size of the title's practical range that this section's\n   180\tsmallest tools were run at, the measured CaDiCaL median is $3 \\times 10^{-4}$ s against\n   181\t$2^n$ at $1.4 \\times 10^{8}$ s and $2^{\\rhon}$ at $5.4 \\times 10^{8}$ s, 11.6 and 12.2 decades\n   182\trespectively; at $n = 500$ the decision median is above the budget (8 of 20 finished within 60~s), against\n   183\t$10^{140.6}$ s and $10^{192.2}$ s. Nothing in those decades is about the\n   184\tverified machine: the gap is between what a CDCL solver does on random\n   185\tformulas and what any $2^{\\Theta(n)}$ guarantee says, and it would be\n   186\tthe same gap for the brute-force guarantee alone. The one fact this\n   187\tsection adds to the title is the order of the three objects: at every\n   188\tsize in the table the measurement is far below $2^n$, and $2^{\\rhon}$ is\n   189\tabove $2^n$ from $n = 50$ on, so the verified guarantee is the largest\n   190\tof the three at every practical size and the measurement the smallest.\n   191\t\n   192\tOne remark on what the verified machine's \\emph{running time} would have\n   193\tto be, were it ever run. Nothing in this section bears on it: the\n   194\tconstants $C, A, d$ are unknown (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), the\n   195\tmachine is a Lean term, and an existential statement is not an\n   196\timplementation. The only values on record are the proof's own, $A \\ge\n   197\t257$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$ (Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and\n   198\t\\ref{sec:proof}); a guarantee of $2^{257\\,\\rhon}$ steps is a guarantee\n   199\tand not a measurement, and a different proof of the same statement could\n   200\tcarry any positive constants.\n   201\t\n   202\t\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n   203\t\\label{sec:practitioner-not}\n   204\t\n   205\t\\begin{itemize}\n   206\t\\item \\textbf{Timings on one machine, one day, named versions.} The\n   207\t  seconds are from one AMD Ryzen 9 9950X shared with other work, under\n   208\t  \\texttt{nice -n 19} with eight calls in flight, through Python\n   209\t  bindings; they are reproducible in shape and not in value, and the\n   210\t  versions are those named in Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner-setup} and\n   211\t  in \\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}.\n   212\t\\item \\textbf{Random instances only.} Random 3-CNF at the threshold ratio\n   213\t  is one distribution; structured formulas can be harder for CDCL at\n   214\t  far smaller $n$ and easier for counters. Nothing is claimed about any\n   215\t  other distribution, and nothing about worst-case behaviour.\n   216\t\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the verified machine's running time.} It is\n   217\t  unknown, in both directions; no implementation exists to run; the\n   218\t  guarantees drawn are at constant 1 and the constants are existential.\n   219\t\\item \\textbf{Nothing about $k > 3$.} The statement is for every fixed\n   220\t  $k$; the measurement is at $k = 3$.\n   221\t\\item \\textbf{The counters are reported, not tuned.} Ganak ran through\n   222\t  its binding at defaults, preprocessing included; ApproxMC at its\n   223\t  default tolerance; a different configuration gives different seconds.\n   224\t  The check against enumeration is at $n \\le 30$ only.\n   225\t\\item \\textbf{No statement about which tool a practitioner should use.}\n   226\t  The section shows that the verified guarantee is not binding; it does\n   227\t  not rank solvers.\n   228\t\\end{itemize}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GrWnv3jUZhBMLtzg23utaM","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
