{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FrCiA8BNJqF6R9fZz11hCs","type":"tool_result","content":"     1\t% The statement in our Lean -- drafted by loop0015 item 07 (2026-11-05).\n     2\t% Every number here is printed by paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py (the copy, the\n     3\t% statement match, the sorry count; log paper3/lean/rediff.log) or by\n     4\t% `cd lean && lake env lean ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean` (the axiom audit;\n     5\t% log paper3/lean/axiom_check.log). Line numbers in our files are at the\n     6\t% repository commit the log names; line numbers in the clone are at 4eaee875.\n     7\t\\section{The statement in our Lean}\n     8\t\\label{sec:lean}\n     9\t\\begingroup\\sloppy\n    10\t\n    11\tSections~\\ref{sec:statement} and \\ref{sec:proof} read OpenAI's statement\n    12\tin OpenAI's files. This section describes the one thing we wrote in Lean\n    13\tourselves: a copy of the statement inside our own library, checked by the\n    14\tsame kernel, under our own name. The point is modest. Every sentence this\n    15\tpaper says about ``the verified statement'' is pinned to a named object\n    16\tthat the Lean kernel has elaborated, and anyone who wants to use the\n    17\tstatement as a hypothesis later can cite that one name. Nothing is proved\n    18\tabout the hypothesis here, and nothing is claimed beyond what the copy\n    19\tand its audit show.\n    20\t\n    21\tTwo words first. In Lean a \\emph{Prop} is a sentence that may be true or\n    22\tfalse; a \\emph{def} gives a sentence a name without asserting it; a\n    23\t\\emph{theorem} asserts a sentence and carries its proof; an \\emph{axiom}\n    24\tasserts one without a proof. Our restatement is a def. It names the\n    25\tsentence and asserts nothing.\n    26\t\n    27\tThe files are \\repofile{lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/PrimitiveMachine.lean}\n    28\tand \\repofile{lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/Frame.lean}, written\n    29\ton 2026-11-04 for an earlier study of ours on a different question. The\n    30\tdirectory carries that study's name. The owner chose to leave the files\n    31\twhere they are rather than move them (\\repofile{paper3/ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD},\n    32\tS3), and this paper cites nothing from that study: only the copied model,\n    33\tthe restatement, and six small lemmas about them, all listed in\n    34\tAppendix~\\ref{app:lean}.\n    35\t\n    36\t\\subsection{The copy: definitions only}\n    37\t\\label{sec:lean-copy}\n    38\t\n    39\tThe comparator file of family~378,\n    40\t\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean}, has 220\n    41\tlines. Lines 1--190 define the objects the theorem speaks about: the\n    42\tformulas, their canonical encoding as a bit string, the multitape machine,\n    43\tits step and its starting configuration, the running-time bound\n    44\t$\\runtimeScale$ and the wrapper around it, the count word, and the\n    45\tlocated configuration that records which tape cells a run has visited.\n    46\tLines 191--219 state the theorem \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} and end\n    47\tit with \\texttt{sorry}, the placeholder that a solution must replace.\n    48\tLine 220 closes the last namespace.\n    49\t\n    50\tOur \\texttt{PrimitiveMachine.lean} is lines 1--190 and 220, and nothing\n    51\telse: after a thirty-line header that records the source, the commit, the\n    52\tfile's hash and the Apache licence, the file is the copy\n    53\t\\leanref{copy-model}. The header says so, and a script checks it.\n    54\t\\repofile{paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py} reads both files with\n    55\t\\texttt{open()} only, takes everything in ours from the line\n    56\t\\texttt{import Mathlib} on, and compares it byte for byte with the clone's\n    57\tlines 1--190 followed by line 220. The diff is empty: 191 lines on each\n    58\tside, identical. The script also checks the clone file's SHA-256 against\n    59\tthe hash the header records, and that the clone is at commit\n    60\t\\texttt{4eaee875}. The log is \\repofile{paper3/lean/rediff.log}.\n    61\t\n    62\tSo the copy holds the theorem's \\emph{vocabulary} and not the theorem.\n    63\tThe \\texttt{sorry} at line 218 is outside the copied range, and our file\n    64\thas no \\texttt{sorry} and no axiom declaration anywhere (the same script\n    65\tcounts them: zero and zero in each of our two files). Everything in the\n    66\tcopy is a definition. A definition can be badly chosen, but it cannot be\n    67\tfalse; what the definitions mean is Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s\n    68\tsubject, and the copy adds nothing to it.\n    69\t\n    70\t\\subsection{The restatement}\n    71\t\\label{sec:lean-restatement}\n    72\t\n    73\t\\texttt{Frame.lean} defines\n    74\t\\[\n    75\t  \\SubexpCount\\ (k : \\mathbb{N}) : \\mathrm{Prop}\n    76\t\\]\n    77\tas the body of the comparator theorem for width $k$, with the theorem's\n    78\ttwo binders handled thus: $k$ is the argument of the def, and the\n    79\thypothesis $2 \\le k$ is carried by a second def,\n    80\t\\texttt{ExactCountAndSat}, which says ``for every $k \\ge 2$,\n    81\t\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\)'' (Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}, \\ref{lean:subexpcount} and \\ref{lean:exactcountandsat}).\n    82\tIn words, \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) is the sentence of\n    83\tSection~\\ref{sec:statement}: one fixed machine, on every\n    84\tbounded-width formula, writes the exact number of satisfying assignments,\n    85\tanswers whether there is one, runs within the bound built from\n    86\t$\\runtimeScale$, and visits only polynomially many cells.\n    87\t\n    88\tThe same script checks that the body is the comparator's. It takes the\n    89\t23 lines after \\texttt{def SubexpCount (k : }$\\mathbb{N}$\\texttt{) : Prop :=} in our file\n    90\t(lines 85--107) and the 23 lines after the theorem's binders in the clone\n    91\t(lines 195--217, with the closing \\texttt{:= by} removed), and compares\n    92\tthem in two ways: with every run of white space collapsed to one space,\n    93\tand line by line with the indentation stripped. Both comparisons find the\n    94\ttwo texts identical, 1,073 characters. The only difference is that each\n    95\tof our lines starts two spaces further left, because a def's body sits\n    96\ttwo columns inside a theorem's type. We call this ``character for\n    97\tcharacter''; it is a textual match, by the same method as\n    98\tSection~\\ref{sec:proof}'s, and it rests on the file we copied from being\n    99\tthe comparator file at the commit we name.\n   100\t\n   101\tNeither def is proved here, and neither is an axiom. The truth of\n   102\t\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) for $k \\ge 2$ rests on OpenAI's proof and on\n   103\tSections~\\ref{sec:proof} and \\ref{sec:replay}, not on anything in our\n   104\tlibrary. Our library would be unchanged if the proof were wrong.\n   105\t\n   106\t\\subsection{What is proved, and what it means in words}\n   107\t\\label{sec:lean-lemmas}\n   108\t\n   109\tSix small lemmas sit beside the defs, each proved in a few lines\n   110\t(Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}, \\ref{lean:subexpcount-anti}--\\ref{lean:trace-projection}). None of them says\n   111\tanything about any machine's running time or about whether the hypothesis\n   112\tis true.\n   113\t\n   114\t\\begin{itemize}\n   115\t\\item \\textbf{A machine for width $k$ is a machine for every smaller\n   116\t  width.} \\texttt{SubexpCount.anti}: if \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) holds and\n   117\t  $k' \\le k$ then \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k'\\) holds, with the same machine and\n   118\t  the same constants, because a formula whose clauses have at most $k'$\n   119\t  literals has at most $k$. \\texttt{ExactCountAndSat.subexpCount} unpacks\n   120\t  the ``for every $k \\ge 2$'' form.\n   121\t\\item \\textbf{The bound is what it is.} \\texttt{runtimeBound\\_eq}: the\n   122\t  wrapper \\texttt{runtimeBound} $C\\,A\\,d\\,L\\,n$ unfolds, by definition, to\n   123\t  $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\cdot\\rhon}$, the formula Section~\\ref{sec:statement}\n   124\t  reads. The proof is \\texttt{rfl}: the two sides are the same term.\n   125\t\\item \\textbf{The exponent is $o(n)$.} \\texttt{tendsto\\_log\\_sq\\_div\\_sqrt}\n   126\t  says $(\\ln y)^2/\\sqrt{y} \\to 0$ as $y \\to \\infty$, and\n   127\t  \\texttt{runtimeScale\\_isLittleO} says that $\\rhon/n \\to 0$ as\n   128\t  $n \\to \\infty$, that is, $\\rhon = o(n)$ \\leanref{rho-little-o}. This\n   129\t  is the limit fact that Sections~\\ref{sec:statement},\n   130\t  \\ref{sec:beyond} and \\ref{sec:consequences} cite: in the limit the\n   131\t  exponent is smaller than $n$ by any factor one names. It is true at the\n   132\t  same time as the title's fact that $\\rhon > n$ for every\n   133\t  $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, and the Lean says nothing about where the limit\n   134\t  begins to bite. \\texttt{isSubexpExponent\\_runtimeScale} is the same\n   135\t  statement for $A \\cdot \\rhon$, for every constant $A$\n   136\t  \\leanref{is-subexp-exponent}.\n   137\t\\item \\textbf{The time bound is a bound on the plain machine.}\n   138\t  \\texttt{Trace.config\\_of\\_locatedStep}: the comparator states its time\n   139\t  bound on the \\emph{located} step, which records visited cells beside\n   140\t  each configuration. A run of the located step projects to a run of the\n   141\t  plain step with the same number of steps, so the bound is a bound on\n   142\t  the machine itself \\leanref{trace-projection}. The one-line\n   143\t  \\texttt{LocatedCfg.monitor\\_config} is its helper.\n   144\t\\end{itemize}\n   145\t\n   146\t\\subsection{The axiom audit}\n   147\t\\label{sec:lean-audit}\n   148\t\n   149\tLean records, for every proved statement, the axioms its proof uses.\n   150\tThree are standard in Mathlib and in every proof of Sections~\\ref{sec:proof}\n   151\tand \\ref{sec:replay}: \\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Classical.choice} and\n   152\t\\texttt{Quot.sound}. A fou