{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QwH5Kdu43yNfotY8R4E52W","type":"tool_result","content":"     1\t% Related work and context -- drafted by loop0015 item 08 (2026-11-05).\n     2\t% Every quotation below is located by paper3/scripts/related_work.py\n     3\t% (python3 -I; --check verifies without writing), which reads the fetched\n     4\t% texts under paper3/related/fetched/ (hashes in Table 9.1), the clone at\n     5\t% 4eaee875, and the manuscript extract reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt.\n     6\t% Nothing is quoted from memory; nothing in the clone is run.\n     7\t\\section{Related work and context}\n     8\t\\label{sec:related}\n     9\t\\begingroup\\sloppy\n    10\t\n    11\tThis section places the paper beside what others have written about the\n    12\tsame family and about the mechanism that checked it. There are five\n    13\tthings to place it beside: what the collection says about itself; the two\n    14\tpapers that stated the hypotheses; a published human audit of an earlier\n    15\tbatch of OpenAI's results; the checking tool, called the comparator; and\n    16\tthe second proof checker, called nanoda, that we ran in\n    17\tSection~\\ref{sec:replay}. The section ends by saying where we looked for\n    18\tan earlier statement of the title's observation and what we found.\n    19\tTable~\\ref{tab:related-sources} lists every source with where it is on\n    20\tdisk, when it was fetched, and its hash or commit. We quote only what is\n    21\ton disk; nothing in this section is quoted from memory or from the press.\n    22\t\n    23\t\\begin{table}[htbp]\n    24\t\\centering\\small\n    25\t\\caption{The sources of this section. ``Clone'' is the read-only copy of\n    26\tthe OpenAI collection at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}; ``fetched'' files were\n    27\tdownloaded on 2026-11-05 and are kept under \\texttt{paper3/related/fetched/}\n    28\twith their SHA-256 hashes. Written by\n    29\t\\texttt{paper3/scripts/related\\_work.py}.}\n    30\t\\label{tab:related-sources}\n    31\t\\input{tables/related_sources}\n    32\t\\end{table}\n    33\t\n    34\t\\subsection{What the collection says about itself}\n    35\t\\label{sec:related-collection}\n    36\t\n    37\tThe collection keeps a dated log, \\clonefile{history.md} (56 lines). Under\n    38\tthe heading ``October 26, 2026'' (line 11), the entry for this family is\n    39\tone bullet, which we quote in full (line 18):\n    40\t\\begin{quote}\\small\n    41\t``\\emph{The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False.} The paper gives a\n    42\tdeterministic algorithm that exactly counts satisfying assignments of\n    43\tfixed-width CNF formulas in subexponential time and polynomial space. The\n    44\tcase of 3-SAT refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis. An independent\n    45\tworking group of computer scientists led by Russell Impagliazzo,\n    46\tRamamohan Paturi, and Ryan Williams will examine and explain the proof.\n    47\tAt the group’s request, we are making the materials public immediately so\n    48\tothers can examine them as well.''\n    49\t\\end{quote}\n    50\tThree things in this sentence matter for us. First, the group it names is\n    51\tled by two of the three authors of the papers that stated the hypotheses\n    52\t(Section~\\ref{sec:related-hypotheses}). Second, the verb is ``will'': the\n    53\tlog announces an examination to come. Nothing from that group is in the\n    54\tclone, and we fetched nothing from it, so this paper has no finding of\n    55\ttheirs to cite, agree with or disagree with. Whatever that group\n    56\tpublishes about the proof will carry more weight on the proof than\n    57\tSection~\\ref{sec:proof} does, which only inspected and replayed. Third,\n    58\tthe log says ``the paper gives \\ldots{} subexponential time'' with no\n    59\texponent; the exponent is in the scope note.\n    60\t\n    61\tThat scope note is \\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, seventeen lines, quoted\n    62\tin full in Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}. It is the collection's own statement\n    63\tof the gap between what Lean proves (the exponent with the squared\n    64\t$\\log\\log$ factor) and what the manuscript claims (two sharper\n    65\texponents). Its table (line 17) links the result to the one comparator\n    66\tstatement, \\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean}.\n    67\tSo the collection itself records that the verified exponent is the weaker\n    68\tone. What it does not record is what the weaker exponent means at any\n    69\tsize: that $\\rhon$, the verified exponent, is larger than $n$ for every\n    70\t$n$ between $50$ and $e^{5504}$, so that the verified bound with constant\n    71\tone sits above $2^n$ at every size anyone will ever run\n    72\t(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). That is the title's observation, and the\n    73\tnote does not make it.\n    74\t\n    75\tThe third document is \\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/README.md},\n    76\tten lines. It says how the collection's checks are run: ``Install\n    77\t\\texttt{comparator}, \\texttt{landrun}, and \\texttt{lean4export}, and make\n    78\tthem available on \\texttt{PATH}.'' (line 3), then from the \\texttt{lean/}\n    79\tdirectory \\texttt{lake update}, \\texttt{lake exe cache get}, and\n    80\t\\texttt{lake env comparator} on a family's JSON (lines 7--9; the example\n    81\tis another family's). The JSON for this family has six fields\n    82\t(Section~\\ref{sec:related-comparator} reads them).\n    83\t\n    84\t\\subsection{The two papers that stated the hypotheses}\n    85\t\\label{sec:related-hypotheses}\n    86\t\n    87\tETH, the Exponential Time Hypothesis, says that 3-SAT (deciding whether a\n    88\tformula whose clauses have three literals each can be satisfied) has no\n    89\talgorithm whose running time grows slower than every exponential in the\n    90\tnumber of variables. SETH, the Strong form, says that as the clause width\n    91\tgrows, the best possible base of the exponential tends to $2$, the base of\n    92\ttrying every assignment. Both are from \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}; the\n    93\tstandard setting of SETH, and the lemma that lets one move between the\n    94\tnumber of variables and the number of clauses, are from\n    95\t\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}. The manuscript cites both as\n    96\t\\texttt{[IP01]} and \\texttt{[IPZ01]} (extract page 66, lines 3733--3736)\n    97\tand states each hypothesis in its own words in its \\S1.1.1, which\n    98\tSection~\\ref{sec:consequences} quotes with page and line. We do not hold\n    99\teither paper: \\repofile{literature/} has no file for them, and\n   100\t\\repofile{literature/MISSING.md} now records both with their DOIs. Every\n   101\tstatement of a hypothesis in this paper is therefore the manuscript's\n   102\tstatement of it, not the original's, and we quote nothing from the two\n   103\toriginals. The manuscript's own account of what motivated SETH is one\n   104\tsentence we can locate (page 4, lines 170--171): the improvements of the\n   105\tknown algorithms ``over exhaustive search diminish as $k$ grows [IP01,\n   106\tOpen Problems, item 2]''. Exhaustive search is the ``trying all $2^n$\n   107\tassignments'' of the title.\n   108\t\n   109\t\\subsection{A human audit of an earlier batch of results}\n   110\t\\label{sec:related-audit}\n   111\t\n   112\t\\citet{SienickiSienicki2026} is a published human audit of OpenAI's\n   113\tAI-generated proofs. We fetched its arXiv abstract page and API record\n   114\t(v1 of 3 August 2026, v2 of 25 August, v3 of 9 September 2026; 13 pages;\n   115\tprimary class cs.AI). It is not about this family. Its abstract says\n   116\twhat it covers: ``We assess 18 chapter-specific reviews of the ten\n   117\tmathematical results announced by OpenAI on 1 August 2026, alongside\n   118\treview standards, Lean formalizations, subsequent research, and\n   119\tmathematical references.'' The family this paper reads is dated 17\n   120\tOctober 2026, revised 25 October, and entered the collection on 26","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"a334657a-cb77-424c-9a7b-411512c6db0b","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:31:32.148Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"     1\t% Related work and context -- drafted by loop0015 item 08 (2026-11-05).\n     2\t% Every quotation below is located by paper3/scripts/related_work.py\n     3\t% (python3 -I; --check verifies without writing), which reads the fetched\n     4\t% texts under paper3/related/fetched/ (hashes in Table 9.1), the clone at\n     5\t% 4eaee875, and the manuscript extract reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt.\n     6\t% Nothing is quoted from memory; nothing in the clone is run.\n     7\t\\section{Related work and context}\n     8\t\\label{sec:related}\n     9\t\\begingroup\\sloppy\n    10\t\n    11\tThis section places the paper beside what others have written about the\n    12\tsame family and about the mechanism that checked it. There are five\n    13\tthings to place it beside: what the collection says about itself; the two\n    14\tpapers that stated the hypotheses; a published human audit of an earlier\n    15\tbatch of OpenAI's results; the checking tool, called the comparator; and\n    16\tthe second proof checker, called nanoda, that we ran in\n    17\tSection~\\ref{sec:replay}. The section ends by saying where we looked for\n    18\tan earlier statement of the title's observation and what we found.\n    19\tTable~\\ref{tab:related-sources} lists every source with where it is on\n    20\tdisk, when it was fetched, and its hash or commit. We quote only what is\n    21\ton disk; nothing in this section is quoted from memory or from the press.\n    22\t\n    23\t\\begin{table}[htbp]\n    24\t\\centering\\small\n    25\t\\caption{The sources of this section. ``Clone'' is the read-only copy of\n    26\tthe OpenAI collection at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}; ``fe