{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgjK6BRYyPDBEmGaKxFQa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016ubcrM2u3RGBfan8DTW7qB","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/secB_appendix_technical.tex","content":"% Appendix B: the technical detail behind the plain text -- created by loop0015\n% item 08x (2026-11-05), the plain-English rewrite. Every passage here was\n% moved out of Sections 2, 4, 6 and 7 unchanged in content: every line number\n% is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone (read with cat/sed -n only), every\n% manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt, and every number names its script in\n% the section that cites it. The plain text cites each subsection by label.\n\\section{Technical detail behind the plain text}\n\\label{app:tech}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nThe body of the paper is written so that a reader with no background in\ncomplexity theory or in proof assistants can follow it. The passages that\ncould not be made plain without losing precision are collected here, each\nunder the heading of the section that cites it. Nothing in this appendix\nis new; it is the dense form of what the body says in words.\n\n\\subsection{The definitions of the comparator file, line by line}\n\\label{app:tech-definitions}\n\nThis is the detail behind Section~\\ref{sec:statement-definitions}. Line\nnumbers are those of\n\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean} at commit\n\\texttt{4eaee875}.\n\n\\paragraph{Formulas and the count (lines 13--51).}\nA \\texttt{Literal V} is a variable with a sign (lines 19--22); a\n\\texttt{Clause V} is a \\texttt{Finset} of literals and a \\texttt{CNF V} a\n\\texttt{Finset} of clauses (lines 24--25). An assignment is any function\n\\(V \\to \\mathtt{Bool}\\) (line 17). A literal evaluates to the Boolean\n``the assignment agrees with its sign'' (lines 29--30), a clause to ``some\nliteral is true'' (lines 32--33), a formula to ``every clause is true''\n(lines 35--36). \\texttt{WidthAtMost k F} says every clause has at most\n\\(k\\) literals as a set (line 38). Over a finite variable type,\n\\texttt{satisfyingAssignments F} filters the universe of all assignments\n(lines 44--45), and \\texttt{count F} is its cardinality (line 47): the\nnumber of satisfying assignments among all \\(2^n\\) when\n\\(V = \\mathtt{Fin}\\ n\\). The \\texttt{Finset} choices change nothing in the\nmeaning: a repeated clause or literal collapses, the empty clause is\nallowed and unsatisfiable, a tautological clause \\(\\{x, \\neg x\\}\\) is\nallowed and counts two toward the width. That is consistent with the\nmanuscript's ``Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than \\(k\\)\nliterals'' (p.~1, line~44).\n\n\\paragraph{The input encoding (lines 53--81).}\n\\texttt{encodeNat n} is \\(n\\) ones followed by a zero (line 55): unary.\n\\texttt{frame} doubles a bit string, prefixing each bit with a one, and\nends it with a zero (lines 57--58). \\texttt{encodeList enc} prefixes each\nelement's frame with a one and ends the list with a zero (lines 60--62).\nA literal becomes the number \\(2\\cdot\\mathrm{var} + [\\text{positive}]\\),\nat most \\(2n - 1\\) (lines 64--65); a clause is its sorted list of literal\nnumbers, a formula its sorted list of clauses (lines 67--71).\n\\texttt{encodeCNF F} is \\texttt{encodeNat n} followed by the formula's\nlist of lists of unary numbers (lines 73--77), and\n\\texttt{formulaBitLength F} is the length of that bit string (line 79).\nTwo things follow. The number of variables \\(n\\) is part of the input,\nso a variable that occurs in no clause is still counted. And the length\nis polynomial: a clause of \\(j \\le k\\) literals costs \\(2j(4n+1)+4\\) bits,\nso\n\\[\n  L \\;=\\; \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F \\;\\le\\; n + 2 + m\\,\\bigl(2k(4n+1) + 4\\bigr)\n  \\;=\\; O(k\\,n\\,m)\n\\]\nfor \\(m\\) clauses. The solution itself proves\n\\((L+1)^d \\le (8k+7)^d\\,(n + |F| + 1)^{2d}\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Statement.lean}, lines 9--14). Emulating the\ndefinitions (\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}),\na formula with \\(n = 125\\), \\(m = 500\\), \\(k = 4\\) encodes to\n\\(L = 1{,}006{,}223\\) bits and one with \\(n = 1{,}000\\), \\(m = 4{,}000\\),\n\\(k = 3\\) to \\(47.9\\) million, both under the bound. Nothing in the\nencoding is exponential; the unary numbers are linear in \\(n\\).\n\n\\paragraph{The machine (lines 83--128).}\n\\texttt{PrimitiveMachine} (lines 92--99) is a finite object: a number\n\\texttt{tapeCount} of tapes and a number \\texttt{stateCount} of states,\nan input tape and a start state, a table \\texttt{scan} saying which tape\neach state reads, and a table \\texttt{transition} from\n\\(\\mathtt{Fin}\\ \\mathtt{stateCount} \\times \\mathtt{Option\\ Bool}\\)---a\ndomain of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) points---into either a halting\nanswer (a \\texttt{Bool}) or a next state with one action. The action type\nis Mathlib's \\texttt{Turing.TM0.Stmt (Option Bool)}: move the head left or\nright, or write one of the three symbols \\texttt{none}, \\texttt{some\nfalse}, \\texttt{some true}, five values in all\n(\\path{Mathlib/Computability/TuringMachine/PostTuringMachine.lean}\nlines 118--120 and \\path{Tape.lean} lines 421--423 at Mathlib\n\\texttt{d13f23b7}, the clone's pinned revision). A configuration\n\\texttt{Cfg} (lines 103--105) is a state, halted or not, and one Mathlib\n\\texttt{Tape} per tape index (a head symbol and two blank-padded halves,\n\\path{Tape.lean} lines 408--414, blank \\(=\\) \\texttt{none}).\n\\texttt{step} (lines 107--119) returns \\texttt{none} in a halted state;\notherwise it reads the head of the one tape the state scans, looks the\npair up in the table, and either halts with the answer, or writes one\nsymbol or moves that one head by one cell and changes state. \\texttt{init}\n(lines 121--124) puts the input on the input tape with the head at its\nfirst bit and every other tape blank (\\texttt{Tape.mk}$_1$,\n\\path{Tape.lean} line 494). A Lean function on a finite domain is,\nwhatever term defines it, a finite table; so a step is one lookup in a\ntable of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) rows, and no field of the\nstructure can carry unbounded computation.\n\n\\paragraph{Traces (lines 87--90).}\n\\texttt{Trace step t a c} is an inductive proposition with two\nconstructors: \\texttt{refl}, a trace of length 0 from \\(a\\) to \\(a\\)\nitself, and \\texttt{cons}, which extends a trace from \\(b\\) by one\nsuccessful step \\(a \\mapsto b\\). So it holds exactly when \\(c\\) is\nreached from \\(a\\) by \\(t\\) successful applications of \\texttt{step}, and\na trace of length 0 to a different configuration is impossible. Since\n\\texttt{step} is a function, the run is deterministic.\n\n\\paragraph{Space monitoring (lines 147--189).}\n\\texttt{LocatedCfg} (lines 149--153) pairs a configuration with, per tape,\nthe head's current position and the least and greatest positions seen so\nfar, as integers; \\texttt{locatedInit} starts every head at 0 with the\ninput tape's range covering the input (lines 155--159);\n\\texttt{headDisplacement} is \\(-1\\), \\(+1\\) or \\(0\\) for a move left, a\nmove right or a write (lines 161--164); \\texttt{monitor} (lines 166--182)\napplies the same table lookup to update the position and the running\nminimum and maximum of the scanned tape, and nothing else;\n\\texttt{locatedStep} is \\texttt{step} on the configuration followed by\n\\texttt{monitor} (lines 184--186). \\texttt{visitedSpace} (lines 188--189)\nis the sum over tapes of \\(\\mathrm{upper} - \\mathrm{lower} + 1\\): the\nnumber of cells whose head position was ever visited. \\texttt{countWord a}\nis the binary expansion of \\(a\\), least significant bit first, with\n\\texttt{[false]} for \\(0\\) (line 147), and \\texttt{toNat} (lines 7--9)\ndecodes such a word.\n\n\\paragraph{The exponent and the bound (lines 130--141).}\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n\\texttt{runtimeScale n = n * (Real.log (Real.log (n + 16)))\\^{}2 /}\\\\\n\\texttt{\\quad Real.sqrt (Real.log (n + 16))}\n\\end{quote}\n(lines 132--134, transcribed; the casts to \\(\\mathbb{R}\\) omitted), that is\n\\[\n  \\rhon \\;=\\; \\frac{n\\,\\bigl(\\ln\\ln(n+16)\\bigr)^2}{\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}},\n\\]\nwith \\texttt{Real.log} the natural logarithm. The manuscript fixes the\nsame base: ``Throughout, log denotes the natural logarithm'' (p.~1, lines\n48--49; again at p.~7, lines 326--327). The shift by 16 keeps\n\\(\\ln\\ln\\) defined and positive at every \\(n \\ge 0\\) (\\(\\ln 16 = 2.77\\)),\nso the theorem can be stated for all \\(n\\) with no proviso. Then\n\\begin{quote}\n\\texttt{runtimeBound C A d L n = C * (L + 1)\\^{}d * 2\\^{}(A * runtimeScale n)}\n\\end{quote}\n(lines 136--139, transcribed), a real number, with the real-exponent\npower.\n\n\\subsection{The theorem, with its quantifiers}\n\\label{app:tech-theorem}\n\nThis is the detail behind Section~\\ref{sec:statement-theorem}. Lines\n194--217 of the comparator file state, for every \\(k\\) with \\(2 \\le k\\):\n\\[\n\\small\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\exists\\ \\mathtt{machine},\\ \\mathtt{countTape},\\ \\mathtt{answerTape},\\\n  C, A, S \\in \\mathbb{R},\\ d, e \\in \\mathbb{N},\\\\[2pt]\n\\quad 0 < C \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < A \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < S \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < d \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < e\\ \\wedge\\\\[2pt]\n\\quad \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ \\forall\\, F : \\mathtt{CNF}\\ (\\mathtt{Fin}\\ n),\\\n  \\mathtt{WidthAtMost}\\ k\\ F \\;\\rightarrow\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad \\exists\\ \\mathtt{time} \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ \\mathtt{final} : \\mathtt{LocatedCfg}\\ \\mathtt{machine},\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\mathtt{Trace}\\ (\\mathtt{locatedStep}\\ \\mathtt{machine})\\ \\mathtt{time}\\\n  (\\mathtt{locatedInit}\\ \\mathtt{machine}\\ (\\mathtt{encodeCNF}\\ F))\\ \\mathtt{final}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\text{(lines 201--202)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the count tape holds \\texttt{countWord (count F)}}\n  \\quad\\text{(203--204)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the answer tape holds the bit \\([0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]\\)}\n  \\quad\\text{(205--206)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the halting state carries that bit}\n  \\quad\\text{(207)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\mathtt{toNat}\\ (\\mathtt{countWord}\\ (\\mathtt{count}\\ F)) = \\mathtt{count}\\ F\n  \\quad\\text{(208)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ |\\mathtt{countWord}\\ (\\mathtt{count}\\ F)| \\le n + 1\n  \\quad\\text{(209)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\mathtt{time} \\le C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\,\\rhon},\\ L = \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F\n  \\quad\\text{(210--211)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\forall\\ \\text{prefix of the trace},\\\n  \\mathtt{visitedSpace} \\le S\\,(L+1)^e\n  \\quad\\text{(212--217).}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe machine and the five constants are quantified after \\(k\\) and before\n\\(n\\) and \\(F\\); inside the universal quantifier only \\texttt{time} and\n\\texttt{final} are chosen. This is the standard \\(O_k(1)\\) reading: for\neach \\(k\\) one fixed finite machine and one set of constants serve every\nformula of every size. The table's size is \\(O_k(1)\\) in the same sense,\nwith no explicit function of \\(k\\). There is no lower bound on \\(n\\): the\nmanuscript's Theorem~1.1 and Proposition~6.4 are stated for \\(n \\ge 20\\)\n(p.~2, lines 56--57; p.~43, lines 2474--2475), and the solution's\nauxiliary \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} keeps \\(20 \\le n\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 104--109); the exported\ntheorem does not, and the \\(+16\\) in \\(\\rhon\\) is what makes that\npossible.\n\nThe proof's own constants, read for their values only: at the point where\nthe large-input run is assembled, the exponent constant is set to\n\\(A = 256 + A_t\\) with \\(A_t \\ge 1\\) taken from a leaf contract\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 51;\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean}, lines 792--793), and\n\\(1 \\le C\\), \\(1 \\le A\\) are in the large-input run's own statement\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 12). The witness\nmachine is compiled from a program over 80 one-bit registers and 1,220\ntapes, with \\(\\mathtt{countTape} = 256\\) and \\(\\mathtt{answerTape} = 1216\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 98--101); its state count is\na finite type's cardinality that nothing computes. The solution's\ninternal \\(\\rho\\) (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean}, lines\n10--11) is in base 2 and is related to \\texttt{runtimeScale} by\n\\(\\mathtt{runtimeScale}\\ n \\le 4\\,\\rho_2(n)\\) for \\(n \\ge 20\\)\n(lines 190--191); the base changes the exponent by a constant factor\nthat \\(A\\) absorbs.\n\nThe clause-count bound used in Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and\n\\ref{sec:consequences}: a \\texttt{Finset} of clauses of width at most\n\\(k\\) over \\(n\\) variables has at most \\(\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j} =\nO(n^k)\\) members (\\(2{,}604{,}376\\) at \\(n = 125\\), \\(k = 3\\)), so\n\\(m = O(n^k)\\), \\(L = O(k\\,n\\,m) = O(n^{k+1})\\), and the whole bound is\n\\(n^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\), which is \\(2^{o(n)}\\) in \\(n\\) alone because\n\\(\\rhon/n \\to 0\\) \\leanref{rho-little-o}.\n\n\\subsection{The spine of the proof, with every line}\n\\label{app:tech-spine}\n\nThis is the detail behind Section~\\ref{sec:proof-spine}. Unqualified\npaths are relative to \\path{lean/OAI/Algorithms/SubexponentialSAT/} in\nthe clone.\n\n\\paragraph{The machine.} The theorem's machine is\n\\texttt{GuardedCompiler.primitive program} with the count tape\n\\texttt{256} and the answer tape \\texttt{1216} (both \\texttt{: Fin 1220};\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~100), where \\texttt{program}\nis \\texttt{Native.countingProgram cutoff k} \\texttt{(NativeLeafProgram.command k)}\n(line~40), a \\texttt{BitProgram.Program 80 1220 labels} (line~11): a\nfirst-order program over 80 one-bit registers, 1,220 Boolean stacks and\na finite label set, with twelve instruction kinds\n(\\clonefile{BitProgram.lean}, lines 10--26). \\texttt{primitive}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~131) is a\n\\texttt{noncomputable abbrev}: it takes the transition table\n\\texttt{transition p} (lines 30--47, one case per instruction) and the\nstack selector \\texttt{selected p} (lines 14--17), feeds them to the\nstack compiler's controller, and renames the finite state type to\n\\texttt{Fin stateCount} through \\texttt{Fintype.equivFin} (line~132).\nThe machine is thus a concrete table defined by pattern matching on the\nprogram, composed with a classical renaming of states; the theorem is\nexistential, so the noncomputability costs it nothing.\n\n\\paragraph{The time bound, and the constants.} For \\texttt{cutoff < n},\n\\path{NativeLargeEntry.run} (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean},\nlines 11--33) supplies a trace of length at most\n\\texttt{runtimeBound C A d L n} (line~27) with $1 \\le C \\wedge 1 \\le A$\n(line~12). Its exponent constant is \\texttt{256 + At} (line~51), where\n\\texttt{At} comes from the leaf program's uniform contract\n(\\path{NativeLeafContract.uniform_contract}, line~39; the contract\ngives $1 \\le \\mathtt{At}$, \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean},\nline~793), and its cutoff is \\texttt{max 20 (max Nleaf Ns)} (line~50),\nwith \\texttt{Ns} obtained non-constructively from an eventual inequality\n(\\path{Filter.eventually_atTop}, line~48). The $2^{A\\cdot\\mathtt{runtimeScale}\\,n}$\nfactor is assembled by \\path{runtime_of_tree_and_leaf} (line~46)\nfrom the DFS visit bound of \\path{Native.outer_from_setup}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean}, lines 53--56: visits at most\na square polynomial in $n$ times $2^{n/\\mathtt{sparsifySchedule}\\,n}$)\nand the leaf time. For $n \\le \\mathtt{cutoff}$,\n\\path{Native.entry_small_primitive}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeEntry.lean}, lines 81--100) gives\n$\\mathtt{time} \\le 28700\\,(L+1)^2$ (line~92) through the finite scanner\nof \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/FiniteFallback.lean}. The two branches are\nfolded by \\texttt{runtime\\_with\\_fallback} and\n\\texttt{space\\_with\\_fallback}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuterBudgets.lean}, lines 297 and 317)\ninto one triple, and the witnesses the final theorem exhibits are\n\\texttt{C + 28700, A + 1, S + 5022741, d + 2, e + 2}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~41). So the proof as written\nhas an exponent constant of at least $258$ in front of $\\rhon$, a\npolynomial degree \\texttt{d} that depends on $k$ (line~51: $\\mathtt{db} + (4(k-2)+2) + \\mathtt{df} + 2$) and a leading constant of at least\n$28{,}701$; none of these is in the statement, which asserts only that\nthey are positive, and none of \\texttt{At}, \\texttt{db}, \\texttt{df} is\ncomputed anywhere.\n\n\\paragraph{Where a hidden cost model would live, and why it cannot.}\nThe compile lemmas conclude in\nthe comparator's own vocabulary: \\path{GuardedCompiler.primitive_execution}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, lines 158--162) turns a\nsource run of \\texttt{n} steps into \\texttt{RunsIn (primitive p).step\n(7 * n)}, with the overhead \\texttt{7} proved as a theorem\n(\\texttt{compiler\\_overhead}, lines 152--153), and\n\\path{CompileSpace.compile_word_answer_space}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/CompileSpace.lean}, lines 125--143) produces a\n\\texttt{Computation.Trace (locatedStep (primitive p)) time \\ldots} with\n$\\mathtt{time} \\le 7\\cdot\\mathtt{sourceSteps}$ and\n$\\mathtt{visitedSpace}\\ \\mathtt{intermediate} \\le s\\,(\\mathtt{bound} + \\mathtt{input.length} + 15)$ on\nevery prefix (lines 134, 135 and 143); \\path{primitive_located_step}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~600) is the per-step\nform. \\texttt{Trace}, \\texttt{locatedStep} and \\texttt{visitedSpace} are\nthe challenge's definitions (its lines 87--90 and 149--189), and\n\\texttt{time} in the final theorem is literally the length of a\n\\texttt{Computation.Trace} of \\texttt{PrimitiveMachine.step} (its\nline~201). No cost function of the compiler's appears in the statement;\na wrong cost model would make these lemmas unprovable, not the theorem\nfalse.\n\n\\paragraph{Correctness of the count.} \\path{Native.outer_from_setup}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean}, lines 50--51) yields\n\\texttt{final.stacks ABI.countStack = countWord (SAT.count F)} and\n\\texttt{final.stacks ABI.answerStack = [decide (0 < SAT.count F)]} from\nthe canonical DFS execution, and\n\\path{Native.counting_primitive_from_entry} (line~73) transports\nboth to the primitive machine's tapes; \\texttt{countWord\\_toNat} and\n\\texttt{countWord\\_count\\_length} supply the decoding and length clauses\nof the statement.\n\n\\paragraph{The statement match, counted.} The solution file does not\nimport the challenge file: \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} begins\n(lines 1--3) with \\texttt{NativeLargeEntry}, \\texttt{SqrtLogSpace.Statement}\nand \\texttt{SqrtLog.StatementCanonical}. The library re-declares every\ndefinition the statement uses under the same fully qualified names, in\nseven files: the SAT vocabulary in \\clonefile{SAT.lean}, the bit\narithmetic in \\clonefile{BinaryArithmetic.lean}, the nine encoding\nfunctions in \\clonefile{Encoding.lean}, the machine in\n\\clonefile{Computation.lean}, \\texttt{runtimeScale} and\n\\texttt{runtimeBound} in \\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean}, the\nspace instrumentation in \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/VisitedTape.lean} and\n\\texttt{countWord} in \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/EncodingBounds.lean}\n(line~17, the challenge's line~147). The definition diff compares 35\ndeclarations: 33 are identical character for character after whitespace\nnormalisation, and the remaining two, \\texttt{runtimeScale} and\n\\texttt{runtimeBound}, differ only in that the challenge writes\n\\texttt{noncomputable def} where the library opens a\n\\texttt{noncomputable section} (\\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean},\nline~15). The theorem statement itself, from \\texttt{theorem\nexact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} to \\texttt{:= by}, is identical in the two files,\n1,353 characters raw and 1,124 after normalisation, at line~194 of the\nchallenge and line~74 of \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}; the\nchallenge's proof is the single word \\texttt{sorry} at its line~218.\n\n\\subsection{The keyword sweep, item by item}\n\\label{app:tech-keywords}\n\nThis is the detail behind Section~\\ref{sec:proof-keywords}. The only\n\\texttt{set\\_option}s in the closure are two\n\\texttt{set\\_option quotPrecheck false}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryQuery.lean}, line~15, and\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryWitness.lean}, line~14),\nwhich switch off a pre-check on local notation and have no bearing on\nsoundness; there is no \\texttt{maxHeartbeats}, \\texttt{maxRecDepth} or\n\\texttt{autoImplicit} option anywhere in the closure, and the lakefile\nsets \\texttt{autoImplicit} to \\texttt{false} for the whole package\n(\\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}, line~9). Nine declarations are\n\\texttt{opaque}, and every one carries a body: three recovery functions\n(\\texttt{queryRecovery} at line~59 of\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryQuery.lean}, with its\n\\texttt{:= by} at line~86; \\texttt{rawRecovery} and\n\\texttt{fixedRecovery} at lines 178 and 198 of\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryWitness.lean}) and six\n\\texttt{private opaque} constants of a subtype\n\\texttt{\\{ports // ports = \\ldots\\}}, each with an explicit value\n(line~22 of \\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramQueryCleanup.lean} and five\nothers). An \\texttt{opaque} with a body is a definition whose unfolding\nis hidden from elaboration; the kernel still checks the body against the\ntype. The 1,048 \\texttt{noncomputable} declarations, the eight\n\\texttt{open Classical}, and the four explicit uses each of\n\\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{propext} are the classical\nreasoning the comparator permits. The 148 \\texttt{instance}s and 40\n\\texttt{local instance}s are \\texttt{Fintype}, \\texttt{Decidable} and\nalgebraic instances; an instance cannot be ``inconsistent'' without an\naxiom, and there is none. The 14,034 lines with \\texttt{decide} are\nkernel-evaluated decision procedures, not the native evaluator. Nine\n\\texttt{termination\\_by} and five \\texttt{decreasing\\_by} clauses are the\nrecursions the kernel checks.\n\nThe lakefile's two hooks: a \\texttt{run\\_cmd} block (lines 290--304) that\nevaluates dependency declarations with \\texttt{unsafe env.evalConstCheck}\nand calls \\path{preparePatchedDependencies}, which clones and patches\neleven dependencies, and a \\texttt{post\\_update} hook (line~306 onward)\nthat verifies or applies the patches for the rest. They touch only\n\\texttt{.lake/packages}, and only non-Mathlib packages. The 23\ncompatibility patches under \\clonefile{lean/patches/} are for third-party\npackages (\\texttt{AINTLIB}, \\texttt{PrimeNumberTheoremAnd},\n\\texttt{carleson}, \\texttt{iut}, \\ldots) that this closure does not\nimport. The registry \\clonefile{lean/formalization.yaml} (lines\n1683--1685) points at the same declaration and file as the comparator's\nJSON.\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses in quantifier form}\n\\label{app:tech-hypotheses}\n\nThis is the detail behind Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-hypotheses}.\nThe property used throughout: for each fixed $k \\ge 2$ there is one\ndeterministic machine which, on the canonical encoding of any\n\\texttt{WidthAtMost}~$k$ formula $F$ on $n$ variables (comparator line\n38), halts with \\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape (lines\n203--204, 147), the bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ on its answer tape and\nin its halting state (lines 205--207), within $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$\nsteps (lines 210--211). Since $\\rhon/n \\to 0$ \\leanref{rho-little-o},\nfor every $\\delta > 0$ there is an $n_0(k, \\delta)$ beyond which the\nbound is below $2^{\\delta n}$; below $n_0$ the bound is a constant\ndepending on $k$ and $\\delta$. So the running time is\n$O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every $\\delta > 0$, and\n$2^{\\delta n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ with the polynomial written out.\n\n\\paragraph{ETH.} In quantifiers: there is a $\\delta > 0$ such that no\ndeterministic algorithm decides 3-SAT within $2^{\\delta n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$\nsteps on all inputs. Its negation: for every $\\delta > 0$ there is one.\nThe verified statement at $k = 3$ supplies, for every $\\delta > 0$, the\nsame machine, whose answer bit is the decision, within\n$O_{\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps. This holds on every\ninput of every size, so it refutes the hypothesis under either reading\nof ``$2^{\\Omega(n)}$'' (a lower bound at infinitely many $n$, or at all\nlarge $n$). In the vocabulary of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, where\n$s_k$ is the infimum of the $\\delta$ for which $k$-SAT has an\n$O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm and ETH is $s_3 > 0$: the verified statement\ngives $s_k = 0$ for every $k \\ge 2$, by one algorithm per $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{SETH.} The manuscript's form: for every\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$, there is a fixed $k$ for which no deterministic\nalgorithm solves $k$-SAT in $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ time. The\nnegation: there is an $\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ such that for every fixed\n$k$ there is a deterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$\nalgorithm. The verified statement gives more: for every\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ and every $k \\ge 2$, the machine for $k$ runs\nwithin $C_k(L+1)^{d_k}\\,2^{A_k\\rhon}$ steps, and\n$A_k\\rhon \\le (1-\\varepsilon)n$ for all $n \\ge n_0(k, \\varepsilon)$\nbecause $\\rhon = o(n)$; below $n_0$ the exponential factor is a\nconstant. So each $k$ has a $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$\nalgorithm, and the polynomial's degree depending on $k$ is exactly what\nthe hypothesis's ``for a fixed $k$'' permits. For the record, SETH\nimplies ETH: if 3-SAT had a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm then, by the\nSparsification Lemma of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}, which reduces\n$k$-SAT on $n$ variables to $2^{\\varepsilon n}$ instances with\n$O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ clauses and so, after the clause-splitting\nreduction, to 3-SAT on $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ variables, so would\n$k$-SAT for every fixed $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{Gap-ETH.} The negation of the manuscript's form: for every\n$\\delta, \\gamma > 0$ there is an $O(2^{\\gamma n})$ algorithm that\nseparates a satisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment\nviolates at least a $\\delta$ fraction of the clauses, already at\n$m = O(n)$. Exact decision separates them, since a formula in which\nevery assignment violates a positive fraction of the clauses is\nunsatisfiable; and the verified machine at $k = 3$ decides within\n$n^{O(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon} = O_{\\gamma}(2^{\\gamma n})$ steps for every\n$\\gamma > 0$, at every $m$ (at $m = O(n)$ the polynomial is\n$O(n^{2d})$). The promise is never used: the machine answers correctly\non every input, promised or not, with probability $1$, which refutes\nthe randomised form as well.\n\n\\paragraph{NSETH.} The negation of the manuscript's form: there is an\n$\\varepsilon > 0$ such that every fixed $k$ has a nondeterministic\n$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm for unsatisfiability. The\nverified machine's answer bit is \\texttt{false} exactly when\n$\\mathtt{count}\\ F = 0$ (lines 205--207), so it decides unsatisfiability\ndeterministically within the SETH bound above for every $\\varepsilon$\nand every $k$; and a deterministic machine is a nondeterministic one\nwhose only computation path is the deterministic run, so every\nacceptance convention for nondeterministic time is met.\n\n\\paragraph{\\#ETH.} The negation: for every $\\delta > 0$ there is a\ndeterministic $O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm that outputs the exact count.\nThe verified machine at $k = 3$ halts with \\texttt{countWord (count F)}\non its count tape, a binary word of length at most $n + 1$ that decodes\nto $\\mathtt{count}\\ F$ (lines 203--204, 208--209), within the same bound.\n\n\\paragraph{Shape (S) in detail.} For a theorem ``SETH $\\Rightarrow$ no\n$(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$'', a $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm\nfor $Y$ obtained from the verified statement would need the exponent of\nthe SAT instance to be below $k \\log_2 c$ by a constant factor, and the\nverified statement's exponent is $A\\rho(n')$ in the variable count $n'$\nof the SAT instance with $A$ unknown; no value of $A$ is on record below\n$257$, and no base $c$ is derived from the verified statement for any\n$Y$.\n\n\\subsection{How the two guarantee curves are scaled}\n\\label{app:tech-scaling}\n\nThis is the detail behind Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner-guarantees}.\nBoth guarantees are taken at constant 1 (the polynomial factors, $C$,\n$A$ and $d$ set aside) and both are multiplied by the same factor\n$t_{20}/2^{20}$, where $t_{20} = 1.28 \\times 10^{-4}$~s is CaDiCaL's\nmedian at $n = 20$. The factor is one number for both curves, so their\nratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves, $2^{\\rhon - n}$: below\n1 for $n < 50$, equal to 1 at $n = 50$ up to the rounding of\n$\\rho(50) = 50.13$, and above 1 from there ($2.8 \\times 10^{3}$ at\n$n = 100$ and $4.0 \\times 10^{51}$ at $n = 500$). A reader who prefers\neach curve scaled to the $n = 20$ point on its own would multiply\n$2^{\\rhon}$ by a further $2^{20 - \\rho(20)} = 2^{2.79}$ and see the two\ncurves touch at $n = 20$ and the verified one above the trivial one\nfrom there on; the common factor keeps the crossing where the statement\nputs it (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a). In that scale the\nbrute-force guarantee reaches one second near $n = 33$ and the verified\nguarantee near $n = 35$; at $n = 60$ the measured CaDiCaL median is\n$3 \\times 10^{-4}$~s against $2^n$ at $1.4 \\times 10^{8}$~s and\n$2^{\\rhon}$ at $5.4 \\times 10^{8}$~s, 11.6 and 12.2 decades\nrespectively; at $n = 500$ the decision median is above the budget\n(8 of 20 finished within 60~s), against $10^{140.6}$~s and\n$10^{192.2}$~s. Regenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.\n\n\\par\\endgroup\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4490,"cache_read_input_tokens":177713,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4490,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"f54efa48-d5ad-4b54-a005-e7253dc32f35","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:34:15.989Z","request_id":"req_011CgjK6B1zZrgdW1MRVLS77","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_016ubcrM2u3RGBfan8DTW7qB":{"file_path":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/secB_appendix_technical.tex","content":"% Appendix B: the technical detail behind the plain text -- created by loop0015\n% item 08x (2026-11-05), the plain-English rewrite. Every passage here was\n% moved out of Sections 2, 4, 6 and 7 unchanged in content: every line number\n% is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone (read with cat/sed -n only), every\n% manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt, and every number names its script in\n% the section that cites it. The plain text cites each subsection by label.\n\\section{Technical detail behind the plain text}\n\\label{app:tech}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nThe body of the paper is written so that a reader with no background in\ncomplexity theory or in proof assistants can follow it. The passages that\ncould not be made plain without losing precision are collected here, each\nunder the heading of the section that cites it. Nothing in this appendix\nis new; it is the dense form of what the body says in words.\n\n\\subsection{The definitions of the comparator file, line by line}\n\\label{app:tech-definitions}\n\nThis is the detail behind Section~\\ref{sec:statement-definitions}. Line\nnumbers are those of\n\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean} at commit\n\\texttt{4eaee875}.\n\n\\paragraph{Formulas and the count (lines 13--51).}\nA \\texttt{Literal V} is a variable with a sign (lines 19--22); a\n\\texttt{Clause V} is a \\texttt{Finset} of literals and a \\texttt{CNF V} a\n\\texttt{Finset} of clauses (lines 24--25). An assignment is any function\n\\(V \\to \\mathtt{Bool}\\) (line 17). A literal evaluates to the Boolean\n``the assignment agrees with its sign'' (lines 29--30), a clause to ``some\nliteral is true'' (lines 32--33), a formula to ``every clause is true''\n(lines 35--36). \\texttt{WidthAtMost k F} says every clause has at most\n\\(k\\) literals as a set (line 38). Over a finite variable type,\n\\texttt{satisfyingAssignments F} filters the universe of all assignments\n(lines 44--45), and \\texttt{count F} is its cardinality (line 47): the\nnumber of satisfying assignments among all \\(2^n\\) when\n\\(V = \\mathtt{Fin}\\ n\\). The \\texttt{Finset} choices change nothing in the\nmeaning: a repeated clause or literal collapses, the empty clause is\nallowed and unsatisfiable, a tautological clause \\(\\{x, \\neg x\\}\\) is\nallowed and counts two toward the width. That is consistent with the\nmanuscript's ``Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than \\(k\\)\nliterals'' (p.~1, line~44).\n\n\\paragraph{The input encoding (lines 53--81).}\n\\texttt{encodeNat n} is \\(n\\) ones followed by a zero (line 55): unary.\n\\texttt{frame} doubles a bit string, prefixing each bit with a one, and\nends it with a zero (lines 57--58). \\texttt{encodeList enc} prefixes each\nelement's frame with a one and ends the list with a zero (lines 60--62).\nA literal becomes the number \\(2\\cdot\\mathrm{var} + [\\text{positive}]\\),\nat most \\(2n - 1\\) (lines 64--65); a clause is its sorted list of literal\nnumbers, a formula its sorted list of clauses (lines 67--71).\n\\texttt{encodeCNF F} is \\texttt{encodeNat n} followed by the formula's\nlist of lists of unary numbers (lines 73--77), and\n\\texttt{formulaBitLength F} is the length of that bit string (line 79).\nTwo things follow. The number of variables \\(n\\) is part of the input,\nso a variable that occurs in no clause is still counted. And the length\nis polynomial: a clause of \\(j \\le k\\) literals costs \\(2j(4n+1)+4\\) bits,\nso\n\\[\n  L \\;=\\; \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F \\;\\le\\; n + 2 + m\\,\\bigl(2k(4n+1) + 4\\bigr)\n  \\;=\\; O(k\\,n\\,m)\n\\]\nfor \\(m\\) clauses. The solution itself proves\n\\((L+1)^d \\le (8k+7)^d\\,(n + |F| + 1)^{2d}\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Statement.lean}, lines 9--14). Emulating the\ndefinitions (\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}),\na formula with \\(n = 125\\), \\(m = 500\\), \\(k = 4\\) encodes to\n\\(L = 1{,}006{,}223\\) bits and one with \\(n = 1{,}000\\), \\(m = 4{,}000\\),\n\\(k = 3\\) to \\(47.9\\) million, both under the bound. Nothing in the\nencoding is exponential; the unary numbers are linear in \\(n\\).\n\n\\paragraph{The machine (lines 83--128).}\n\\texttt{PrimitiveMachine} (lines 92--99) is a finite object: a number\n\\texttt{tapeCount} of tapes and a number \\texttt{stateCount} of states,\nan input tape and a start state, a table \\texttt{scan} saying which tape\neach state reads, and a table \\texttt{transition} from\n\\(\\mathtt{Fin}\\ \\mathtt{stateCount} \\times \\mathtt{Option\\ Bool}\\)---a\ndomain of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) points---into either a halting\nanswer (a \\texttt{Bool}) or a next state with one action. The action type\nis Mathlib's \\texttt{Turing.TM0.Stmt (Option Bool)}: move the head left or\nright, or write one of the three symbols \\texttt{none}, \\texttt{some\nfalse}, \\texttt{some true}, five values in all\n(\\path{Mathlib/Computability/TuringMachine/PostTuringMachine.lean}\nlines 118--120 and \\path{Tape.lean} lines 421--423 at Mathlib\n\\texttt{d13f23b7}, the clone's pinned revision). A configuration\n\\texttt{Cfg} (lines 103--105) is a state, halted or not, and one Mathlib\n\\texttt{Tape} per tape index (a head symbol and two blank-padded halves,\n\\path{Tape.lean} lines 408--414, blank \\(=\\) \\texttt{none}).\n\\texttt{step} (lines 107--119) returns \\texttt{none} in a halted state;\notherwise it reads the head of the one tape the state scans, looks the\npair up in the table, and either halts with the answer, or writes one\nsymbol or moves that one head by one cell and changes state. \\texttt{init}\n(lines 121--124) puts the input on the input tape with the head at its\nfirst bit and every other tape blank (\\texttt{Tape.mk}$_1$,\n\\path{Tape.lean} line 494). A Lean function on a finite domain is,\nwhatever term defines it, a finite table; so a step is one lookup in a\ntable of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) rows, and no field of the\nstructure can carry unbounded computation.\n\n\\paragraph{Traces (lines 87--90).}\n\\texttt{Trace step t a c} is an inductive proposition with two\nconstructors: \\texttt{refl}, a trace of length 0 from \\(a\\) to \\(a\\)\nitself, and \\texttt{cons}, which extends a trace from \\(b\\) by one\nsuccessful step \\(a \\mapsto b\\). So it holds exactly when \\(c\\) is\nreached from \\(a\\) by \\(t\\) successful applications of \\texttt{step}, and\na trace of length 0 to a different configuration is impossible. Since\n\\texttt{step} is a function, the run is deterministic.\n\n\\paragraph{Space monitoring (lines 147--189).}\n\\texttt{LocatedCfg} (lines 149--153) pairs a configuration with, per tape,\nthe head's current position and the least and greatest positions seen so\nfar, as integers; \\texttt{locatedInit} starts every head at 0 with the\ninput tape's range covering the input (lines 155--159);\n\\texttt{headDisplacement} is \\(-1\\), \\(+1\\) or \\(0\\) for a move left, a\nmove right or a write (lines 161--164); \\texttt{monitor} (lines 166--182)\napplies the same table lookup to update the position and the running\nminimum and maximum of the scanned tape, and nothing else;\n\\texttt{locatedStep} is \\texttt{step} on the configuration followed by\n\\texttt{monitor} (lines 184--186). \\texttt{visitedSpace} (lines 188--189)\nis the sum over tapes of \\(\\mathrm{upper} - \\mathrm{lower} + 1\\): the\nnumber of cells whose head position was ever visited. \\texttt{countWord a}\nis the binary expansion of \\(a\\), least significant bit first, with\n\\texttt{[false]} for \\(0\\) (line 147), and \\texttt{toNat} (lines 7--9)\ndecodes such a word.\n\n\\paragraph{The exponent and the bound (lines 130--141).}\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n\\texttt{runtimeScale n = n * (Real.log (Real.log (n + 16)))\\^{}2 /}\\\\\n\\texttt{\\quad Real.sqrt (Real.log (n + 16))}\n\\end{quote}\n(lines 132--134, transcribed; the casts to \\(\\mathbb{R}\\) omitted), that is\n\\[\n  \\rhon \\;=\\; \\frac{n\\,\\bigl(\\ln\\ln(n+16)\\bigr)^2}{\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}},\n\\]\nwith \\texttt{Real.log} the natural logarithm. The manuscript fixes the\nsame base: ``Throughout, log denotes the natural logarithm'' (p.~1, lines\n48--49; again at p.~7, lines 326--327). The shift by 16 keeps\n\\(\\ln\\ln\\) defined and positive at every \\(n \\ge 0\\) (\\(\\ln 16 = 2.77\\)),\nso the theorem can be stated for all \\(n\\) with no proviso. Then\n\\begin{quote}\n\\texttt{runtimeBound C A d L n = C * (L + 1)\\^{}d * 2\\^{}(A * runtimeScale n)}\n\\end{quote}\n(lines 136--139, transcribed), a real number, with the real-exponent\npower.\n\n\\subsection{The theorem, with its quantifiers}\n\\label{app:tech-theorem}\n\nThis is the detail behind Section~\\ref{sec:statement-theorem}. Lines\n194--217 of the comparator file state, for every \\(k\\) with \\(2 \\le k\\):\n\\[\n\\small\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\exists\\ \\mathtt{machine},\\ \\mathtt{countTape},\\ \\mathtt{answerTape},\\\n  C, A, S \\in \\mathbb{R},\\ d, e \\in \\mathbb{N},\\\\[2pt]\n\\quad 0 < C \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < A \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < S \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < d \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < e\\ \\wedge\\\\[2pt]\n\\quad \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ \\forall\\, F : \\mathtt{CNF}\\ (\\mathtt{Fin}\\ n),\\\n  \\mathtt{WidthAtMost}\\ k\\ F \\;\\rightarrow\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad \\exists\\ \\mathtt{time} \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ \\mathtt{final} : \\mathtt{LocatedCfg}\\ \\mathtt{machine},\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\mathtt{Trace}\\ (\\mathtt{locatedStep}\\ \\mathtt{machine})\\ \\mathtt{time}\\\n  (\\mathtt{locatedInit}\\ \\mathtt{machine}\\ (\\mathtt{encodeCNF}\\ F))\\ \\mathtt{final}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\text{(lines 201--202)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the count tape holds \\texttt{countWord (count F)}}\n  \\quad\\text{(203--204)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the answer tape holds the bit \\([0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]\\)}\n  \\quad\\text{(205--206)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the halting state carries that bit}\n  \\quad\\text{(207)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\mathtt{toNat}\\ (\\mathtt{countWord}\\ (\\mathtt{count}\\ F)) = \\mathtt{count}\\ F\n  \\quad\\text{(208)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ |\\mathtt{countWord}\\ (\\mathtt{count}\\ F)| \\le n + 1\n  \\quad\\text{(209)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\mathtt{time} \\le C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\,\\rhon},\\ L = \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F\n  \\quad\\text{(210--211)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\forall\\ \\text{prefix of the trace},\\\n  \\mathtt{visitedSpace} \\le S\\,(L+1)^e\n  \\quad\\text{(212--217).}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe machine and the five constants are quantified after \\(k\\) and before\n\\(n\\) and \\(F\\); inside the universal quantifier only \\texttt{time} and\n\\texttt{final} are chosen. This is the standard \\(O_k(1)\\) reading: for\neach \\(k\\) one fixed finite machine and one set of constants serve every\nformula of every size. The table's size is \\(O_k(1)\\) in the same sense,\nwith no explicit function of \\(k\\). There is no lower bound on \\(n\\): the\nmanuscript's Theorem~1.1 and Proposition~6.4 are stated for \\(n \\ge 20\\)\n(p.~2, lines 56--57; p.~43, lines 2474--2475), and the solution's\nauxiliary \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} keeps \\(20 \\le n\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 104--109); the exported\ntheorem does not, and the \\(+16\\) in \\(\\rhon\\) is what makes that\npossible.\n\nThe proof's own constants, read for their values only: at the point where\nthe large-input run is assembled, the exponent constant is set to\n\\(A = 256 + A_t\\) with \\(A_t \\ge 1\\) taken from a leaf contract\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 51;\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean}, lines 792--793), and\n\\(1 \\le C\\), \\(1 \\le A\\) are in the large-input run's own statement\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 12). The witness\nmachine is compiled from a program over 80 one-bit registers and 1,220\ntapes, with \\(\\mathtt{countTape} = 256\\) and \\(\\mathtt{answerTape} = 1216\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 98--101); its state count is\na finite type's cardinality that nothing computes. The solution's\ninternal \\(\\rho\\) (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean}, lines\n10--11) is in base 2 and is related to \\texttt{runtimeScale} by\n\\(\\mathtt{runtimeScale}\\ n \\le 4\\,\\rho_2(n)\\) for \\(n \\ge 20\\)\n(lines 190--191); the base changes the exponent by a constant factor\nthat \\(A\\) absorbs.\n\nThe clause-count bound used in Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and\n\\ref{sec:consequences}: a \\texttt{Finset} of clauses of width at most\n\\(k\\) over \\(n\\) variables has at most \\(\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j} =\nO(n^k)\\) members (\\(2{,}604{,}376\\) at \\(n = 125\\), \\(k = 3\\)), so\n\\(m = O(n^k)\\), \\(L = O(k\\,n\\,m) = O(n^{k+1})\\), and the whole bound is\n\\(n^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\), which is \\(2^{o(n)}\\) in \\(n\\) alone because\n\\(\\rhon/n \\to 0\\) \\leanref{rho-little-o}.\n\n\\subsection{The spine of the proof, with every line}\n\\label{app:tech-spine}\n\nThis is the detail behind Section~\\ref{sec:proof-spine}. Unqualified\npaths are relative to \\path{lean/OAI/Algorithms/SubexponentialSAT/} in\nthe clone.\n\n\\paragraph{The machine.} The theorem's machine is\n\\texttt{GuardedCompiler.primitive program} with the count tape\n\\texttt{256} and the answer tape \\texttt{1216} (both \\texttt{: Fin 1220};\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~100), where \\texttt{program}\nis \\texttt{Native.countingProgram cutoff k} \\texttt{(NativeLeafProgram.command k)}\n(line~40), a \\texttt{BitProgram.Program 80 1220 labels} (line~11): a\nfirst-order program over 80 one-bit registers, 1,220 Boolean stacks and\na finite label set, with twelve instruction kinds\n(\\clonefile{BitProgram.lean}, lines 10--26). \\texttt{primitive}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~131) is a\n\\texttt{noncomputable abbrev}: it takes the transition table\n\\texttt{transition p} (lines 30--47, one case per instruction) and the\nstack selector \\texttt{selected p} (lines 14--17), feeds them to the\nstack compiler's controller, and renames the finite state type to\n\\texttt{Fin stateCount} through \\texttt{Fintype.equivFin} (line~132).\nThe machine is thus a concrete table defined by pattern matching on the\nprogram, composed with a classical renaming of states; the theorem is\nexistential, so the noncomputability costs it nothing.\n\n\\paragraph{The time bound, and the constants.} For \\texttt{cutoff < n},\n\\path{NativeLargeEntry.run} (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean},\nlines 11--33) supplies a trace of length at most\n\\texttt{runtimeBound C A d L n} (line~27) with $1 \\le C \\wedge 1 \\le A$\n(line~12). Its exponent constant is \\texttt{256 + At} (line~51), where\n\\texttt{At} comes from the leaf program's uniform contract\n(\\path{NativeLeafContract.uniform_contract}, line~39; the contract\ngives $1 \\le \\mathtt{At}$, \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean},\nline~793), and its cutoff is \\texttt{max 20 (max Nleaf Ns)} (line~50),\nwith \\texttt{Ns} obtained non-constructively from an eventual inequality\n(\\path{Filter.eventually_atTop}, line~48). The $2^{A\\cdot\\mathtt{runtimeScale}\\,n}$\nfactor is assembled by \\path{runtime_of_tree_and_leaf} (line~46)\nfrom the DFS visit bound of \\path{Native.outer_from_setup}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean}, lines 53--56: visits at most\na square polynomial in $n$ times $2^{n/\\mathtt{sparsifySchedule}\\,n}$)\nand the leaf time. For $n \\le \\mathtt{cutoff}$,\n\\path{Native.entry_small_primitive}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeEntry.lean}, lines 81--100) gives\n$\\mathtt{time} \\le 28700\\,(L+1)^2$ (line~92) through the finite scanner\nof \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/FiniteFallback.lean}. The two branches are\nfolded by \\texttt{runtime\\_with\\_fallback} and\n\\texttt{space\\_with\\_fallback}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuterBudgets.lean}, lines 297 and 317)\ninto one triple, and the witnesses the final theorem exhibits are\n\\texttt{C + 28700, A + 1, S + 5022741, d + 2, e + 2}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~41). So the proof as written\nhas an exponent constant of at least $258$ in front of $\\rhon$, a\npolynomial degree \\texttt{d} that depends on $k$ (line~51: $\\mathtt{db} + (4(k-2)+2) + \\mathtt{df} + 2$) and a leading constant of at least\n$28{,}701$; none of these is in the statement, which asserts only that\nthey are positive, and none of \\texttt{At}, \\texttt{db}, \\texttt{df} is\ncomputed anywhere.\n\n\\paragraph{Where a hidden cost model would live, and why it cannot.}\nThe compile lemmas conclude in\nthe comparator's own vocabulary: \\path{GuardedCompiler.primitive_execution}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, lines 158--162) turns a\nsource run of \\texttt{n} steps into \\texttt{RunsIn (primitive p).step\n(7 * n)}, with the overhead \\texttt{7} proved as a theorem\n(\\texttt{compiler\\_overhead}, lines 152--153), and\n\\path{CompileSpace.compile_word_answer_space}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/CompileSpace.lean}, lines 125--143) produces a\n\\texttt{Computation.Trace (locatedStep (primitive p)) time \\ldots} with\n$\\mathtt{time} \\le 7\\cdot\\mathtt{sourceSteps}$ and\n$\\mathtt{visitedSpace}\\ \\mathtt{intermediate} \\le s\\,(\\mathtt{bound} + \\mathtt{input.length} + 15)$ on\nevery prefix (lines 134, 135 and 143); \\path{primitive_located_step}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~600) is the per-step\nform. \\texttt{Trace}, \\texttt{locatedStep} and \\texttt{visitedSpace} are\nthe challenge's definitions (its lines 87--90 and 149--189), and\n\\texttt{time} in the final theorem is literally the length of a\n\\texttt{Computation.Trace} of \\texttt{PrimitiveMachine.step} (its\nline~201). No cost function of the compiler's appears in the statement;\na wrong cost model would make these lemmas unprovable, not the theorem\nfalse.\n\n\\paragraph{Correctness of the count.} \\path{Native.outer_from_setup}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean}, lines 50--51) yields\n\\texttt{final.stacks ABI.countStack = countWord (SAT.count F)} and\n\\texttt{final.stacks ABI.answerStack = [decide (0 < SAT.count F)]} from\nthe canonical DFS execution, and\n\\path{Native.counting_primitive_from_entry} (line~73) transports\nboth to the primitive machine's tapes; \\texttt{countWord\\_toNat} and\n\\texttt{countWord\\_count\\_length} supply the decoding and length clauses\nof the statement.\n\n\\paragraph{The statement match, counted.} The solution file does not\nimport the challenge file: \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} begins\n(lines 1--3) with \\texttt{NativeLargeEntry}, \\texttt{SqrtLogSpace.Statement}\nand \\texttt{SqrtLog.StatementCanonical}. The library re-declares every\ndefinition the statement uses under the same fully qualified names, in\nseven files: the SAT vocabulary in \\clonefile{SAT.lean}, the bit\narithmetic in \\clonefile{BinaryArithmetic.lean}, the nine encoding\nfunctions in \\clonefile{Encoding.lean}, the machine in\n\\clonefile{Computation.lean}, \\texttt{runtimeScale} and\n\\texttt{runtimeBound} in \\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean}, the\nspace instrumentation in \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/VisitedTape.lean} and\n\\texttt{countWord} in \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/EncodingBounds.lean}\n(line~17, the challenge's line~147). The definition diff compares 35\ndeclarations: 33 are identical character for character after whitespace\nnormalisation, and the remaining two, \\texttt{runtimeScale} and\n\\texttt{runtimeBound}, differ only in that the challenge writes\n\\texttt{noncomputable def} where the library opens a\n\\texttt{noncomputable section} (\\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean},\nline~15). The theorem statement itself, from \\texttt{theorem\nexact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} to \\texttt{:= by}, is identical in the two files,\n1,353 characters raw and 1,124 after normalisation, at line~194 of the\nchallenge and line~74 of \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}; the\nchallenge's proof is the single word \\texttt{sorry} at its line~218.\n\n\\subsection{The keyword sweep, item by item}\n\\label{app:tech-keywords}\n\nThis is the detail behind Section~\\ref{sec:proof-keywords}. The only\n\\texttt{set\\_option}s in the closure are two\n\\texttt{set\\_option quotPrecheck false}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryQuery.lean}, line~15, and\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryWitness.lean}, line~14),\nwhich switch off a pre-check on local notation and have no bearing on\nsoundness; there is no \\texttt{maxHeartbeats}, \\texttt{maxRecDepth} or\n\\texttt{autoImplicit} option anywhere in the closure, and the lakefile\nsets \\texttt{autoImplicit} to \\texttt{false} for the whole package\n(\\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}, line~9). Nine declarations are\n\\texttt{opaque}, and every one carries a body: three recovery functions\n(\\texttt{queryRecovery} at line~59 of\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryQuery.lean}, with its\n\\texttt{:= by} at line~86; \\texttt{rawRecovery} and\n\\texttt{fixedRecovery} at lines 178 and 198 of\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryWitness.lean}) and six\n\\texttt{private opaque} constants of a subtype\n\\texttt{\\{ports // ports = \\ldots\\}}, each with an explicit value\n(line~22 of \\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramQueryCleanup.lean} and five\nothers). An \\texttt{opaque} with a body is a definition whose unfolding\nis hidden from elaboration; the kernel still checks the body against the\ntype. The 1,048 \\texttt{noncomputable} declarations, the eight\n\\texttt{open Classical}, and the four explicit uses each of\n\\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{propext} are the classical\nreasoning the comparator permits. The 148 \\texttt{instance}s and 40\n\\texttt{local instance}s are \\texttt{Fintype}, \\texttt{Decidable} and\nalgebraic instances; an instance cannot be ``inconsistent'' without an\naxiom, and there is none. The 14,034 lines with \\texttt{decide} are\nkernel-evaluated decision procedures, not the native evaluator. Nine\n\\texttt{termination\\_by} and five \\texttt{decreasing\\_by} clauses are the\nrecursions the kernel checks.\n\nThe lakefile's two hooks: a \\texttt{run\\_cmd} block (lines 290--304) that\nevaluates dependency declarations with \\texttt{unsafe env.evalConstCheck}\nand calls \\path{preparePatchedDependencies}, which clones and patches\neleven dependencies, and a \\texttt{post\\_update} hook (line~306 onward)\nthat verifies or applies the patches for the rest. They touch only\n\\texttt{.lake/packages}, and only non-Mathlib packages. The 23\ncompatibility patches under \\clonefile{lean/patches/} are for third-party\npackages (\\texttt{AINTLIB}, \\texttt{PrimeNumberTheoremAnd},\n\\texttt{carleson}, \\texttt{iut}, \\ldots) that this closure does not\nimport. The registry \\clonefile{lean/formalization.yaml} (lines\n1683--1685) points at the same declaration and file as the comparator's\nJSON.\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses in quantifier form}\n\\label{app:tech-hypotheses}\n\nThis is the detail behind Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-hypotheses}.\nThe property used throughout: for each fixed $k \\ge 2$ there is one\ndeterministic machine which, on the canonical encoding of any\n\\texttt{WidthAtMost}~$k$ formula $F$ on $n$ variables (comparator line\n38), halts with \\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape (lines\n203--204, 147), the bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ on its answer tape and\nin its halting state (lines 205--207), within $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$\nsteps (lines 210--211). Since $\\rhon/n \\to 0$ \\leanref{rho-little-o},\nfor every $\\delta > 0$ there is an $n_0(k, \\delta)$ beyond which the\nbound is below $2^{\\delta n}$; below $n_0$ the bound is a constant\ndepending on $k$ and $\\delta$. So the running time is\n$O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every $\\delta > 0$, and\n$2^{\\delta n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ with the polynomial written out.\n\n\\paragraph{ETH.} In quantifiers: there is a $\\delta > 0$ such that no\ndeterministic algorithm decides 3-SAT within $2^{\\delta n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$\nsteps on all inputs. Its negation: for every $\\delta > 0$ there is one.\nThe verified statement at $k = 3$ supplies, for every $\\delta > 0$, the\nsame machine, whose answer bit is the decision, within\n$O_{\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps. This holds on every\ninput of every size, so it refutes the hypothesis under either reading\nof ``$2^{\\Omega(n)}$'' (a lower bound at infinitely many $n$, or at all\nlarge $n$). In the vocabulary of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, where\n$s_k$ is the infimum of the $\\delta$ for which $k$-SAT has an\n$O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm and ETH is $s_3 > 0$: the verified statement\ngives $s_k = 0$ for every $k \\ge 2$, by one algorithm per $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{SETH.} The manuscript's form: for every\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$, there is a fixed $k$ for which no deterministic\nalgorithm solves $k$-SAT in $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ time. The\nnegation: there is an $\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ such that for every fixed\n$k$ there is a deterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$\nalgorithm. The verified statement gives more: for every\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ and every $k \\ge 2$, the machine for $k$ runs\nwithin $C_k(L+1)^{d_k}\\,2^{A_k\\rhon}$ steps, and\n$A_k\\rhon \\le (1-\\varepsilon)n$ for all $n \\ge n_0(k, \\varepsilon)$\nbecause $\\rhon = o(n)$; below $n_0$ the exponential factor is a\nconstant. So each $k$ has a $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$\nalgorithm, and the polynomial's degree depending on $k$ is exactly what\nthe hypothesis's ``for a fixed $k$'' permits. For the record, SETH\nimplies ETH: if 3-SAT had a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm then, by the\nSparsification Lemma of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}, which reduces\n$k$-SAT on $n$ variables to $2^{\\varepsilon n}$ instances with\n$O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ clauses and so, after the clause-splitting\nreduction, to 3-SAT on $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ variables, so would\n$k$-SAT for every fixed $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{Gap-ETH.} The negation of the manuscript's form: for every\n$\\delta, \\gamma > 0$ there is an $O(2^{\\gamma n})$ algorithm that\nseparates a satisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment\nviolates at least a $\\delta$ fraction of the clauses, already at\n$m = O(n)$. Exact decision separates them, since a formula in which\nevery assignment violates a positive fraction of the clauses is\nunsatisfiable; and the verified machine at $k = 3$ decides within\n$n^{O(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon} = O_{\\gamma}(2^{\\gamma n})$ steps for every\n$\\gamma > 0$, at every $m$ (at $m = O(n)$ the polynomial is\n$O(n^{2d})$). The promise is never used: the machine answers correctly\non every input, promised or not, with probability $1$, which refutes\nthe randomised form as well.\n\n\\paragraph{NSETH.} The negation of the manuscript's form: there is an\n$\\varepsilon > 0$ such that every fixed $k$ has a nondeterministic\n$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm for unsatisfiability. The\nverified machine's answer bit is \\texttt{false} exactly when\n$\\mathtt{count}\\ F = 0$ (lines 205--207), so it decides unsatisfiability\ndeterministically within the SETH bound above for every $\\varepsilon$\nand every $k$; and a deterministic machine is a nondeterministic one\nwhose only computation path is the deterministic run, so every\nacceptance convention for nondeterministic time is met.\n\n\\paragraph{\\#ETH.} The negation: for every $\\delta > 0$ there is a\ndeterministic $O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm that outputs the exact count.\nThe verified machine at $k = 3$ halts with \\texttt{countWord (count F)}\non its count tape, a binary word of length at most $n + 1$ that decodes\nto $\\mathtt{count}\\ F$ (lines 203--204, 208--209), within the same bound.\n\n\\paragraph{Shape (S) in detail.} For a theorem ``SETH $\\Rightarrow$ no\n$(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$'', a $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm\nfor $Y$ obtained from the verified statement would need the exponent of\nthe SAT instance to be below $k \\log_2 c$ by a constant factor, and the\nverified statement's exponent is $A\\rho(n')$ in the variable count $n'$\nof the SAT instance with $A$ unknown; no value of $A$ is on record below\n$257$, and no base $c$ is derived from the verified statement for any\n$Y$.\n\n\\subsection{How the two guarantee curves are scaled}\n\\label{app:tech-scaling}\n\nThis is the detail behind Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner-guarantees}.\nBoth guarantees are taken at constant 1 (the polynomial factors, $C$,\n$A$ and $d$ set aside) and both are multiplied by the same factor\n$t_{20}/2^{20}$, where $t_{20} = 1.28 \\times 10^{-4}$~s is CaDiCaL's\nmedian at $n = 20$. The factor is one number for both curves, so their\nratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves, $2^{\\rhon - n}$: below\n1 for $n < 50$, equal to 1 at $n = 50$ up to the rounding of\n$\\rho(50) = 50.13$, and above 1 from there ($2.8 \\times 10^{3}$ at\n$n = 100$ and $4.0 \\times 10^{51}$ at $n = 500$). A reader who prefers\neach curve scaled to the $n = 20$ point on its own would multiply\n$2^{\\rhon}$ by a further $2^{20 - \\rho(20)} = 2^{2.79}$ and see the two\ncurves touch at $n = 20$ and the verified one above the trivial one\nfrom there on; the common factor keeps the crossing where the statement\nputs it (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a). In that scale the\nbrute-force guarantee reaches one second near $n = 33$ and the verified\nguarantee near $n = 35$; at $n = 60$ the measured CaDiCaL median is\n$3 \\times 10^{-4}$~s against $2^n$ at $1.4 \\times 10^{8}$~s and\n$2^{\\rhon}$ at $5.4 \\times 10^{8}$~s, 11.6 and 12.2 decades\nrespectively; at $n = 500$ the decision median is above the budget\n(8 of 20 finished within 60~s), against $10^{140.6}$~s and\n$10^{192.2}$~s. Regenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.\n\n\\par\\endgroup\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016ubcrM2u3RGBfan8DTW7qB","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/secB_appendix_technical.tex (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"f0354fe2-9022-437b-89c1-49ffe8d4bc1e","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:34:16.024Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/secB_appendix_technical.tex","content":"% Appendix B: the technical detail behind the plain text -- created by loop0015\n% item 08x (2026-11-05), the plain-English rewrite. Every passage here was\n% moved out of Sections 2, 4, 6 and 7 unchanged in content: every line number\n% is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone (read with cat/sed -n only), every\n% manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt, and every number names its script in\n% the section that cites it. The plain text cites each subsection by label.\n\\section{Technical detail behind the plain text}\n\\label{app:tech}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nThe body of the paper is written so that a reader with no background in\ncomplexity theory or in proof assistants can follow it. The passages that\ncould not be made plain without losing precision are collected here, each\nunder the heading of the section that cites it. Nothing in this appendix\nis new; it is the dense form of what the body says in words.\n\n\\subsection{The definitions of the comparator file, line by line}\n\\label{app:tech-definitions}\n\nThis is the detail behind Section~\\ref{sec:statement-definitions}. Line\nnumbers are those of\n\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean} at commit\n\\texttt{4eaee875}.\n\n\\paragraph{Formulas and the count (lines 13--51).}\nA \\texttt{Literal V} is a variable with a sign (lines 19--22); a\n\\texttt{Clause V} is a \\texttt{Finset} of literals and a \\texttt{CNF V} a\n\\texttt{Finset} of clauses (lines 24--25). An assignment is any function\n\\(V \\to \\mathtt{Bool}\\) (line 17). A literal evaluates to the Boolean\n``the assignment agrees with its sign'' (lines 29--30), a clause to ``some\nliteral is true'' (lines 32--33), a formula to ``every clause is true''\n(lines 35--36). \\texttt{WidthAtMost k F} says every clause has at most\n\\(k\\) literals as a set (line 38). Over a finite variable type,\n\\texttt{satisfyingAssignments F} filters the universe of all assignments\n(lines 44--45), and \\texttt{count F} is its cardinality (line 47): the\nnumber of satisfying assignments among all \\(2^n\\) when\n\\(V = \\mathtt{Fin}\\ n\\). The \\texttt{Finset} choices change nothing in the\nmeaning: a repeated clause or literal collapses, the empty clause is\nallowed and unsatisfiable, a tautological clause \\(\\{x, \\neg x\\}\\) is\nallowed and counts two toward the width. That is consistent with the\nmanuscript's ``Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than \\(k\\)\nliterals'' (p.~1, line~44).\n\n\\paragraph{The input encoding (lines 53--81).}\n\\texttt{encodeNat n} is \\(n\\) ones followed by a zero (line 55): unary.\n\\texttt{frame} doubles a bit string, prefixing each bit with a one, and\nends it with a zero (lines 57--58). \\texttt{encodeList enc} prefixes each\nelement's frame with a one and ends the list with a zero (lines 60--62).\nA literal becomes the number \\(2\\cdot\\mathrm{var} + [\\text{positive}]\\),\nat most \\(2n - 1\\) (lines 64--65); a clause is its sorted list of literal\nnumbers, a formula its sorted list of clauses (lines 67--71).\n\\texttt{encodeCNF F} is \\texttt{encodeNat n} followed by the formula's\nlist of lists of unary numbers (lines 73--77), and\n\\texttt{formulaBitLength F} is the length of that bit string (line 79).\nTwo things follow. The number of variables \\(n\\) is part of the input,\nso a variable that occurs in no clause is still counted. And the length\nis polynomial: a clause of \\(j \\le k\\) literals costs \\(2j(4n+1)+4\\) bits,\nso\n\\[\n  L \\;=\\; \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F \\;\\le\\; n + 2 + m\\,\\bigl(2k(4n+1) + 4\\bigr)\n  \\;=\\; O(k\\,n\\,m)\n\\]\nfor \\(m\\) clauses. The solution itself proves\n\\((L+1)^d \\le (8k+7)^d\\,(n + |F| + 1)^{2d}\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Statement.lean}, lines 9--14). Emulating the\ndefinitions (\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}),\na formula with \\(n = 125\\), \\(m = 500\\), \\(k = 4\\) encodes to\n\\(L = 1{,}006{,}223\\) bits and one with \\(n = 1{,}000\\), \\(m = 4{,}000\\),\n\\(k = 3\\) to \\(47.9\\) million, both under the bound. Nothing in the\nencoding is exponential; the unary numbers are linear in \\(n\\).\n\n\\paragraph{The machine (lines 83--128).}\n\\texttt{PrimitiveMachine} (lines 92--99) is a finite object: a number\n\\texttt{tapeCount} of tapes and a number \\texttt{stateCount} of states,\nan input tape and a start state, a table \\texttt{scan} saying which tape\neach state reads, and a table \\texttt{transition} from\n\\(\\mathtt{Fin}\\ \\mathtt{stateCount} \\times \\mathtt{Option\\ Bool}\\)---a\ndomain of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) points---into either a halting\nanswer (a \\texttt{Bool}) or a next state with one action. The action type\nis Mathlib's \\texttt{Turing.TM0.Stmt (Option Bool)}: move the head left or\nright, or write one of the three symbols \\texttt{none}, \\texttt{some\nfalse}, \\texttt{some true}, five values in all\n(\\path{Mathlib/Computability/TuringMachine/PostTuringMachine.lean}\nlines 118--120 and \\path{Tape.lean} lines 421--423 at Mathlib\n\\texttt{d13f23b7}, the clone's pinned revision). A configuration\n\\texttt{Cfg} (lines 103--105) is a state, halted or not, and one Mathlib\n\\texttt{Tape} per tape index (a head symbol and two blank-padded halves,\n\\path{Tape.lean} lines 408--414, blank \\(=\\) \\texttt{none}).\n\\texttt{step} (lines 107--119) returns \\texttt{none} in a halted state;\notherwise it reads the head of the one tape the state scans, looks the\npair up in the table, and either halts with the answer, or writes one\nsymbol or moves that one head by one cell and changes state. \\texttt{init}\n(lines 121--124) puts the input on the input tape with the head at its\nfirst bit and every other tape blank (\\texttt{Tape.mk}$_1$,\n\\path{Tape.lean} line 494). A Lean function on a finite domain is,\nwhatever term defines it, a finite table; so a step is one lookup in a\ntable of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) rows, and no field of the\nstructure can carry unbounded computation.\n\n\\paragraph{Traces (lines 87--90).}\n\\texttt{Trace step t a c} is an inductive proposition with two\nconstructors: \\texttt{refl}, a trace of length 0 from \\(a\\) to \\(a\\)\nitself, and \\texttt{cons}, which extends a trace from \\(b\\) by one\nsuccessful step \\(a \\mapsto b\\). So it holds exactly when \\(c\\) is\nreached from \\(a\\) by \\(t\\) successful applications of \\texttt{step}, and\na trace of length 0 to a different configuration is impossible. Since\n\\texttt{step} is a function, the run is deterministic.\n\n\\paragraph{Space monitoring (lines 147--189).}\n\\texttt{LocatedCfg} (lines 149--153) pairs a configuration with, per tape,\nthe head's current position and the least and greatest positions seen so\nfar, as integers; \\texttt{locatedInit} starts every head at 0 with the\ninput tape's range covering the input (lines 155--159);\n\\texttt{headDisplacement} is \\(-1\\), \\(+1\\) or \\(0\\) for a move left, a\nmove right or a write (lines 161--164); \\texttt{monitor} (lines 166--182)\napplies the same table lookup to update the position and the running\nminimum and maximum of the scanned tape, and nothing else;\n\\texttt{locatedStep} is \\texttt{step} on the configuration followed by\n\\texttt{monitor} (lines 184--186). \\texttt{visitedSpace} (lines 188--189)\nis the sum over tapes of \\(\\mathrm{upper} - \\mathrm{lower} + 1\\): the\nnumber of cells whose head position was ever visited. \\texttt{countWord a}\nis the binary expansion of \\(a\\), least significant bit first, with\n\\texttt{[false]} for \\(0\\) (line 147), and \\texttt{toNat} (lines 7--9)\ndecodes such a word.\n\n\\paragraph{The exponent and the bound (lines 130--141).}\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n\\texttt{runtimeScale n = n * (Real.log (Real.log (n + 16)))\\^{}2 /}\\\\\n\\texttt{\\quad Real.sqrt (Real.log (n + 16))}\n\\end{quote}\n(lines 132--134, transcribed; the casts to \\(\\mathbb{R}\\) omitted), that is\n\\[\n  \\rhon \\;=\\; \\frac{n\\,\\bigl(\\ln\\ln(n+16)\\bigr)^2}{\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}},\n\\]\nwith \\texttt{Real.log} the natural logarithm. The manuscript fixes the\nsame base: ``Throughout, log denotes the natural logarithm'' (p.~1, lines\n48--49; again at p.~7, lines 326--327). The shift by 16 keeps\n\\(\\ln\\ln\\) defined and positive at every \\(n \\ge 0\\) (\\(\\ln 16 = 2.77\\)),\nso the theorem can be stated for all \\(n\\) with no proviso. Then\n\\begin{quote}\n\\texttt{runtimeBound C A d L n = C * (L + 1)\\^{}d * 2\\^{}(A * runtimeScale n)}\n\\end{quote}\n(lines 136--139, transcribed), a real number, with the real-exponent\npower.\n\n\\subsection{The theorem, with its quantifiers}\n\\label{app:tech-theorem}\n\nThis is the detail behind Section~\\ref{sec:statement-theorem}. Lines\n194--217 of the comparator file state, for every \\(k\\) with \\(2 \\le k\\):\n\\[\n\\small\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\exists\\ \\mathtt{machine},\\ \\mathtt{countTape},\\ \\mathtt{answerTape},\\\n  C, A, S \\in \\mathbb{R},\\ d, e \\in \\mathbb{N},\\\\[2pt]\n\\quad 0 < C \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < A \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < S \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < d \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < e\\ \\wedge\\\\[2pt]\n\\quad \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ \\forall\\, F : \\mathtt{CNF}\\ (\\mathtt{Fin}\\ n),\\\n  \\mathtt{WidthAtMost}\\ k\\ F \\;\\rightarrow\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad \\exists\\ \\mathtt{time} \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ \\mathtt{final} : \\mathtt{LocatedCfg}\\ \\mathtt{machine},\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\mathtt{Trace}\\ (\\mathtt{locatedStep}\\ \\mathtt{machine})\\ \\mathtt{time}\\\n  (\\mathtt{locatedInit}\\ \\mathtt{machine}\\ (\\mathtt{encodeCNF}\\ F))\\ \\mathtt{final}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\text{(lines 201--202)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the count tape holds \\texttt{countWord (count F)}}\n  \\quad\\text{(203--204)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the answer tape holds the bit \\([0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]\\)}\n  \\quad\\text{(205--206)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the halting state carries that bit}\n  \\quad\\text{(207)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\mathtt{toNat}\\ (\\mathtt{countWord}\\ (\\mathtt{count}\\ F)) = \\mathtt{count}\\ F\n  \\quad\\text{(208)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ |\\mathtt{countWord}\\ (\\mathtt{count}\\ F)| \\le n + 1\n  \\quad\\text{(209)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\mathtt{time} \\le C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\,\\rhon},\\ L = \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F\n  \\quad\\text{(210--211)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\forall\\ \\text{prefix of the trace},\\\n  \\mathtt{visitedSpace} \\le S\\,(L+1)^e\n  \\quad\\text{(212--217).}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe machine and the five constants are quantified after \\(k\\) and before\n\\(n\\) and \\(F\\); inside the universal quantifier only \\texttt{time} and\n\\texttt{final} are chosen. This is the standard \\(O_k(1)\\) reading: for\neach \\(k\\) one fixed finite machine and one set of constants serve every\nformula of every size. The table's size is \\(O_k(1)\\) in the same sense,\nwith no explicit function of \\(k\\). There is no lower bound on \\(n\\): the\nmanuscript's Theorem~1.1 and Proposition~6.4 are stated for \\(n \\ge 20\\)\n(p.~2, lines 56--57; p.~43, lines 2474--2475), and the solution's\nauxiliary \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} keeps \\(20 \\le n\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 104--109); the exported\ntheorem does not, and the \\(+16\\) in \\(\\rhon\\) is what makes that\npossible.\n\nThe proof's own constants, read for their values only: at the point where\nthe large-input run is assembled, the exponent constant is set to\n\\(A = 256 + A_t\\) with \\(A_t \\ge 1\\) taken from a leaf contract\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 51;\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean}, lines 792--793), and\n\\(1 \\le C\\), \\(1 \\le A\\) are in the large-input run's own statement\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 12). The witness\nmachine is compiled from a program over 80 one-bit registers and 1,220\ntapes, with \\(\\mathtt{countTape} = 256\\) and \\(\\mathtt{answerTape} = 1216\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 98--101); its state count is\na finite type's cardinality that nothing computes. The solution's\ninternal \\(\\rho\\) (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean}, lines\n10--11) is in base 2 and is related to \\texttt{runtimeScale} by\n\\(\\mathtt{runtimeScale}\\ n \\le 4\\,\\rho_2(n)\\) for \\(n \\ge 20\\)\n(lines 190--191); the base changes the exponent by a constant factor\nthat \\(A\\) absorbs.\n\nThe clause-count bound used in Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and\n\\ref{sec:consequences}: a \\texttt{Finset} of clauses of width at most\n\\(k\\) over \\(n\\) variables has at most \\(\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j} =\nO(n^k)\\) members (\\(2{,}604{,}376\\) at \\(n = 125\\), \\(k = 3\\)), so\n\\(m = O(n^k)\\), \\(L = O(k\\,n\\,m) = O(n^{k+1})\\), and the whole bound is\n\\(n^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\), which is \\(2^{o(n)}\\) in \\(n\\) alone because\n\\(\\rhon/n \\to 0\\) \\leanref{rho-little-o}.\n\n\\subsection{The spine of the proof, with every line}\n\\label{app:tech-spine}\n\nThis is the detail behind Section~\\ref{sec:proof-spine}. Unqualified\npaths are relative to \\path{lean/OAI/Algorithms/SubexponentialSAT/} in\nthe clone.\n\n\\paragraph{The machine.} The theorem's machine is\n\\texttt{GuardedCompiler.primitive program} with the count tape\n\\texttt{256} and the answer tape \\texttt{1216} (both \\texttt{: Fin 1220};\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~100), where \\texttt{program}\nis \\texttt{Native.countingProgram cutoff k} \\texttt{(NativeLeafProgram.command k)}\n(line~40), a \\texttt{BitProgram.Program 80 1220 labels} (line~11): a\nfirst-order program over 80 one-bit registers, 1,220 Boolean stacks and\na finite label set, with twelve instruction kinds\n(\\clonefile{BitProgram.lean}, lines 10--26). \\texttt{primitive}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~131) is a\n\\texttt{noncomputable abbrev}: it takes the transition table\n\\texttt{transition p} (lines 30--47, one case per instruction) and the\nstack selector \\texttt{selected p} (lines 14--17), feeds them to the\nstack compiler's controller, and renames the finite state type to\n\\texttt{Fin stateCount} through \\texttt{Fintype.equivFin} (line~132).\nThe machine is thus a concrete table defined by pattern matching on the\nprogram, composed with a classical renaming of states; the theorem is\nexistential, so the noncomputability costs it nothing.\n\n\\paragraph{The time bound, and the constants.} For \\texttt{cutoff < n},\n\\path{NativeLargeEntry.run} (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean},\nlines 11--33) supplies a trace of length at most\n\\texttt{runtimeBound C A d L n} (line~27) with $1 \\le C \\wedge 1 \\le A$\n(line~12). Its exponent constant is \\texttt{256 + At} (line~51), where\n\\texttt{At} comes from the leaf program's uniform contract\n(\\path{NativeLeafContract.uniform_contract}, line~39; the contract\ngives $1 \\le \\mathtt{At}$, \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean},\nline~793), and its cutoff is \\texttt{max 20 (max Nleaf Ns)} (line~50),\nwith \\texttt{Ns} obtained non-constructively from an eventual inequality\n(\\path{Filter.eventually_atTop}, line~48). The $2^{A\\cdot\\mathtt{runtimeScale}\\,n}$\nfactor is assembled by \\path{runtime_of_tree_and_leaf} (line~46)\nfrom the DFS visit bound of \\path{Native.outer_from_setup}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean}, lines 53--56: visits at most\na square polynomial in $n$ times $2^{n/\\mathtt{sparsifySchedule}\\,n}$)\nand the leaf time. For $n \\le \\mathtt{cutoff}$,\n\\path{Native.entry_small_primitive}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeEntry.lean}, lines 81--100) gives\n$\\mathtt{time} \\le 28700\\,(L+1)^2$ (line~92) through the finite scanner\nof \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/FiniteFallback.lean}. The two branches are\nfolded by \\texttt{runtime\\_with\\_fallback} and\n\\texttt{space\\_with\\_fallback}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuterBudgets.lean}, lines 297 and 317)\ninto one triple, and the witnesses the final theorem exhibits are\n\\texttt{C + 28700, A + 1, S + 5022741, d + 2, e + 2}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~41). So the proof as written\nhas an exponent constant of at least $258$ in front of $\\rhon$, a\npolynomial degree \\texttt{d} that depends on $k$ (line~51: $\\mathtt{db} + (4(k-2)+2) + \\mathtt{df} + 2$) and a leading constant of at least\n$28{,}701$; none of these is in the statement, which asserts only that\nthey are positive, and none of \\texttt{At}, \\texttt{db}, \\texttt{df} is\ncomputed anywhere.\n\n\\paragraph{Where a hidden cost model would live, and why it cannot.}\nThe compile lemmas conclude in\nthe comparator's own vocabulary: \\path{GuardedCompiler.primitive_execution}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, lines 158--162) turns a\nsource run of \\texttt{n} steps into \\texttt{RunsIn (primitive p).step\n(7 * n)}, with the overhead \\texttt{7} proved as a theorem\n(\\texttt{compiler\\_overhead}, lines 152--153), and\n\\path{CompileSpace.compile_word_answer_space}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/CompileSpace.lean}, lines 125--143) produces a\n\\texttt{Computation.Trace (locatedStep (primitive p)) time \\ldots} with\n$\\mathtt{time} \\le 7\\cdot\\mathtt{sourceSteps}$ and\n$\\mathtt{visitedSpace}\\ \\mathtt{intermediate} \\le s\\,(\\mathtt{bound} + \\mathtt{input.length} + 15)$ on\nevery prefix (lines 134, 135 and 143); \\path{primitive_located_step}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~600) is the per-step\nform. \\texttt{Trace}, \\texttt{locatedStep} and \\texttt{visitedSpace} are\nthe challenge's definitions (its lines 87--90 and 149--189), and\n\\texttt{time} in the final theorem is literally the length of a\n\\texttt{Computation.Trace} of \\texttt{PrimitiveMachine.step} (its\nline~201). No cost function of the compiler's appears in the statement;\na wrong cost model would make these lemmas unprovable, not the theorem\nfalse.\n\n\\paragraph{Correctness of the count.} \\path{Native.outer_from_setup}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean}, lines 50--51) yields\n\\texttt{final.stacks ABI.countStack = countWord (SAT.count F)} and\n\\texttt{final.stacks ABI.answerStack = [decide (0 < SAT.count F)]} from\nthe canonical DFS execution, and\n\\path{Native.counting_primitive_from_entry} (line~73) transports\nboth to the primitive machine's tapes; \\texttt{countWord\\_toNat} and\n\\texttt{countWord\\_count\\_length} supply the decoding and length clauses\nof the statement.\n\n\\paragraph{The statement match, counted.} The solution file does not\nimport the challenge file: \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} begins\n(lines 1--3) with \\texttt{NativeLargeEntry}, \\texttt{SqrtLogSpace.Statement}\nand \\texttt{SqrtLog.StatementCanonical}. The library re-declares every\ndefinition the statement uses under the same fully qualified names, in\nseven files: the SAT vocabulary in \\clonefile{SAT.lean}, the bit\narithmetic in \\clonefile{BinaryArithmetic.lean}, the nine encoding\nfunctions in \\clonefile{Encoding.lean}, the machine in\n\\clonefile{Computation.lean}, \\texttt{runtimeScale} and\n\\texttt{runtimeBound} in \\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean}, the\nspace instrumentation in \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/VisitedTape.lean} and\n\\texttt{countWord} in \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/EncodingBounds.lean}\n(line~17, the challenge's line~147). The definition diff compares 35\ndeclarations: 33 are identical character for character after whitespace\nnormalisation, and the remaining two, \\texttt{runtimeScale} and\n\\texttt{runtimeBound}, differ only in that the challenge writes\n\\texttt{noncomputable def} where the library opens a\n\\texttt{noncomputable section} (\\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean},\nline~15). The theorem statement itself, from \\texttt{theorem\nexact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} to \\texttt{:= by}, is identical in the two files,\n1,353 characters raw and 1,124 after normalisation, at line~194 of the\nchallenge and line~74 of \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}; the\nchallenge's proof is the single word \\texttt{sorry} at its line~218.\n\n\\subsection{The keyword sweep, item by item}\n\\label{app:tech-keywords}\n\nThis is the detail behind Section~\\ref{sec:proof-keywords}. The only\n\\texttt{set\\_option}s in the closure are two\n\\texttt{set\\_option quotPrecheck false}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryQuery.lean}, line~15, and\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryWitness.lean}, line~14),\nwhich switch off a pre-check on local notation and have no bearing on\nsoundness; there is no \\texttt{maxHeartbeats}, \\texttt{maxRecDepth} or\n\\texttt{autoImplicit} option anywhere in the closure, and the lakefile\nsets \\texttt{autoImplicit} to \\texttt{false} for the whole package\n(\\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}, line~9). Nine declarations are\n\\texttt{opaque}, and every one carries a body: three recovery functions\n(\\texttt{queryRecovery} at line~59 of\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryQuery.lean}, with its\n\\texttt{:= by} at line~86; \\texttt{rawRecovery} and\n\\texttt{fixedRecovery} at lines 178 and 198 of\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryWitness.lean}) and six\n\\texttt{private opaque} constants of a subtype\n\\texttt{\\{ports // ports = \\ldots\\}}, each with an explicit value\n(line~22 of \\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramQueryCleanup.lean} and five\nothers). An \\texttt{opaque} with a body is a definition whose unfolding\nis hidden from elaboration; the kernel still checks the body against the\ntype. The 1,048 \\texttt{noncomputable} declarations, the eight\n\\texttt{open Classical}, and the four explicit uses each of\n\\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{propext} are the classical\nreasoning the comparator permits. The 148 \\texttt{instance}s and 40\n\\texttt{local instance}s are \\texttt{Fintype}, \\texttt{Decidable} and\nalgebraic instances; an instance cannot be ``inconsistent'' without an\naxiom, and there is none. The 14,034 lines with \\texttt{decide} are\nkernel-evaluated decision procedures, not the native evaluator. Nine\n\\texttt{termination\\_by} and five \\texttt{decreasing\\_by} clauses are the\nrecursions the kernel checks.\n\nThe lakefile's two hooks: a \\texttt{run\\_cmd} block (lines 290--304) that\nevaluates dependency declarations with \\texttt{unsafe env.evalConstCheck}\nand calls \\path{preparePatchedDependencies}, which clones and patches\neleven dependencies, and a \\texttt{post\\_update} hook (line~306 onward)\nthat verifies or applies the patches for the rest. They touch only\n\\texttt{.lake/packages}, and only non-Mathlib packages. The 23\ncompatibility patches under \\clonefile{lean/patches/} are for third-party\npackages (\\texttt{AINTLIB}, \\texttt{PrimeNumberTheoremAnd},\n\\texttt{carleson}, \\texttt{iut}, \\ldots) that this closure does not\nimport. The registry \\clonefile{lean/formalization.yaml} (lines\n1683--1685) points at the same declaration and file as the comparator's\nJSON.\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses in quantifier form}\n\\label{app:tech-hypotheses}\n\nThis is the detail behind Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-hypotheses}.\nThe property used throughout: for each fixed $k \\ge 2$ there is one\ndeterministic machine which, on the canonical encoding of any\n\\texttt{WidthAtMost}~$k$ formula $F$ on $n$ variables (comparator line\n38), halts with \\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape (lines\n203--204, 147), the bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ on its answer tape and\nin its halting state (lines 205--207), within $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$\nsteps (lines 210--211). Since $\\rhon/n \\to 0$ \\leanref{rho-little-o},\nfor every $\\delta > 0$ there is an $n_0(k, \\delta)$ beyond which the\nbound is below $2^{\\delta n}$; below $n_0$ the bound is a constant\ndepending on $k$ and $\\delta$. So the running time is\n$O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every $\\delta > 0$, and\n$2^{\\delta n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ with the polynomial written out.\n\n\\paragraph{ETH.} In quantifiers: there is a $\\delta > 0$ such that no\ndeterministic algorithm decides 3-SAT within $2^{\\delta n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$\nsteps on all inputs. Its negation: for every $\\delta > 0$ there is one.\nThe verified statement at $k = 3$ supplies, for every $\\delta > 0$, the\nsame machine, whose answer bit is the decision, within\n$O_{\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps. This holds on every\ninput of every size, so it refutes the hypothesis under either reading\nof ``$2^{\\Omega(n)}$'' (a lower bound at infinitely many $n$, or at all\nlarge $n$). In the vocabulary of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, where\n$s_k$ is the infimum of the $\\delta$ for which $k$-SAT has an\n$O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm and ETH is $s_3 > 0$: the verified statement\ngives $s_k = 0$ for every $k \\ge 2$, by one algorithm per $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{SETH.} The manuscript's form: for every\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$, there is a fixed $k$ for which no deterministic\nalgorithm solves $k$-SAT in $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ time. The\nnegation: there is an $\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ such that for every fixed\n$k$ there is a deterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$\nalgorithm. The verified statement gives more: for every\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ and every $k \\ge 2$, the machine for $k$ runs\nwithin $C_k(L+1)^{d_k}\\,2^{A_k\\rhon}$ steps, and\n$A_k\\rhon \\le (1-\\varepsilon)n$ for all $n \\ge n_0(k, \\varepsilon)$\nbecause $\\rhon = o(n)$; below $n_0$ the exponential factor is a\nconstant. So each $k$ has a $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$\nalgorithm, and the polynomial's degree depending on $k$ is exactly what\nthe hypothesis's ``for a fixed $k$'' permits. For the record, SETH\nimplies ETH: if 3-SAT had a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm then, by the\nSparsification Lemma of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}, which reduces\n$k$-SAT on $n$ variables to $2^{\\varepsilon n}$ instances with\n$O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ clauses and so, after the clause-splitting\nreduction, to 3-SAT on $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ variables, so would\n$k$-SAT for every fixed $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{Gap-ETH.} The negation of the manuscript's form: for every\n$\\delta, \\gamma > 0$ there is an $O(2^{\\gamma n})$ algorithm that\nseparates a satisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment\nviolates at least a $\\delta$ fraction of the clauses, already at\n$m = O(n)$. Exact decision separates them, since a formula in which\nevery assignment violates a positive fraction of the clauses is\nunsatisfiable; and the verified machine at $k = 3$ decides within\n$n^{O(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon} = O_{\\gamma}(2^{\\gamma n})$ steps for every\n$\\gamma > 0$, at every $m$ (at $m = O(n)$ the polynomial is\n$O(n^{2d})$). The promise is never used: the machine answers correctly\non every input, promised or not, with probability $1$, which refutes\nthe randomised form as well.\n\n\\paragraph{NSETH.} The negation of the manuscript's form: there is an\n$\\varepsilon > 0$ such that every fixed $k$ has a nondeterministic\n$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm for unsatisfiability. The\nverified machine's answer bit is \\texttt{false} exactly when\n$\\mathtt{count}\\ F = 0$ (lines 205--207), so it decides unsatisfiability\ndeterministically within the SETH bound above for every $\\varepsilon$\nand every $k$; and a deterministic machine is a nondeterministic one\nwhose only computation path is the deterministic run, so every\nacceptance convention for nondeterministic time is met.\n\n\\paragraph{\\#ETH.} The negation: for every $\\delta > 0$ there is a\ndeterministic $O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm that outputs the exact count.\nThe verified machine at $k = 3$ halts with \\texttt{countWord (count F)}\non its count tape, a binary word of length at most $n + 1$ that decodes\nto $\\mathtt{count}\\ F$ (lines 203--204, 208--209), within the same bound.\n\n\\paragraph{Shape (S) in detail.} For a theorem ``SETH $\\Rightarrow$ no\n$(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$'', a $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm\nfor $Y$ obtained from the verified statement would need the exponent of\nthe SAT instance to be below $k \\log_2 c$ by a constant factor, and the\nverified statement's exponent is $A\\rho(n')$ in the variable count $n'$\nof the SAT instance with $A$ unknown; no value of $A$ is on record below\n$257$, and no base $c$ is derived from the verified statement for any\n$Y$.\n\n\\subsection{How the two guarantee curves are scaled}\n\\label{app:tech-scaling}\n\nThis is the detail behind Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner-guarantees}.\nBoth guarantees are taken at constant 1 (the polynomial factors, $C$,\n$A$ and $d$ set aside) and both are multiplied by the same factor\n$t_{20}/2^{20}$, where $t_{20} = 1.28 \\times 10^{-4}$~s is CaDiCaL's\nmedian at $n = 20$. The factor is one number for both curves, so their\nratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves, $2^{\\rhon - n}$: below\n1 for $n < 50$, equal to 1 at $n = 50$ up to the rounding of\n$\\rho(50) = 50.13$, and above 1 from there ($2.8 \\times 10^{3}$ at\n$n = 100$ and $4.0 \\times 10^{51}$ at $n = 500$). A reader who prefers\neach curve scaled to the $n = 20$ point on its own would multiply\n$2^{\\rhon}$ by a further $2^{20 - \\rho(20)} = 2^{2.79}$ and see the two\ncurves touch at $n = 20$ and the verified one above the trivial one\nfrom there on; the common factor keeps the crossing where the statement\nputs it (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a). In that scale the\nbrute-force guarantee reaches one second near $n = 33$ and the verified\nguarantee near $n = 35$; at $n = 60$ the measured CaDiCaL median is\n$3 \\times 10^{-4}$~s against $2^n$ at $1.4 \\times 10^{8}$~s and\n$2^{\\rhon}$ at $5.4 \\times 10^{8}$~s, 11.6 and 12.2 decades\nrespectively; at $n = 500$ the decision median is above the budget\n(8 of 20 finished within 60~s), against $10^{140.6}$~s and\n$10^{192.2}$~s. Regenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.\n\n\\par\\endgroup\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016ubcrM2u3RGBfan8DTW7qB","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
