{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgjKFz3UYPGjhs1GMdSUU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PxjxBDNMfFbtkQH2DHfCZd","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec2_statement.tex","content":"% The verified statement, read exactly -- drafted by loop0015 item 02 (2026-11-05),\n% rewritten in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05, owner's rule of 05:30).\n% Every line number of the comparator file is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone\n% (~/dev/untrusted/openai-math, read with cat/sed -n only); every manuscript quote\n% gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt.\n% Every number of this section: python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py\n% (the rho(n)/n table and the crossings) and python3 -I\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py (the encoding lengths).\n% The definition-by-definition reading and the theorem with its quantifiers\n% are in Appendix B (secB_appendix_technical.tex), cited from here.\n\\section{The verified statement, read exactly}\n\\label{sec:statement}\n\n\\subsection{What we are reading, and the words we need}\n\\label{sec:statement-words}\n\nOpenAI's collection \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}\nholds, for the result it calls family 378, one machine-checked theorem.\nIt is named \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}. It is stated in a file of\n220 lines, \\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean},\nat commit \\texttt{4eaee875} of the collection. This section reads that\nfile and says what the theorem asserts and what it does not. Line\nnumbers are those of the file. We quote nothing from memory.\n\nA few words first, each in one sentence.\n\\emph{Lean} is a proof assistant: a program that checks every step of a\nproof and refuses a proof with a gap.\nThe \\emph{kernel} is the small core of Lean that does the final\nchecking; when we say a statement is verified, we mean the kernel\naccepted a proof of it.\nA \\emph{formula} here is a Boolean formula in conjunctive normal form: a\nlist of \\emph{clauses}, each clause an ``or'' of a few variables or\nnegated variables, and the whole formula true when every clause is\ntrue. A \\emph{$k$-CNF} is such a formula whose clauses have at most $k$\nvariables each; 3-SAT is the problem of deciding whether a 3-CNF can be\nmade true.\nA \\emph{satisfying assignment} is a choice of true or false for every\nvariable that makes the formula true; with $n$ variables there are\n$2^n$ assignments to try, and \\emph{trying all $2^n$ assignments} is the\nmethod the title names.\nThe \\emph{comparator} is OpenAI's checking tool. It takes a\n\\emph{challenge file}, which states a theorem and leaves the proof blank,\nand a \\emph{solution}, which proves it, and checks that the two state\nthe same theorem and that the proof uses only three standard axioms.\nThe file we read is the challenge file. It carries the statement with\nthe placeholder \\texttt{sorry} where the proof would go (line 218). The\nsolution proves the same statement in 3,091 files\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). We rebuilt that proof ourselves and the\nkernel reported the three standard axioms and nothing else\n(Section~\\ref{sec:replay}).\n\n\\subsection{The definitions, in words}\n\\label{sec:statement-definitions}\n\nThe theorem speaks about five kinds of object. Each is defined in the\nfile before the theorem. Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-definitions} gives\nevery definition with its line numbers; here is what each one means.\n\n\\paragraph{Formulas and the count (lines 13--51).}\nA formula is a finite set of clauses over $n$ variables, and a clause is\na finite set of signed variables. The formula \\texttt{F} has width at\nmost $k$ when every clause has at most $k$ members (line 38). The number\n\\texttt{count F} is how many of the $2^n$ assignments make the formula\ntrue (line 47). Because clauses and formulas are sets, a repeated clause\ncollapses into one; the empty clause is allowed and can never be\nsatisfied; and a clause holding both a variable and its negation is\nallowed and counts as two members. The manuscript says the same thing in\nits own words: ``Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than\n\\(k\\) literals'' (p.~1, line~44).\n\n\\paragraph{The input encoding (lines 53--81).}\nThe machine does not read a formula; it reads a string of bits. The\nfile fixes how a formula becomes bits. Numbers are written in unary,\nthat is, $n$ is written as $n$ ones and a zero. Each clause becomes a\nsorted list of such numbers, the formula becomes a list of clauses, and\nthe number of variables is written in front. Two things matter. First,\nthe number of variables is part of the input, so a variable used in no\nclause still counts. Second, the string is not long: a formula with\n$m$ clauses of width $k$ on $n$ variables takes at most about\n$8\\,k\\,n\\,m$ bits. In symbols, with $L$ the length of the string,\n\\[\n  L \\;\\le\\; n + 2 + m\\,\\bigl(2k(4n+1) + 4\\bigr) \\;=\\; O(k\\,n\\,m).\n\\]\nA formula with 125 variables, 500 clauses and width 4 becomes\n$1{,}006{,}223$ bits; one with 1,000 variables, 4,000 clauses and width\n3 becomes 47.9 million bits. Both numbers are from a script that\nimitates the definitions,\n\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}. Nothing in the\nencoding grows exponentially.\n\n\\paragraph{The machine (lines 83--128).}\nThe machine is a Turing machine with several tapes: a finite table of\nstates, a finite number of tapes, and a rule that, in each state, reads\none cell of one tape and then either stops with a yes-or-no answer, or\nwrites one symbol, or moves one head by one cell. Every part of it is\nfinite. A step is one lookup in a table with three rows per state. No\npart of the machine can hide an unbounded computation inside a step.\nThe tapes and the moves are Mathlib's standard ones (Mathlib is the\ncommunity library of mathematics for Lean, at the revision the\ncollection pins, \\texttt{d13f23b7}).\n\n\\paragraph{Runs (lines 87--90).}\nA \\emph{trace} of length $t$ is a record that the machine, started in\none configuration, reached another in exactly $t$ successful steps. The\nstep rule is a function, so each run is determined by its input; there\nis no randomness and no guessing.\n\n\\paragraph{Space monitoring (lines 147--189).}\nBeside each configuration the file keeps, for every tape, the furthest\ncells the head has reached to the left and to the right. The\n\\emph{visited space} is the total number of cells ever visited, summed\nover the tapes. The \\emph{count word} is a number written in binary,\nleast significant bit first (line 147).\n\n\\paragraph{The exponent and the bound (lines 130--141).}\nThe theorem's running-time bound is built from one function of $n$,\nwhich the file calls \\texttt{runtimeScale} and we write $\\rhon$:\n\\[\n  \\rhon \\;=\\; \\frac{n\\,\\bigl(\\ln\\ln(n+16)\\bigr)^2}{\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}},\n\\]\nwith $\\ln$ the natural logarithm. In words: take $n$; multiply it by\nthe square of the logarithm of the logarithm of $n + 16$; divide by the\nsquare root of the logarithm of $n + 16$. The manuscript uses the same\nlogarithm: ``Throughout, log denotes the natural logarithm'' (p.~1,\nlines 48--49; again at p.~7, lines 326--327). The $+16$ keeps the double\nlogarithm defined and positive at every $n$, including $n = 0$, so the\ntheorem needs no condition on $n$. The bound itself, called\n\\texttt{runtimeBound} (lines 136--139), is\n\\[\n  C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\,\\rhon},\n\\]\nthat is, a constant $C$, times a power $d$ of the input length $L$,\ntimes two raised to $A$ times $\\rhon$. We call $\\rhon$ \\emph{the\nexponent} and $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ \\emph{the bound} throughout the\npaper. The constants $C$, $A$ and $d$ are the subject of the next\nsubsection.\n\n\\subsection{What the theorem says, and in what order}\n\\label{sec:statement-theorem}\n\nLines 194--217 state the theorem. Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-theorem}\ndisplays it with every quantifier and the line of every clause. In\nwords, for every fixed width $k$ of at least 2:\n\n\\begin{quote}\nThere exist a machine, a count tape, an answer tape, and five positive\nconstants $C, A, S, d, e$, such that for every number of variables $n$\nand every formula $F$ on $n$ variables of width at most $k$, the\nmachine started on the encoding of $F$ halts after some number of steps,\n\\texttt{time}, with the count of satisfying assignments written in\nbinary on the count tape, the bit ``is the count positive'' on the\nanswer tape and in its halting state, a count word of at most $n + 1$\nbits, \\texttt{time} at most $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, and never more\nthan $S\\,(L+1)^e$ cells visited.\n\\end{quote}\n\nThree features of the order of the words matter for everything that\nfollows.\n\n\\paragraph{The machine and the constants come after $k$ and before $n$.}\nFor each width $k$ there is one machine and one set of constants, and\nthey serve every formula of every size. They may depend on $k$ and on\nnothing else. This is what complexity theorists write as $O_k(1)$: a\nconstant that depends only on $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{There is no lower limit on $n$.}\nThe manuscript's Theorem~1.1 and its Proposition~6.4 are stated for\n$n \\ge 20$ (p.~2, lines 56--57; p.~43, lines 2474--2475). The verified\ntheorem has no such condition; the $+16$ in the exponent is what makes\nthat possible.\n\n\\paragraph{The constants exist, and that is all.}\nThe theorem says that values of $C, A, S, d, e$ exist. It names none\nof them. The proof obtains them from other existence lemmas and never\nwrites a number down. We read the proof for the values it does pin:\nat the point where the main run is assembled, the exponent constant is\nset to $A = 256 + A_t$ with $A_t$ at least $1$\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 51;\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean}, lines 792--793). So\nthe proof as written has $A \\ge 257$, and the theorem that the\ncomparator exhibits adds one more\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}, $A \\ge 258$). No closed value of any of the\nfive constants exists anywhere. The machine is explicit in one respect:\nit is compiled from a program with 80 one-bit registers and 1,220 tapes\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 98--101). How many states it\nhas is never computed.\n\n\\subsection{The exponent, in numbers}\n\\label{sec:statement-numbers}\n\nEverything the title says rests on one comparison: the exponent $\\rhon$\nagainst $n$ itself. If $\\rhon$ is below $n$, the bound's exponential\npart $2^{\\rhon}$ is below $2^n$, the cost of trying every assignment.\nIf $\\rhon$ is above $n$, it is not.\n\nThe ratio $\\rhon / n$ depends only on $y = \\ln(n+16)$: it equals\n$(\\ln y)^2 / \\sqrt{y}$. That ratio starts at $0.64$ for $n = 1$ and\nrises. It crosses $1$ between $n = 49$ and $n = 50$: $\\rho(49)/49 =\n0.9994$ and $\\rho(50)/50 = 1.0027$. It keeps rising to a peak of\n$16/e^2 = 2.165$, reached when $\\ln y = 4$, which is at $n \\approx\n10^{23.7}$, a number with 24 digits. Then it falls, and it is back below\n$1$ only when $y = 5503.7$, which is at $n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx\n10^{2390}$, a number with 2,391 digits.\nTable~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale} gives the values and\nFigures~\\ref{fig:central} and \\ref{fig:runtime-scale} draw them. Every\nnumber is printed by \\repofile{paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py}, run\nunder \\texttt{python3 -I}; it imports nothing from the clone and works\nin $y$, so that no $n$ beyond the range of floating-point arithmetic is\never formed.\n\nTwo facts are true at once, and the title is about the second. In the\nlimit, $\\rhon / n$ goes to $0$: the exponent grows more slowly than\n$n$, which is what ``subexponential'' means, and that is a theorem in\nour companion Lean \\leanref{rho-little-o}. And on every size anyone\nwill ever write down, from $50$ to a number with 2,390 digits, the\nexponent is above $n$.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The verified exponent compared with $n$. The second column is\nthe ratio $\\rhon/n$; where it is above $1$, the bound's exponential\npart $2^{\\rhon}$ (with $A = 1$) is above $2^n$, the cost of trying\nevery assignment. The third column is $\\rhon$ itself. $\\rhon =\nn(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$, the comparator's\n\\texttt{runtimeScale} (lines 132--134), natural logarithms. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}\n\\label{tab:runtime-scale}\n\\input{tables/runtime_scale}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n.pdf}\\\\[4pt]\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n_practical.pdf}\n\\caption{The verified exponent against trying every assignment. Each\ncurve is an exponent divided by $n$; the dashed line at $1$ is $2^n$,\nthe cost of trying every assignment, and a curve above the line is a\nguarantee worse than that. Top: the whole range, the horizontal axis\nbeing the number of digits of $n$, from $n = 10$ to $n = 10^{3000}$.\nThe solid curve is the verified exponent $\\rhon/n$; the two curves\nbeneath it are the manuscript's two sharper exponents,\n$n\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (Theorem~1.1, general) and $n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$\n(fixed clause density), which are not in the Lean library\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}). Bottom: the same three curves over the\npractical range $n = 10$ to $10^6$, with $\\rhon/n$ marked at $n = 50,\n125, 10^3, 10^4, 10^6$. All curves take the constant $A$ to be $1$; the\nverified $A$ is unknown and at least 257 in the proof on record.\nRegenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/rho\\_over\\_n.py}.}\n\\label{fig:central}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{runtime_scale.pdf}\n\\caption{The ratio $\\rhon/n$ alone, over an enormous range: the\nhorizontal axis is the number of digits of $n$, from $n = 10$ to\n$n = 10^{2500}$. The level $1$ is the cost of trying every assignment.\nMarked: the first whole number above the level ($n = 50$); the peak\n($16/e^2 = 2.17$, at $n \\approx 10^{23.7}$); the return below the level\n($n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx 10^{2390}$); and, shaded, the practical\nrange $50 \\le n \\le 10^6$. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}\n\\label{fig:runtime-scale}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{The title's claim, made precise}\n\\label{sec:statement-title}\n\nThe title says the verified algorithm is slower than trying all $2^n$\nassignments at every practical size. Read against the theorem, that\nmeans the following four things, and nothing more.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{What is verified is a guarantee, not a running time.} The\n  theorem says the machine halts within $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps.\n  It does not say how many steps the machine actually takes on any\n  input. No implementation of the machine exists to be timed. A bound\n  above $2^n$ does not say the machine is slow; it says the proof\n  promises nothing better than $2^n$ there.\n\\item \\textbf{The constants are never computed.} The theorem asserts\n  that $C, A, S, d, e$ exist. The proof builds them from other\n  existence lemmas and states no value, beyond the $A \\ge 257$ read\n  above. The machine's number of states is likewise never computed.\n\\item \\textbf{With $A = 1$, the verified exponent is above $n$ for every\n  $n$ from 50 up to about $10^{2390}$.} That is\n  Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}. Over that range the exponential part\n  of the bound is at least $2^n$, and at its worst it is $2^{2.17\\,n}$.\n  With the true $A$, whatever it is, the exponential part is $2^{A\\rhon}$,\n  which is at least $2^{\\rhon}$ as soon as $A \\ge 1$; and the proof's\n  own construction has $A \\ge 257$. So nothing on record puts the bound\n  below $2^n$ at any practical size.\n\\item \\textbf{Hence, at every practical size, the verified guarantee is\n  weaker than the guarantee of trying all assignments.} Trying all\n  $2^n$ assignments and checking each against the $m$ clauses counts\n  the satisfying ones in $2^n$ times a small polynomial in $n + m$\n  steps, with constants one can write down. The verified guarantee is\n  $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ with $\\rhon > n$ on $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$ and\n  $C, A, d$ unknown. For every $C \\ge 1$, $A \\ge 1$ and $d \\ge 1$ the\n  verified bound is above $2^n$ on every $n$ in that range. The only\n  values on record are the proof's own, which has $1 \\le C$ and\n  $1 \\le A$ in the statement of its main run\n  (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 12) and\n  $A \\ge 257$ in its construction. A proof with $A < 1$ would be a\n  different proof; none is on record.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nNone of this is a defect of the formalisation or of the proof. It is\nwhat a theorem of this shape means when the constants in it are not\ntracked. The manuscript itself calls $\\rhon$ ``the weaker exponent''\n(p.~2, line~63), and its sharper exponents are not in the Lean library\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}). One detail of bookkeeping: inside the\nsolution the exponent is written with base-2 logarithms, and a lemma\nrelates the two forms by a constant factor that $A$ absorbs\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-theorem}); nothing above depends on it.\n\n\\subsection{What the statement does establish}\n\\label{sec:statement-establishes}\n\nFor each fixed width $k \\ge 2$, there is a deterministic machine that,\ngiven any $k$-CNF on $n$ variables with $m$ clauses in the fixed\nencoding, halts with the exact number of satisfying assignments written\nin binary on a named tape, within $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps. Here\n$L$ grows no faster than a polynomial in $n + m$, and $\\rhon$ grows more\nslowly than $n$. A formula of width $k$ on $n$ variables cannot have\nmore than about $n^k$ distinct clauses ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$,\n$k = 3$; Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-theorem}), so the whole bound is\nsubexponential in $n$ alone. In the standard vocabulary: a deterministic\n$2^{o(n)} \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ algorithm for counting the solutions\nof $k$-SAT, and therefore for deciding it. That refutes the Exponential\nTime Hypothesis \\citep{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, the conjecture that\n3-SAT needs time exponential in $n$, as stated. The verified theorem is\nthe manuscript's own verified form, the weaker exponent of its\nProposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476), without the $n \\ge 20$\ncondition. We found no field, definition or quantifier through which\nthe statement could be accepted by the kernel and yet mean something\nweaker than this; the audit behind that sentence is\n\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/statement_audit.md}. Our own\nrestatement of the theorem as a named Lean sentence, over a verbatim\ncopy of the machine model, is \\leanref{subexpcount}; it is never proved\nand never assumed there (Section~\\ref{sec:lean}).\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:statement-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No running time of any machine.} Nothing here measures or\n  estimates how long the verified machine, or any program, takes on any\n  input. The title compares two \\emph{guarantees}.\n  Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner} is the only place in the paper where\n  anything is timed, and it times ordinary solvers, not this machine.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement about the proof.} That the statement means\n  what we say it means is a reading of 220 lines. Whether the proof is\n  sound is Section~\\ref{sec:proof} and Section~\\ref{sec:replay}, which\n  found no flaw. The one proof-side fact quoted here, $A = 256 + A_t$,\n  is a line of the solution read for the value it assigns, not a claim\n  about the argument.\n\\item \\textbf{No value of the constants.} $A \\ge 257$ is what the proof\n  writes down, not what the machine needs; a different proof of the\n  same statement could have any $A > 0$. The statement itself fixes\n  nothing.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the manuscript's sharper exponents}, which\n  are not in the Lean library and are Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}'s\n  subject.\n\\item \\textbf{No claim about sizes beyond $e^{5504}$.} Past\n  $n \\approx 10^{2390}$ the verified exponent is below $n$ again at\n  $A = 1$; we say nothing about that range except that it is not a\n  practical one.\n\\end{itemize}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14281,"cache_read_input_tokens":182203,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":14281,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"94c621b7-bb9e-4b1f-b2c7-cb793e1ef3cd","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:35:44.153Z","request_id":"req_011CgjKFyHaURuJxg4qMJ3uQ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01PxjxBDNMfFbtkQH2DHfCZd":{"file_path":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec2_statement.tex","content":"% The verified statement, read exactly -- drafted by loop0015 item 02 (2026-11-05),\n% rewritten in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05, owner's rule of 05:30).\n% Every line number of the comparator file is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone\n% (~/dev/untrusted/openai-math, read with cat/sed -n only); every manuscript quote\n% gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt.\n% Every number of this section: python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py\n% (the rho(n)/n table and the crossings) and python3 -I\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py (the encoding lengths).\n% The definition-by-definition reading and the theorem with its quantifiers\n% are in Appendix B (secB_appendix_technical.tex), cited from here.\n\\section{The verified statement, read exactly}\n\\label{sec:statement}\n\n\\subsection{What we are reading, and the words we need}\n\\label{sec:statement-words}\n\nOpenAI's collection \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}\nholds, for the result it calls family 378, one machine-checked theorem.\nIt is named \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}. It is stated in a file of\n220 lines, \\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean},\nat commit \\texttt{4eaee875} of the collection. This section reads that\nfile and says what the theorem asserts and what it does not. Line\nnumbers are those of the file. We quote nothing from memory.\n\nA few words first, each in one sentence.\n\\emph{Lean} is a proof assistant: a program that checks every step of a\nproof and refuses a proof with a gap.\nThe \\emph{kernel} is the small core of Lean that does the final\nchecking; when we say a statement is verified, we mean the kernel\naccepted a proof of it.\nA \\emph{formula} here is a Boolean formula in conjunctive normal form: a\nlist of \\emph{clauses}, each clause an ``or'' of a few variables or\nnegated variables, and the whole formula true when every clause is\ntrue. A \\emph{$k$-CNF} is such a formula whose clauses have at most $k$\nvariables each; 3-SAT is the problem of deciding whether a 3-CNF can be\nmade true.\nA \\emph{satisfying assignment} is a choice of true or false for every\nvariable that makes the formula true; with $n$ variables there are\n$2^n$ assignments to try, and \\emph{trying all $2^n$ assignments} is the\nmethod the title names.\nThe \\emph{comparator} is OpenAI's checking tool. It takes a\n\\emph{challenge file}, which states a theorem and leaves the proof blank,\nand a \\emph{solution}, which proves it, and checks that the two state\nthe same theorem and that the proof uses only three standard axioms.\nThe file we read is the challenge file. It carries the statement with\nthe placeholder \\texttt{sorry} where the proof would go (line 218). The\nsolution proves the same statement in 3,091 files\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). We rebuilt that proof ourselves and the\nkernel reported the three standard axioms and nothing else\n(Section~\\ref{sec:replay}).\n\n\\subsection{The definitions, in words}\n\\label{sec:statement-definitions}\n\nThe theorem speaks about five kinds of object. Each is defined in the\nfile before the theorem. Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-definitions} gives\nevery definition with its line numbers; here is what each one means.\n\n\\paragraph{Formulas and the count (lines 13--51).}\nA formula is a finite set of clauses over $n$ variables, and a clause is\na finite set of signed variables. The formula \\texttt{F} has width at\nmost $k$ when every clause has at most $k$ members (line 38). The number\n\\texttt{count F} is how many of the $2^n$ assignments make the formula\ntrue (line 47). Because clauses and formulas are sets, a repeated clause\ncollapses into one; the empty clause is allowed and can never be\nsatisfied; and a clause holding both a variable and its negation is\nallowed and counts as two members. The manuscript says the same thing in\nits own words: ``Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than\n\\(k\\) literals'' (p.~1, line~44).\n\n\\paragraph{The input encoding (lines 53--81).}\nThe machine does not read a formula; it reads a string of bits. The\nfile fixes how a formula becomes bits. Numbers are written in unary,\nthat is, $n$ is written as $n$ ones and a zero. Each clause becomes a\nsorted list of such numbers, the formula becomes a list of clauses, and\nthe number of variables is written in front. Two things matter. First,\nthe number of variables is part of the input, so a variable used in no\nclause still counts. Second, the string is not long: a formula with\n$m$ clauses of width $k$ on $n$ variables takes at most about\n$8\\,k\\,n\\,m$ bits. In symbols, with $L$ the length of the string,\n\\[\n  L \\;\\le\\; n + 2 + m\\,\\bigl(2k(4n+1) + 4\\bigr) \\;=\\; O(k\\,n\\,m).\n\\]\nA formula with 125 variables, 500 clauses and width 4 becomes\n$1{,}006{,}223$ bits; one with 1,000 variables, 4,000 clauses and width\n3 becomes 47.9 million bits. Both numbers are from a script that\nimitates the definitions,\n\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}. Nothing in the\nencoding grows exponentially.\n\n\\paragraph{The machine (lines 83--128).}\nThe machine is a Turing machine with several tapes: a finite table of\nstates, a finite number of tapes, and a rule that, in each state, reads\none cell of one tape and then either stops with a yes-or-no answer, or\nwrites one symbol, or moves one head by one cell. Every part of it is\nfinite. A step is one lookup in a table with three rows per state. No\npart of the machine can hide an unbounded computation inside a step.\nThe tapes and the moves are Mathlib's standard ones (Mathlib is the\ncommunity library of mathematics for Lean, at the revision the\ncollection pins, \\texttt{d13f23b7}).\n\n\\paragraph{Runs (lines 87--90).}\nA \\emph{trace} of length $t$ is a record that the machine, started in\none configuration, reached another in exactly $t$ successful steps. The\nstep rule is a function, so each run is determined by its input; there\nis no randomness and no guessing.\n\n\\paragraph{Space monitoring (lines 147--189).}\nBeside each configuration the file keeps, for every tape, the furthest\ncells the head has reached to the left and to the right. The\n\\emph{visited space} is the total number of cells ever visited, summed\nover the tapes. The \\emph{count word} is a number written in binary,\nleast significant bit first (line 147).\n\n\\paragraph{The exponent and the bound (lines 130--141).}\nThe theorem's running-time bound is built from one function of $n$,\nwhich the file calls \\texttt{runtimeScale} and we write $\\rhon$:\n\\[\n  \\rhon \\;=\\; \\frac{n\\,\\bigl(\\ln\\ln(n+16)\\bigr)^2}{\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}},\n\\]\nwith $\\ln$ the natural logarithm. In words: take $n$; multiply it by\nthe square of the logarithm of the logarithm of $n + 16$; divide by the\nsquare root of the logarithm of $n + 16$. The manuscript uses the same\nlogarithm: ``Throughout, log denotes the natural logarithm'' (p.~1,\nlines 48--49; again at p.~7, lines 326--327). The $+16$ keeps the double\nlogarithm defined and positive at every $n$, including $n = 0$, so the\ntheorem needs no condition on $n$. The bound itself, called\n\\texttt{runtimeBound} (lines 136--139), is\n\\[\n  C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\,\\rhon},\n\\]\nthat is, a constant $C$, times a power $d$ of the input length $L$,\ntimes two raised to $A$ times $\\rhon$. We call $\\rhon$ \\emph{the\nexponent} and $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ \\emph{the bound} throughout the\npaper. The constants $C$, $A$ and $d$ are the subject of the next\nsubsection.\n\n\\subsection{What the theorem says, and in what order}\n\\label{sec:statement-theorem}\n\nLines 194--217 state the theorem. Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-theorem}\ndisplays it with every quantifier and the line of every clause. In\nwords, for every fixed width $k$ of at least 2:\n\n\\begin{quote}\nThere exist a machine, a count tape, an answer tape, and five positive\nconstants $C, A, S, d, e$, such that for every number of variables $n$\nand every formula $F$ on $n$ variables of width at most $k$, the\nmachine started on the encoding of $F$ halts after some number of steps,\n\\texttt{time}, with the count of satisfying assignments written in\nbinary on the count tape, the bit ``is the count positive'' on the\nanswer tape and in its halting state, a count word of at most $n + 1$\nbits, \\texttt{time} at most $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, and never more\nthan $S\\,(L+1)^e$ cells visited.\n\\end{quote}\n\nThree features of the order of the words matter for everything that\nfollows.\n\n\\paragraph{The machine and the constants come after $k$ and before $n$.}\nFor each width $k$ there is one machine and one set of constants, and\nthey serve every formula of every size. They may depend on $k$ and on\nnothing else. This is what complexity theorists write as $O_k(1)$: a\nconstant that depends only on $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{There is no lower limit on $n$.}\nThe manuscript's Theorem~1.1 and its Proposition~6.4 are stated for\n$n \\ge 20$ (p.~2, lines 56--57; p.~43, lines 2474--2475). The verified\ntheorem has no such condition; the $+16$ in the exponent is what makes\nthat possible.\n\n\\paragraph{The constants exist, and that is all.}\nThe theorem says that values of $C, A, S, d, e$ exist. It names none\nof them. The proof obtains them from other existence lemmas and never\nwrites a number down. We read the proof for the values it does pin:\nat the point where the main run is assembled, the exponent constant is\nset to $A = 256 + A_t$ with $A_t$ at least $1$\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 51;\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean}, lines 792--793). So\nthe proof as written has $A \\ge 257$, and the theorem that the\ncomparator exhibits adds one more\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}, $A \\ge 258$). No closed value of any of the\nfive constants exists anywhere. The machine is explicit in one respect:\nit is compiled from a program with 80 one-bit registers and 1,220 tapes\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 98--101). How many states it\nhas is never computed.\n\n\\subsection{The exponent, in numbers}\n\\label{sec:statement-numbers}\n\nEverything the title says rests on one comparison: the exponent $\\rhon$\nagainst $n$ itself. If $\\rhon$ is below $n$, the bound's exponential\npart $2^{\\rhon}$ is below $2^n$, the cost of trying every assignment.\nIf $\\rhon$ is above $n$, it is not.\n\nThe ratio $\\rhon / n$ depends only on $y = \\ln(n+16)$: it equals\n$(\\ln y)^2 / \\sqrt{y}$. That ratio starts at $0.64$ for $n = 1$ and\nrises. It crosses $1$ between $n = 49$ and $n = 50$: $\\rho(49)/49 =\n0.9994$ and $\\rho(50)/50 = 1.0027$. It keeps rising to a peak of\n$16/e^2 = 2.165$, reached when $\\ln y = 4$, which is at $n \\approx\n10^{23.7}$, a number with 24 digits. Then it falls, and it is back below\n$1$ only when $y = 5503.7$, which is at $n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx\n10^{2390}$, a number with 2,391 digits.\nTable~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale} gives the values and\nFigures~\\ref{fig:central} and \\ref{fig:runtime-scale} draw them. Every\nnumber is printed by \\repofile{paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py}, run\nunder \\texttt{python3 -I}; it imports nothing from the clone and works\nin $y$, so that no $n$ beyond the range of floating-point arithmetic is\never formed.\n\nTwo facts are true at once, and the title is about the second. In the\nlimit, $\\rhon / n$ goes to $0$: the exponent grows more slowly than\n$n$, which is what ``subexponential'' means, and that is a theorem in\nour companion Lean \\leanref{rho-little-o}. And on every size anyone\nwill ever write down, from $50$ to a number with 2,390 digits, the\nexponent is above $n$.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The verified exponent compared with $n$. The second column is\nthe ratio $\\rhon/n$; where it is above $1$, the bound's exponential\npart $2^{\\rhon}$ (with $A = 1$) is above $2^n$, the cost of trying\nevery assignment. The third column is $\\rhon$ itself. $\\rhon =\nn(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$, the comparator's\n\\texttt{runtimeScale} (lines 132--134), natural logarithms. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}\n\\label{tab:runtime-scale}\n\\input{tables/runtime_scale}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n.pdf}\\\\[4pt]\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n_practical.pdf}\n\\caption{The verified exponent against trying every assignment. Each\ncurve is an exponent divided by $n$; the dashed line at $1$ is $2^n$,\nthe cost of trying every assignment, and a curve above the line is a\nguarantee worse than that. Top: the whole range, the horizontal axis\nbeing the number of digits of $n$, from $n = 10$ to $n = 10^{3000}$.\nThe solid curve is the verified exponent $\\rhon/n$; the two curves\nbeneath it are the manuscript's two sharper exponents,\n$n\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (Theorem~1.1, general) and $n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$\n(fixed clause density), which are not in the Lean library\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}). Bottom: the same three curves over the\npractical range $n = 10$ to $10^6$, with $\\rhon/n$ marked at $n = 50,\n125, 10^3, 10^4, 10^6$. All curves take the constant $A$ to be $1$; the\nverified $A$ is unknown and at least 257 in the proof on record.\nRegenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/rho\\_over\\_n.py}.}\n\\label{fig:central}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{runtime_scale.pdf}\n\\caption{The ratio $\\rhon/n$ alone, over an enormous range: the\nhorizontal axis is the number of digits of $n$, from $n = 10$ to\n$n = 10^{2500}$. The level $1$ is the cost of trying every assignment.\nMarked: the first whole number above the level ($n = 50$); the peak\n($16/e^2 = 2.17$, at $n \\approx 10^{23.7}$); the return below the level\n($n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx 10^{2390}$); and, shaded, the practical\nrange $50 \\le n \\le 10^6$. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}\n\\label{fig:runtime-scale}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{The title's claim, made precise}\n\\label{sec:statement-title}\n\nThe title says the verified algorithm is slower than trying all $2^n$\nassignments at every practical size. Read against the theorem, that\nmeans the following four things, and nothing more.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{What is verified is a guarantee, not a running time.} The\n  theorem says the machine halts within $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps.\n  It does not say how many steps the machine actually takes on any\n  input. No implementation of the machine exists to be timed. A bound\n  above $2^n$ does not say the machine is slow; it says the proof\n  promises nothing better than $2^n$ there.\n\\item \\textbf{The constants are never computed.} The theorem asserts\n  that $C, A, S, d, e$ exist. The proof builds them from other\n  existence lemmas and states no value, beyond the $A \\ge 257$ read\n  above. The machine's number of states is likewise never computed.\n\\item \\textbf{With $A = 1$, the verified exponent is above $n$ for every\n  $n$ from 50 up to about $10^{2390}$.} That is\n  Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}. Over that range the exponential part\n  of the bound is at least $2^n$, and at its worst it is $2^{2.17\\,n}$.\n  With the true $A$, whatever it is, the exponential part is $2^{A\\rhon}$,\n  which is at least $2^{\\rhon}$ as soon as $A \\ge 1$; and the proof's\n  own construction has $A \\ge 257$. So nothing on record puts the bound\n  below $2^n$ at any practical size.\n\\item \\textbf{Hence, at every practical size, the verified guarantee is\n  weaker than the guarantee of trying all assignments.} Trying all\n  $2^n$ assignments and checking each against the $m$ clauses counts\n  the satisfying ones in $2^n$ times a small polynomial in $n + m$\n  steps, with constants one can write down. The verified guarantee is\n  $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ with $\\rhon > n$ on $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$ and\n  $C, A, d$ unknown. For every $C \\ge 1$, $A \\ge 1$ and $d \\ge 1$ the\n  verified bound is above $2^n$ on every $n$ in that range. The only\n  values on record are the proof's own, which has $1 \\le C$ and\n  $1 \\le A$ in the statement of its main run\n  (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 12) and\n  $A \\ge 257$ in its construction. A proof with $A < 1$ would be a\n  different proof; none is on record.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nNone of this is a defect of the formalisation or of the proof. It is\nwhat a theorem of this shape means when the constants in it are not\ntracked. The manuscript itself calls $\\rhon$ ``the weaker exponent''\n(p.~2, line~63), and its sharper exponents are not in the Lean library\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}). One detail of bookkeeping: inside the\nsolution the exponent is written with base-2 logarithms, and a lemma\nrelates the two forms by a constant factor that $A$ absorbs\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-theorem}); nothing above depends on it.\n\n\\subsection{What the statement does establish}\n\\label{sec:statement-establishes}\n\nFor each fixed width $k \\ge 2$, there is a deterministic machine that,\ngiven any $k$-CNF on $n$ variables with $m$ clauses in the fixed\nencoding, halts with the exact number of satisfying assignments written\nin binary on a named tape, within $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps. Here\n$L$ grows no faster than a polynomial in $n + m$, and $\\rhon$ grows more\nslowly than $n$. A formula of width $k$ on $n$ variables cannot have\nmore than about $n^k$ distinct clauses ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$,\n$k = 3$; Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-theorem}), so the whole bound is\nsubexponential in $n$ alone. In the standard vocabulary: a deterministic\n$2^{o(n)} \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ algorithm for counting the solutions\nof $k$-SAT, and therefore for deciding it. That refutes the Exponential\nTime Hypothesis \\citep{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, the conjecture that\n3-SAT needs time exponential in $n$, as stated. The verified theorem is\nthe manuscript's own verified form, the weaker exponent of its\nProposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476), without the $n \\ge 20$\ncondition. We found no field, definition or quantifier through which\nthe statement could be accepted by the kernel and yet mean something\nweaker than this; the audit behind that sentence is\n\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/statement_audit.md}. Our own\nrestatement of the theorem as a named Lean sentence, over a verbatim\ncopy of the machine model, is \\leanref{subexpcount}; it is never proved\nand never assumed there (Section~\\ref{sec:lean}).\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:statement-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No running time of any machine.} Nothing here measures or\n  estimates how long the verified machine, or any program, takes on any\n  input. The title compares two \\emph{guarantees}.\n  Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner} is the only place in the paper where\n  anything is timed, and it times ordinary solvers, not this machine.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement about the proof.} That the statement means\n  what we say it means is a reading of 220 lines. Whether the proof is\n  sound is Section~\\ref{sec:proof} and Section~\\ref{sec:replay}, which\n  found no flaw. The one proof-side fact quoted here, $A = 256 + A_t$,\n  is a line of the solution read for the value it assigns, not a claim\n  about the argument.\n\\item \\textbf{No value of the constants.} $A \\ge 257$ is what the proof\n  writes down, not what the machine needs; a different proof of the\n  same statement could have any $A > 0$. The statement itself fixes\n  nothing.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the manuscript's sharper exponents}, which\n  are not in the Lean library and are Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}'s\n  subject.\n\\item \\textbf{No claim about sizes beyond $e^{5504}$.} Past\n  $n \\approx 10^{2390}$ the verified exponent is below $n$ again at\n  $A = 1$; we say nothing about that range except that it is not a\n  practical one.\n\\end{itemize}\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PxjxBDNMfFbtkQH2DHfCZd","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec2_statement.tex has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"165c5908-ef1e-4f19-a508-15c72f5a7014","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:35:44.177Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec2_statement.tex","content":"% The verified statement, read exactly -- drafted by loop0015 item 02 (2026-11-05),\n% rewritten in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05, owner's rule of 05:30).\n% Every line number of the comparator file is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone\n% (~/dev/untrusted/openai-math, read with cat/sed -n only); every manuscript quote\n% gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt.\n% Every number of this section: python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py\n% (the rho(n)/n table and the crossings) and python3 -I\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py (the encoding lengths).\n% The definition-by-definition reading and the theorem with its quantifiers\n% are in Appendix B (secB_appendix_technical.tex), cited from here.\n\\section{The verified statement, read exactly}\n\\label{sec:statement}\n\n\\subsection{What we are reading, and the words we need}\n\\label{sec:statement-words}\n\nOpenAI's collection \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}\nholds, for the result it calls family 378, one machine-checked theorem.\nIt is named \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}. It is stated in a file of\n220 lines, \\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean},\nat commit \\texttt{4eaee875} of the collection. This section reads that\nfile and says what the theorem asserts and what it does not. Line\nnumbers are those of the file. We quote nothing from memory.\n\nA few words first, each in one sentence.\n\\emph{Lean} is a proof assistant: a program that checks every step of a\nproof and refuses a proof with a gap.\nThe \\emph{kernel} is the small core of Lean that does the final\nchecking; when we say a statement is verified, we mean the kernel\naccepted a proof of it.\nA \\emph{formula} here is a Boolean formula in conjunctive normal form: a\nlist of \\emph{clauses}, each clause an ``or'' of a few variables or\nnegated variables, and the whole formula true when every clause is\ntrue. A \\emph{$k$-CNF} is such a formula whose clauses have at most $k$\nvariables each; 3-SAT is the problem of deciding whether a 3-CNF can be\nmade true.\nA \\emph{satisfying assignment} is a choice of true or false for every\nvariable that makes the formula true; with $n$ variables there are\n$2^n$ assignments to try, and \\emph{trying all $2^n$ assignments} is the\nmethod the title names.\nThe \\emph{comparator} is OpenAI's checking tool. It takes a\n\\emph{challenge file}, which states a theorem and leaves the proof blank,\nand a \\emph{solution}, which proves it, and checks that the two state\nthe same theorem and that the proof uses only three standard axioms.\nThe file we read is the challenge file. It carries the statement with\nthe placeholder \\texttt{sorry} where the proof would go (line 218). The\nsolution proves the same statement in 3,091 files\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). We rebuilt that proof ourselves and the\nkernel reported the three standard axioms and nothing else\n(Section~\\ref{sec:replay}).\n\n\\subsection{The definitions, in words}\n\\label{sec:statement-definitions}\n\nThe theorem speaks about five kinds of object. Each is defined in the\nfile before the theorem. Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-definitions} gives\nevery definition with its line numbers; here is what each one means.\n\n\\paragraph{Formulas and the count (lines 13--51).}\nA formula is a finite set of clauses over $n$ variables, and a clause is\na finite set of signed variables. The formula \\texttt{F} has width at\nmost $k$ when every clause has at most $k$ members (line 38). The number\n\\texttt{count F} is how many of the $2^n$ assignments make the formula\ntrue (line 47). Because clauses and formulas are sets, a repeated clause\ncollapses into one; the empty clause is allowed and can never be\nsatisfied; and a clause holding both a variable and its negation is\nallowed and counts as two members. The manuscript says the same thing in\nits own words: ``Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than\n\\(k\\) literals'' (p.~1, line~44).\n\n\\paragraph{The input encoding (lines 53--81).}\nThe machine does not read a formula; it reads a string of bits. The\nfile fixes how a formula becomes bits. Numbers are written in unary,\nthat is, $n$ is written as $n$ ones and a zero. Each clause becomes a\nsorted list of such numbers, the formula becomes a list of clauses, and\nthe number of variables is written in front. Two things matter. First,\nthe number of variables is part of the input, so a variable used in no\nclause still counts. Second, the string is not long: a formula with\n$m$ clauses of width $k$ on $n$ variables takes at most about\n$8\\,k\\,n\\,m$ bits. In symbols, with $L$ the length of the string,\n\\[\n  L \\;\\le\\; n + 2 + m\\,\\bigl(2k(4n+1) + 4\\bigr) \\;=\\; O(k\\,n\\,m).\n\\]\nA formula with 125 variables, 500 clauses and width 4 becomes\n$1{,}006{,}223$ bits; one with 1,000 variables, 4,000 clauses and width\n3 becomes 47.9 million bits. Both numbers are from a script that\nimitates the definitions,\n\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}. Nothing in the\nencoding grows exponentially.\n\n\\paragraph{The machine (lines 83--128).}\nThe machine is a Turing machine with several tapes: a finite table of\nstates, a finite number of tapes, and a rule that, in each state, reads\none cell of one tape and then either stops with a yes-or-no answer, or\nwrites one symbol, or moves one head by one cell. Every part of it is\nfinite. A step is one lookup in a table with three rows per state. No\npart of the machine can hide an unbounded computation inside a step.\nThe tapes and the moves are Mathlib's standard ones (Mathlib is the\ncommunity library of mathematics for Lean, at the revision the\ncollection pins, \\texttt{d13f23b7}).\n\n\\paragraph{Runs (lines 87--90).}\nA \\emph{trace} of length $t$ is a record that the machine, started in\none configuration, reached another in exactly $t$ successful steps. The\nstep rule is a function, so each run is determined by its input; there\nis no randomness and no guessing.\n\n\\paragraph{Space monitoring (lines 147--189).}\nBeside each configuration the file keeps, for every tape, the furthest\ncells the head has reached to the left and to the right. The\n\\emph{visited space} is the total number of cells ever visited, summed\nover the tapes. The \\emph{count word} is a number written in binary,\nleast significant bit first (line 147).\n\n\\paragraph{The exponent and the bound (lines 130--141).}\nThe theorem's running-time bound is built from one function of $n$,\nwhich the file calls \\texttt{runtimeScale} and we write $\\rhon$:\n\\[\n  \\rhon \\;=\\; \\frac{n\\,\\bigl(\\ln\\ln(n+16)\\bigr)^2}{\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}},\n\\]\nwith $\\ln$ the natural logarithm. In words: take $n$; multiply it by\nthe square of the logarithm of the logarithm of $n + 16$; divide by the\nsquare root of the logarithm of $n + 16$. The manuscript uses the same\nlogarithm: ``Throughout, log denotes the natural logarithm'' (p.~1,\nlines 48--49; again at p.~7, lines 326--327). The $+16$ keeps the double\nlogarithm defined and positive at every $n$, including $n = 0$, so the\ntheorem needs no condition on $n$. The bound itself, called\n\\texttt{runtimeBound} (lines 136--139), is\n\\[\n  C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\,\\rhon},\n\\]\nthat is, a constant $C$, times a power $d$ of the input length $L$,\ntimes two raised to $A$ times $\\rhon$. We call $\\rhon$ \\emph{the\nexponent} and $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ \\emph{the bound} throughout the\npaper. The constants $C$, $A$ and $d$ are the subject of the next\nsubsection.\n\n\\subsection{What the theorem says, and in what order}\n\\label{sec:statement-theorem}\n\nLines 194--217 state the theorem. Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-theorem}\ndisplays it with every quantifier and the line of every clause. In\nwords, for every fixed width $k$ of at least 2:\n\n\\begin{quote}\nThere exist a machine, a count tape, an answer tape, and five positive\nconstants $C, A, S, d, e$, such that for every number of variables $n$\nand every formula $F$ on $n$ variables of width at most $k$, the\nmachine started on the encoding of $F$ halts after some number of steps,\n\\texttt{time}, with the count of satisfying assignments written in\nbinary on the count tape, the bit ``is the count positive'' on the\nanswer tape and in its halting state, a count word of at most $n + 1$\nbits, \\texttt{time} at most $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, and never more\nthan $S\\,(L+1)^e$ cells visited.\n\\end{quote}\n\nThree features of the order of the words matter for everything that\nfollows.\n\n\\paragraph{The machine and the constants come after $k$ and before $n$.}\nFor each width $k$ there is one machine and one set of constants, and\nthey serve every formula of every size. They may depend on $k$ and on\nnothing else. This is what complexity theorists write as $O_k(1)$: a\nconstant that depends only on $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{There is no lower limit on $n$.}\nThe manuscript's Theorem~1.1 and its Proposition~6.4 are stated for\n$n \\ge 20$ (p.~2, lines 56--57; p.~43, lines 2474--2475). The verified\ntheorem has no such condition; the $+16$ in the exponent is what makes\nthat possible.\n\n\\paragraph{The constants exist, and that is all.}\nThe theorem says that values of $C, A, S, d, e$ exist. It names none\nof them. The proof obtains them from other existence lemmas and never\nwrites a number down. We read the proof for the values it does pin:\nat the point where the main run is assembled, the exponent constant is\nset to $A = 256 + A_t$ with $A_t$ at least $1$\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 51;\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean}, lines 792--793). So\nthe proof as written has $A \\ge 257$, and the theorem that the\ncomparator exhibits adds one more\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}, $A \\ge 258$). No closed value of any of the\nfive constants exists anywhere. The machine is explicit in one respect:\nit is compiled from a program with 80 one-bit registers and 1,220 tapes\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 98--101). How many states it\nhas is never computed.\n\n\\subsection{The exponent, in numbers}\n\\label{sec:statement-numbers}\n\nEverything the title says rests on one comparison: the exponent $\\rhon$\nagainst $n$ itself. If $\\rhon$ is below $n$, the bound's exponential\npart $2^{\\rhon}$ is below $2^n$, the cost of trying every assignment.\nIf $\\rhon$ is above $n$, it is not.\n\nThe ratio $\\rhon / n$ depends only on $y = \\ln(n+16)$: it equals\n$(\\ln y)^2 / \\sqrt{y}$. That ratio starts at $0.64$ for $n = 1$ and\nrises. It crosses $1$ between $n = 49$ and $n = 50$: $\\rho(49)/49 =\n0.9994$ and $\\rho(50)/50 = 1.0027$. It keeps rising to a peak of\n$16/e^2 = 2.165$, reached when $\\ln y = 4$, which is at $n \\approx\n10^{23.7}$, a number with 24 digits. Then it falls, and it is back below\n$1$ only when $y = 5503.7$, which is at $n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx\n10^{2390}$, a number with 2,391 digits.\nTable~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale} gives the values and\nFigures~\\ref{fig:central} and \\ref{fig:runtime-scale} draw them. Every\nnumber is printed by \\repofile{paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py}, run\nunder \\texttt{python3 -I}; it imports nothing from the clone and works\nin $y$, so that no $n$ beyond the range of floating-point arithmetic is\never formed.\n\nTwo facts are true at once, and the title is about the second. In the\nlimit, $\\rhon / n$ goes to $0$: the exponent grows more slowly than\n$n$, which is what ``subexponential'' means, and that is a theorem in\nour companion Lean \\leanref{rho-little-o}. And on every size anyone\nwill ever write down, from $50$ to a number with 2,390 digits, the\nexponent is above $n$.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The verified exponent compared with $n$. The second column is\nthe ratio $\\rhon/n$; where it is above $1$, the bound's exponential\npart $2^{\\rhon}$ (with $A = 1$) is above $2^n$, the cost of trying\nevery assignment. The third column is $\\rhon$ itself. $\\rhon =\nn(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$, the comparator's\n\\texttt{runtimeScale} (lines 132--134), natural logarithms. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}\n\\label{tab:runtime-scale}\n\\input{tables/runtime_scale}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n.pdf}\\\\[4pt]\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n_practical.pdf}\n\\caption{The verified exponent against trying every assignment. Each\ncurve is an exponent divided by $n$; the dashed line at $1$ is $2^n$,\nthe cost of trying every assignment, and a curve above the line is a\nguarantee worse than that. Top: the whole range, the horizontal axis\nbeing the number of digits of $n$, from $n = 10$ to $n = 10^{3000}$.\nThe solid curve is the verified exponent $\\rhon/n$; the two curves\nbeneath it are the manuscript's two sharper exponents,\n$n\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (Theorem~1.1, general) and $n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$\n(fixed clause density), which are not in the Lean library\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}). Bottom: the same three curves over the\npractical range $n = 10$ to $10^6$, with $\\rhon/n$ marked at $n = 50,\n125, 10^3, 10^4, 10^6$. All curves take the constant $A$ to be $1$; the\nverified $A$ is unknown and at least 257 in the proof on record.\nRegenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/rho\\_over\\_n.py}.}\n\\label{fig:central}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{runtime_scale.pdf}\n\\caption{The ratio $\\rhon/n$ alone, over an enormous range: the\nhorizontal axis is the number of digits of $n$, from $n = 10$ to\n$n = 10^{2500}$. The level $1$ is the cost of trying every assignment.\nMarked: the first whole number above the level ($n = 50$); the peak\n($16/e^2 = 2.17$, at $n \\approx 10^{23.7}$); the return below the level\n($n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx 10^{2390}$); and, shaded, the practical\nrange $50 \\le n \\le 10^6$. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}\n\\label{fig:runtime-scale}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{The title's claim, made precise}\n\\label{sec:statement-title}\n\nThe title says the verified algorithm is slower than trying all $2^n$\nassignments at every practical size. Read against the theorem, that\nmeans the following four things, and nothing more.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{What is verified is a guarantee, not a running time.} The\n  theorem says the machine halts within $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps.\n  It does not say how many steps the machine actually takes on any\n  input. No implementation of the machine exists to be timed. A bound\n  above $2^n$ does not say the machine is slow; it says the proof\n  promises nothing better than $2^n$ there.\n\\item \\textbf{The constants are never computed.} The theorem asserts\n  that $C, A, S, d, e$ exist. The proof builds them from other\n  existence lemmas and states no value, beyond the $A \\ge 257$ read\n  above. The machine's number of states is likewise never computed.\n\\item \\textbf{With $A = 1$, the verified exponent is above $n$ for every\n  $n$ from 50 up to about $10^{2390}$.} That is\n  Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}. Over that range the exponential part\n  of the bound is at least $2^n$, and at its worst it is $2^{2.17\\,n}$.\n  With the true $A$, whatever it is, the exponential part is $2^{A\\rhon}$,\n  which is at least $2^{\\rhon}$ as soon as $A \\ge 1$; and the proof's\n  own construction has $A \\ge 257$. So nothing on record puts the bound\n  below $2^n$ at any practical size.\n\\item \\textbf{Hence, at every practical size, the verified guarantee is\n  weaker than the guarantee of trying all assignments.} Trying all\n  $2^n$ assignments and checking each against the $m$ clauses counts\n  the satisfying ones in $2^n$ times a small polynomial in $n + m$\n  steps, with constants one can write down. The verified guarantee is\n  $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ with $\\rhon > n$ on $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$ and\n  $C, A, d$ unknown. For every $C \\ge 1$, $A \\ge 1$ and $d \\ge 1$ the\n  verified bound is above $2^n$ on every $n$ in that range. The only\n  values on record are the proof's own, which has $1 \\le C$ and\n  $1 \\le A$ in the statement of its main run\n  (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 12) and\n  $A \\ge 257$ in its construction. A proof with $A < 1$ would be a\n  different proof; none is on record.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nNone of this is a defect of the formalisation or of the proof. It is\nwhat a theorem of this shape means when the constants in it are not\ntracked. The manuscript itself calls $\\rhon$ ``the weaker exponent''\n(p.~2, line~63), and its sharper exponents are not in the Lean library\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}). One detail of bookkeeping: inside the\nsolution the exponent is written with base-2 logarithms, and a lemma\nrelates the two forms by a constant factor that $A$ absorbs\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-theorem}); nothing above depends on it.\n\n\\subsection{What the statement does establish}\n\\label{sec:statement-establishes}\n\nFor each fixed width $k \\ge 2$, there is a deterministic machine that,\ngiven any $k$-CNF on $n$ variables with $m$ clauses in the fixed\nencoding, halts with the exact number of satisfying assignments written\nin binary on a named tape, within $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps. Here\n$L$ grows no faster than a polynomial in $n + m$, and $\\rhon$ grows more\nslowly than $n$. A formula of width $k$ on $n$ variables cannot have\nmore than about $n^k$ distinct clauses ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$,\n$k = 3$; Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-theorem}), so the whole bound is\nsubexponential in $n$ alone. In the standard vocabulary: a deterministic\n$2^{o(n)} \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ algorithm for counting the solutions\nof $k$-SAT, and therefore for deciding it. That refutes the Exponential\nTime Hypothesis \\citep{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, the conjecture that\n3-SAT needs time exponential in $n$, as stated. The verified theorem is\nthe manuscript's own verified form, the weaker exponent of its\nProposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476), without the $n \\ge 20$\ncondition. We found no field, definition or quantifier through which\nthe statement could be accepted by the kernel and yet mean something\nweaker than this; the audit behind that sentence is\n\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/statement_audit.md}. Our own\nrestatement of the theorem as a named Lean sentence, over a verbatim\ncopy of the machine model, is \\leanref{subexpcount}; it is never proved\nand never assumed there (Section~\\ref{sec:lean}).\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:statement-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No running time of any machine.} Nothing here measures or\n  estimates how long the verified machine, or any program, takes on any\n  input. The title compares two \\emph{guarantees}.\n  Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner} is the only place in the paper where\n  anything is timed, and it times ordinary solvers, not this machine.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement about the proof.} That the statement means\n  what we say it means is a reading of 220 lines. Whether the proof is\n  sound is Section~\\ref{sec:proof} and Section~\\ref{sec:replay}, which\n  found no flaw. The one proof-side fact quoted here, $A = 256 + A_t$,\n  is a line of the solution read for the value it assigns, not a claim\n  about the argument.\n\\item \\textbf{No value of the constants.} $A \\ge 257$ is what the proof\n  writes down, not what the machine needs; a different proof of the\n  same statement could have any $A > 0$. The statement itself fixes\n  nothing.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the manuscript's sharper exponents}, which\n  are not in the Lean library and are Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}'s\n  subject.\n\\item \\textbf{No claim about sizes beyond $e^{5504}$.} Past\n  $n \\approx 10^{2390}$ the verified exponent is below $n$ again at\n  $A = 1$; we say nothing about that range except that it is not a\n  practical one.\n\\end{itemize}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":247,"newStart":1,"newLines":229,"lines":["-% The verified statement, read exactly -- drafted by loop0015 item 02 (2026-11-05).","+% The verified statement, read exactly -- drafted by loop0015 item 02 (2026-11-05),","+% rewritten in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05, owner's rule of 05:30)."," % Every line number of the comparator file is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone"," % (~/dev/untrusted/openai-math, read with cat/sed -n only); every manuscript quote"," % gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt."," % Every number of this section: python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py"," % (the rho(n)/n table and the crossings) and python3 -I"," % reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py (the encoding lengths).","+% The definition-by-definition reading and the theorem with its quantifiers","+% are in Appendix B (secB_appendix_technical.tex), cited from here."," \\section{The verified statement, read exactly}"," \\label{sec:statement}"," ","-The whole of what is machine-checked about family 378 is one theorem,","-\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, stated in the 220-line comparator file","-\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean} of the","-collection \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}","-at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}. The file carries the statement with a","-\\texttt{sorry} for its proof (line 218); the solution tree proves the same","-statement, and the collection's comparator checks that the two agree","-(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). The replay of Section~\\ref{sec:replay}","-rebuilt that proof and found the three standard axioms and nothing else.","-This section reads the statement, definition by definition, and says what","-it asserts and what it does not. Line numbers are those of the comparator","-file; we quote nothing from memory.","+\\subsection{What we are reading, and the words we need}","+\\label{sec:statement-words}"," ","-\\subsection{The definitions, one by one}","+OpenAI's collection \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}","+holds, for the result it calls family 378, one machine-checked theorem.","+It is named \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}. It is stated in a file of","+220 lines, \\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean},","+at commit \\texttt{4eaee875} of the collection. This section reads that","+file and says what the theorem asserts and what it does not. Line","+numbers are those of the file. We quote nothing from memory."," ","+A few words first, each in one sentence.","+\\emph{Lean} is a proof assistant: a program that checks every step of a","+proof and refuses a proof with a gap.","+The \\emph{kernel} is the small core of Lean that does the final","+checking; when we say a statement is verified, we mean the kernel","+accepted a proof of it.","+A \\emph{formula} here is a Boolean formula in conjunctive normal form: a","+list of \\emph{clauses}, each clause an ``or'' of a few variables or","+negated variables, and the whole formula true when every clause is","+true. A \\emph{$k$-CNF} is such a formula whose clauses have at most $k$","+variables each; 3-SAT is the problem of deciding whether a 3-CNF can be","+made true.","+A \\emph{satisfying assignment} is a choice of true or false for every","+variable that makes the formula true; with $n$ variables there are","+$2^n$ assignments to try, and \\emph{trying all $2^n$ assignments} is the","+method the title names.","+The \\emph{comparator} is OpenAI's checking tool. It takes a","+\\emph{challenge file}, which states a theorem and leaves the proof blank,","+and a \\emph{solution}, which proves it, and checks that the two state","+the same theorem and that the proof uses only three standard axioms.","+The file we read is the challenge file. It carries the statement with","+the placeholder \\texttt{sorry} where the proof would go (line 218). The","+solution proves the same statement in 3,091 files","+(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). We rebuilt that proof ourselves and the","+kernel reported the three standard axioms and nothing else","+(Section~\\ref{sec:replay}).","+","+\\subsection{The definitions, in words}","+\\label{sec:statement-definitions}","+","+The theorem speaks about five kinds of object. Each is defined in the","+file before the theorem. Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-definitions} gives","+every definition with its line numbers; here is what each one means.","+"," \\paragraph{Formulas and the count (lines 13--51).}","-A \\texttt{Literal V} is a variable with a sign (lines 19--22); a","-\\texttt{Clause V} is a \\texttt{Finset} of literals and a \\texttt{CNF V} a","-\\texttt{Finset} of clauses (lines 24--25). An assignment is any function","-\\(V \\to \\mathtt{Bool}\\) (line 17). A literal evaluates to the Boolean","-``the assignment agrees with its sign'' (lines 29--30), a clause to ``some","-literal is true'' (lines 32--33), a formula to ``every clause is true''","-(lines 35--36). \\texttt{WidthAtMost k F} says every clause has at most","-\\(k\\) literals as a set (line 38). Over a finite variable type,","-\\texttt{satisfyingAssignments F} filters the universe of all assignments","-(lines 44--45), and \\texttt{count F} is its cardinality (line 47): the","-number of satisfying assignments among all \\(2^n\\) when","-\\(V = \\mathtt{Fin}\\ n\\). The \\texttt{Finset} choices change nothing in the","-meaning: a repeated clause or literal collapses, the empty clause is","-allowed and unsatisfiable, a tautological clause \\(\\{x, \\neg x\\}\\) is","-allowed and counts two toward the width. That is consistent with the","-manuscript's ``Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than \\(k\\)","-literals'' (p.~1, line~44).","+A formula is a finite set of clauses over $n$ variables, and a clause is","+a finite set of signed variables. The formula \\texttt{F} has width at","+most $k$ when every clause has at most $k$ members (line 38). The number","+\\texttt{count F} is how many of the $2^n$ assignments make the formula","+true (line 47). Because clauses and formulas are sets, a repeated clause","+collapses into one; the empty clause is allowed and can never be","+satisfied; and a clause holding both a variable and its negation is","+allowed and counts as two members. The manuscript says the same thing in","+its own words: ``Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than","+\\(k\\) literals'' (p.~1, line~44)."," "," \\paragraph{The input encoding (lines 53--81).}","-\\texttt{encodeNat n} is \\(n\\) ones followed by a zero (line 55): unary.","-\\texttt{frame} doubles a bit string, prefixing each bit with a one, and","-ends it with a zero (lines 57--58). \\texttt{encodeList enc} prefixes each","-element's frame with a one and ends the list with a zero (lines 60--62).","-A literal becomes the number \\(2\\cdot\\mathrm{var} + [\\text{positive}]\\),","-at most \\(2n - 1\\) (lines 64--65); a clause is its sorted list of literal","-numbers, a formula its sorted list of clauses (lines 67--71).","-\\texttt{encodeCNF F} is \\texttt{encodeNat n} followed by the formula's","-list of lists of unary numbers (lines 73--77), and","-\\texttt{formulaBitLength F} is the length of that bit string (line 79).","-Two things follow. The number of variables \\(n\\) is part of the input,","-so a variable that occurs in no clause is still counted. And the length","-is polynomial: a clause of \\(j \\le k\\) literals costs \\(2j(4n+1)+4\\) bits,","-so","+The machine does not read a formula; it reads a string of bits. The","+file fixes how a formula becomes bits. Numbers are written in unary,","+that is, $n$ is written as $n$ ones and a zero. Each clause becomes a","+sorted list of such numbers, the formula becomes a list of clauses, and","+the number of variables is written in front. Two things matter. First,","+the number of variables is part of the input, so a variable used in no","+clause still counts. Second, the string is not long: a formula with","+$m$ clauses of width $k$ on $n$ variables takes at most about","+$8\\,k\\,n\\,m$ bits. In symbols, with $L$ the length of the string,"," \\[","-  L \\;=\\; \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F \\;\\le\\; n + 2 + m\\,\\bigl(2k(4n+1) + 4\\bigr)","-  \\;=\\; O(k\\,n\\,m)","+  L \\;\\le\\; n + 2 + m\\,\\bigl(2k(4n+1) + 4\\bigr) \\;=\\; O(k\\,n\\,m)."," \\]","-for \\(m\\) clauses. The solution itself proves","-\\((L+1)^d \\le (8k+7)^d\\,(n + |F| + 1)^{2d}\\)","-(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Statement.lean}, lines 9--14). Emulating the","-definitions (\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}),","-a formula with \\(n = 125\\), \\(m = 500\\), \\(k = 4\\) encodes to","-\\(L = 1{,}006{,}223\\) bits and one with \\(n = 1{,}000\\), \\(m = 4{,}000\\),","-\\(k = 3\\) to \\(47.9\\) million, both under the bound. Nothing in the","-encoding is exponential; the unary numbers are linear in \\(n\\).","+A formula with 125 variables, 500 clauses and width 4 becomes","+$1{,}006{,}223$ bits; one with 1,000 variables, 4,000 clauses and width","+3 becomes 47.9 million bits. Both numbers are from a script that","+imitates the definitions,","+\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}. Nothing in the","+encoding grows exponentially."," "," \\paragraph{The machine (lines 83--128).}","-\\texttt{PrimitiveMachine} (lines 92--99) is a finite object: a number","-\\texttt{tapeCount} of tapes and a number \\texttt{stateCount} of states,","-an input tape and a start state, a table \\texttt{scan} saying which tape","-each state reads, and a table \\texttt{transition} from","-\\(\\mathtt{Fin}\\ \\mathtt{stateCount} \\times \\mathtt{Option\\ Bool}\\)---a","-domain of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) points---into either a halting","-answer (a \\texttt{Bool}) or a next state with one action. The action type","-is Mathlib's \\texttt{Turing.TM0.Stmt (Option Bool)}: move the head left or","-right, or write one of the three symbols \\texttt{none}, \\texttt{some","-false}, \\texttt{some true}, five values in all","-(\\path{Mathlib/Computability/TuringMachine/PostTuringMachine.lean}","-lines 118--120 and \\path{Tape.lean} lines 421--423 at Mathlib","-\\texttt{d13f23b7}, the clone's pinned revision). A configuration","-\\texttt{Cfg} (lines 103--105) is a state, halted or not, and one Mathlib","-\\texttt{Tape} per tape index (a head symbol and two blank-padded halves,","-\\path{Tape.lean} lines 408--414, blank \\(=\\) \\texttt{none}).","-\\texttt{step} (lines 107--119) returns \\texttt{none} in a halted state;","-otherwise it reads the head of the one tape the state scans, looks the","-pair up in the table, and either halts with the answer, or writes one","-symbol or moves that one head by one cell and changes state. \\texttt{init}","-(lines 121--124) puts the input on the input tape with the head at its","-first bit and every other tape blank (\\texttt{Tape.mk}$_1$,","-\\path{Tape.lean} line 494). A Lean function on a finite domain is,","-whatever term defines it, a finite table; so a step is one lookup in a","-table of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) rows, and no field of the","-structure can carry unbounded computation.","+The machine is a Turing machine with several tapes: a finite table of","+states, a finite number of tapes, and a rule that, in each state, reads","+one cell of one tape and then either stops with a yes-or-no answer, or","+writes one symbol, or moves one head by one cell. Every part of it is","+finite. A step is one lookup in a table with three rows per state. No","+part of the machine can hide an unbounded computation inside a step.","+The tapes and the moves are Mathlib's standard ones (Mathlib is the","+community library of mathematics for Lean, at the revision the","+collection pins, \\texttt{d13f23b7})."," ","-\\paragraph{Traces (lines 87--90).}","-\\texttt{Trace step t a c} is an inductive proposition with two","-constructors: \\texttt{refl}, a trace of length 0 from \\(a\\) to \\(a\\)","-itself, and \\texttt{cons}, which extends a trace from \\(b\\) by one","-successful step \\(a \\mapsto b\\). So it holds exactly when \\(c\\) is","-reached from \\(a\\) by \\(t\\) successful applications of \\texttt{step}, and","-a trace of length 0 to a different configuration is impossible. Since","-\\texttt{step} is a function, the run is deterministic.","+\\paragraph{Runs (lines 87--90).}","+A \\emph{trace} of length $t$ is a record that the machine, started in","+one configuration, reached another in exactly $t$ successful steps. The","+step rule is a function, so each run is determined by its input; there","+is no randomness and no guessing."," "," \\paragraph{Space monitoring (lines 147--189).}","-\\texttt{LocatedCfg} (lines 149--153) pairs a configuration with, per tape,","-the head's current position and the least and greatest positions seen so","-far, as integers; \\texttt{locatedInit} starts every head at 0 with the","-input tape's range covering the input (lines 155--159);","-\\texttt{headDisplacement} is \\(-1\\), \\(+1\\) or \\(0\\) for a move left, a","-move right or a write (lines 161--164); \\texttt{monitor} (lines 166--182)","-applies the same table lookup to update the position and the running","-minimum and maximum of the scanned tape, and nothing else;","-\\texttt{locatedStep} is \\texttt{step} on the configuration followed by","-\\texttt{monitor} (lines 184--186). \\texttt{visitedSpace} (lines 188--189)","-is the sum over tapes of \\(\\mathrm{upper} - \\mathrm{lower} + 1\\): the","-number of cells whose head position was ever visited. \\texttt{countWord a}","-is the binary expansion of \\(a\\), least significant bit first, with","-\\texttt{[false]} for \\(0\\) (line 147), and \\texttt{toNat} (lines 7--9)","-decodes such a word.","+Beside each configuration the file keeps, for every tape, the furthest","+cells the head has reached to the left and to the right. The","+\\emph{visited space} is the total number of cells ever visited, summed","+over the tapes. The \\emph{count word} is a number written in binary,","+least significant bit first (line 147)."," "," \\paragraph{The exponent and the bound (lines 130--141).}","-\\begin{quote}\\small","-\\texttt{runtimeScale n = n * (Real.log (Real.log (n + 16)))\\^{}2 /}\\\\","-\\texttt{\\quad Real.sqrt (Real.log (n + 16))}","-\\end{quote}","-(lines 132--134, transcribed; the casts to \\(\\mathbb{R}\\) omitted), that is","+The theorem's running-time bound is built from one function of $n$,","+which the file calls \\texttt{runtimeScale} and we write $\\rhon$:"," \\[","   \\rhon \\;=\\; \\frac{n\\,\\bigl(\\ln\\ln(n+16)\\bigr)^2}{\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}},"," \\]","-with \\texttt{Real.log} the natural logarithm. The manuscript fixes the","-same base: ``Throughout, log denotes the natural logarithm'' (p.~1, lines","-48--49; again at p.~7, lines 326--327). The shift by 16 keeps","-\\(\\ln\\ln\\) defined and positive at every \\(n \\ge 0\\) (\\(\\ln 16 = 2.77\\)),","-so the theorem can be stated for all \\(n\\) with no proviso. Then","+with $\\ln$ the natural logarithm. In words: take $n$; multiply it by","+the square of the logarithm of the logarithm of $n + 16$; divide by the","+square root of the logarithm of $n + 16$. The manuscript uses the same","+logarithm: ``Throughout, log denotes the natural logarithm'' (p.~1,","+lines 48--49; again at p.~7, lines 326--327). The $+16$ keeps the double","+logarithm defined and positive at every $n$, including $n = 0$, so the","+theorem needs no condition on $n$. The bound itself, called","+\\texttt{runtimeBound} (lines 136--139), is","+\\[","+  C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\,\\rhon},","+\\]","+that is, a constant $C$, times a power $d$ of the input length $L$,","+times two raised to $A$ times $\\rhon$. We call $\\rhon$ \\emph{the","+exponent} and $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ \\emph{the bound} throughout the","+paper. The constants $C$, $A$ and $d$ are the subject of the next","+subsection.","+","+\\subsection{What the theorem says, and in what order}","+\\label{sec:statement-theorem}","+","+Lines 194--217 state the theorem. Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-theorem}","+displays it with every quantifier and the line of every clause. In","+words, for every fixed width $k$ of at least 2:","+"," \\begin{quote}","-\\texttt{runtimeBound C A d L n = C * (L + 1)\\^{}d * 2\\^{}(A * runtimeScale n)}","+There exist a machine, a count tape, an answer tape, and five positive","+constants $C, A, S, d, e$, such that for every number of variables $n$","+and every formula $F$ on $n$ variables of width at most $k$, the","+machine started on the encoding of $F$ halts after some number of steps,","+\\texttt{time}, with the count of satisfying assignments written in","+binary on the count tape, the bit ``is the count positive'' on the","+answer tape and in its halting state, a count word of at most $n + 1$","+bits, \\texttt{time} at most $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, and never more","+than $S\\,(L+1)^e$ cells visited."," \\end{quote}","-(lines 136--139, transcribed), a real number, with the real-exponent","-power. We write \\(\\rhon\\) for \\texttt{runtimeScale n} throughout the","-paper."," ","-\\subsection{The theorem, and the order of its quantifiers}","+Three features of the order of the words matter for everything that","+follows."," ","-Lines 194--217 state, for every \\(k\\) with \\(2 \\le k\\):","-\\[","-\\small","-\\begin{array}{l}","-\\exists\\ \\mathtt{machine},\\ \\mathtt{countTape},\\ \\mathtt{answerTape},\\","-  C, A, S \\in \\mathbb{R},\\ d, e \\in \\mathbb{N},\\\\[2pt]","-\\quad 0 < C \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < A \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < S \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < d \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < e\\ \\wedge\\\\[2pt]","-\\quad \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ \\forall\\, F : \\mathtt{CNF}\\ (\\mathtt{Fin}\\ n),\\","-  \\mathtt{WidthAtMost}\\ k\\ F \\;\\rightarrow\\\\[2pt]","-\\qquad \\exists\\ \\mathtt{time} \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ \\mathtt{final} : \\mathtt{LocatedCfg}\\ \\mathtt{machine},\\\\[2pt]","-\\qquad\\quad \\mathtt{Trace}\\ (\\mathtt{locatedStep}\\ \\mathtt{machine})\\ \\mathtt{time}\\","-  (\\mathtt{locatedInit}\\ \\mathtt{machine}\\ (\\mathtt{encodeCNF}\\ F))\\ \\mathtt{final}\\\\[2pt]","-\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\text{(lines 201--202)}\\\\[2pt]","-\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the count tape holds \\texttt{countWord (count F)}}","-  \\quad\\text{(203--204)}\\\\[2pt]","-\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the answer tape holds the bit \\([0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]\\)}","-  \\quad\\text{(205--206)}\\\\[2pt]","-\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the halting state carries that bit}","-  \\quad\\text{(207)}\\\\[2pt]","-\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\mathtt{toNat}\\ (\\mathtt{countWord}\\ (\\mathtt{count}\\ F)) = \\mathtt{count}\\ F","-  \\quad\\text{(208)}\\\\[2pt]","-\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ |\\mathtt{countWord}\\ (\\mathtt{count}\\ F)| \\le n + 1","-  \\quad\\text{(209)}\\\\[2pt]","-\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\mathtt{time} \\le C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\,\\rhon},\\ L = \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F","-  \\quad\\text{(210--211)}\\\\[2pt]","-\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\forall\\ \\text{prefix of the trace},\\","-  \\mathtt{visitedSpace} \\le S\\,(L+1)^e","-  \\quad\\text{(212--217).}","-\\end{array}","-\\]","-Three features of the order matter.","+\\paragraph{The machine and the constants come after $k$ and before $n$.}","+For each width $k$ there is one machine and one set of constants, and","+they serve every formula of every size. They may depend on $k$ and on","+nothing else. This is what complexity theorists write as $O_k(1)$: a","+constant that depends only on $k$."," ","-\\emph{The machine and the five constants come after \\(k\\) and before","-\\(n\\) and \\(F\\).} They may depend on \\(k\\) and on nothing else; inside","-the universal quantifier only \\texttt{time} and \\texttt{final} are","-chosen. So for each \\(k\\) one fixed finite machine serves every formula","-of every size, and \\(C, A, S, d, e\\) are the same for all of them: the","-standard \\(O_k(1)\\) reading. The table's size is \\(O_k(1)\\) in the same","-sense, with no explicit function of \\(k\\).","+\\paragraph{There is no lower limit on $n$.}","+The manuscript's Theorem~1.1 and its Proposition~6.4 are stated for","+$n \\ge 20$ (p.~2, lines 56--57; p.~43, lines 2474--2475). The verified","+theorem has no such condition; the $+16$ in the exponent is what makes","+that possible."," ","-\\emph{There is no lower bound on \\(n\\).} The manuscript's Theorem~1.1","-and Proposition~6.4 are stated for \\(n \\ge 20\\) (p.~2, lines 56--57;","-p.~43, lines 2474--2475), and the solution's auxiliary","-\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} keeps \\(20 \\le n\\)","-(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 104--109); the exported","-theorem does not. The \\(+16\\) in \\(\\rhon\\) is what makes that possible.","-","-\\emph{The constants are existential.} The statement asserts that values","-of \\(C, A, S, d, e\\) exist; it names none. The proof obtains them from","-existential lemmas and never evaluates them: at the point where the","-large-input run is assembled, the exponent constant is set to","-\\(A = 256 + A_t\\) with \\(A_t \\ge 1\\) taken from a leaf contract","+\\paragraph{The constants exist, and that is all.}","+The theorem says that values of $C, A, S, d, e$ exist. It names none","+of them. The proof obtains them from other existence lemmas and never","+writes a number down. We read the proof for the values it does pin:","+at the point where the main run is assembled, the exponent constant is","+set to $A = 256 + A_t$ with $A_t$ at least $1$"," (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 51;","-\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean}, lines 792--793), and","-the degree and the polynomial constants are built the same way. So the","-proof as written has \\(A \\ge 257\\), and no closed value of any of the","-five. The witness machine is explicit in one respect: it is compiled","-from a program over 80 one-bit registers and 1,220 tapes, with","-\\(\\mathtt{countTape} = 256\\) and \\(\\mathtt{answerTape} = 1216\\)","-(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 98--101); its state count is","-a finite type's cardinality that nothing computes. Section~\\ref{sec:proof}","-is the inspection of the proof; this section only reads what the","-statement fixes and what it leaves open.","+\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean}, lines 792--793). So","+the proof as written has $A \\ge 257$, and the theorem that the","+comparator exhibits adds one more","+(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}, $A \\ge 258$). No closed value of any of the","+five constants exists anywhere. The machine is explicit in one respect:","+it is compiled from a program with 80 one-bit registers and 1,220 tapes","+(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 98--101). How many states it","+has is never computed."," "," \\subsection{The exponent, in numbers}","+\\label{sec:statement-numbers}"," ","-Everything the title says rests on one function. With \\(y = \\ln(n+16)\\),","-\\[","-  \\frac{\\rhon}{n} \\;=\\; \\frac{(\\ln y)^2}{\\sqrt{y}},","-\\]","-which rises from \\(0.64\\) at \\(n = 1\\), crosses \\(1\\) between","-\\(n = 49\\) and \\(n = 50\\), peaks at \\(16/e^2 = 2.165\\) where","-\\(\\ln y = 4\\), i.e.\\ \\(y = e^4\\), \\(n = e^{e^4} - 16 \\approx 10^{23.7}\\),","-and returns below \\(1\\) only where \\((\\ln y)^2 = \\sqrt{y}\\) again, at","-\\(y = 5503.7\\), i.e.\\ \\(n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx 10^{2390}\\).","+Everything the title says rests on one comparison: the exponent $\\rhon$","+against $n$ itself. If $\\rhon$ is below $n$, the bound's exponential","+part $2^{\\rhon}$ is below $2^n$, the cost of trying every assignment.","+If $\\rhon$ is above $n$, it is not.","+","+The ratio $\\rhon / n$ depends only on $y = \\ln(n+16)$: it equals","+$(\\ln y)^2 / \\sqrt{y}$. That ratio starts at $0.64$ for $n = 1$ and","+rises. It crosses $1$ between $n = 49$ and $n = 50$: $\\rho(49)/49 =","+0.9994$ and $\\rho(50)/50 = 1.0027$. It keeps rising to a peak of","+$16/e^2 = 2.165$, reached when $\\ln y = 4$, which is at $n \\approx","+10^{23.7}$, a number with 24 digits. Then it falls, and it is back below","+$1$ only when $y = 5503.7$, which is at $n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx","+10^{2390}$, a number with 2,391 digits."," Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale} gives the values and","-Figures~\\ref{fig:central} and \\ref{fig:runtime-scale} draw them; every","-number is printed by \\repofile{paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py}","-(run under \\texttt{python3 -I}; it imports nothing from the clone and","-computes in \\(y\\), so that no \\(n\\) beyond floating-point range is ever","-formed). The crossing at 50 is a fact about integers:","-\\(\\rho(49)/49 = 0.9994\\) and \\(\\rho(50)/50 = 1.0027\\). In the limit","-\\(\\rhon/n \\to 0\\), since \\((\\ln y)^2/\\sqrt{y} \\to 0\\): the exponent is","-\\(o(n)\\), and that is a theorem in our companion Lean","-\\leanref{rho-little-o}. Both facts are true at once, and the second is","-the one the title is about: the function is \\(o(n)\\), and it is above","-\\(n\\) on every size anyone will ever write down.","+Figures~\\ref{fig:central} and \\ref{fig:runtime-scale} draw them. Every","+number is printed by \\repofile{paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py}, run","+under \\texttt{python3 -I}; it imports nothing from the clone and works","+in $y$, so that no $n$ beyond the range of floating-point arithmetic is","+ever formed."," ","+Two facts are true at once, and the title is about the second. In the","+limit, $\\rhon / n$ goes to $0$: the exponent grows more slowly than","+$n$, which is what ``subexponential'' means, and that is a theorem in","+our companion Lean \\leanref{rho-little-o}. And on every size anyone","+will ever write down, from $50$ to a number with 2,390 digits, the","+exponent is above $n$.","+"," \\begin{table}[htbp]"," \\centering","-\\caption{The verified exponent relative to \\(n\\). \\(\\rhon = n(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}\\),","-the comparator's \\texttt{runtimeScale} (lines 132--134), natural logarithms.","-The verified bound is \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\); at \\(A = 1\\) its exponential","-factor is \\(2^{(\\rhon/n)\\cdot n}\\), against \\(2^n\\) for trying every assignment.","-Regenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}","+\\caption{The verified exponent compared with $n$. The second column is","+the ratio $\\rhon/n$; where it is above $1$, the bound's exponential","+part $2^{\\rhon}$ (with $A = 1$) is above $2^n$, the cost of trying","+every assignment. The third column is $\\rhon$ itself. $\\rhon =","+n(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$, the comparator's","+\\texttt{runtimeScale} (lines 132--134), natural logarithms. Regenerate:","+\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}"," \\label{tab:runtime-scale}"," \\input{tables/runtime_scale}"," \\end{table}"]},{"oldStart":250,"oldLines":131,"newStart":232,"newLines":137,"lines":[" \\centering"," \\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n.pdf}\\\\[4pt]"," \\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n_practical.pdf}","-\\caption{The verified exponent against brute force. Top: \\(\\rhon/n\\) over","-the whole range, the axis in \\(\\log_{10} n\\) from \\(n = 10\\) to","-\\(n = 10^{3000}\\); the dashed level is \\(2^n\\); beneath it the manuscript's two","-sharper exponents, \\(n\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}\\) (Theorem~1.1, general) and","-\\(n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}\\) (fixed clause density), which are not in the Lean","-library (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}). Bottom: the same three curves over the","-practical range \\(n = 10\\) to \\(10^6\\), with \\(\\rhon/n\\) marked at","-\\(n = 50, 125, 10^3, 10^4, 10^6\\). All curves at the constant \\(A = 1\\);","-the verified \\(A\\) is unknown. Regenerate:","-\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/rho\\_over\\_n.py}.}","+\\caption{The verified exponent against trying every assignment. Each","+curve is an exponent divided by $n$; the dashed line at $1$ is $2^n$,","+the cost of trying every assignment, and a curve above the line is a","+guarantee worse than that. Top: the whole range, the horizontal axis","+being the number of digits of $n$, from $n = 10$ to $n = 10^{3000}$.","+The solid curve is the verified exponent $\\rhon/n$; the two curves","+beneath it are the manuscript's two sharper exponents,","+$n\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (Theorem~1.1, general) and $n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$","+(fixed clause density), which are not in the Lean library","+(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}). Bottom: the same three curves over the","+practical range $n = 10$ to $10^6$, with $\\rhon/n$ marked at $n = 50,","+125, 10^3, 10^4, 10^6$. All curves take the constant $A$ to be $1$; the","+verified $A$ is unknown and at least 257 in the proof on record.","+Regenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/rho\\_over\\_n.py}.}"," \\label{fig:central}"," \\end{figure}"," "," \\begin{figure}[htbp]"," \\centering"," \\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{runtime_scale.pdf}","-\\caption{\\(\\rhon/n\\) alone, on a logarithmic axis in \\(\\log_{10} n\\) from","-\\(n = 10\\) to \\(n = 10^{2500}\\), with the level \\(1\\) (brute force, \\(2^n\\)),","-the first integer above the level (\\(n = 50\\)), the peak (\\(16/e^2 = 2.17\\)","-at \\(\\ln(n+16) = e^4\\), \\(n \\approx 10^{23.7}\\)), the return below the level","-(\\(n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx 10^{2390}\\)), and the practical range","-\\(50 \\le n \\le 10^6\\) shaded. Regenerate:","+\\caption{The ratio $\\rhon/n$ alone, over an enormous range: the","+horizontal axis is the number of digits of $n$, from $n = 10$ to","+$n = 10^{2500}$. The level $1$ is the cost of trying every assignment.","+Marked: the first whole number above the level ($n = 50$); the peak","+($16/e^2 = 2.17$, at $n \\approx 10^{23.7}$); the return below the level","+($n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx 10^{2390}$); and, shaded, the practical","+range $50 \\le n \\le 10^6$. Regenerate:"," \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}"," \\label{fig:runtime-scale}"," \\end{figure}"," "," \\subsection{The title's claim, made precise}","+\\label{sec:statement-title}"," ","-The title says the verified algorithm is slower than trying all \\(2^n\\)","-assignments at every practical size. Read against the statement, that is","-the following, and nothing more.","+The title says the verified algorithm is slower than trying all $2^n$","+assignments at every practical size. Read against the theorem, that","+means the following four things, and nothing more."," "," \\begin{enumerate}"," \\item \\textbf{What is verified is a guarantee, not a running time.} The","-  theorem says the machine halts within \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\) steps.","-  It does not say how many steps the machine takes on any input, and no","-  implementation of the machine exists to be timed. A bound above","-  \\(2^n\\) does not say the machine is slow; it says the proof promises","-  nothing better than \\(2^n\\) there.","-\\item \\textbf{The constants \\(C, A, S, d, e\\) are existential and never","-  computed.} The comparator asserts their existence; the proof builds","-  them from existential lemmas (above, with \\(A \\ge 257\\) as","-  constructed) and states no value. The machine's state count is likewise","-  never computed.","-\\item \\textbf{With \\(A = 1\\), the verified exponent exceeds \\(n\\) for","-  every \\(50 \\le n < e^{5504}\\).} That is Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}:","-  the exponential factor of the bound is at least \\(2^n\\) from 50","-  variables up to about \\(10^{2390}\\), and \\(2^{2.17\\,n}\\) at its","-  worst. With the true \\(A\\), whatever it is, the factor is","-  \\(2^{A\\rhon} \\ge 2^{\\rhon}\\) once \\(A \\ge 1\\), and the proof's own","-  construction has \\(A \\ge 257\\); so nothing is known that would put the","-  bound below \\(2^n\\) at any practical size.","+  theorem says the machine halts within $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps.","+  It does not say how many steps the machine actually takes on any","+  input. No implementation of the machine exists to be timed. A bound","+  above $2^n$ does not say the machine is slow; it says the proof","+  promises nothing better than $2^n$ there.","+\\item \\textbf{The constants are never computed.} The theorem asserts","+  that $C, A, S, d, e$ exist. The proof builds them from other","+  existence lemmas and states no value, beyond the $A \\ge 257$ read","+  above. The machine's number of states is likewise never computed.","+\\item \\textbf{With $A = 1$, the verified exponent is above $n$ for every","+  $n$ from 50 up to about $10^{2390}$.} That is","+  Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}. Over that range the exponential part","+  of the bound is at least $2^n$, and at its worst it is $2^{2.17\\,n}$.","+  With the true $A$, whatever it is, the exponential part is $2^{A\\rhon}$,","+  which is at least $2^{\\rhon}$ as soon as $A \\ge 1$; and the proof's","+  own construction has $A \\ge 257$. So nothing on record puts the bound","+  below $2^n$ at any practical size."," \\item \\textbf{Hence, at every practical size, the verified guarantee is","-  weaker than the guarantee of trying all assignments.} Trying all \\(2^n\\)","-  assignments and checking each against the \\(m\\) clauses counts the","-  models in \\(2^n \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)\\) steps on any reasonable","-  machine, with constants one can write down. The verified guarantee is","-  \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\) with \\(\\rhon > n\\) on \\(50 \\le n < e^{5504}\\)","-  and \\(C, A, d\\) unknown. For every \\(C \\ge 1\\), \\(A \\ge 1\\) and","-  \\(d \\ge 1\\) the verified bound is above \\(2^n\\) on every \\(n\\) in that","-  range, and the only values on record are the proof's, which has","-  \\(1 \\le C\\) and \\(1 \\le A\\) in the large-input run's own statement","+  weaker than the guarantee of trying all assignments.} Trying all","+  $2^n$ assignments and checking each against the $m$ clauses counts","+  the satisfying ones in $2^n$ times a small polynomial in $n + m$","+  steps, with constants one can write down. The verified guarantee is","+  $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ with $\\rhon > n$ on $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$ and","+  $C, A, d$ unknown. For every $C \\ge 1$, $A \\ge 1$ and $d \\ge 1$ the","+  verified bound is above $2^n$ on every $n$ in that range. The only","+  values on record are the proof's own, which has $1 \\le C$ and","+  $1 \\le A$ in the statement of its main run","   (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 12) and","-  \\(A \\ge 257\\) in its construction. A proof with \\(A < 1\\) would be a","+  $A \\ge 257$ in its construction. A proof with $A < 1$ would be a","   different proof; none is on record."," \\end{enumerate}"," ","-None of this is a defect of the formalisation or of the proof; it is","-what an \\(O_k(1)\\) statement with a \\(2^{o(n)}\\) exponent of this shape","-means when the exponent's constants are not tracked. The manuscript","-itself calls \\(\\rhon\\) ``the weaker exponent'' (p.~2, line~63), and its","-sharper exponents are not in the Lean library (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).","-One more detail of base: the solution's internal \\(\\rho\\)","-(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean}, lines 10--11) is in base 2 and","-is related to \\texttt{runtimeScale} by","-\\(\\mathtt{runtimeScale}\\ n \\le 4\\,\\rho_2(n)\\) for \\(n \\ge 20\\)","-(lines 190--191); the base changes the exponent by a constant factor","-that \\(A\\) absorbs, so nothing above depends on it.","+None of this is a defect of the formalisation or of the proof. It is","+what a theorem of this shape means when the constants in it are not","+tracked. The manuscript itself calls $\\rhon$ ``the weaker exponent''","+(p.~2, line~63), and its sharper exponents are not in the Lean library","+(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}). One detail of bookkeeping: inside the","+solution the exponent is written with base-2 logarithms, and a lemma","+relates the two forms by a constant factor that $A$ absorbs","+(Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-theorem}); nothing above depends on it."," "," \\subsection{What the statement does establish}","+\\label{sec:statement-establishes}"," ","-For each fixed \\(k \\ge 2\\), a deterministic machine that, on the canonical","-encoding of any \\(k\\)-CNF on \\(n\\) variables with \\(m\\) clauses, halts with","-the exact number of satisfying assignments in binary on a named tape,","-within \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\) steps, where \\(L = O(k\\,n\\,m)\\) is","-polynomial in \\(n + m\\) and \\(\\rhon = o(n)\\); and, because a","-\\texttt{Finset} of clauses of width at most \\(k\\) has at most","-\\(\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j} = O(n^k)\\) members (\\(2{,}604{,}376\\) at","-\\(n = 125\\), \\(k = 3\\)), the whole bound is \\(2^{o(n)}\\) in \\(n\\) alone.","-That is a deterministic \\(2^{o(n)} \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)\\) algorithm","-for \\#\\(k\\)-SAT in the standard sense, and therefore for \\(k\\)-SAT; it","-refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis \\citep{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001} as","-stated. The statement reads as the manuscript's verified form, the","-weaker exponent of its Proposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476), without","-the \\(n \\ge 20\\) proviso. We found no field, definition or quantifier","-through which the statement could be kernel-true and mean something","-weaker than this; the audit it summarises is","+For each fixed width $k \\ge 2$, there is a deterministic machine that,","+given any $k$-CNF on $n$ variables with $m$ clauses in the fixed","+encoding, halts with the exact number of satisfying assignments written","+in binary on a named tape, within $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps. Here","+$L$ grows no faster than a polynomial in $n + m$, and $\\rhon$ grows more","+slowly than $n$. A formula of width $k$ on $n$ variables cannot have","+more than about $n^k$ distinct clauses ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$,","+$k = 3$; Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-theorem}), so the whole bound is","+subexponential in $n$ alone. In the standard vocabulary: a deterministic","+$2^{o(n)} \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ algorithm for counting the solutions","+of $k$-SAT, and therefore for deciding it. That refutes the Exponential","+Time Hypothesis \\citep{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, the conjecture that","+3-SAT needs time exponential in $n$, as stated. The verified theorem is","+the manuscript's own verified form, the weaker exponent of its","+Proposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476), without the $n \\ge 20$","+condition. We found no field, definition or quantifier through which","+the statement could be accepted by the kernel and yet mean something","+weaker than this; the audit behind that sentence is"," \\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/statement_audit.md}. Our own","-restatement of the statement as a Lean \\texttt{Prop}, character for","-character over a verbatim copy of the machine model, is","-\\leanref{subexpcount}; it is never proved and never an axiom there","-(Section~\\ref{sec:lean}).","+restatement of the theorem as a named Lean sentence, over a verbatim","+copy of the machine model, is \\leanref{subexpcount}; it is never proved","+and never assumed there (Section~\\ref{sec:lean})."," "," \\subsection{What this does not claim}","+\\label{sec:statement-not}"," "," \\begin{itemize}"," \\item \\textbf{No running time of any machine.} Nothing here measures or","   estimates how long the verified machine, or any program, takes on any","-  input. The title compares two \\emph{guarantees}; Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}","-  is the only place in the paper where anything is timed, and it times","-  ordinary solvers, not this machine.","+  input. The title compares two \\emph{guarantees}.","+  Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner} is the only place in the paper where","+  anything is timed, and it times ordinary solvers, not this machine."," \\item \\textbf{No statement about the proof.} That the statement means","-  what we say it means is a reading of 220 lines; whether the proof of","-  it is sound is Section~\\ref{sec:proof} and Section~\\ref{sec:replay},","-  which found no flaw. The one proof-side fact quoted here, \\(A = 256 +","-  A_t\\), is a line of the solution read for the value it assigns, not a","-  claim about the argument.","-\\item \\textbf{No value of the constants.} \\(A \\ge 257\\) is what the","-  proof writes down, not what the machine needs; a different proof of","-  the same statement could have any \\(A > 0\\). The statement itself","-  fixes nothing.","+  what we say it means is a reading of 220 lines. Whether the proof is","+  sound is Section~\\ref{sec:proof} and Section~\\ref{sec:replay}, which","+  found no flaw. The one proof-side fact quoted here, $A = 256 + A_t$,","+  is a line of the solution read for the value it assigns, not a claim","+  about the argument.","+\\item \\textbf{No value of the constants.} $A \\ge 257$ is what the proof","+  writes down, not what the machine needs; a different proof of the","+  same statement could have any $A > 0$. The statement itself fixes","+  nothing."," \\item \\textbf{Nothing about the manuscript's sharper exponents}, which","-  are not in the Lean library and are Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}'s subject.","-\\item \\textbf{No claim about sizes beyond \\(e^{5504}\\).} Past","-  \\(n \\approx 10^{2390}\\) the verified factor is below \\(2^n\\) at","-  \\(A = 1\\); we say nothing about that range except that it is not a","+  are not in the Lean library and are Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}'s","+  subject.","+\\item \\textbf{No claim about sizes beyond $e^{5504}$.} Past","+  $n \\approx 10^{2390}$ the verified exponent is below $n$ again at","+  $A = 1$; we say nothing about that range except that it is not a","   practical one."," \\end{itemize}"]}],"originalFile":"% The verified statement, read exactly -- drafted by loop0015 item 02 (2026-11-05).\n% Every line number of the comparator file is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone\n% (~/dev/untrusted/openai-math, read with cat/sed -n only); every manuscript quote\n% gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt.\n% Every number of this section: python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py\n% (the rho(n)/n table and the crossings) and python3 -I\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py (the encoding lengths).\n\\section{The verified statement, read exactly}\n\\label{sec:statement}\n\nThe whole of what is machine-checked about family 378 is one theorem,\n\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, stated in the 220-line comparator file\n\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean} of the\ncollection \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}\nat commit \\texttt{4eaee875}. The file carries the statement with a\n\\texttt{sorry} for its proof (line 218); the solution tree proves the same\nstatement, and the collection's comparator checks that the two agree\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). The replay of Section~\\ref{sec:replay}\nrebuilt that proof and found the three standard axioms and nothing else.\nThis section reads the statement, definition by definition, and says what\nit asserts and what it does not. Line numbers are those of the comparator\nfile; we quote nothing from memory.\n\n\\subsection{The definitions, one by one}\n\n\\paragraph{Formulas and the count (lines 13--51).}\nA \\texttt{Literal V} is a variable with a sign (lines 19--22); a\n\\texttt{Clause V} is a \\texttt{Finset} of literals and a \\texttt{CNF V} a\n\\texttt{Finset} of clauses (lines 24--25). An assignment is any function\n\\(V \\to \\mathtt{Bool}\\) (line 17). A literal evaluates to the Boolean\n``the assignment agrees with its sign'' (lines 29--30), a clause to ``some\nliteral is true'' (lines 32--33), a formula to ``every clause is true''\n(lines 35--36). \\texttt{WidthAtMost k F} says every clause has at most\n\\(k\\) literals as a set (line 38). Over a finite variable type,\n\\texttt{satisfyingAssignments F} filters the universe of all assignments\n(lines 44--45), and \\texttt{count F} is its cardinality (line 47): the\nnumber of satisfying assignments among all \\(2^n\\) when\n\\(V = \\mathtt{Fin}\\ n\\). The \\texttt{Finset} choices change nothing in the\nmeaning: a repeated clause or literal collapses, the empty clause is\nallowed and unsatisfiable, a tautological clause \\(\\{x, \\neg x\\}\\) is\nallowed and counts two toward the width. That is consistent with the\nmanuscript's ``Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than \\(k\\)\nliterals'' (p.~1, line~44).\n\n\\paragraph{The input encoding (lines 53--81).}\n\\texttt{encodeNat n} is \\(n\\) ones followed by a zero (line 55): unary.\n\\texttt{frame} doubles a bit string, prefixing each bit with a one, and\nends it with a zero (lines 57--58). \\texttt{encodeList enc} prefixes each\nelement's frame with a one and ends the list with a zero (lines 60--62).\nA literal becomes the number \\(2\\cdot\\mathrm{var} + [\\text{positive}]\\),\nat most \\(2n - 1\\) (lines 64--65); a clause is its sorted list of literal\nnumbers, a formula its sorted list of clauses (lines 67--71).\n\\texttt{encodeCNF F} is \\texttt{encodeNat n} followed by the formula's\nlist of lists of unary numbers (lines 73--77), and\n\\texttt{formulaBitLength F} is the length of that bit string (line 79).\nTwo things follow. The number of variables \\(n\\) is part of the input,\nso a variable that occurs in no clause is still counted. And the length\nis polynomial: a clause of \\(j \\le k\\) literals costs \\(2j(4n+1)+4\\) bits,\nso\n\\[\n  L \\;=\\; \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F \\;\\le\\; n + 2 + m\\,\\bigl(2k(4n+1) + 4\\bigr)\n  \\;=\\; O(k\\,n\\,m)\n\\]\nfor \\(m\\) clauses. The solution itself proves\n\\((L+1)^d \\le (8k+7)^d\\,(n + |F| + 1)^{2d}\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Statement.lean}, lines 9--14). Emulating the\ndefinitions (\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}),\na formula with \\(n = 125\\), \\(m = 500\\), \\(k = 4\\) encodes to\n\\(L = 1{,}006{,}223\\) bits and one with \\(n = 1{,}000\\), \\(m = 4{,}000\\),\n\\(k = 3\\) to \\(47.9\\) million, both under the bound. Nothing in the\nencoding is exponential; the unary numbers are linear in \\(n\\).\n\n\\paragraph{The machine (lines 83--128).}\n\\texttt{PrimitiveMachine} (lines 92--99) is a finite object: a number\n\\texttt{tapeCount} of tapes and a number \\texttt{stateCount} of states,\nan input tape and a start state, a table \\texttt{scan} saying which tape\neach state reads, and a table \\texttt{transition} from\n\\(\\mathtt{Fin}\\ \\mathtt{stateCount} \\times \\mathtt{Option\\ Bool}\\)---a\ndomain of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) points---into either a halting\nanswer (a \\texttt{Bool}) or a next state with one action. The action type\nis Mathlib's \\texttt{Turing.TM0.Stmt (Option Bool)}: move the head left or\nright, or write one of the three symbols \\texttt{none}, \\texttt{some\nfalse}, \\texttt{some true}, five values in all\n(\\path{Mathlib/Computability/TuringMachine/PostTuringMachine.lean}\nlines 118--120 and \\path{Tape.lean} lines 421--423 at Mathlib\n\\texttt{d13f23b7}, the clone's pinned revision). A configuration\n\\texttt{Cfg} (lines 103--105) is a state, halted or not, and one Mathlib\n\\texttt{Tape} per tape index (a head symbol and two blank-padded halves,\n\\path{Tape.lean} lines 408--414, blank \\(=\\) \\texttt{none}).\n\\texttt{step} (lines 107--119) returns \\texttt{none} in a halted state;\notherwise it reads the head of the one tape the state scans, looks the\npair up in the table, and either halts with the answer, or writes one\nsymbol or moves that one head by one cell and changes state. \\texttt{init}\n(lines 121--124) puts the input on the input tape with the head at its\nfirst bit and every other tape blank (\\texttt{Tape.mk}$_1$,\n\\path{Tape.lean} line 494). A Lean function on a finite domain is,\nwhatever term defines it, a finite table; so a step is one lookup in a\ntable of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) rows, and no field of the\nstructure can carry unbounded computation.\n\n\\paragraph{Traces (lines 87--90).}\n\\texttt{Trace step t a c} is an inductive proposition with two\nconstructors: \\texttt{refl}, a trace of length 0 from \\(a\\) to \\(a\\)\nitself, and \\texttt{cons}, which extends a trace from \\(b\\) by one\nsuccessful step \\(a \\mapsto b\\). So it holds exactly when \\(c\\) is\nreached from \\(a\\) by \\(t\\) successful applications of \\texttt{step}, and\na trace of length 0 to a different configuration is impossible. Since\n\\texttt{step} is a function, the run is deterministic.\n\n\\paragraph{Space monitoring (lines 147--189).}\n\\texttt{LocatedCfg} (lines 149--153) pairs a configuration with, per tape,\nthe head's current position and the least and greatest positions seen so\nfar, as integers; \\texttt{locatedInit} starts every head at 0 with the\ninput tape's range covering the input (lines 155--159);\n\\texttt{headDisplacement} is \\(-1\\), \\(+1\\) or \\(0\\) for a move left, a\nmove right or a write (lines 161--164); \\texttt{monitor} (lines 166--182)\napplies the same table lookup to update the position and the running\nminimum and maximum of the scanned tape, and nothing else;\n\\texttt{locatedStep} is \\texttt{step} on the configuration followed by\n\\texttt{monitor} (lines 184--186). \\texttt{visitedSpace} (lines 188--189)\nis the sum over tapes of \\(\\mathrm{upper} - \\mathrm{lower} + 1\\): the\nnumber of cells whose head position was ever visited. \\texttt{countWord a}\nis the binary expansion of \\(a\\), least significant bit first, with\n\\texttt{[false]} for \\(0\\) (line 147), and \\texttt{toNat} (lines 7--9)\ndecodes such a word.\n\n\\paragraph{The exponent and the bound (lines 130--141).}\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n\\texttt{runtimeScale n = n * (Real.log (Real.log (n + 16)))\\^{}2 /}\\\\\n\\texttt{\\quad Real.sqrt (Real.log (n + 16))}\n\\end{quote}\n(lines 132--134, transcribed; the casts to \\(\\mathbb{R}\\) omitted), that is\n\\[\n  \\rhon \\;=\\; \\frac{n\\,\\bigl(\\ln\\ln(n+16)\\bigr)^2}{\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}},\n\\]\nwith \\texttt{Real.log} the natural logarithm. The manuscript fixes the\nsame base: ``Throughout, log denotes the natural logarithm'' (p.~1, lines\n48--49; again at p.~7, lines 326--327). The shift by 16 keeps\n\\(\\ln\\ln\\) defined and positive at every \\(n \\ge 0\\) (\\(\\ln 16 = 2.77\\)),\nso the theorem can be stated for all \\(n\\) with no proviso. Then\n\\begin{quote}\n\\texttt{runtimeBound C A d L n = C * (L + 1)\\^{}d * 2\\^{}(A * runtimeScale n)}\n\\end{quote}\n(lines 136--139, transcribed), a real number, with the real-exponent\npower. We write \\(\\rhon\\) for \\texttt{runtimeScale n} throughout the\npaper.\n\n\\subsection{The theorem, and the order of its quantifiers}\n\nLines 194--217 state, for every \\(k\\) with \\(2 \\le k\\):\n\\[\n\\small\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\exists\\ \\mathtt{machine},\\ \\mathtt{countTape},\\ \\mathtt{answerTape},\\\n  C, A, S \\in \\mathbb{R},\\ d, e \\in \\mathbb{N},\\\\[2pt]\n\\quad 0 < C \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < A \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < S \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < d \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < e\\ \\wedge\\\\[2pt]\n\\quad \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ \\forall\\, F : \\mathtt{CNF}\\ (\\mathtt{Fin}\\ n),\\\n  \\mathtt{WidthAtMost}\\ k\\ F \\;\\rightarrow\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad \\exists\\ \\mathtt{time} \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ \\mathtt{final} : \\mathtt{LocatedCfg}\\ \\mathtt{machine},\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\mathtt{Trace}\\ (\\mathtt{locatedStep}\\ \\mathtt{machine})\\ \\mathtt{time}\\\n  (\\mathtt{locatedInit}\\ \\mathtt{machine}\\ (\\mathtt{encodeCNF}\\ F))\\ \\mathtt{final}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\text{(lines 201--202)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the count tape holds \\texttt{countWord (count F)}}\n  \\quad\\text{(203--204)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the answer tape holds the bit \\([0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]\\)}\n  \\quad\\text{(205--206)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the halting state carries that bit}\n  \\quad\\text{(207)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\mathtt{toNat}\\ (\\mathtt{countWord}\\ (\\mathtt{count}\\ F)) = \\mathtt{count}\\ F\n  \\quad\\text{(208)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ |\\mathtt{countWord}\\ (\\mathtt{count}\\ F)| \\le n + 1\n  \\quad\\text{(209)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\mathtt{time} \\le C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\,\\rhon},\\ L = \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F\n  \\quad\\text{(210--211)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\forall\\ \\text{prefix of the trace},\\\n  \\mathtt{visitedSpace} \\le S\\,(L+1)^e\n  \\quad\\text{(212--217).}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThree features of the order matter.\n\n\\emph{The machine and the five constants come after \\(k\\) and before\n\\(n\\) and \\(F\\).} They may depend on \\(k\\) and on nothing else; inside\nthe universal quantifier only \\texttt{time} and \\texttt{final} are\nchosen. So for each \\(k\\) one fixed finite machine serves every formula\nof every size, and \\(C, A, S, d, e\\) are the same for all of them: the\nstandard \\(O_k(1)\\) reading. The table's size is \\(O_k(1)\\) in the same\nsense, with no explicit function of \\(k\\).\n\n\\emph{There is no lower bound on \\(n\\).} The manuscript's Theorem~1.1\nand Proposition~6.4 are stated for \\(n \\ge 20\\) (p.~2, lines 56--57;\np.~43, lines 2474--2475), and the solution's auxiliary\n\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} keeps \\(20 \\le n\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 104--109); the exported\ntheorem does not. The \\(+16\\) in \\(\\rhon\\) is what makes that possible.\n\n\\emph{The constants are existential.} The statement asserts that values\nof \\(C, A, S, d, e\\) exist; it names none. The proof obtains them from\nexistential lemmas and never evaluates them: at the point where the\nlarge-input run is assembled, the exponent constant is set to\n\\(A = 256 + A_t\\) with \\(A_t \\ge 1\\) taken from a leaf contract\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 51;\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean}, lines 792--793), and\nthe degree and the polynomial constants are built the same way. So the\nproof as written has \\(A \\ge 257\\), and no closed value of any of the\nfive. The witness machine is explicit in one respect: it is compiled\nfrom a program over 80 one-bit registers and 1,220 tapes, with\n\\(\\mathtt{countTape} = 256\\) and \\(\\mathtt{answerTape} = 1216\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 98--101); its state count is\na finite type's cardinality that nothing computes. Section~\\ref{sec:proof}\nis the inspection of the proof; this section only reads what the\nstatement fixes and what it leaves open.\n\n\\subsection{The exponent, in numbers}\n\nEverything the title says rests on one function. With \\(y = \\ln(n+16)\\),\n\\[\n  \\frac{\\rhon}{n} \\;=\\; \\frac{(\\ln y)^2}{\\sqrt{y}},\n\\]\nwhich rises from \\(0.64\\) at \\(n = 1\\), crosses \\(1\\) between\n\\(n = 49\\) and \\(n = 50\\), peaks at \\(16/e^2 = 2.165\\) where\n\\(\\ln y = 4\\), i.e.\\ \\(y = e^4\\), \\(n = e^{e^4} - 16 \\approx 10^{23.7}\\),\nand returns below \\(1\\) only where \\((\\ln y)^2 = \\sqrt{y}\\) again, at\n\\(y = 5503.7\\), i.e.\\ \\(n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx 10^{2390}\\).\nTable~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale} gives the values and\nFigures~\\ref{fig:central} and \\ref{fig:runtime-scale} draw them; every\nnumber is printed by \\repofile{paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py}\n(run under \\texttt{python3 -I}; it imports nothing from the clone and\ncomputes in \\(y\\), so that no \\(n\\) beyond floating-point range is ever\nformed). The crossing at 50 is a fact about integers:\n\\(\\rho(49)/49 = 0.9994\\) and \\(\\rho(50)/50 = 1.0027\\). In the limit\n\\(\\rhon/n \\to 0\\), since \\((\\ln y)^2/\\sqrt{y} \\to 0\\): the exponent is\n\\(o(n)\\), and that is a theorem in our companion Lean\n\\leanref{rho-little-o}. Both facts are true at once, and the second is\nthe one the title is about: the function is \\(o(n)\\), and it is above\n\\(n\\) on every size anyone will ever write down.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The verified exponent relative to \\(n\\). \\(\\rhon = n(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}\\),\nthe comparator's \\texttt{runtimeScale} (lines 132--134), natural logarithms.\nThe verified bound is \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\); at \\(A = 1\\) its exponential\nfactor is \\(2^{(\\rhon/n)\\cdot n}\\), against \\(2^n\\) for trying every assignment.\nRegenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}\n\\label{tab:runtime-scale}\n\\input{tables/runtime_scale}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n.pdf}\\\\[4pt]\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n_practical.pdf}\n\\caption{The verified exponent against brute force. Top: \\(\\rhon/n\\) over\nthe whole range, the axis in \\(\\log_{10} n\\) from \\(n = 10\\) to\n\\(n = 10^{3000}\\); the dashed level is \\(2^n\\); beneath it the manuscript's two\nsharper exponents, \\(n\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}\\) (Theorem~1.1, general) and\n\\(n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}\\) (fixed clause density), which are not in the Lean\nlibrary (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}). Bottom: the same three curves over the\npractical range \\(n = 10\\) to \\(10^6\\), with \\(\\rhon/n\\) marked at\n\\(n = 50, 125, 10^3, 10^4, 10^6\\). All curves at the constant \\(A = 1\\);\nthe verified \\(A\\) is unknown. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/rho\\_over\\_n.py}.}\n\\label{fig:central}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{runtime_scale.pdf}\n\\caption{\\(\\rhon/n\\) alone, on a logarithmic axis in \\(\\log_{10} n\\) from\n\\(n = 10\\) to \\(n = 10^{2500}\\), with the level \\(1\\) (brute force, \\(2^n\\)),\nthe first integer above the level (\\(n = 50\\)), the peak (\\(16/e^2 = 2.17\\)\nat \\(\\ln(n+16) = e^4\\), \\(n \\approx 10^{23.7}\\)), the return below the level\n(\\(n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx 10^{2390}\\)), and the practical range\n\\(50 \\le n \\le 10^6\\) shaded. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}\n\\label{fig:runtime-scale}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{The title's claim, made precise}\n\nThe title says the verified algorithm is slower than trying all \\(2^n\\)\nassignments at every practical size. Read against the statement, that is\nthe following, and nothing more.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{What is verified is a guarantee, not a running time.} The\n  theorem says the machine halts within \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\) steps.\n  It does not say how many steps the machine takes on any input, and no\n  implementation of the machine exists to be timed. A bound above\n  \\(2^n\\) does not say the machine is slow; it says the proof promises\n  nothing better than \\(2^n\\) there.\n\\item \\textbf{The constants \\(C, A, S, d, e\\) are existential and never\n  computed.} The comparator asserts their existence; the proof builds\n  them from existential lemmas (above, with \\(A \\ge 257\\) as\n  constructed) and states no value. The machine's state count is likewise\n  never computed.\n\\item \\textbf{With \\(A = 1\\), the verified exponent exceeds \\(n\\) for\n  every \\(50 \\le n < e^{5504}\\).} That is Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}:\n  the exponential factor of the bound is at least \\(2^n\\) from 50\n  variables up to about \\(10^{2390}\\), and \\(2^{2.17\\,n}\\) at its\n  worst. With the true \\(A\\), whatever it is, the factor is\n  \\(2^{A\\rhon} \\ge 2^{\\rhon}\\) once \\(A \\ge 1\\), and the proof's own\n  construction has \\(A \\ge 257\\); so nothing is known that would put the\n  bound below \\(2^n\\) at any practical size.\n\\item \\textbf{Hence, at every practical size, the verified guarantee is\n  weaker than the guarantee of trying all assignments.} Trying all \\(2^n\\)\n  assignments and checking each against the \\(m\\) clauses counts the\n  models in \\(2^n \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)\\) steps on any reasonable\n  machine, with constants one can write down. The verified guarantee is\n  \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\) with \\(\\rhon > n\\) on \\(50 \\le n < e^{5504}\\)\n  and \\(C, A, d\\) unknown. For every \\(C \\ge 1\\), \\(A \\ge 1\\) and\n  \\(d \\ge 1\\) the verified bound is above \\(2^n\\) on every \\(n\\) in that\n  range, and the only values on record are the proof's, which has\n  \\(1 \\le C\\) and \\(1 \\le A\\) in the large-input run's own statement\n  (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 12) and\n  \\(A \\ge 257\\) in its construction. A proof with \\(A < 1\\) would be a\n  different proof; none is on record.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nNone of this is a defect of the formalisation or of the proof; it is\nwhat an \\(O_k(1)\\) statement with a \\(2^{o(n)}\\) exponent of this shape\nmeans when the exponent's constants are not tracked. The manuscript\nitself calls \\(\\rhon\\) ``the weaker exponent'' (p.~2, line~63), and its\nsharper exponents are not in the Lean library (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\nOne more detail of base: the solution's internal \\(\\rho\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean}, lines 10--11) is in base 2 and\nis related to \\texttt{runtimeScale} by\n\\(\\mathtt{runtimeScale}\\ n \\le 4\\,\\rho_2(n)\\) for \\(n \\ge 20\\)\n(lines 190--191); the base changes the exponent by a constant factor\nthat \\(A\\) absorbs, so nothing above depends on it.\n\n\\subsection{What the statement does establish}\n\nFor each fixed \\(k \\ge 2\\), a deterministic machine that, on the canonical\nencoding of any \\(k\\)-CNF on \\(n\\) variables with \\(m\\) clauses, halts with\nthe exact number of satisfying assignments in binary on a named tape,\nwithin \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\) steps, where \\(L = O(k\\,n\\,m)\\) is\npolynomial in \\(n + m\\) and \\(\\rhon = o(n)\\); and, because a\n\\texttt{Finset} of clauses of width at most \\(k\\) has at most\n\\(\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j} = O(n^k)\\) members (\\(2{,}604{,}376\\) at\n\\(n = 125\\), \\(k = 3\\)), the whole bound is \\(2^{o(n)}\\) in \\(n\\) alone.\nThat is a deterministic \\(2^{o(n)} \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)\\) algorithm\nfor \\#\\(k\\)-SAT in the standard sense, and therefore for \\(k\\)-SAT; it\nrefutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis \\citep{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001} as\nstated. The statement reads as the manuscript's verified form, the\nweaker exponent of its Proposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476), without\nthe \\(n \\ge 20\\) proviso. We found no field, definition or quantifier\nthrough which the statement could be kernel-true and mean something\nweaker than this; the audit it summarises is\n\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/statement_audit.md}. Our own\nrestatement of the statement as a Lean \\texttt{Prop}, character for\ncharacter over a verbatim copy of the machine model, is\n\\leanref{subexpcount}; it is never proved and never an axiom there\n(Section~\\ref{sec:lean}).\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No running time of any machine.} Nothing here measures or\n  estimates how long the verified machine, or any program, takes on any\n  input. The title compares two \\emph{guarantees}; Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}\n  is the only place in the paper where anything is timed, and it times\n  ordinary solvers, not this machine.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement about the proof.} That the statement means\n  what we say it means is a reading of 220 lines; whether the proof of\n  it is sound is Section~\\ref{sec:proof} and Section~\\ref{sec:replay},\n  which found no flaw. The one proof-side fact quoted here, \\(A = 256 +\n  A_t\\), is a line of the solution read for the value it assigns, not a\n  claim about the argument.\n\\item \\textbf{No value of the constants.} \\(A \\ge 257\\) is what the\n  proof writes down, not what the machine needs; a different proof of\n  the same statement could have any \\(A > 0\\). The statement itself\n  fixes nothing.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the manuscript's sharper exponents}, which\n  are not in the Lean library and are Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}'s subject.\n\\item \\textbf{No claim about sizes beyond \\(e^{5504}\\).} Past\n  \\(n \\approx 10^{2390}\\) the verified factor is below \\(2^n\\) at\n  \\(A = 1\\); we say nothing about that range except that it is not a\n  practical one.\n\\end{itemize}\n","userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PxjxBDNMfFbtkQH2DHfCZd","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
