00 lines are committed.\n\nSo the sentence every later section relies on reads: \\emph{replayed}.\nThe solution builds from source at the pinned Lean version with\nunpatched Mathlib, and the kernel reports the three standard axioms and\nnothing else for the theorem the comparator names. This moves the first\nthree items of the inspection's trust boundary (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}):\nthe build is no longer OpenAI's unpublished one, the axiom output is now\non record, and the Mathlib revision was fetched from upstream and not\npatched.\n\n\\subsection{The second checker}\n\nThe comparator's settings for the family have \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda:\nfalse}: OpenAI's check of this family ran the Lean kernel only. We ran a\nsecond checker, in the same sandbox, after the build\n(\\repofile{paper3/replay/nanoda.sh}, recorded in the README before it\nran). Two tools are involved. \\texttt{lean4export} (tag\n\\texttt{v4.34.0}, commit \\texttt{076e8e57}, built with the pinned\n\\texttt{v4.34.1} Lean so that it reads the compiled files the build\nproduced) wrote out \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} and everything it\ndepends on from the built environment, as a plain-text file:\n2,355,666,449 bytes, 43,633,742 lines, format version 3.1.0, in\n68~seconds. Then \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib} \\citep{Nanoda} (version 0.4.31,\ncommit \\texttt{8de7e0c4}, 2026-10-29), a checker for Lean~4 proofs\nwritten in Rust that shares no code with the Lean kernel, read that\nfile and re-checked every declaration in it, with \\texttt{propext},\n\\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{Quot.sound} as the permitted\naxioms. Its output (\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/nanoda/nanoda_check.txt}):\n\\begin{quote}\n\\texttt{Checked 95176 declarations with no errors}\n\\end{quote}\nin 110~seconds on eight cores, exit code~0, after printing back the\ntheorem's statement and the three axioms from its own reading of the\nfile. The export contains exactly three \\texttt{axiom} declarations\n(\\texttt{grep -c '\"axiom\