vision; it","+\\item The replay trusts the Lean kernel of the pinned version","+  (\\texttt{v4.34.1}) and upstream Mathlib at the pinned revision. It","   moves the proof's trust from ``OpenAI's unpublished build'' to ``a","-  build we ran'', not from the Lean kernel to anything else.","-  The second kernel removes the Lean kernel from the list for the","-  theorem's dependencies as exported, but adds \\texttt{lean4export}","-  (that it exports what the environment holds) and \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib}","-  (that it checks what it parses) to it; two independent checkers","-  agreeing is evidence, not a proof that either is correct.","+  build we ran'', not from the Lean kernel to anything else. The second","+  checker removes the Lean kernel from the list for the theorem's","+  dependencies as exported, but adds \\texttt{lean4export} (that it","+  writes out what the environment holds) and \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib} (that","+  it checks what it reads) to it. Two independent checkers agreeing is","+  evidence, not a proof that either is correct."," \\item It is not the comparator run. The comparator checks that the","-  solution's exported statement matches the challenge's by","-  \\texttt{lean4export}; we did not run that tool chain, and the","-  statement-level match the paper relies on is the textual one of","-  Section~\\ref{sec:proof} (the 35 definitions and the theorem statement,","-  identical character for character after whitespace normalisation, two","-  of them up to the \\texttt{noncomputable} keyword).","+  solution's exported statement matches the challenge's; we did not run","+  that tool chain, and the statement-level match the paper relies on is","+  the textual one of Section~\\ref{sec:proof} (the 35 definitions and the","+  theorem statement, identical after normalising spacing, two of them up","+  to the \\texttt{noncomputable} keyword)."," \\item The counts above are counts of lines in one build's log on one","-  machine with the named versions; a different Lake would print different","-  job totals (the 12,014 includes the cached Mathlib modules), and a","-  different machine a different time.","+  machine with the named versions; a different build tool would print","+  different job totals (the 12,014 includes the cached Mathlib modules),","+  and a different machine a different time."," \\item Nothing here bears on what the theorem \\emph{says}","   (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}) or on the manuscript's unverified","   exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}); a replayed proof of a weak bound"]}],"originalFile":"% Independent replay -- drafted by loop0015 item 01 (2026-11-05).\n% Every number here is read from paper3/replay/logs/ (regenerate: re-run\n% ~/dev/untrusted/replay/run_sandbox.sh as paper3/replay/README.md records;\n% the counts below come from `grep -c` on the copied logs, see\n% paper3/replay/logs/run2/counts.txt).\n\\section{Independent replay}\n\\label{sec:replay}\n\nThe repository holds no recorded \\texttt{\\#print axioms} output for the\nfamily, no continuous integration that builds its Lean, and\n\\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false} in the comparator's JSON\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). We therefore built the solution's import\nclosure ourselves, in isolation, at the toolchain the repository pins, and\nrecorded what the kernel reports. This section says exactly what was run,\nwhere, and what came out; the scripts are quoted verbatim in\n\\repofile{paper3/replay/README.md}, and the logs are under\n\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/}.\n\n\\subsection{What was run, and where}\n\nA fresh copy of the clone (commit \\texttt{4eaee875}) was built inside a\n\\texttt{bwrap} (bubblewrap) sandbox on the project's machine: the system\ndirectories bound read-only, a private \\texttt{tmpfs} home with nothing of\nthe real home or of this repository visible, separate PID, IPC and UTS\nnamespaces, the whole sandbox confined to eight cores\n(\\texttt{taskset -c 0-7}) at the lowest scheduling priority, and the\nnetwork on, because the repository's \\texttt{lakefile} clones its 41\ndependencies at build time and the Mathlib object files come from the\nMathlib cache. Inside, \\texttt{elan} was installed into the private home\nand fetched the pinned toolchain from the repository's own\n\\texttt{lean-toolchain} file; then, in the clone's \\texttt{lean/}\ndirectory, \\texttt{lake exe cache get}, \\texttt{lake build\nOAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main} (the solution's closure, not the\ncollection's default target, which builds all 721 families), and\n\\texttt{\\#print axioms} of the one theorem the comparator names,\n\\texttt{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat},\nthrough \\texttt{lake env lean}. Only text left the sandbox; nothing built\ninside it was executed outside it.\n\nTwo runs were needed. The first, started at 01:03 on 2026-11-05, failed\nat once at the build step because its command line carried \\texttt{-j 8},\nan option this version of Lake does not have (the whole of its build log\nis the one line \\texttt{error: unknown short option '-j'}); nothing of\nthe family was compiled. The second, started at 01:16 with that option\nremoved and the core cap moved to \\texttt{taskset}, is the run reported\nbelow. Both scripts, both versions, and the launch command are in the\nREADME; the first run's logs are kept beside the second's.\n\n\\subsection{What came out}\n\nThe build finished with exit code~0 in 33~minutes of wall clock on\neight cores (01:16:25 to 01:49:31), compiling \\textbf{3,091} modules\nwith the prefix \\texttt{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT}---exactly the\nimport closure the inspection of Section~\\ref{sec:proof} enumerated, the\nsame 3,091 names with an empty set difference both ways\n(\\repofile{paper3/replay/counts.sh}; Lake's own job total,\n\\texttt{12014}, counts the cached Mathlib and dependency modules, none of\nwhich was compiled). The log has no line containing \\texttt{error} and\nnone containing \\texttt{sorry}; its eleven \\texttt{warning} lines are\nLake noting that the lakefile's compatibility patches leave eleven\ndependencies with ``local changes'', and none of the eleven is Mathlib.\nEight source files of the closure themselves carry nineteen\n\\texttt{\\#print axioms} commands (seventeen files of the family's\ndirectory do, but nine of those are never built), so the build log\nrecords nineteen axiom lines as it goes, every one\n\\texttt{[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]}; among them\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 197--201, prints the axioms of\n\\texttt{compiled\\_exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat},\n\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth}, \\texttt{sat\\_polynomial\\_space} and\n\\texttt{original\\_sat\\_main}. The separate \\texttt{\\#print axioms} run\nafter the build gives, for the comparator's theorem,\n\\begin{quote}\n\\texttt{'OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat'\ndepends on axioms: [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]}\n\\end{quote}\n(\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/run2/axioms.txt}). The versions are\n\\texttt{Lean (version 4.34.1, commit 5045d005…, Release)},\n\\texttt{Lake version 5.0.0-src+5045d00}, and Mathlib at\n\\texttt{d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612} from the clone's own\nmanifest, with its object files from the Mathlib cache (8,908 files).\nThe replay copy is at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}, the same as the pristine\nclone, and the comparator file and the solution's files hash identically\nin both (\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/run2/hashes.txt}; the comparator\nstatement's SHA-256 begins \\texttt{4183b205}). The full build log is\nkept out of the repository by size; its SHA-256\n(\\texttt{d313f594…}) and its last 200 lines are committed.\n\nSo the sentence every later section relies on reads: \\emph{replayed}---the\nsolution's closure builds from source at the pinned toolchain with\nupstream Mathlib, and the kernel reports the three standard axioms and\nnothing else for the theorem the comparator names. This moves the first\nthree items of the inspection's trust boundary (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}):\nthe build is no longer OpenAI's unpublished one, the \\texttt{\\#print axioms}\noutput is now on record, and the Mathlib revision was fetched from\nupstream and not patched.\n\n\\subsection{The second kernel}\n\nThe comparator's JSON for the family has \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false}:\nOpenAI's check of this family ran the Lean kernel only. We ran a second\nkernel, in the same sandbox, after the build\n(\\repofile{paper3/replay/nanoda.sh}, recorded in the README before it\nran): \\texttt{lean4export} (tag \\texttt{v4.34.0}, commit\n\\texttt{076e8e57}, built with the pinned \\texttt{v4.34.1} toolchain so\nthat it reads the object files the build produced) exported\n\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} and its transitive dependencies from the\nbuilt environment---2,355,666,449 bytes of NDJSON, 43,633,742 lines,\nformat version 3.1.0, Lean githash \\texttt{5045d005…}, in 68~seconds---and\n\\texttt{nanoda\\_lib} \\citep{Nanoda} (version 0.4.31, commit\n\\texttt{8de7e0c4}, 2026-10-29; an external type checker for Lean~4\nwritten in Rust, which shares no code with the Lean kernel) checked that\nexport with \\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Classical.choice} and\n\\texttt{Quot.sound} as the permitted axioms and the \\texttt{Nat} and\n\\texttt{String} literal extensions on. Its output\n(\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/nanoda/nanoda_check.txt}):\n\\begin{quote}\n\\texttt{Checked 95176 declarations with no errors}\n\\end{quote}\nin 110~seconds on eight cores, exit code~0, after printing back the\ntheorem's statement and the three axioms from its own parse of the\nexport. The export contains exactly three \\texttt{axiom} declarations\n(\\texttt{grep -c '\"axiom\"'} on the file), so nothing else could have been\npermitted. The export file (SHA-256 \\texttt{bdbc9afe…},\n\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/nanoda/export_sha256.txt}) stays in the\nsandbox directory by size; the configuration, the build logs and the\nchecker's output are committed.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item The replay trusts the Lean kernel of the pinned toolchain\n  (\\texttt{v4.34.1}) and upstream Mathlib at the pinned revision; it\n  moves the proof's trust from ``OpenAI's unpublished build'' to ``a\n  build we ran'', not from the Lean kernel to anything else.\n  The second kernel removes the Lean kernel from the list for the\n  theorem's dependencies as exported, but adds \\texttt{lean4export}\n  (that it exports what the environment holds) and \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib}\n  (that it checks what it parses) to it; two independent checkers\n  agreeing is evidence, not a proof that either is correct.\n\\item It is not the comparator run. The comparator checks that the\n  solution's exported statement matches the challenge's by\n  \\texttt{lean4export}; we did not run that tool chain, and the\n  statement-level match the paper relies on is the textual one of\n  Section~\\ref{sec:proof} (the 35 definitions and the theorem statement,\n  identical character for character after whitespace normalisation, two\n  of them up to the \\texttt{noncomputable} keyword).\n\\item The counts above are counts of lines in one build's log on one\n  machine with the named versions; a different Lake would print different\n  job totals (the 12,014 includes the cached Mathlib modules), and a\n  different machine a different time.\n\\item Nothing here bears on what the theorem \\emph{says}\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}) or on the manuscript's unverified\n  exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}); a replayed proof of a weak bound\n  is a replayed proof of a weak bound.\n\\end{itemize}\n","userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Y4JsgYMQRL86smcDk6PTz6","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
