{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgjKf2fsgRBSvxjyD9Pkw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SKfLBaUTtGymW22rhfj1WH","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec6_consequences.tex","content":"% What follows, in general terms -- drafted by loop0015 item 05 (2026-11-05),\n% rewritten in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05).\n% Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the line + 1);\n% every comparator line is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone, read with cat/sed -n\n% only. Every page, line, fragment and the one table of this section are checked\n% and written by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py\n% which exits non-zero if anything has moved. The section is in general terms\n% by the owner's rule (2026-11-05): no example from any width parameter, no\n% problem named beyond what the manuscript's §1.1.1 and §1.1.3 name. The\n% quantifier forms of the hypotheses and their negations are in Appendix B.\n\\section{What follows, in general terms}\n\\label{sec:consequences}\n\nSections~\\ref{sec:statement}--\\ref{sec:replay} read the verified\nstatement, drew the line between it and the manuscript, inspected the\nproof and replayed it. This section asks what follows from the verified\nstatement alone. Two things: which of the standard hardness hypotheses\nit refutes, and by what step; and what a lower bound that was derived\nfrom one of those hypotheses has lost. The manuscript answers both\nquestions itself, in its \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 (p.~4--5) and \\S1.1.3 (p.~5),\nand we quote it. What we add is the exact logical form of each\nhypothesis, the one line of the comparator statement that denies it, and\nthe distinction, stated in general terms, between a theorem whose\npremise has become false and a statement that has been refuted. Nothing\nhere names a problem the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 do not name,\nand nothing here is about any problem's complexity beyond what those two\nsections state.\n\nA \\emph{hypothesis}, in this section, is a conjecture about how hard\nsatisfiability is. The Exponential Time Hypothesis, ETH, says that\n3-SAT needs time exponential in the number of variables. Its strong\nform, SETH, says that as the clause width $k$ grows, no algorithm for\n$k$-SAT does much better than trying all $2^n$ assignments. Several\nvariants exist, and the manuscript lists them. A \\emph{lower bound\nderived from a hypothesis} is a theorem of the form ``if the hypothesis\nholds, then problem $X$ has no fast algorithm''; hundreds of such\ntheorems exist in the literature.\n\n\\subsection{The one property every refutation uses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-property}\n\nEvery refutation below rests on the same parts of the verified theorem\n(comparator file lines 194--217, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). For each\nfixed width $k \\ge 2$ there is one deterministic machine which, given\nany formula of width at most $k$ on $n$ variables, halts with the number\nof satisfying assignments on its count tape (lines 203--204), the\nanswer ``is that number positive'' on its answer tape and in its\nhalting state (lines 205--207), within the bound $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$\n(lines 210--211). Four features of that property do the work.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{It is deterministic.} The step rule is a function; there\n  is no random choice and no guess. A deterministic algorithm is also a\n  randomised algorithm, one that happens never to err, and also a\n  nondeterministic algorithm, one that happens never to guess. So each\n  refutation of a hypothesis about deterministic algorithms is a\n  refutation of its randomised and nondeterministic forms by the same\n  machine.\n\\item \\textbf{The bound is subexponential in $n$ alone.} A formula of\n  width $k$ on $n$ variables has at most about $n^k$ distinct clauses\n  ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$, $k = 3$), so the input length $L$ is at\n  most a polynomial in $n$, and the whole bound is a polynomial in $n$\n  times $2^{A\\rhon}$. Since $\\rhon/n$ goes to zero\n  \\leanref{rho-little-o}, for every rate $\\delta > 0$ there is a size\n  beyond which the bound is below $2^{\\delta n}$; below that size the\n  bound is a constant depending on $k$ and $\\delta$. In the standard\n  notation, the running time is $O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every\n  $\\delta > 0$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses}). This is a fact\n  about the limit, and the hypotheses are statements about the limit.\n  It coexists with Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s observation that\n  $\\rhon > n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, which is about where the\n  limit begins.\n\\item \\textbf{One machine per $k$, for every $n$.} The machine and the\n  constants are chosen after $k$ and before $n$\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there is no $n \\ge 20$ condition in the\n  comparator statement, though the manuscript's Theorem~1.1 carries one\n  (p.~2, lines 56--57). So the verified form is the strong, uniform\n  denial: a single algorithm that runs within $2^{\\delta n}$ times a\n  polynomial for every $\\delta$ at once, where the denial of a\n  hypothesis of the form ``for every $\\delta$ there is an algorithm''\n  would have allowed a different algorithm for each $\\delta$.\n\\item \\textbf{The machine model is the conservative one.} The\n  comparator's machine is a multitape Turing machine that moves one head\n  one cell or writes one symbol per step. Every step of such a machine\n  is a bounded amount of work on any of the standard models in which the\n  hypotheses are stated, so the bound transfers to those models with at\n  most a constant factor per step, which the polynomial absorbs. The\n  input is the manuscript's own encoding, of length polynomial in\n  $n + m$; converting from any other reasonable encoding is polynomial\n  too.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses, one by one}\n\\label{sec:consequences-hypotheses}\n\nThe manuscript's own sentence is (p.~4, lines 187--191):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing\nwhether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability\nand unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for\nevery fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise\nproblem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\nrandomized versions of ETH [DHM$^+$14, Section 1] and SETH [CDL$^+$16,\nDefinition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH.''\n\\end{quote}\nand, for counting (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1)\nsolves \\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time\nand therefore refutes \\#ETH.'' We take each hypothesis in the form the\nmanuscript states it, with the manuscript's own source for that form,\nand say which part of the verified theorem denies it.\nTable~\\ref{tab:hypotheses} is the summary, and\nAppendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses} gives each hypothesis and its denial\nwith the quantifiers written out. In every case the only thing used is\nthe property of Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}; nothing is used\nfrom the manuscript's sharper exponents, which are not verified\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n\n\\paragraph{ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 165--166): ``The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH)\nasserted that 3-SAT requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time in the\nworst case, and hence so does $k$-SAT for every fixed $k \\ge 3$ [IP01,\nTheorem~1],'' citing \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}. In words: there is\nsome fixed rate $\\delta > 0$ such that no deterministic algorithm decides\n3-SAT within $2^{\\delta n}$ times a polynomial, on all inputs. Its\ndenial: for every rate $\\delta > 0$ there is such an algorithm. The\nverified statement at $k = 3$ supplies, for every $\\delta$, the same\nmachine, whose answer bit is the decision, within that time (item 2\nabove). This holds on every input of every size, so it refutes the\nhypothesis whether ``requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ time'' is read as a lower\nbound at infinitely many sizes or at all large sizes. In the vocabulary\nof \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, where $s_k$ is the best possible rate\nfor $k$-SAT and ETH is $s_3 > 0$: the verified statement gives $s_k = 0$\nfor every $k \\ge 2$, by one algorithm per $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{SETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 166--169): ``The Strong Exponential Time\nHypothesis (SETH) made the sharper prediction that $k$-SAT requires\n$2^{n(1-o_k(1))}$ time as $k$ grows. Precisely, for every\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$, there is a fixed $k$ for which no deterministic\nalgorithm solves $k$-SAT in $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ time,''\nwith the motivation attributed to \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}'s open\nproblems (lines 169--171). In words: however small a saving\n$\\varepsilon$ one asks for over trying all $2^n$ assignments, some clause\nwidth $k$ makes that saving impossible. Its denial: there is a saving\n$\\varepsilon$ that every fixed width $k$ allows. The verified statement\ngives more: for \\emph{every} saving $\\varepsilon$ and every $k \\ge 2$,\nthe machine for $k$ runs within $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}$ times a\npolynomial for all large $n$, because $\\rhon$ grows more slowly than\n$n$; below that size the exponential factor is a constant. The\npolynomial's degree depends on $k$, and that is exactly what the\nhypothesis's ``for a fixed $k$'' permits. The refutation does not pass\nthrough ETH; it reads the statement at each $k$. It is also standard\nthat SETH implies ETH, through the Sparsification Lemma of\n\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001} (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses});\nso the refutation of ETH alone would already refute SETH. We note the\nimplication for the record; the direct reading above is what the paper\nrests on.\n\n\\paragraph{Gap-ETH, and its randomised form.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 172--174): ``The Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis\n(Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants $\\delta, \\gamma > 0$\nfor which no $O(2^{\\gamma n})$-time algorithm can distinguish a\nsatisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment violates at least a\n$\\delta$ fraction of the clauses, even when $m = O(n)$ [Din16, MR17],''\nciting \\citet{Dinur2016} and \\citet{ManurangsiRaghavendra2017}; and\n(lines 175--176) ``The randomized version rules out an algorithm with\nthe same running time that distinguishes the two cases with probability\nat least $2/3$ on every promised input [CCK$^+$20, Conjecture 2.5],''\nciting \\citet{Chalermsook2020}. In words: it should be hard even to tell\na satisfiable formula from one that is far from satisfiable. Its\ndenial: for every rate $\\gamma$ there is an algorithm within\n$O(2^{\\gamma n})$ that tells the two apart, already when the number of\nclauses is proportional to $n$. Exact decision tells them apart, since a\nformula in which every assignment violates some clauses is\nunsatisfiable; and the verified machine at $k = 3$ decides within\n$O(2^{\\gamma n})$ for every $\\gamma$, at every number of clauses (item\n2). The promise is never used: the machine answers correctly on every\ninput, promised or not, with probability $1$, which refutes the\nrandomised form by item 1.\n\n\\paragraph{NSETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 181--183): ``The Nondeterministic Strong\nExponential Time Hypothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every\n$\\varepsilon > 0$, some fixed $k$ admits no nondeterministic\n$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}\\allowbreak(n+m)^{O(1)}$-time algorithm for deciding whether a\n$k$-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a $k$-DNF is a\ntautology [CGI$^+$16],'' citing \\citet{Carmosino2016}. In words: even an\nalgorithm allowed to guess should not be able to certify\nunsatisfiability with a saving over $2^n$. Its denial: there is a saving\nthat every fixed $k$ allows, for a guessing algorithm. The verified\nmachine's answer bit is \\texttt{false} exactly when the count is zero\n(lines 205--207), so it decides unsatisfiability deterministically within\nthe SETH bound of the previous paragraph for every $\\varepsilon$ and\nevery $k$; and a deterministic machine is a guessing machine that never\nguesses, so every convention for nondeterministic time is met. The\nmanuscript's ``decides both satisfiability and unsatisfiability'' (line\n188) is this.\n\n\\paragraph{\\#ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 204--205): ``The counting Exponential Time\nHypothesis (\\#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying\nassignments of a 3-CNF requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time\n[DHM$^+$14, Section 1],'' citing \\citet{Dell2014}. Its denial: for every\nrate $\\delta > 0$ there is a deterministic algorithm within\n$O(2^{\\delta n})$ that outputs the exact count. The verified machine at\n$k = 3$ halts with the count on its count tape, a binary number of at\nmost $n + 1$ bits (lines 203--204, 208--209), within the same bound. This\nis the one hypothesis for which the yes-or-no bit is not enough and the\ncount tape is what refutes it. It is also the hypothesis the comparator\nstatement was written for: the count is the theorem's first conclusion\nand the decision its corollary.\n\n\\paragraph{The bounded-error randomised ETH and SETH.}\nThe manuscript names them by citation only (p.~4, lines 190--191):\n``[DHM$^+$14, Section 1]'' for ETH \\citep{Dell2014} and ``[CDL$^+$16,\nDefinition 2.1]'' for SETH \\citep{Cygan2016}. In each, the algorithm\nmay toss coins and must be right with probability clearly above one\nhalf on every input. A deterministic algorithm is such an algorithm\nthat is right with probability $1$ (item 1), so the refutations of ETH\nand SETH above refute these forms by the same machine, with no change\nto the bound. We have not re-read the two sources' definitions; we\nstate the forms as the manuscript cites them, and a reader who needs the\nexact error threshold or the exact model of the source should go to the\ncited section and definition.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\caption{The hypotheses the manuscript's \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 list, each in\nthe form the manuscript states it (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of\nthe committed extract), with the manuscript's own source for that form,\nthe logical form in symbols, and the part of the verified theorem that\ndenies it (lines 194--217 of the comparator file at \\texttt{4eaee875}).\n$A$, $C_k$, $d_k$ are the never-computed constants of\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement}. Generated, with every page and fragment\nchecked against the extract and the clone, by\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py}.}\n\\label{tab:hypotheses}\n\\scriptsize\n\\input{tables/hypotheses}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{What the manuscript places beside the list.}\nTwo sentences follow the refutation paragraph (p.~4, lines 191--194):\nthe result ``does not conflict with unconditional exponential lower\nbounds for restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13],''\nand ``In 2016, Ryan Williams refuted the Merlin--Arthur version of SETH\n(MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies $k$-CNF unsatisfiability\nin $2^{n/2}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3]''\n\\citep{Williams2016}. Both are context. The first says that a lower\nbound proved outright for one restricted way of writing proofs is not a\nstatement about algorithms in general and is not touched. The second\nsays that one member of the family was already false before this\nresult. Neither is used below.\n\n\\subsection{What a derived lower bound loses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-lower-bounds}\n\nThe manuscript's \\S1.1.3 opens with a list of lower bounds that were\nproved from ETH or from SETH (p.~5, lines 213--225), for problems with\nexponential algorithms and for problems with polynomial ones, and then\nsays what its result does to them (lines 226--227):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness\nclaims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from weaker\nhypotheses that may still hold.''\n\\end{quote}\nWe say what that means in general terms, for a theorem of either of the\ntwo standard shapes,\n\\begin{align*}\n  \\text{(E)}&\\quad \\text{ETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } 2^{o(n)} \\text{ algorithm for } X,\\\\\n  \\text{(S)}&\\quad \\text{SETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } (c - \\varepsilon)^{k} \\text{ algorithm for } Y \\text{ for any } \\varepsilon > 0,\n\\end{align*}\nwhere $n$ and $k$ are whatever measure of the input the theorem names\nand $c$ is its constant. In words: (E) says that if ETH holds, problem\n$X$ has no subexponential algorithm; (S) says that if SETH holds, no\nalgorithm for problem $Y$ beats the base $c$. Nothing below depends on\nwhat $X$ and $Y$ are.\n\n\\paragraph{The theorem stands; its premise is false; it derives nothing.}\nA theorem of shape (E) or (S) is an ``if \\ldots{} then''. It is proved\nby a reduction: a recipe that turns a fast algorithm for $X$ or $Y$ into\na fast algorithm for $k$-SAT. The recipe is unaffected by anything in\nthe verified statement; the ``if \\ldots{} then'' remains a theorem. What\nhas changed is that its ``if'' is now known to be false\n(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-hypotheses}), so the theorem can no\nlonger be applied: it derives nothing about $X$ or $Y$. That is the\nwhole of what ``removes one basis'' means.\n\n\\paragraph{The conclusion is not refuted.}\nA false ``if'' does not make the ``then'' false. The statement ``no\n$2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' and the statement ``no\n$(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$'' are untouched by anything in the\ncomparator statement, which speaks about $k$-CNF formulas and nothing\nelse. To refute either, one would need an algorithm for $X$ or $Y$, and\nthe verified statement supplies one only through a reduction in the\nopposite direction to the one the lower bound used. The manuscript says\nexactly this (p.~5, lines 237--238): ``To obtain a faster algorithm for\na target problem from our SAT algorithm, we need a reduction in the\nother direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT, that controls the\nnumber of variables.'' And it places the general caveat on itself at\np.~2, lines 95--99:\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Note that runtime bounds of the form $2^{o(n)}$ do not transfer under\ngeneral polynomial-time reductions ($2^{n^{o(1)}}$ would be needed for\nthis), so our result does not imply that general NP problems admit\nsubexponential time algorithms.''\n\\end{quote}\nSo for shape (E): a subexponential algorithm for $X$ follows only from a\nreduction of $X$ to fixed-width SAT in which the number of SAT variables\nis proportional to $X$'s own size measure, and the manuscript gives one\nsuch transfer (below). For shape (S): an algorithm for $Y$ with base\nbelow $c$ would need the SAT instance's exponent to be below $k \\log_2 c$\nby a constant factor, and the verified statement's exponent is $A$ times\n$\\rho$ of the SAT instance's variable count, with $A$ unknown and no\nvalue below $257$ on record (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses}). No\nstatement of shape ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is refuted by\nanything in this paper or, so far as we can read, in the manuscript.\n\n\\paragraph{Hardness as a classification.}\nA statement of the form ``$Y$ is SETH-hard'' (or ETH-hard) records that\na reduction exists from $k$-SAT to $Y$ with the stated control of the\nsize measure. The reduction still exists, and the statement is still\ntrue as a statement about reductions. What it used to convey, that a\nfast algorithm for $Y$ is unlikely because it would refute SETH, it no\nlonger conveys: a fast algorithm for $Y$ would refute SETH, and SETH is\nrefuted. The classification is now a fact about reductions and no\nlonger a fact about $Y$'s running time.\n\n\\paragraph{The weaker hypotheses the manuscript names are untouched.}\nThe manuscript gives two (p.~5, lines 229--236). The first is the\nOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture, which it states and then relates to SETH:\n``The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal Vectors shows that SETH implies\nOVC [Wil05, VW19]'' (lines 231--232). That implication is a theorem\nwith a false premise, like (E) and (S): it says nothing about OVC either\nway, and nothing in the verified statement is about orthogonal vectors.\nThe second is the form of SETH for formulas of unrestricted width, which\nwe quote with the manuscript's list of the problems it covers elided\n(lines 233--236):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``The lower bounds for [\\ldots] also follow from the hypothesis that\nunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$-time\nalgorithm for any constant $\\delta > 0$ [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem\n1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each fixed clause width $k$,\nwhile this hypothesis allows the width to grow with $n$.''\n\\end{quote}\nThe verified statement cannot reach this hypothesis, and the reason is\nvisible in the order of its words (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}): the\nmachine and the constants $C, A, S, d, e$ are chosen after $k$, and\nnothing in the statement bounds how they grow with $k$. For a formula\nwhose width grows with $n$ there is no single machine and no single\nbound in the statement at all; even the trivial observation that every\n$k$-CNF is a CNF runs the wrong way, since the hypothesis speaks about\nall widths at once. So the unrestricted-width hypothesis, and every\nlower bound that follows from it, is exactly where it was.\n\n\\paragraph{What a transferred algorithm inherits.}\nThe manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of\nsize at most $k$ (p.~5, lines 239--243): one variable per element and\ntwo clauses per set give a $k$-CNF with ``one variable per universe\nelement, so for fixed $k$ Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential time in the\nuniverse size, up to a polynomial input-size factor,'' citing\n\\citet{Cygan2016}'s Observation~3.6. Section~\\ref{sec:beyond} tags this\nsupported: the encoding is on paper, the counter is the verified one.\nWhat such a transfer inherits is the verified guarantee on the SAT\ninstance it builds, $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rho(n')}$ with $n'$ the number of\nSAT variables, and therefore the title's limitation with it: at $A = 1$\nthe exponential factor is above $2^{n'}$ for every\n$50 \\le n' < e^{5504}$ (Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}), with $C, A, d$\nunknown. In general terms: an algorithm obtained from the verified\nstatement through a variable-controlling reduction is a subexponential\nalgorithm whose verified guarantee is weaker, at every practical size,\nthan trying every assignment of the SAT instance it constructs. That is\nnot a defect of the transfer; it is the same sentence as the title,\nread through a reduction.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:consequences-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is\n  refuted}, for any problem, any size measure or any constant $c$. Such\n  a statement, proved from SETH, has lost its proof and kept its truth\n  value, which is unknown.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' is\n  refuted} either, unless $X$ has a reduction to fixed-width SAT that\n  controls the number of variables; the manuscript names one such\n  problem (Set Splitting) and we add none.\n\\item \\textbf{The unrestricted-width hypothesis is untouched}, and so is\n  the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, and so is every lower bound proved\n  from either; the verified statement is for each fixed $k$ and says\n  nothing about widths that grow with $n$.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about any specific problem's complexity} beyond\n  what the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 state and we quote. The\n  problems the manuscript lists at lines 213--225 and 233--235 are not\n  discussed here, and no example from any other setting is given, by\n  the scope of this paper.\n\\item \\textbf{No re-reading of the sources.} Each hypothesis is stated\n  in the form the manuscript gives it, with the manuscript's citation;\n  we did not check that the cited sections and definitions state it in\n  the same words. If a source's form is stronger than the manuscript's,\n  the refutation of the manuscript's form may not reach it, and the\n  reader should check the source.\n\\item \\textbf{About the limit only.} Every refutation here denies a\n  statement about the limit and uses only that $\\rhon$ grows more\n  slowly than $n$. None says that any machine decides or counts\n  anything fast at any size; the constants are as unknown here as in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, and the verified bound is above $2^n$ on\n  every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the verified statement.} The manuscript's\n  sharper exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) are not used; where the\n  manuscript derives a consequence from them, it is not a consequence\n  of what is verified and is not claimed here.\n\\end{itemize}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3832,"cache_read_input_tokens":224111,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3832,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"3702ae27-6cf8-4365-b625-03ef3194afee","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:41:03.359Z","request_id":"req_011CgjKf1whwaJqwDehYJVoA","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01SKfLBaUTtGymW22rhfj1WH":{"file_path":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec6_consequences.tex","content":"% What follows, in general terms -- drafted by loop0015 item 05 (2026-11-05),\n% rewritten in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05).\n% Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the line + 1);\n% every comparator line is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone, read with cat/sed -n\n% only. Every page, line, fragment and the one table of this section are checked\n% and written by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py\n% which exits non-zero if anything has moved. The section is in general terms\n% by the owner's rule (2026-11-05): no example from any width parameter, no\n% problem named beyond what the manuscript's §1.1.1 and §1.1.3 name. The\n% quantifier forms of the hypotheses and their negations are in Appendix B.\n\\section{What follows, in general terms}\n\\label{sec:consequences}\n\nSections~\\ref{sec:statement}--\\ref{sec:replay} read the verified\nstatement, drew the line between it and the manuscript, inspected the\nproof and replayed it. This section asks what follows from the verified\nstatement alone. Two things: which of the standard hardness hypotheses\nit refutes, and by what step; and what a lower bound that was derived\nfrom one of those hypotheses has lost. The manuscript answers both\nquestions itself, in its \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 (p.~4--5) and \\S1.1.3 (p.~5),\nand we quote it. What we add is the exact logical form of each\nhypothesis, the one line of the comparator statement that denies it, and\nthe distinction, stated in general terms, between a theorem whose\npremise has become false and a statement that has been refuted. Nothing\nhere names a problem the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 do not name,\nand nothing here is about any problem's complexity beyond what those two\nsections state.\n\nA \\emph{hypothesis}, in this section, is a conjecture about how hard\nsatisfiability is. The Exponential Time Hypothesis, ETH, says that\n3-SAT needs time exponential in the number of variables. Its strong\nform, SETH, says that as the clause width $k$ grows, no algorithm for\n$k$-SAT does much better than trying all $2^n$ assignments. Several\nvariants exist, and the manuscript lists them. A \\emph{lower bound\nderived from a hypothesis} is a theorem of the form ``if the hypothesis\nholds, then problem $X$ has no fast algorithm''; hundreds of such\ntheorems exist in the literature.\n\n\\subsection{The one property every refutation uses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-property}\n\nEvery refutation below rests on the same parts of the verified theorem\n(comparator file lines 194--217, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). For each\nfixed width $k \\ge 2$ there is one deterministic machine which, given\nany formula of width at most $k$ on $n$ variables, halts with the number\nof satisfying assignments on its count tape (lines 203--204), the\nanswer ``is that number positive'' on its answer tape and in its\nhalting state (lines 205--207), within the bound $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$\n(lines 210--211). Four features of that property do the work.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{It is deterministic.} The step rule is a function; there\n  is no random choice and no guess. A deterministic algorithm is also a\n  randomised algorithm, one that happens never to err, and also a\n  nondeterministic algorithm, one that happens never to guess. So each\n  refutation of a hypothesis about deterministic algorithms is a\n  refutation of its randomised and nondeterministic forms by the same\n  machine.\n\\item \\textbf{The bound is subexponential in $n$ alone.} A formula of\n  width $k$ on $n$ variables has at most about $n^k$ distinct clauses\n  ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$, $k = 3$), so the input length $L$ is at\n  most a polynomial in $n$, and the whole bound is a polynomial in $n$\n  times $2^{A\\rhon}$. Since $\\rhon/n$ goes to zero\n  \\leanref{rho-little-o}, for every rate $\\delta > 0$ there is a size\n  beyond which the bound is below $2^{\\delta n}$; below that size the\n  bound is a constant depending on $k$ and $\\delta$. In the standard\n  notation, the running time is $O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every\n  $\\delta > 0$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses}). This is a fact\n  about the limit, and the hypotheses are statements about the limit.\n  It coexists with Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s observation that\n  $\\rhon > n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, which is about where the\n  limit begins.\n\\item \\textbf{One machine per $k$, for every $n$.} The machine and the\n  constants are chosen after $k$ and before $n$\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there is no $n \\ge 20$ condition in the\n  comparator statement, though the manuscript's Theorem~1.1 carries one\n  (p.~2, lines 56--57). So the verified form is the strong, uniform\n  denial: a single algorithm that runs within $2^{\\delta n}$ times a\n  polynomial for every $\\delta$ at once, where the denial of a\n  hypothesis of the form ``for every $\\delta$ there is an algorithm''\n  would have allowed a different algorithm for each $\\delta$.\n\\item \\textbf{The machine model is the conservative one.} The\n  comparator's machine is a multitape Turing machine that moves one head\n  one cell or writes one symbol per step. Every step of such a machine\n  is a bounded amount of work on any of the standard models in which the\n  hypotheses are stated, so the bound transfers to those models with at\n  most a constant factor per step, which the polynomial absorbs. The\n  input is the manuscript's own encoding, of length polynomial in\n  $n + m$; converting from any other reasonable encoding is polynomial\n  too.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses, one by one}\n\\label{sec:consequences-hypotheses}\n\nThe manuscript's own sentence is (p.~4, lines 187--191):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing\nwhether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability\nand unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for\nevery fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise\nproblem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\nrandomized versions of ETH [DHM$^+$14, Section 1] and SETH [CDL$^+$16,\nDefinition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH.''\n\\end{quote}\nand, for counting (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1)\nsolves \\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time\nand therefore refutes \\#ETH.'' We take each hypothesis in the form the\nmanuscript states it, with the manuscript's own source for that form,\nand say which part of the verified theorem denies it.\nTable~\\ref{tab:hypotheses} is the summary, and\nAppendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses} gives each hypothesis and its denial\nwith the quantifiers written out. In every case the only thing used is\nthe property of Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}; nothing is used\nfrom the manuscript's sharper exponents, which are not verified\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n\n\\paragraph{ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 165--166): ``The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH)\nasserted that 3-SAT requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time in the\nworst case, and hence so does $k$-SAT for every fixed $k \\ge 3$ [IP01,\nTheorem~1],'' citing \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}. In words: there is\nsome fixed rate $\\delta > 0$ such that no deterministic algorithm decides\n3-SAT within $2^{\\delta n}$ times a polynomial, on all inputs. Its\ndenial: for every rate $\\delta > 0$ there is such an algorithm. The\nverified statement at $k = 3$ supplies, for every $\\delta$, the same\nmachine, whose answer bit is the decision, within that time (item 2\nabove). This holds on every input of every size, so it refutes the\nhypothesis whether ``requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ time'' is read as a lower\nbound at infinitely many sizes or at all large sizes. In the vocabulary\nof \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, where $s_k$ is the best possible rate\nfor $k$-SAT and ETH is $s_3 > 0$: the verified statement gives $s_k = 0$\nfor every $k \\ge 2$, by one algorithm per $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{SETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 166--169): ``The Strong Exponential Time\nHypothesis (SETH) made the sharper prediction that $k$-SAT requires\n$2^{n(1-o_k(1))}$ time as $k$ grows. Precisely, for every\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$, there is a fixed $k$ for which no deterministic\nalgorithm solves $k$-SAT in $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ time,''\nwith the motivation attributed to \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}'s open\nproblems (lines 169--171). In words: however small a saving\n$\\varepsilon$ one asks for over trying all $2^n$ assignments, some clause\nwidth $k$ makes that saving impossible. Its denial: there is a saving\n$\\varepsilon$ that every fixed width $k$ allows. The verified statement\ngives more: for \\emph{every} saving $\\varepsilon$ and every $k \\ge 2$,\nthe machine for $k$ runs within $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}$ times a\npolynomial for all large $n$, because $\\rhon$ grows more slowly than\n$n$; below that size the exponential factor is a constant. The\npolynomial's degree depends on $k$, and that is exactly what the\nhypothesis's ``for a fixed $k$'' permits. The refutation does not pass\nthrough ETH; it reads the statement at each $k$. It is also standard\nthat SETH implies ETH, through the Sparsification Lemma of\n\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001} (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses});\nso the refutation of ETH alone would already refute SETH. We note the\nimplication for the record; the direct reading above is what the paper\nrests on.\n\n\\paragraph{Gap-ETH, and its randomised form.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 172--174): ``The Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis\n(Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants $\\delta, \\gamma > 0$\nfor which no $O(2^{\\gamma n})$-time algorithm can distinguish a\nsatisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment violates at least a\n$\\delta$ fraction of the clauses, even when $m = O(n)$ [Din16, MR17],''\nciting \\citet{Dinur2016} and \\citet{ManurangsiRaghavendra2017}; and\n(lines 175--176) ``The randomized version rules out an algorithm with\nthe same running time that distinguishes the two cases with probability\nat least $2/3$ on every promised input [CCK$^+$20, Conjecture 2.5],''\nciting \\citet{Chalermsook2020}. In words: it should be hard even to tell\na satisfiable formula from one that is far from satisfiable. Its\ndenial: for every rate $\\gamma$ there is an algorithm within\n$O(2^{\\gamma n})$ that tells the two apart, already when the number of\nclauses is proportional to $n$. Exact decision tells them apart, since a\nformula in which every assignment violates some clauses is\nunsatisfiable; and the verified machine at $k = 3$ decides within\n$O(2^{\\gamma n})$ for every $\\gamma$, at every number of clauses (item\n2). The promise is never used: the machine answers correctly on every\ninput, promised or not, with probability $1$, which refutes the\nrandomised form by item 1.\n\n\\paragraph{NSETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 181--183): ``The Nondeterministic Strong\nExponential Time Hypothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every\n$\\varepsilon > 0$, some fixed $k$ admits no nondeterministic\n$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}\\allowbreak(n+m)^{O(1)}$-time algorithm for deciding whether a\n$k$-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a $k$-DNF is a\ntautology [CGI$^+$16],'' citing \\citet{Carmosino2016}. In words: even an\nalgorithm allowed to guess should not be able to certify\nunsatisfiability with a saving over $2^n$. Its denial: there is a saving\nthat every fixed $k$ allows, for a guessing algorithm. The verified\nmachine's answer bit is \\texttt{false} exactly when the count is zero\n(lines 205--207), so it decides unsatisfiability deterministically within\nthe SETH bound of the previous paragraph for every $\\varepsilon$ and\nevery $k$; and a deterministic machine is a guessing machine that never\nguesses, so every convention for nondeterministic time is met. The\nmanuscript's ``decides both satisfiability and unsatisfiability'' (line\n188) is this.\n\n\\paragraph{\\#ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 204--205): ``The counting Exponential Time\nHypothesis (\\#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying\nassignments of a 3-CNF requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time\n[DHM$^+$14, Section 1],'' citing \\citet{Dell2014}. Its denial: for every\nrate $\\delta > 0$ there is a deterministic algorithm within\n$O(2^{\\delta n})$ that outputs the exact count. The verified machine at\n$k = 3$ halts with the count on its count tape, a binary number of at\nmost $n + 1$ bits (lines 203--204, 208--209), within the same bound. This\nis the one hypothesis for which the yes-or-no bit is not enough and the\ncount tape is what refutes it. It is also the hypothesis the comparator\nstatement was written for: the count is the theorem's first conclusion\nand the decision its corollary.\n\n\\paragraph{The bounded-error randomised ETH and SETH.}\nThe manuscript names them by citation only (p.~4, lines 190--191):\n``[DHM$^+$14, Section 1]'' for ETH \\citep{Dell2014} and ``[CDL$^+$16,\nDefinition 2.1]'' for SETH \\citep{Cygan2016}. In each, the algorithm\nmay toss coins and must be right with probability clearly above one\nhalf on every input. A deterministic algorithm is such an algorithm\nthat is right with probability $1$ (item 1), so the refutations of ETH\nand SETH above refute these forms by the same machine, with no change\nto the bound. We have not re-read the two sources' definitions; we\nstate the forms as the manuscript cites them, and a reader who needs the\nexact error threshold or the exact model of the source should go to the\ncited section and definition.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\caption{The hypotheses the manuscript's \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 list, each in\nthe form the manuscript states it (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of\nthe committed extract), with the manuscript's own source for that form,\nthe logical form in symbols, and the part of the verified theorem that\ndenies it (lines 194--217 of the comparator file at \\texttt{4eaee875}).\n$A$, $C_k$, $d_k$ are the never-computed constants of\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement}. Generated, with every page and fragment\nchecked against the extract and the clone, by\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py}.}\n\\label{tab:hypotheses}\n\\scriptsize\n\\input{tables/hypotheses}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{What the manuscript places beside the list.}\nTwo sentences follow the refutation paragraph (p.~4, lines 191--194):\nthe result ``does not conflict with unconditional exponential lower\nbounds for restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13],''\nand ``In 2016, Ryan Williams refuted the Merlin--Arthur version of SETH\n(MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies $k$-CNF unsatisfiability\nin $2^{n/2}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3]''\n\\citep{Williams2016}. Both are context. The first says that a lower\nbound proved outright for one restricted way of writing proofs is not a\nstatement about algorithms in general and is not touched. The second\nsays that one member of the family was already false before this\nresult. Neither is used below.\n\n\\subsection{What a derived lower bound loses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-lower-bounds}\n\nThe manuscript's \\S1.1.3 opens with a list of lower bounds that were\nproved from ETH or from SETH (p.~5, lines 213--225), for problems with\nexponential algorithms and for problems with polynomial ones, and then\nsays what its result does to them (lines 226--227):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness\nclaims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from weaker\nhypotheses that may still hold.''\n\\end{quote}\nWe say what that means in general terms, for a theorem of either of the\ntwo standard shapes,\n\\begin{align*}\n  \\text{(E)}&\\quad \\text{ETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } 2^{o(n)} \\text{ algorithm for } X,\\\\\n  \\text{(S)}&\\quad \\text{SETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } (c - \\varepsilon)^{k} \\text{ algorithm for } Y \\text{ for any } \\varepsilon > 0,\n\\end{align*}\nwhere $n$ and $k$ are whatever measure of the input the theorem names\nand $c$ is its constant. In words: (E) says that if ETH holds, problem\n$X$ has no subexponential algorithm; (S) says that if SETH holds, no\nalgorithm for problem $Y$ beats the base $c$. Nothing below depends on\nwhat $X$ and $Y$ are.\n\n\\paragraph{The theorem stands; its premise is false; it derives nothing.}\nA theorem of shape (E) or (S) is an ``if \\ldots{} then''. It is proved\nby a reduction: a recipe that turns a fast algorithm for $X$ or $Y$ into\na fast algorithm for $k$-SAT. The recipe is unaffected by anything in\nthe verified statement; the ``if \\ldots{} then'' remains a theorem. What\nhas changed is that its ``if'' is now known to be false\n(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-hypotheses}), so the theorem can no\nlonger be applied: it derives nothing about $X$ or $Y$. That is the\nwhole of what ``removes one basis'' means.\n\n\\paragraph{The conclusion is not refuted.}\nA false ``if'' does not make the ``then'' false. The statement ``no\n$2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' and the statement ``no\n$(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$'' are untouched by anything in the\ncomparator statement, which speaks about $k$-CNF formulas and nothing\nelse. To refute either, one would need an algorithm for $X$ or $Y$, and\nthe verified statement supplies one only through a reduction in the\nopposite direction to the one the lower bound used. The manuscript says\nexactly this (p.~5, lines 237--238): ``To obtain a faster algorithm for\na target problem from our SAT algorithm, we need a reduction in the\nother direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT, that controls the\nnumber of variables.'' And it places the general caveat on itself at\np.~2, lines 95--99:\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Note that runtime bounds of the form $2^{o(n)}$ do not transfer under\ngeneral polynomial-time reductions ($2^{n^{o(1)}}$ would be needed for\nthis), so our result does not imply that general NP problems admit\nsubexponential time algorithms.''\n\\end{quote}\nSo for shape (E): a subexponential algorithm for $X$ follows only from a\nreduction of $X$ to fixed-width SAT in which the number of SAT variables\nis proportional to $X$'s own size measure, and the manuscript gives one\nsuch transfer (below). For shape (S): an algorithm for $Y$ with base\nbelow $c$ would need the SAT instance's exponent to be below $k \\log_2 c$\nby a constant factor, and the verified statement's exponent is $A$ times\n$\\rho$ of the SAT instance's variable count, with $A$ unknown and no\nvalue below $257$ on record (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses}). No\nstatement of shape ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is refuted by\nanything in this paper or, so far as we can read, in the manuscript.\n\n\\paragraph{Hardness as a classification.}\nA statement of the form ``$Y$ is SETH-hard'' (or ETH-hard) records that\na reduction exists from $k$-SAT to $Y$ with the stated control of the\nsize measure. The reduction still exists, and the statement is still\ntrue as a statement about reductions. What it used to convey, that a\nfast algorithm for $Y$ is unlikely because it would refute SETH, it no\nlonger conveys: a fast algorithm for $Y$ would refute SETH, and SETH is\nrefuted. The classification is now a fact about reductions and no\nlonger a fact about $Y$'s running time.\n\n\\paragraph{The weaker hypotheses the manuscript names are untouched.}\nThe manuscript gives two (p.~5, lines 229--236). The first is the\nOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture, which it states and then relates to SETH:\n``The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal Vectors shows that SETH implies\nOVC [Wil05, VW19]'' (lines 231--232). That implication is a theorem\nwith a false premise, like (E) and (S): it says nothing about OVC either\nway, and nothing in the verified statement is about orthogonal vectors.\nThe second is the form of SETH for formulas of unrestricted width, which\nwe quote with the manuscript's list of the problems it covers elided\n(lines 233--236):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``The lower bounds for [\\ldots] also follow from the hypothesis that\nunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$-time\nalgorithm for any constant $\\delta > 0$ [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem\n1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each fixed clause width $k$,\nwhile this hypothesis allows the width to grow with $n$.''\n\\end{quote}\nThe verified statement cannot reach this hypothesis, and the reason is\nvisible in the order of its words (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}): the\nmachine and the constants $C, A, S, d, e$ are chosen after $k$, and\nnothing in the statement bounds how they grow with $k$. For a formula\nwhose width grows with $n$ there is no single machine and no single\nbound in the statement at all; even the trivial observation that every\n$k$-CNF is a CNF runs the wrong way, since the hypothesis speaks about\nall widths at once. So the unrestricted-width hypothesis, and every\nlower bound that follows from it, is exactly where it was.\n\n\\paragraph{What a transferred algorithm inherits.}\nThe manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of\nsize at most $k$ (p.~5, lines 239--243): one variable per element and\ntwo clauses per set give a $k$-CNF with ``one variable per universe\nelement, so for fixed $k$ Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential time in the\nuniverse size, up to a polynomial input-size factor,'' citing\n\\citet{Cygan2016}'s Observation~3.6. Section~\\ref{sec:beyond} tags this\nsupported: the encoding is on paper, the counter is the verified one.\nWhat such a transfer inherits is the verified guarantee on the SAT\ninstance it builds, $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rho(n')}$ with $n'$ the number of\nSAT variables, and therefore the title's limitation with it: at $A = 1$\nthe exponential factor is above $2^{n'}$ for every\n$50 \\le n' < e^{5504}$ (Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}), with $C, A, d$\nunknown. In general terms: an algorithm obtained from the verified\nstatement through a variable-controlling reduction is a subexponential\nalgorithm whose verified guarantee is weaker, at every practical size,\nthan trying every assignment of the SAT instance it constructs. That is\nnot a defect of the transfer; it is the same sentence as the title,\nread through a reduction.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:consequences-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is\n  refuted}, for any problem, any size measure or any constant $c$. Such\n  a statement, proved from SETH, has lost its proof and kept its truth\n  value, which is unknown.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' is\n  refuted} either, unless $X$ has a reduction to fixed-width SAT that\n  controls the number of variables; the manuscript names one such\n  problem (Set Splitting) and we add none.\n\\item \\textbf{The unrestricted-width hypothesis is untouched}, and so is\n  the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, and so is every lower bound proved\n  from either; the verified statement is for each fixed $k$ and says\n  nothing about widths that grow with $n$.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about any specific problem's complexity} beyond\n  what the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 state and we quote. The\n  problems the manuscript lists at lines 213--225 and 233--235 are not\n  discussed here, and no example from any other setting is given, by\n  the scope of this paper.\n\\item \\textbf{No re-reading of the sources.} Each hypothesis is stated\n  in the form the manuscript gives it, with the manuscript's citation;\n  we did not check that the cited sections and definitions state it in\n  the same words. If a source's form is stronger than the manuscript's,\n  the refutation of the manuscript's form may not reach it, and the\n  reader should check the source.\n\\item \\textbf{About the limit only.} Every refutation here denies a\n  statement about the limit and uses only that $\\rhon$ grows more\n  slowly than $n$. None says that any machine decides or counts\n  anything fast at any size; the constants are as unknown here as in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, and the verified bound is above $2^n$ on\n  every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the verified statement.} The manuscript's\n  sharper exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) are not used; where the\n  manuscript derives a consequence from them, it is not a consequence\n  of what is verified and is not claimed here.\n\\end{itemize}\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SKfLBaUTtGymW22rhfj1WH","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec6_consequences.tex has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"7fd8ca38-78dd-4a36-ba01-47885b6765a7","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:41:03.380Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec6_consequences.tex","content":"% What follows, in general terms -- drafted by loop0015 item 05 (2026-11-05),\n% rewritten in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05).\n% Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the line + 1);\n% every comparator line is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone, read with cat/sed -n\n% only. Every page, line, fragment and the one table of this section are checked\n% and written by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py\n% which exits non-zero if anything has moved. The section is in general terms\n% by the owner's rule (2026-11-05): no example from any width parameter, no\n% problem named beyond what the manuscript's §1.1.1 and §1.1.3 name. The\n% quantifier forms of the hypotheses and their negations are in Appendix B.\n\\section{What follows, in general terms}\n\\label{sec:consequences}\n\nSections~\\ref{sec:statement}--\\ref{sec:replay} read the verified\nstatement, drew the line between it and the manuscript, inspected the\nproof and replayed it. This section asks what follows from the verified\nstatement alone. Two things: which of the standard hardness hypotheses\nit refutes, and by what step; and what a lower bound that was derived\nfrom one of those hypotheses has lost. The manuscript answers both\nquestions itself, in its \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 (p.~4--5) and \\S1.1.3 (p.~5),\nand we quote it. What we add is the exact logical form of each\nhypothesis, the one line of the comparator statement that denies it, and\nthe distinction, stated in general terms, between a theorem whose\npremise has become false and a statement that has been refuted. Nothing\nhere names a problem the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 do not name,\nand nothing here is about any problem's complexity beyond what those two\nsections state.\n\nA \\emph{hypothesis}, in this section, is a conjecture about how hard\nsatisfiability is. The Exponential Time Hypothesis, ETH, says that\n3-SAT needs time exponential in the number of variables. Its strong\nform, SETH, says that as the clause width $k$ grows, no algorithm for\n$k$-SAT does much better than trying all $2^n$ assignments. Several\nvariants exist, and the manuscript lists them. A \\emph{lower bound\nderived from a hypothesis} is a theorem of the form ``if the hypothesis\nholds, then problem $X$ has no fast algorithm''; hundreds of such\ntheorems exist in the literature.\n\n\\subsection{The one property every refutation uses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-property}\n\nEvery refutation below rests on the same parts of the verified theorem\n(comparator file lines 194--217, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). For each\nfixed width $k \\ge 2$ there is one deterministic machine which, given\nany formula of width at most $k$ on $n$ variables, halts with the number\nof satisfying assignments on its count tape (lines 203--204), the\nanswer ``is that number positive'' on its answer tape and in its\nhalting state (lines 205--207), within the bound $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$\n(lines 210--211). Four features of that property do the work.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{It is deterministic.} The step rule is a function; there\n  is no random choice and no guess. A deterministic algorithm is also a\n  randomised algorithm, one that happens never to err, and also a\n  nondeterministic algorithm, one that happens never to guess. So each\n  refutation of a hypothesis about deterministic algorithms is a\n  refutation of its randomised and nondeterministic forms by the same\n  machine.\n\\item \\textbf{The bound is subexponential in $n$ alone.} A formula of\n  width $k$ on $n$ variables has at most about $n^k$ distinct clauses\n  ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$, $k = 3$), so the input length $L$ is at\n  most a polynomial in $n$, and the whole bound is a polynomial in $n$\n  times $2^{A\\rhon}$. Since $\\rhon/n$ goes to zero\n  \\leanref{rho-little-o}, for every rate $\\delta > 0$ there is a size\n  beyond which the bound is below $2^{\\delta n}$; below that size the\n  bound is a constant depending on $k$ and $\\delta$. In the standard\n  notation, the running time is $O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every\n  $\\delta > 0$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses}). This is a fact\n  about the limit, and the hypotheses are statements about the limit.\n  It coexists with Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s observation that\n  $\\rhon > n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, which is about where the\n  limit begins.\n\\item \\textbf{One machine per $k$, for every $n$.} The machine and the\n  constants are chosen after $k$ and before $n$\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there is no $n \\ge 20$ condition in the\n  comparator statement, though the manuscript's Theorem~1.1 carries one\n  (p.~2, lines 56--57). So the verified form is the strong, uniform\n  denial: a single algorithm that runs within $2^{\\delta n}$ times a\n  polynomial for every $\\delta$ at once, where the denial of a\n  hypothesis of the form ``for every $\\delta$ there is an algorithm''\n  would have allowed a different algorithm for each $\\delta$.\n\\item \\textbf{The machine model is the conservative one.} The\n  comparator's machine is a multitape Turing machine that moves one head\n  one cell or writes one symbol per step. Every step of such a machine\n  is a bounded amount of work on any of the standard models in which the\n  hypotheses are stated, so the bound transfers to those models with at\n  most a constant factor per step, which the polynomial absorbs. The\n  input is the manuscript's own encoding, of length polynomial in\n  $n + m$; converting from any other reasonable encoding is polynomial\n  too.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses, one by one}\n\\label{sec:consequences-hypotheses}\n\nThe manuscript's own sentence is (p.~4, lines 187--191):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing\nwhether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability\nand unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for\nevery fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise\nproblem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\nrandomized versions of ETH [DHM$^+$14, Section 1] and SETH [CDL$^+$16,\nDefinition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH.''\n\\end{quote}\nand, for counting (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1)\nsolves \\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time\nand therefore refutes \\#ETH.'' We take each hypothesis in the form the\nmanuscript states it, with the manuscript's own source for that form,\nand say which part of the verified theorem denies it.\nTable~\\ref{tab:hypotheses} is the summary, and\nAppendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses} gives each hypothesis and its denial\nwith the quantifiers written out. In every case the only thing used is\nthe property of Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}; nothing is used\nfrom the manuscript's sharper exponents, which are not verified\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n\n\\paragraph{ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 165--166): ``The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH)\nasserted that 3-SAT requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time in the\nworst case, and hence so does $k$-SAT for every fixed $k \\ge 3$ [IP01,\nTheorem~1],'' citing \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}. In words: there is\nsome fixed rate $\\delta > 0$ such that no deterministic algorithm decides\n3-SAT within $2^{\\delta n}$ times a polynomial, on all inputs. Its\ndenial: for every rate $\\delta > 0$ there is such an algorithm. The\nverified statement at $k = 3$ supplies, for every $\\delta$, the same\nmachine, whose answer bit is the decision, within that time (item 2\nabove). This holds on every input of every size, so it refutes the\nhypothesis whether ``requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ time'' is read as a lower\nbound at infinitely many sizes or at all large sizes. In the vocabulary\nof \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, where $s_k$ is the best possible rate\nfor $k$-SAT and ETH is $s_3 > 0$: the verified statement gives $s_k = 0$\nfor every $k \\ge 2$, by one algorithm per $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{SETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 166--169): ``The Strong Exponential Time\nHypothesis (SETH) made the sharper prediction that $k$-SAT requires\n$2^{n(1-o_k(1))}$ time as $k$ grows. Precisely, for every\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$, there is a fixed $k$ for which no deterministic\nalgorithm solves $k$-SAT in $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ time,''\nwith the motivation attributed to \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}'s open\nproblems (lines 169--171). In words: however small a saving\n$\\varepsilon$ one asks for over trying all $2^n$ assignments, some clause\nwidth $k$ makes that saving impossible. Its denial: there is a saving\n$\\varepsilon$ that every fixed width $k$ allows. The verified statement\ngives more: for \\emph{every} saving $\\varepsilon$ and every $k \\ge 2$,\nthe machine for $k$ runs within $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}$ times a\npolynomial for all large $n$, because $\\rhon$ grows more slowly than\n$n$; below that size the exponential factor is a constant. The\npolynomial's degree depends on $k$, and that is exactly what the\nhypothesis's ``for a fixed $k$'' permits. The refutation does not pass\nthrough ETH; it reads the statement at each $k$. It is also standard\nthat SETH implies ETH, through the Sparsification Lemma of\n\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001} (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses});\nso the refutation of ETH alone would already refute SETH. We note the\nimplication for the record; the direct reading above is what the paper\nrests on.\n\n\\paragraph{Gap-ETH, and its randomised form.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 172--174): ``The Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis\n(Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants $\\delta, \\gamma > 0$\nfor which no $O(2^{\\gamma n})$-time algorithm can distinguish a\nsatisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment violates at least a\n$\\delta$ fraction of the clauses, even when $m = O(n)$ [Din16, MR17],''\nciting \\citet{Dinur2016} and \\citet{ManurangsiRaghavendra2017}; and\n(lines 175--176) ``The randomized version rules out an algorithm with\nthe same running time that distinguishes the two cases with probability\nat least $2/3$ on every promised input [CCK$^+$20, Conjecture 2.5],''\nciting \\citet{Chalermsook2020}. In words: it should be hard even to tell\na satisfiable formula from one that is far from satisfiable. Its\ndenial: for every rate $\\gamma$ there is an algorithm within\n$O(2^{\\gamma n})$ that tells the two apart, already when the number of\nclauses is proportional to $n$. Exact decision tells them apart, since a\nformula in which every assignment violates some clauses is\nunsatisfiable; and the verified machine at $k = 3$ decides within\n$O(2^{\\gamma n})$ for every $\\gamma$, at every number of clauses (item\n2). The promise is never used: the machine answers correctly on every\ninput, promised or not, with probability $1$, which refutes the\nrandomised form by item 1.\n\n\\paragraph{NSETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 181--183): ``The Nondeterministic Strong\nExponential Time Hypothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every\n$\\varepsilon > 0$, some fixed $k$ admits no nondeterministic\n$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}\\allowbreak(n+m)^{O(1)}$-time algorithm for deciding whether a\n$k$-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a $k$-DNF is a\ntautology [CGI$^+$16],'' citing \\citet{Carmosino2016}. In words: even an\nalgorithm allowed to guess should not be able to certify\nunsatisfiability with a saving over $2^n$. Its denial: there is a saving\nthat every fixed $k$ allows, for a guessing algorithm. The verified\nmachine's answer bit is \\texttt{false} exactly when the count is zero\n(lines 205--207), so it decides unsatisfiability deterministically within\nthe SETH bound of the previous paragraph for every $\\varepsilon$ and\nevery $k$; and a deterministic machine is a guessing machine that never\nguesses, so every convention for nondeterministic time is met. The\nmanuscript's ``decides both satisfiability and unsatisfiability'' (line\n188) is this.\n\n\\paragraph{\\#ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 204--205): ``The counting Exponential Time\nHypothesis (\\#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying\nassignments of a 3-CNF requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time\n[DHM$^+$14, Section 1],'' citing \\citet{Dell2014}. Its denial: for every\nrate $\\delta > 0$ there is a deterministic algorithm within\n$O(2^{\\delta n})$ that outputs the exact count. The verified machine at\n$k = 3$ halts with the count on its count tape, a binary number of at\nmost $n + 1$ bits (lines 203--204, 208--209), within the same bound. This\nis the one hypothesis for which the yes-or-no bit is not enough and the\ncount tape is what refutes it. It is also the hypothesis the comparator\nstatement was written for: the count is the theorem's first conclusion\nand the decision its corollary.\n\n\\paragraph{The bounded-error randomised ETH and SETH.}\nThe manuscript names them by citation only (p.~4, lines 190--191):\n``[DHM$^+$14, Section 1]'' for ETH \\citep{Dell2014} and ``[CDL$^+$16,\nDefinition 2.1]'' for SETH \\citep{Cygan2016}. In each, the algorithm\nmay toss coins and must be right with probability clearly above one\nhalf on every input. A deterministic algorithm is such an algorithm\nthat is right with probability $1$ (item 1), so the refutations of ETH\nand SETH above refute these forms by the same machine, with no change\nto the bound. We have not re-read the two sources' definitions; we\nstate the forms as the manuscript cites them, and a reader who needs the\nexact error threshold or the exact model of the source should go to the\ncited section and definition.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\caption{The hypotheses the manuscript's \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 list, each in\nthe form the manuscript states it (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of\nthe committed extract), with the manuscript's own source for that form,\nthe logical form in symbols, and the part of the verified theorem that\ndenies it (lines 194--217 of the comparator file at \\texttt{4eaee875}).\n$A$, $C_k$, $d_k$ are the never-computed constants of\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement}. Generated, with every page and fragment\nchecked against the extract and the clone, by\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py}.}\n\\label{tab:hypotheses}\n\\scriptsize\n\\input{tables/hypotheses}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{What the manuscript places beside the list.}\nTwo sentences follow the refutation paragraph (p.~4, lines 191--194):\nthe result ``does not conflict with unconditional exponential lower\nbounds for restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13],''\nand ``In 2016, Ryan Williams refuted the Merlin--Arthur version of SETH\n(MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies $k$-CNF unsatisfiability\nin $2^{n/2}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3]''\n\\citep{Williams2016}. Both are context. The first says that a lower\nbound proved outright for one restricted way of writing proofs is not a\nstatement about algorithms in general and is not touched. The second\nsays that one member of the family was already false before this\nresult. Neither is used below.\n\n\\subsection{What a derived lower bound loses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-lower-bounds}\n\nThe manuscript's \\S1.1.3 opens with a list of lower bounds that were\nproved from ETH or from SETH (p.~5, lines 213--225), for problems with\nexponential algorithms and for problems with polynomial ones, and then\nsays what its result does to them (lines 226--227):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness\nclaims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from weaker\nhypotheses that may still hold.''\n\\end{quote}\nWe say what that means in general terms, for a theorem of either of the\ntwo standard shapes,\n\\begin{align*}\n  \\text{(E)}&\\quad \\text{ETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } 2^{o(n)} \\text{ algorithm for } X,\\\\\n  \\text{(S)}&\\quad \\text{SETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } (c - \\varepsilon)^{k} \\text{ algorithm for } Y \\text{ for any } \\varepsilon > 0,\n\\end{align*}\nwhere $n$ and $k$ are whatever measure of the input the theorem names\nand $c$ is its constant. In words: (E) says that if ETH holds, problem\n$X$ has no subexponential algorithm; (S) says that if SETH holds, no\nalgorithm for problem $Y$ beats the base $c$. Nothing below depends on\nwhat $X$ and $Y$ are.\n\n\\paragraph{The theorem stands; its premise is false; it derives nothing.}\nA theorem of shape (E) or (S) is an ``if \\ldots{} then''. It is proved\nby a reduction: a recipe that turns a fast algorithm for $X$ or $Y$ into\na fast algorithm for $k$-SAT. The recipe is unaffected by anything in\nthe verified statement; the ``if \\ldots{} then'' remains a theorem. What\nhas changed is that its ``if'' is now known to be false\n(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-hypotheses}), so the theorem can no\nlonger be applied: it derives nothing about $X$ or $Y$. That is the\nwhole of what ``removes one basis'' means.\n\n\\paragraph{The conclusion is not refuted.}\nA false ``if'' does not make the ``then'' false. The statement ``no\n$2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' and the statement ``no\n$(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$'' are untouched by anything in the\ncomparator statement, which speaks about $k$-CNF formulas and nothing\nelse. To refute either, one would need an algorithm for $X$ or $Y$, and\nthe verified statement supplies one only through a reduction in the\nopposite direction to the one the lower bound used. The manuscript says\nexactly this (p.~5, lines 237--238): ``To obtain a faster algorithm for\na target problem from our SAT algorithm, we need a reduction in the\nother direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT, that controls the\nnumber of variables.'' And it places the general caveat on itself at\np.~2, lines 95--99:\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Note that runtime bounds of the form $2^{o(n)}$ do not transfer under\ngeneral polynomial-time reductions ($2^{n^{o(1)}}$ would be needed for\nthis), so our result does not imply that general NP problems admit\nsubexponential time algorithms.''\n\\end{quote}\nSo for shape (E): a subexponential algorithm for $X$ follows only from a\nreduction of $X$ to fixed-width SAT in which the number of SAT variables\nis proportional to $X$'s own size measure, and the manuscript gives one\nsuch transfer (below). For shape (S): an algorithm for $Y$ with base\nbelow $c$ would need the SAT instance's exponent to be below $k \\log_2 c$\nby a constant factor, and the verified statement's exponent is $A$ times\n$\\rho$ of the SAT instance's variable count, with $A$ unknown and no\nvalue below $257$ on record (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses}). No\nstatement of shape ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is refuted by\nanything in this paper or, so far as we can read, in the manuscript.\n\n\\paragraph{Hardness as a classification.}\nA statement of the form ``$Y$ is SETH-hard'' (or ETH-hard) records that\na reduction exists from $k$-SAT to $Y$ with the stated control of the\nsize measure. The reduction still exists, and the statement is still\ntrue as a statement about reductions. What it used to convey, that a\nfast algorithm for $Y$ is unlikely because it would refute SETH, it no\nlonger conveys: a fast algorithm for $Y$ would refute SETH, and SETH is\nrefuted. The classification is now a fact about reductions and no\nlonger a fact about $Y$'s running time.\n\n\\paragraph{The weaker hypotheses the manuscript names are untouched.}\nThe manuscript gives two (p.~5, lines 229--236). The first is the\nOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture, which it states and then relates to SETH:\n``The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal Vectors shows that SETH implies\nOVC [Wil05, VW19]'' (lines 231--232). That implication is a theorem\nwith a false premise, like (E) and (S): it says nothing about OVC either\nway, and nothing in the verified statement is about orthogonal vectors.\nThe second is the form of SETH for formulas of unrestricted width, which\nwe quote with the manuscript's list of the problems it covers elided\n(lines 233--236):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``The lower bounds for [\\ldots] also follow from the hypothesis that\nunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$-time\nalgorithm for any constant $\\delta > 0$ [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem\n1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each fixed clause width $k$,\nwhile this hypothesis allows the width to grow with $n$.''\n\\end{quote}\nThe verified statement cannot reach this hypothesis, and the reason is\nvisible in the order of its words (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}): the\nmachine and the constants $C, A, S, d, e$ are chosen after $k$, and\nnothing in the statement bounds how they grow with $k$. For a formula\nwhose width grows with $n$ there is no single machine and no single\nbound in the statement at all; even the trivial observation that every\n$k$-CNF is a CNF runs the wrong way, since the hypothesis speaks about\nall widths at once. So the unrestricted-width hypothesis, and every\nlower bound that follows from it, is exactly where it was.\n\n\\paragraph{What a transferred algorithm inherits.}\nThe manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of\nsize at most $k$ (p.~5, lines 239--243): one variable per element and\ntwo clauses per set give a $k$-CNF with ``one variable per universe\nelement, so for fixed $k$ Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential time in the\nuniverse size, up to a polynomial input-size factor,'' citing\n\\citet{Cygan2016}'s Observation~3.6. Section~\\ref{sec:beyond} tags this\nsupported: the encoding is on paper, the counter is the verified one.\nWhat such a transfer inherits is the verified guarantee on the SAT\ninstance it builds, $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rho(n')}$ with $n'$ the number of\nSAT variables, and therefore the title's limitation with it: at $A = 1$\nthe exponential factor is above $2^{n'}$ for every\n$50 \\le n' < e^{5504}$ (Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}), with $C, A, d$\nunknown. In general terms: an algorithm obtained from the verified\nstatement through a variable-controlling reduction is a subexponential\nalgorithm whose verified guarantee is weaker, at every practical size,\nthan trying every assignment of the SAT instance it constructs. That is\nnot a defect of the transfer; it is the same sentence as the title,\nread through a reduction.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:consequences-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is\n  refuted}, for any problem, any size measure or any constant $c$. Such\n  a statement, proved from SETH, has lost its proof and kept its truth\n  value, which is unknown.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' is\n  refuted} either, unless $X$ has a reduction to fixed-width SAT that\n  controls the number of variables; the manuscript names one such\n  problem (Set Splitting) and we add none.\n\\item \\textbf{The unrestricted-width hypothesis is untouched}, and so is\n  the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, and so is every lower bound proved\n  from either; the verified statement is for each fixed $k$ and says\n  nothing about widths that grow with $n$.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about any specific problem's complexity} beyond\n  what the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 state and we quote. The\n  problems the manuscript lists at lines 213--225 and 233--235 are not\n  discussed here, and no example from any other setting is given, by\n  the scope of this paper.\n\\item \\textbf{No re-reading of the sources.} Each hypothesis is stated\n  in the form the manuscript gives it, with the manuscript's citation;\n  we did not check that the cited sections and definitions state it in\n  the same words. If a source's form is stronger than the manuscript's,\n  the refutation of the manuscript's form may not reach it, and the\n  reader should check the source.\n\\item \\textbf{About the limit only.} Every refutation here denies a\n  statement about the limit and uses only that $\\rhon$ grows more\n  slowly than $n$. None says that any machine decides or counts\n  anything fast at any size; the constants are as unknown here as in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, and the verified bound is above $2^n$ on\n  every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the verified statement.} The manuscript's\n  sharper exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) are not used; where the\n  manuscript derives a consequence from them, it is not a consequence\n  of what is verified and is not claimed here.\n\\end{itemize}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":4,"newStart":1,"newLines":5,"lines":["-% What follows, in general terms -- drafted by loop0015 item 05 (2026-11-05).","+% What follows, in general terms -- drafted by loop0015 item 05 (2026-11-05),","+% rewritten in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05)."," % Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of"," % reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the line + 1);"," % every comparator line is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone, read with cat/sed -n"]},{"oldStart":7,"oldLines":76,"newStart":8,"newLines":86,"lines":[" %   python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py"," % which exits non-zero if anything has moved. The section is in general terms"," % by the owner's rule (2026-11-05): no example from any width parameter, no","-% problem named beyond what the manuscript's §1.1.1 and §1.1.3 name.","+% problem named beyond what the manuscript's §1.1.1 and §1.1.3 name. The","+% quantifier forms of the hypotheses and their negations are in Appendix B."," \\section{What follows, in general terms}"," \\label{sec:consequences}"," ","-Sections~\\ref{sec:statement}--\\ref{sec:replay} read the verified statement,","-drew the line between it and the manuscript, inspected the proof and","-replayed it. This section asks what follows from the verified statement","-alone: which hypotheses it refutes and by what step, and what a lower","-bound that was derived from one of those hypotheses has lost. The","-manuscript answers both questions itself, in \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 (p.~4--5)","-and \\S1.1.3 (p.~5), and we quote it; what we add is the quantifier form","-of each hypothesis, the one line of the comparator statement that negates","-it, and the distinction, stated in general terms, between a theorem whose","+Sections~\\ref{sec:statement}--\\ref{sec:replay} read the verified","+statement, drew the line between it and the manuscript, inspected the","+proof and replayed it. This section asks what follows from the verified","+statement alone. Two things: which of the standard hardness hypotheses","+it refutes, and by what step; and what a lower bound that was derived","+from one of those hypotheses has lost. The manuscript answers both","+questions itself, in its \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 (p.~4--5) and \\S1.1.3 (p.~5),","+and we quote it. What we add is the exact logical form of each","+hypothesis, the one line of the comparator statement that denies it, and","+the distinction, stated in general terms, between a theorem whose"," premise has become false and a statement that has been refuted. Nothing","-here names a problem the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 do not name, and","-nothing here is about any problem's complexity beyond what those two","+here names a problem the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 do not name,","+and nothing here is about any problem's complexity beyond what those two"," sections state."," ","+A \\emph{hypothesis}, in this section, is a conjecture about how hard","+satisfiability is. The Exponential Time Hypothesis, ETH, says that","+3-SAT needs time exponential in the number of variables. Its strong","+form, SETH, says that as the clause width $k$ grows, no algorithm for","+$k$-SAT does much better than trying all $2^n$ assignments. Several","+variants exist, and the manuscript lists them. A \\emph{lower bound","+derived from a hypothesis} is a theorem of the form ``if the hypothesis","+holds, then problem $X$ has no fast algorithm''; hundreds of such","+theorems exist in the literature.","+"," \\subsection{The one property every refutation uses}"," \\label{sec:consequences-property}"," ","-Every refutation below rests on the same conjuncts of","-\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (comparator file lines 194--217,","-Section~\\ref{sec:statement}): for each fixed $k \\ge 2$ there is one","-deterministic machine which, on the canonical encoding of any","-\\texttt{WidthAtMost}~$k$ formula $F$ on $n$ variables (line 38), halts","-with \\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape (lines 203--204, 147),","-the bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ on its answer tape and in its halting","-state (lines 205--207), within $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps (lines","-210--211). Four features of that property do the work.","+Every refutation below rests on the same parts of the verified theorem","+(comparator file lines 194--217, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). For each","+fixed width $k \\ge 2$ there is one deterministic machine which, given","+any formula of width at most $k$ on $n$ variables, halts with the number","+of satisfying assignments on its count tape (lines 203--204), the","+answer ``is that number positive'' on its answer tape and in its","+halting state (lines 205--207), within the bound $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$","+(lines 210--211). Four features of that property do the work."," "," \\begin{enumerate}","-\\item \\textbf{It is deterministic.} \\texttt{step} is a function and","-  \\texttt{Trace} is its iteration (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there","-  is no random tape and no nondeterministic choice. A deterministic","-  algorithm is also a bounded-error randomised algorithm, with error","-  $0$, and also a nondeterministic algorithm, with no guess; so each","-  refutation of a deterministic hypothesis is a refutation of its","-  randomised and nondeterministic forms by the same machine.","-\\item \\textbf{The bound is $2^{o(n)}$ in $n$ alone.} A \\texttt{Finset} of","-  clauses of width at most $k$ over $n$ variables has at most","-  $\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j}$ members ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$,","-  $k = 3$), so $m = O(n^k)$ and $L = O(k\\,n\\,m) = O(n^{k+1})$","-  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); hence the bound is","-  $n^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, and since $\\rhon/n \\to 0$","-  \\leanref{rho-little-o}, for every $\\delta > 0$ there is an","-  $n_0(k, \\delta)$ beyond which it is below $2^{\\delta n}$. Below","-  $n_0$ the bound is a constant depending on $k$ and $\\delta$. So the","-  running time is $O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every $\\delta > 0$,","-  and $2^{\\delta n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ with the polynomial written out. This","-  is an asymptotic fact and it is the only kind of fact the hypotheses","-  are about; it coexists with Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s observation","-  that $\\rhon > n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, which is about where","-  the asymptotics begin.","+\\item \\textbf{It is deterministic.} The step rule is a function; there","+  is no random choice and no guess. A deterministic algorithm is also a","+  randomised algorithm, one that happens never to err, and also a","+  nondeterministic algorithm, one that happens never to guess. So each","+  refutation of a hypothesis about deterministic algorithms is a","+  refutation of its randomised and nondeterministic forms by the same","+  machine.","+\\item \\textbf{The bound is subexponential in $n$ alone.} A formula of","+  width $k$ on $n$ variables has at most about $n^k$ distinct clauses","+  ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$, $k = 3$), so the input length $L$ is at","+  most a polynomial in $n$, and the whole bound is a polynomial in $n$","+  times $2^{A\\rhon}$. Since $\\rhon/n$ goes to zero","+  \\leanref{rho-little-o}, for every rate $\\delta > 0$ there is a size","+  beyond which the bound is below $2^{\\delta n}$; below that size the","+  bound is a constant depending on $k$ and $\\delta$. In the standard","+  notation, the running time is $O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every","+  $\\delta > 0$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses}). This is a fact","+  about the limit, and the hypotheses are statements about the limit.","+  It coexists with Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s observation that","+  $\\rhon > n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, which is about where the","+  limit begins."," \\item \\textbf{One machine per $k$, for every $n$.} The machine and the","-  constants are chosen after $k$ and before $n$ and $F$","-  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there is no $n \\ge 20$ proviso in the","+  constants are chosen after $k$ and before $n$","+  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there is no $n \\ge 20$ condition in the","   comparator statement, though the manuscript's Theorem~1.1 carries one","   (p.~2, lines 56--57). So the verified form is the strong, uniform","-  negation: a single algorithm that is $O(2^{\\delta n})$ for every","-  $\\delta$ at once, where a hypothesis of the form ``for every $\\delta$","-  there is an algorithm'' would have allowed a different algorithm for","-  each $\\delta$.","-\\item \\textbf{The model is the conservative one.} The comparator's","-  machine is a multitape Turing machine with one head per tape, moving","-  one cell or writing one symbol per step. Every step of such a machine","+  denial: a single algorithm that runs within $2^{\\delta n}$ times a","+  polynomial for every $\\delta$ at once, where the denial of a","+  hypothesis of the form ``for every $\\delta$ there is an algorithm''","+  would have allowed a different algorithm for each $\\delta$.","+\\item \\textbf{The machine model is the conservative one.} The","+  comparator's machine is a multitape Turing machine that moves one head","+  one cell or writes one symbol per step. Every step of such a machine","   is a bounded amount of work on any of the standard models in which the","   hypotheses are stated, so the bound transfers to those models with at","   most a constant factor per step, which the polynomial absorbs. The","-  input is the manuscript's canonical encoding, of length polynomial in","+  input is the manuscript's own encoding, of length polynomial in","   $n + m$; converting from any other reasonable encoding is polynomial","   too."," \\end{enumerate}"]},{"oldStart":98,"oldLines":30,"newStart":109,"newLines":30,"lines":[" solves \\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time"," and therefore refutes \\#ETH.'' We take each hypothesis in the form the"," manuscript states it, with the manuscript's own source for that form,","-and say which conjunct negates it. Table~\\ref{tab:hypotheses} is the","-summary. In every case the only thing used is the property of","-Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}; nothing is used from the","-manuscript's sharper exponents, which are not verified","+and say which part of the verified theorem denies it.","+Table~\\ref{tab:hypotheses} is the summary, and","+Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses} gives each hypothesis and its denial","+with the quantifiers written out. In every case the only thing used is","+the property of Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}; nothing is used","+from the manuscript's sharper exponents, which are not verified"," (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond})."," "," \\paragraph{ETH.}"," As stated (p.~4, lines 165--166): ``The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH)"," asserted that 3-SAT requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time in the"," worst case, and hence so does $k$-SAT for every fixed $k \\ge 3$ [IP01,","-Theorem~1],'' citing \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}. In quantifiers:","-there is a $\\delta > 0$ such that no deterministic algorithm decides","-3-SAT within $2^{\\delta n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps on all inputs.","-Its negation is: for every $\\delta > 0$ there is one. The verified","-statement at $k = 3$ supplies, for every $\\delta > 0$, the same machine,","-whose answer bit is $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$, that is, the decision,","-within $O_{\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps","-(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}, item 2). This holds on every","-input of every size, so it refutes the hypothesis under either reading","-of ``$2^{\\Omega(n)}$'' (a lower bound at infinitely many $n$, or at all","-large $n$). In the vocabulary of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, where","-$s_k$ is the infimum of the $\\delta$ for which $k$-SAT has an","-$O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm and ETH is $s_3 > 0$: the verified statement","-gives $s_k = 0$ for every $k \\ge 2$, by one algorithm per $k$.","+Theorem~1],'' citing \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}. In words: there is","+some fixed rate $\\delta > 0$ such that no deterministic algorithm decides","+3-SAT within $2^{\\delta n}$ times a polynomial, on all inputs. Its","+denial: for every rate $\\delta > 0$ there is such an algorithm. The","+verified statement at $k = 3$ supplies, for every $\\delta$, the same","+machine, whose answer bit is the decision, within that time (item 2","+above). This holds on every input of every size, so it refutes the","+hypothesis whether ``requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ time'' is read as a lower","+bound at infinitely many sizes or at all large sizes. In the vocabulary","+of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, where $s_k$ is the best possible rate","+for $k$-SAT and ETH is $s_3 > 0$: the verified statement gives $s_k = 0$","+for every $k \\ge 2$, by one algorithm per $k$."," "," \\paragraph{SETH.}"," As stated (p.~4, lines 166--169): ``The Strong Exponential Time"]},{"oldStart":130,"oldLines":25,"newStart":141,"newLines":22,"lines":[" $\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$, there is a fixed $k$ for which no deterministic"," algorithm solves $k$-SAT in $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ time,''"," with the motivation attributed to \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}'s open","-problems (lines 169--171). The negation is: there is an","-$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ such that for every fixed $k$ there is a","-deterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm. The verified","-statement gives more: for \\emph{every} $\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ and every","-$k \\ge 2$, the machine for $k$ runs within","-$C_k(L+1)^{d_k}\\,2^{A_k\\rhon}$ steps, and $A_k\\rhon \\le (1-\\varepsilon)n$","-for all $n \\ge n_0(k, \\varepsilon)$ because $\\rhon = o(n)$; below $n_0$","-the exponential factor is a constant. So each $k$ has a","-$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ algorithm, and the polynomial's","-degree depending on $k$ is exactly what the hypothesis's ``for a fixed","-$k$'' permits. The refutation does not pass through ETH; it reads the","-statement at each $k$. It is also standard that SETH implies ETH: if","-3-SAT had a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm then, by the Sparsification Lemma of","-\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}, which reduces $k$-SAT on $n$ variables","-to $2^{\\varepsilon n}$ instances with $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ clauses and","-so, after the clause-splitting reduction, to 3-SAT on $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$","-variables, so would $k$-SAT for every fixed $k$; so the refutation of","-ETH alone would already refute SETH. We note the implication for the","-record; the direct reading above is what the paper rests on.","+problems (lines 169--171). In words: however small a saving","+$\\varepsilon$ one asks for over trying all $2^n$ assignments, some clause","+width $k$ makes that saving impossible. Its denial: there is a saving","+$\\varepsilon$ that every fixed width $k$ allows. The verified statement","+gives more: for \\emph{every} saving $\\varepsilon$ and every $k \\ge 2$,","+the machine for $k$ runs within $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}$ times a","+polynomial for all large $n$, because $\\rhon$ grows more slowly than","+$n$; below that size the exponential factor is a constant. The","+polynomial's degree depends on $k$, and that is exactly what the","+hypothesis's ``for a fixed $k$'' permits. The refutation does not pass","+through ETH; it reads the statement at each $k$. It is also standard","+that SETH implies ETH, through the Sparsification Lemma of","+\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001} (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses});","+so the refutation of ETH alone would already refute SETH. We note the","+implication for the record; the direct reading above is what the paper","+rests on."," "," \\paragraph{Gap-ETH, and its randomised form.}"," As stated (p.~4, lines 172--174): ``The Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis"]},{"oldStart":160,"oldLines":16,"newStart":168,"newLines":17,"lines":[" (lines 175--176) ``The randomized version rules out an algorithm with"," the same running time that distinguishes the two cases with probability"," at least $2/3$ on every promised input [CCK$^+$20, Conjecture 2.5],''","-citing \\citet{Chalermsook2020}. The negation is: for every","-$\\delta, \\gamma > 0$ there is an $O(2^{\\gamma n})$ algorithm that","-separates the two cases, already at $m = O(n)$. Exact decision separates","-them, since a formula in which every assignment violates a positive","-fraction of the clauses is unsatisfiable; and the verified machine at","-$k = 3$ decides within $n^{O(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon} = O_{\\gamma}(2^{\\gamma n})$","-steps for every $\\gamma > 0$, at every $m$ (item 2 above; at $m = O(n)$","-the polynomial is $O(n^{2d})$). The promise is never used: the machine","-answers correctly on every input, promised or not, with probability $1$,","-which refutes the randomised form by item 1.","+citing \\citet{Chalermsook2020}. In words: it should be hard even to tell","+a satisfiable formula from one that is far from satisfiable. Its","+denial: for every rate $\\gamma$ there is an algorithm within","+$O(2^{\\gamma n})$ that tells the two apart, already when the number of","+clauses is proportional to $n$. Exact decision tells them apart, since a","+formula in which every assignment violates some clauses is","+unsatisfiable; and the verified machine at $k = 3$ decides within","+$O(2^{\\gamma n})$ for every $\\gamma$, at every number of clauses (item","+2). The promise is never used: the machine answers correctly on every","+input, promised or not, with probability $1$, which refutes the","+randomised form by item 1."," "," \\paragraph{NSETH.}"," As stated (p.~4, lines 181--183): ``The Nondeterministic Strong"]},{"oldStart":177,"oldLines":53,"newStart":186,"newLines":52,"lines":[" $\\varepsilon > 0$, some fixed $k$ admits no nondeterministic"," $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}\\allowbreak(n+m)^{O(1)}$-time algorithm for deciding whether a"," $k$-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a $k$-DNF is a","-tautology [CGI$^+$16],'' citing \\citet{Carmosino2016}. The negation is:","-there is an $\\varepsilon > 0$ such that every fixed $k$ has a","-nondeterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm for","-unsatisfiability. The verified machine's answer bit is \\texttt{false}","-exactly when $\\mathtt{count}\\ F = 0$ (lines 205--207), so it decides","-unsatisfiability deterministically within the SETH bound of the previous","-paragraph for every $\\varepsilon$ and every $k$; and a deterministic","-machine is a nondeterministic one whose only computation path is the","-deterministic run, so every acceptance convention for nondeterministic","-time is met. The manuscript's ``decides both satisfiability and","-unsatisfiability'' (line 188) is this.","+tautology [CGI$^+$16],'' citing \\citet{Carmosino2016}. In words: even an","+algorithm allowed to guess should not be able to certify","+unsatisfiability with a saving over $2^n$. Its denial: there is a saving","+that every fixed $k$ allows, for a guessing algorithm. The verified","+machine's answer bit is \\texttt{false} exactly when the count is zero","+(lines 205--207), so it decides unsatisfiability deterministically within","+the SETH bound of the previous paragraph for every $\\varepsilon$ and","+every $k$; and a deterministic machine is a guessing machine that never","+guesses, so every convention for nondeterministic time is met. The","+manuscript's ``decides both satisfiability and unsatisfiability'' (line","+188) is this."," "," \\paragraph{\\#ETH.}"," As stated (p.~4, lines 204--205): ``The counting Exponential Time"," Hypothesis (\\#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying"," assignments of a 3-CNF requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time","-[DHM$^+$14, Section 1],'' citing \\citet{Dell2014}. The negation is: for","-every $\\delta > 0$ there is a deterministic $O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm","-that outputs the exact count. The verified machine at $k = 3$ halts with","-\\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape, a binary word of length","-at most $n + 1$ that decodes to $\\mathtt{count}\\ F$ (lines 203--204,","-208--209), within the same bound. This is the one hypothesis for which","-the decision bit is not enough and the count tape is what refutes it;","-it is also the hypothesis the comparator statement was written for, the","-count being the theorem's first conclusion and the decision its","-corollary.","+[DHM$^+$14, Section 1],'' citing \\citet{Dell2014}. Its denial: for every","+rate $\\delta > 0$ there is a deterministic algorithm within","+$O(2^{\\delta n})$ that outputs the exact count. The verified machine at","+$k = 3$ halts with the count on its count tape, a binary number of at","+most $n + 1$ bits (lines 203--204, 208--209), within the same bound. This","+is the one hypothesis for which the yes-or-no bit is not enough and the","+count tape is what refutes it. It is also the hypothesis the comparator","+statement was written for: the count is the theorem's first conclusion","+and the decision its corollary."," "," \\paragraph{The bounded-error randomised ETH and SETH.}"," The manuscript names them by citation only (p.~4, lines 190--191):"," ``[DHM$^+$14, Section 1]'' for ETH \\citep{Dell2014} and ``[CDL$^+$16,"," Definition 2.1]'' for SETH \\citep{Cygan2016}. In each, the algorithm","-may be randomised and must be correct with probability bounded away","-from $1/2$ on every input. A deterministic algorithm is such an","-algorithm with error $0$ (item 1), so the refutations of ETH and SETH","-above refute these forms by the same machine, with no change to the","-bound. We have not re-read the two sources' definitions; we state the","-forms as the manuscript cites them, and a reader who needs the exact","-error threshold or the exact model of the source should go to the","+may toss coins and must be right with probability clearly above one","+half on every input. A deterministic algorithm is such an algorithm","+that is right with probability $1$ (item 1), so the refutations of ETH","+and SETH above refute these forms by the same machine, with no change","+to the bound. We have not re-read the two sources' definitions; we","+state the forms as the manuscript cites them, and a reader who needs the","+exact error threshold or the exact model of the source should go to the"," cited section and definition."," "," \\begin{table}[htbp]"," \\caption{The hypotheses the manuscript's \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 list, each in"," the form the manuscript states it (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of"," the committed extract), with the manuscript's own source for that form,","-the quantifier form, and the conjunct of the comparator statement that","-negates it (lines 194--217 of the comparator file at \\texttt{4eaee875}).","-$A$, $C_k$, $d_k$ are the existential constants of","+the logical form in symbols, and the part of the verified theorem that","+denies it (lines 194--217 of the comparator file at \\texttt{4eaee875}).","+$A$, $C_k$, $d_k$ are the never-computed constants of"," Section~\\ref{sec:statement}. Generated, with every page and fragment"," checked against the extract and the clone, by"," \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py}.}"]},{"oldStart":240,"oldLines":45,"newStart":248,"newLines":47,"lines":[" (MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies $k$-CNF unsatisfiability"," in $2^{n/2}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3]''"," \\citep{Williams2016}. Both are context. The first says that a lower","-bound proved unconditionally for a restricted proof system is not a","-statement about algorithms in general and is not touched; the second","-that one member of the family was already false before this result.","-Neither is used below.","+bound proved outright for one restricted way of writing proofs is not a","+statement about algorithms in general and is not touched. The second","+says that one member of the family was already false before this","+result. Neither is used below."," "," \\subsection{What a derived lower bound loses}"," \\label{sec:consequences-lower-bounds}"," "," The manuscript's \\S1.1.3 opens with a list of lower bounds that were"," proved from ETH or from SETH (p.~5, lines 213--225), for problems with","-exponential algorithms and for problems in polynomial time, and then","+exponential algorithms and for problems with polynomial ones, and then"," says what its result does to them (lines 226--227):"," \\begin{quote}\\small"," ``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness"," claims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from weaker"," hypotheses that may still hold.''"," \\end{quote}","-We state what that means in general terms, for a theorem of either of","-the two standard shapes,","+We say what that means in general terms, for a theorem of either of the","+two standard shapes,"," \\begin{align*}","   \\text{(E)}&\\quad \\text{ETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } 2^{o(n)} \\text{ algorithm for } X,\\\\","   \\text{(S)}&\\quad \\text{SETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } (c - \\varepsilon)^{k} \\text{ algorithm for } Y \\text{ for any } \\varepsilon > 0,"," \\end{align*}","-where $n$ and $k$ are whatever parameter of $X$ and $Y$ the theorem","-names and $c$ is its constant. Nothing below depends on what $X$ and $Y$","-are.","+where $n$ and $k$ are whatever measure of the input the theorem names","+and $c$ is its constant. In words: (E) says that if ETH holds, problem","+$X$ has no subexponential algorithm; (S) says that if SETH holds, no","+algorithm for problem $Y$ beats the base $c$. Nothing below depends on","+what $X$ and $Y$ are."," "," \\paragraph{The theorem stands; its premise is false; it derives nothing.}","-A theorem of shape (E) or (S) is an implication, proved by a reduction","-from $k$-SAT to $X$ or $Y$ that turns a fast algorithm for the target","-into a fast algorithm for $k$-SAT. The reduction is unaffected by","-anything in the verified statement; the implication remains a theorem.","-What has changed is that its premise is now known to be false","-(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-hypotheses}), so the implication can no","-longer be discharged: it derives nothing about $X$ or $Y$. That is the","+A theorem of shape (E) or (S) is an ``if \\ldots{} then''. It is proved","+by a reduction: a recipe that turns a fast algorithm for $X$ or $Y$ into","+a fast algorithm for $k$-SAT. The recipe is unaffected by anything in","+the verified statement; the ``if \\ldots{} then'' remains a theorem. What","+has changed is that its ``if'' is now known to be false","+(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-hypotheses}), so the theorem can no","+longer be applied: it derives nothing about $X$ or $Y$. That is the"," whole of what ``removes one basis'' means."," "," \\paragraph{The conclusion is not refuted.}","-A false premise does not make a conclusion false. The statement ``no","+A false ``if'' does not make the ``then'' false. The statement ``no"," $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' and the statement ``no"," $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$'' are untouched by anything in the"," comparator statement, which speaks about $k$-CNF formulas and nothing"]},{"oldStart":296,"oldLines":25,"newStart":306,"newLines":24,"lines":[" this), so our result does not imply that general NP problems admit"," subexponential time algorithms.''"," \\end{quote}","-So for shape (E): a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$ follows only from a","+So for shape (E): a subexponential algorithm for $X$ follows only from a"," reduction of $X$ to fixed-width SAT in which the number of SAT variables","-is $O(n)$ in $X$'s parameter, and the manuscript gives one such transfer","-(below). For shape (S): a $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$ would","-need the exponent of the SAT instance to be below $k \\log_2 c$ by a","-constant factor, and the verified statement's exponent is","-$A\\rhon'$ in the variable count $n'$ of the SAT instance with $A$ unknown","-(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); no value of $A$ is on record below $257$,","-and no base $c$ is derived from the verified statement for any $Y$. No","+is proportional to $X$'s own size measure, and the manuscript gives one","+such transfer (below). For shape (S): an algorithm for $Y$ with base","+below $c$ would need the SAT instance's exponent to be below $k \\log_2 c$","+by a constant factor, and the verified statement's exponent is $A$ times","+$\\rho$ of the SAT instance's variable count, with $A$ unknown and no","+value below $257$ on record (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses}). No"," statement of shape ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is refuted by"," anything in this paper or, so far as we can read, in the manuscript."," "," \\paragraph{Hardness as a classification.}"," A statement of the form ``$Y$ is SETH-hard'' (or ETH-hard) records that","-a reduction exists from $k$-SAT to $Y$ with the stated parameter","-control. The reduction still exists, and the statement is still true as","-a statement about reductions. What it used to convey, that a fast","-algorithm for $Y$ is unlikely because it would refute SETH, it no longer","-conveys: a fast algorithm for $Y$ would refute SETH, and SETH is","+a reduction exists from $k$-SAT to $Y$ with the stated control of the","+size measure. The reduction still exists, and the statement is still","+true as a statement about reductions. What it used to convey, that a","+fast algorithm for $Y$ is unlikely because it would refute SETH, it no","+longer conveys: a fast algorithm for $Y$ would refute SETH, and SETH is"," refuted. The classification is now a fact about reductions and no"," longer a fact about $Y$'s running time."," "]},{"oldStart":322,"oldLines":11,"newStart":331,"newLines":12,"lines":[" The manuscript gives two (p.~5, lines 229--236). The first is the"," Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, which it states and then relates to SETH:"," ``The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal Vectors shows that SETH implies","-OVC [Wil05, VW19]'' (lines 231--232). That implication is a theorem with","-a false premise, like (E) and (S): it says nothing about OVC either way,","-and nothing in the verified statement is about orthogonal vectors. The","-second is the width-unrestricted form of SETH, which we quote with the","-manuscript's list of the problems it covers elided (lines 233--236):","+OVC [Wil05, VW19]'' (lines 231--232). That implication is a theorem","+with a false premise, like (E) and (S): it says nothing about OVC either","+way, and nothing in the verified statement is about orthogonal vectors.","+The second is the form of SETH for formulas of unrestricted width, which","+we quote with the manuscript's list of the problems it covers elided","+(lines 233--236):"," \\begin{quote}\\small"," ``The lower bounds for [\\ldots] also follow from the hypothesis that"," unrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$-time"]},{"oldStart":335,"oldLines":30,"newStart":345,"newLines":30,"lines":[" while this hypothesis allows the width to grow with $n$.''"," \\end{quote}"," The verified statement cannot reach this hypothesis, and the reason is","-visible in its quantifiers (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}): the machine","-and the constants $C, A, S, d, e$ are chosen after $k$, and nothing in","-the statement bounds how they grow with $k$. For a formula whose width","-grows with $n$ there is no single machine and no single bound in the","-statement at all; even the trivial observation that every $k$-CNF is a","-CNF runs the wrong way, since the hypothesis quantifies over all widths","-at once. So the unrestricted-width hypothesis, and every lower bound","-that follows from it, is exactly where it was.","+visible in the order of its words (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}): the","+machine and the constants $C, A, S, d, e$ are chosen after $k$, and","+nothing in the statement bounds how they grow with $k$. For a formula","+whose width grows with $n$ there is no single machine and no single","+bound in the statement at all; even the trivial observation that every","+$k$-CNF is a CNF runs the wrong way, since the hypothesis speaks about","+all widths at once. So the unrestricted-width hypothesis, and every","+lower bound that follows from it, is exactly where it was."," "," \\paragraph{What a transferred algorithm inherits.}"," The manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of","-size at most $k$ (p.~5, lines 239--243): one variable per universe","-element and two clauses per set give a $k$-CNF with ``one variable per","-universe element, so for fixed $k$ Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential","-time in the universe size, up to a polynomial input-size factor,''","-citing \\citet{Cygan2016}'s Observation~3.6. Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}","-tags this supported: the encoding is on paper, the counter is the","-verified one. What such a transfer inherits is the verified guarantee","-on the SAT instance it builds, $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rho(n')}$ with $n'$ the","-number of SAT variables, and therefore the title's limitation with it:","-at $A = 1$ the exponential factor is above $2^{n'}$ for every","+size at most $k$ (p.~5, lines 239--243): one variable per element and","+two clauses per set give a $k$-CNF with ``one variable per universe","+element, so for fixed $k$ Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential time in the","+universe size, up to a polynomial input-size factor,'' citing","+\\citet{Cygan2016}'s Observation~3.6. Section~\\ref{sec:beyond} tags this","+supported: the encoding is on paper, the counter is the verified one.","+What such a transfer inherits is the verified guarantee on the SAT","+instance it builds, $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rho(n')}$ with $n'$ the number of","+SAT variables, and therefore the title's limitation with it: at $A = 1$","+the exponential factor is above $2^{n'}$ for every"," $50 \\le n' < e^{5504}$ (Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}), with $C, A, d$"," unknown. In general terms: an algorithm obtained from the verified","-statement through a variable-controlling reduction is a $2^{o(n')}$","+statement through a variable-controlling reduction is a subexponential"," algorithm whose verified guarantee is weaker, at every practical size,"," than trying every assignment of the SAT instance it constructs. That is"," not a defect of the transfer; it is the same sentence as the title,"]},{"oldStart":369,"oldLines":8,"newStart":379,"newLines":8,"lines":[" "," \\begin{itemize}"," \\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is","-  refuted}, for any problem, any parameter or any constant $c$. Such a","-  statement, proved from SETH, has lost its proof and kept its truth","+  refuted}, for any problem, any size measure or any constant $c$. Such","+  a statement, proved from SETH, has lost its proof and kept its truth","   value, which is unknown."," \\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' is","   refuted} either, unless $X$ has a reduction to fixed-width SAT that"]},{"oldStart":383,"oldLines":19,"newStart":393,"newLines":20,"lines":[" \\item \\textbf{Nothing about any specific problem's complexity} beyond","   what the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 state and we quote. The","   problems the manuscript lists at lines 213--225 and 233--235 are not","-  discussed here, and no example from any other parameterisation is","-  given, by the scope of this paper.","+  discussed here, and no example from any other setting is given, by","+  the scope of this paper."," \\item \\textbf{No re-reading of the sources.} Each hypothesis is stated","   in the form the manuscript gives it, with the manuscript's citation;","   we did not check that the cited sections and definitions state it in","   the same words. If a source's form is stronger than the manuscript's,","   the refutation of the manuscript's form may not reach it, and the","   reader should check the source.","-\\item \\textbf{Asymptotic only.} Every refutation here is the negation of","-  an asymptotic statement and uses only $\\rhon = o(n)$. None says that","-  any machine decides or counts anything fast at any size; the constants","-  are as unknown here as in Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, and the","-  verified bound is above $2^n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$.","+\\item \\textbf{About the limit only.} Every refutation here denies a","+  statement about the limit and uses only that $\\rhon$ grows more","+  slowly than $n$. None says that any machine decides or counts","+  anything fast at any size; the constants are as unknown here as in","+  Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, and the verified bound is above $2^n$ on","+  every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$."," \\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the verified statement.} The manuscript's","   sharper exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) are not used; where the","   manuscript derives a consequence from them, it is not a consequence"]}],"originalFile":"% What follows, in general terms -- drafted by loop0015 item 05 (2026-11-05).\n% Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the line + 1);\n% every comparator line is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone, read with cat/sed -n\n% only. Every page, line, fragment and the one table of this section are checked\n% and written by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py\n% which exits non-zero if anything has moved. The section is in general terms\n% by the owner's rule (2026-11-05): no example from any width parameter, no\n% problem named beyond what the manuscript's §1.1.1 and §1.1.3 name.\n\\section{What follows, in general terms}\n\\label{sec:consequences}\n\nSections~\\ref{sec:statement}--\\ref{sec:replay} read the verified statement,\ndrew the line between it and the manuscript, inspected the proof and\nreplayed it. This section asks what follows from the verified statement\nalone: which hypotheses it refutes and by what step, and what a lower\nbound that was derived from one of those hypotheses has lost. The\nmanuscript answers both questions itself, in \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 (p.~4--5)\nand \\S1.1.3 (p.~5), and we quote it; what we add is the quantifier form\nof each hypothesis, the one line of the comparator statement that negates\nit, and the distinction, stated in general terms, between a theorem whose\npremise has become false and a statement that has been refuted. Nothing\nhere names a problem the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 do not name, and\nnothing here is about any problem's complexity beyond what those two\nsections state.\n\n\\subsection{The one property every refutation uses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-property}\n\nEvery refutation below rests on the same conjuncts of\n\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (comparator file lines 194--217,\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement}): for each fixed $k \\ge 2$ there is one\ndeterministic machine which, on the canonical encoding of any\n\\texttt{WidthAtMost}~$k$ formula $F$ on $n$ variables (line 38), halts\nwith \\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape (lines 203--204, 147),\nthe bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ on its answer tape and in its halting\nstate (lines 205--207), within $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps (lines\n210--211). Four features of that property do the work.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{It is deterministic.} \\texttt{step} is a function and\n  \\texttt{Trace} is its iteration (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there\n  is no random tape and no nondeterministic choice. A deterministic\n  algorithm is also a bounded-error randomised algorithm, with error\n  $0$, and also a nondeterministic algorithm, with no guess; so each\n  refutation of a deterministic hypothesis is a refutation of its\n  randomised and nondeterministic forms by the same machine.\n\\item \\textbf{The bound is $2^{o(n)}$ in $n$ alone.} A \\texttt{Finset} of\n  clauses of width at most $k$ over $n$ variables has at most\n  $\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j}$ members ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$,\n  $k = 3$), so $m = O(n^k)$ and $L = O(k\\,n\\,m) = O(n^{k+1})$\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); hence the bound is\n  $n^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, and since $\\rhon/n \\to 0$\n  \\leanref{rho-little-o}, for every $\\delta > 0$ there is an\n  $n_0(k, \\delta)$ beyond which it is below $2^{\\delta n}$. Below\n  $n_0$ the bound is a constant depending on $k$ and $\\delta$. So the\n  running time is $O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every $\\delta > 0$,\n  and $2^{\\delta n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ with the polynomial written out. This\n  is an asymptotic fact and it is the only kind of fact the hypotheses\n  are about; it coexists with Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s observation\n  that $\\rhon > n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, which is about where\n  the asymptotics begin.\n\\item \\textbf{One machine per $k$, for every $n$.} The machine and the\n  constants are chosen after $k$ and before $n$ and $F$\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there is no $n \\ge 20$ proviso in the\n  comparator statement, though the manuscript's Theorem~1.1 carries one\n  (p.~2, lines 56--57). So the verified form is the strong, uniform\n  negation: a single algorithm that is $O(2^{\\delta n})$ for every\n  $\\delta$ at once, where a hypothesis of the form ``for every $\\delta$\n  there is an algorithm'' would have allowed a different algorithm for\n  each $\\delta$.\n\\item \\textbf{The model is the conservative one.} The comparator's\n  machine is a multitape Turing machine with one head per tape, moving\n  one cell or writing one symbol per step. Every step of such a machine\n  is a bounded amount of work on any of the standard models in which the\n  hypotheses are stated, so the bound transfers to those models with at\n  most a constant factor per step, which the polynomial absorbs. The\n  input is the manuscript's canonical encoding, of length polynomial in\n  $n + m$; converting from any other reasonable encoding is polynomial\n  too.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses, one by one}\n\\label{sec:consequences-hypotheses}\n\nThe manuscript's own sentence is (p.~4, lines 187--191):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing\nwhether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability\nand unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for\nevery fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise\nproblem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\nrandomized versions of ETH [DHM$^+$14, Section 1] and SETH [CDL$^+$16,\nDefinition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH.''\n\\end{quote}\nand, for counting (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1)\nsolves \\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time\nand therefore refutes \\#ETH.'' We take each hypothesis in the form the\nmanuscript states it, with the manuscript's own source for that form,\nand say which conjunct negates it. Table~\\ref{tab:hypotheses} is the\nsummary. In every case the only thing used is the property of\nSection~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}; nothing is used from the\nmanuscript's sharper exponents, which are not verified\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n\n\\paragraph{ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 165--166): ``The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH)\nasserted that 3-SAT requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time in the\nworst case, and hence so does $k$-SAT for every fixed $k \\ge 3$ [IP01,\nTheorem~1],'' citing \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}. In quantifiers:\nthere is a $\\delta > 0$ such that no deterministic algorithm decides\n3-SAT within $2^{\\delta n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps on all inputs.\nIts negation is: for every $\\delta > 0$ there is one. The verified\nstatement at $k = 3$ supplies, for every $\\delta > 0$, the same machine,\nwhose answer bit is $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$, that is, the decision,\nwithin $O_{\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps\n(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}, item 2). This holds on every\ninput of every size, so it refutes the hypothesis under either reading\nof ``$2^{\\Omega(n)}$'' (a lower bound at infinitely many $n$, or at all\nlarge $n$). In the vocabulary of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, where\n$s_k$ is the infimum of the $\\delta$ for which $k$-SAT has an\n$O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm and ETH is $s_3 > 0$: the verified statement\ngives $s_k = 0$ for every $k \\ge 2$, by one algorithm per $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{SETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 166--169): ``The Strong Exponential Time\nHypothesis (SETH) made the sharper prediction that $k$-SAT requires\n$2^{n(1-o_k(1))}$ time as $k$ grows. Precisely, for every\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$, there is a fixed $k$ for which no deterministic\nalgorithm solves $k$-SAT in $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ time,''\nwith the motivation attributed to \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}'s open\nproblems (lines 169--171). The negation is: there is an\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ such that for every fixed $k$ there is a\ndeterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm. The verified\nstatement gives more: for \\emph{every} $\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ and every\n$k \\ge 2$, the machine for $k$ runs within\n$C_k(L+1)^{d_k}\\,2^{A_k\\rhon}$ steps, and $A_k\\rhon \\le (1-\\varepsilon)n$\nfor all $n \\ge n_0(k, \\varepsilon)$ because $\\rhon = o(n)$; below $n_0$\nthe exponential factor is a constant. So each $k$ has a\n$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ algorithm, and the polynomial's\ndegree depending on $k$ is exactly what the hypothesis's ``for a fixed\n$k$'' permits. The refutation does not pass through ETH; it reads the\nstatement at each $k$. It is also standard that SETH implies ETH: if\n3-SAT had a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm then, by the Sparsification Lemma of\n\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}, which reduces $k$-SAT on $n$ variables\nto $2^{\\varepsilon n}$ instances with $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ clauses and\nso, after the clause-splitting reduction, to 3-SAT on $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$\nvariables, so would $k$-SAT for every fixed $k$; so the refutation of\nETH alone would already refute SETH. We note the implication for the\nrecord; the direct reading above is what the paper rests on.\n\n\\paragraph{Gap-ETH, and its randomised form.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 172--174): ``The Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis\n(Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants $\\delta, \\gamma > 0$\nfor which no $O(2^{\\gamma n})$-time algorithm can distinguish a\nsatisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment violates at least a\n$\\delta$ fraction of the clauses, even when $m = O(n)$ [Din16, MR17],''\nciting \\citet{Dinur2016} and \\citet{ManurangsiRaghavendra2017}; and\n(lines 175--176) ``The randomized version rules out an algorithm with\nthe same running time that distinguishes the two cases with probability\nat least $2/3$ on every promised input [CCK$^+$20, Conjecture 2.5],''\nciting \\citet{Chalermsook2020}. The negation is: for every\n$\\delta, \\gamma > 0$ there is an $O(2^{\\gamma n})$ algorithm that\nseparates the two cases, already at $m = O(n)$. Exact decision separates\nthem, since a formula in which every assignment violates a positive\nfraction of the clauses is unsatisfiable; and the verified machine at\n$k = 3$ decides within $n^{O(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon} = O_{\\gamma}(2^{\\gamma n})$\nsteps for every $\\gamma > 0$, at every $m$ (item 2 above; at $m = O(n)$\nthe polynomial is $O(n^{2d})$). The promise is never used: the machine\nanswers correctly on every input, promised or not, with probability $1$,\nwhich refutes the randomised form by item 1.\n\n\\paragraph{NSETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 181--183): ``The Nondeterministic Strong\nExponential Time Hypothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every\n$\\varepsilon > 0$, some fixed $k$ admits no nondeterministic\n$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}\\allowbreak(n+m)^{O(1)}$-time algorithm for deciding whether a\n$k$-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a $k$-DNF is a\ntautology [CGI$^+$16],'' citing \\citet{Carmosino2016}. The negation is:\nthere is an $\\varepsilon > 0$ such that every fixed $k$ has a\nnondeterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm for\nunsatisfiability. The verified machine's answer bit is \\texttt{false}\nexactly when $\\mathtt{count}\\ F = 0$ (lines 205--207), so it decides\nunsatisfiability deterministically within the SETH bound of the previous\nparagraph for every $\\varepsilon$ and every $k$; and a deterministic\nmachine is a nondeterministic one whose only computation path is the\ndeterministic run, so every acceptance convention for nondeterministic\ntime is met. The manuscript's ``decides both satisfiability and\nunsatisfiability'' (line 188) is this.\n\n\\paragraph{\\#ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 204--205): ``The counting Exponential Time\nHypothesis (\\#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying\nassignments of a 3-CNF requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time\n[DHM$^+$14, Section 1],'' citing \\citet{Dell2014}. The negation is: for\nevery $\\delta > 0$ there is a deterministic $O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm\nthat outputs the exact count. The verified machine at $k = 3$ halts with\n\\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape, a binary word of length\nat most $n + 1$ that decodes to $\\mathtt{count}\\ F$ (lines 203--204,\n208--209), within the same bound. This is the one hypothesis for which\nthe decision bit is not enough and the count tape is what refutes it;\nit is also the hypothesis the comparator statement was written for, the\ncount being the theorem's first conclusion and the decision its\ncorollary.\n\n\\paragraph{The bounded-error randomised ETH and SETH.}\nThe manuscript names them by citation only (p.~4, lines 190--191):\n``[DHM$^+$14, Section 1]'' for ETH \\citep{Dell2014} and ``[CDL$^+$16,\nDefinition 2.1]'' for SETH \\citep{Cygan2016}. In each, the algorithm\nmay be randomised and must be correct with probability bounded away\nfrom $1/2$ on every input. A deterministic algorithm is such an\nalgorithm with error $0$ (item 1), so the refutations of ETH and SETH\nabove refute these forms by the same machine, with no change to the\nbound. We have not re-read the two sources' definitions; we state the\nforms as the manuscript cites them, and a reader who needs the exact\nerror threshold or the exact model of the source should go to the\ncited section and definition.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\caption{The hypotheses the manuscript's \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 list, each in\nthe form the manuscript states it (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of\nthe committed extract), with the manuscript's own source for that form,\nthe quantifier form, and the conjunct of the comparator statement that\nnegates it (lines 194--217 of the comparator file at \\texttt{4eaee875}).\n$A$, $C_k$, $d_k$ are the existential constants of\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement}. Generated, with every page and fragment\nchecked against the extract and the clone, by\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py}.}\n\\label{tab:hypotheses}\n\\scriptsize\n\\input{tables/hypotheses}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{What the manuscript places beside the list.}\nTwo sentences follow the refutation paragraph (p.~4, lines 191--194):\nthe result ``does not conflict with unconditional exponential lower\nbounds for restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13],''\nand ``In 2016, Ryan Williams refuted the Merlin--Arthur version of SETH\n(MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies $k$-CNF unsatisfiability\nin $2^{n/2}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3]''\n\\citep{Williams2016}. Both are context. The first says that a lower\nbound proved unconditionally for a restricted proof system is not a\nstatement about algorithms in general and is not touched; the second\nthat one member of the family was already false before this result.\nNeither is used below.\n\n\\subsection{What a derived lower bound loses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-lower-bounds}\n\nThe manuscript's \\S1.1.3 opens with a list of lower bounds that were\nproved from ETH or from SETH (p.~5, lines 213--225), for problems with\nexponential algorithms and for problems in polynomial time, and then\nsays what its result does to them (lines 226--227):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness\nclaims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from weaker\nhypotheses that may still hold.''\n\\end{quote}\nWe state what that means in general terms, for a theorem of either of\nthe two standard shapes,\n\\begin{align*}\n  \\text{(E)}&\\quad \\text{ETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } 2^{o(n)} \\text{ algorithm for } X,\\\\\n  \\text{(S)}&\\quad \\text{SETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } (c - \\varepsilon)^{k} \\text{ algorithm for } Y \\text{ for any } \\varepsilon > 0,\n\\end{align*}\nwhere $n$ and $k$ are whatever parameter of $X$ and $Y$ the theorem\nnames and $c$ is its constant. Nothing below depends on what $X$ and $Y$\nare.\n\n\\paragraph{The theorem stands; its premise is false; it derives nothing.}\nA theorem of shape (E) or (S) is an implication, proved by a reduction\nfrom $k$-SAT to $X$ or $Y$ that turns a fast algorithm for the target\ninto a fast algorithm for $k$-SAT. The reduction is unaffected by\nanything in the verified statement; the implication remains a theorem.\nWhat has changed is that its premise is now known to be false\n(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-hypotheses}), so the implication can no\nlonger be discharged: it derives nothing about $X$ or $Y$. That is the\nwhole of what ``removes one basis'' means.\n\n\\paragraph{The conclusion is not refuted.}\nA false premise does not make a conclusion false. The statement ``no\n$2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' and the statement ``no\n$(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$'' are untouched by anything in the\ncomparator statement, which speaks about $k$-CNF formulas and nothing\nelse. To refute either, one would need an algorithm for $X$ or $Y$, and\nthe verified statement supplies one only through a reduction in the\nopposite direction to the one the lower bound used. The manuscript says\nexactly this (p.~5, lines 237--238): ``To obtain a faster algorithm for\na target problem from our SAT algorithm, we need a reduction in the\nother direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT, that controls the\nnumber of variables.'' And it places the general caveat on itself at\np.~2, lines 95--99:\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Note that runtime bounds of the form $2^{o(n)}$ do not transfer under\ngeneral polynomial-time reductions ($2^{n^{o(1)}}$ would be needed for\nthis), so our result does not imply that general NP problems admit\nsubexponential time algorithms.''\n\\end{quote}\nSo for shape (E): a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$ follows only from a\nreduction of $X$ to fixed-width SAT in which the number of SAT variables\nis $O(n)$ in $X$'s parameter, and the manuscript gives one such transfer\n(below). For shape (S): a $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$ would\nneed the exponent of the SAT instance to be below $k \\log_2 c$ by a\nconstant factor, and the verified statement's exponent is\n$A\\rhon'$ in the variable count $n'$ of the SAT instance with $A$ unknown\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); no value of $A$ is on record below $257$,\nand no base $c$ is derived from the verified statement for any $Y$. No\nstatement of shape ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is refuted by\nanything in this paper or, so far as we can read, in the manuscript.\n\n\\paragraph{Hardness as a classification.}\nA statement of the form ``$Y$ is SETH-hard'' (or ETH-hard) records that\na reduction exists from $k$-SAT to $Y$ with the stated parameter\ncontrol. The reduction still exists, and the statement is still true as\na statement about reductions. What it used to convey, that a fast\nalgorithm for $Y$ is unlikely because it would refute SETH, it no longer\nconveys: a fast algorithm for $Y$ would refute SETH, and SETH is\nrefuted. The classification is now a fact about reductions and no\nlonger a fact about $Y$'s running time.\n\n\\paragraph{The weaker hypotheses the manuscript names are untouched.}\nThe manuscript gives two (p.~5, lines 229--236). The first is the\nOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture, which it states and then relates to SETH:\n``The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal Vectors shows that SETH implies\nOVC [Wil05, VW19]'' (lines 231--232). That implication is a theorem with\na false premise, like (E) and (S): it says nothing about OVC either way,\nand nothing in the verified statement is about orthogonal vectors. The\nsecond is the width-unrestricted form of SETH, which we quote with the\nmanuscript's list of the problems it covers elided (lines 233--236):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``The lower bounds for [\\ldots] also follow from the hypothesis that\nunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$-time\nalgorithm for any constant $\\delta > 0$ [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem\n1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each fixed clause width $k$,\nwhile this hypothesis allows the width to grow with $n$.''\n\\end{quote}\nThe verified statement cannot reach this hypothesis, and the reason is\nvisible in its quantifiers (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}): the machine\nand the constants $C, A, S, d, e$ are chosen after $k$, and nothing in\nthe statement bounds how they grow with $k$. For a formula whose width\ngrows with $n$ there is no single machine and no single bound in the\nstatement at all; even the trivial observation that every $k$-CNF is a\nCNF runs the wrong way, since the hypothesis quantifies over all widths\nat once. So the unrestricted-width hypothesis, and every lower bound\nthat follows from it, is exactly where it was.\n\n\\paragraph{What a transferred algorithm inherits.}\nThe manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of\nsize at most $k$ (p.~5, lines 239--243): one variable per universe\nelement and two clauses per set give a $k$-CNF with ``one variable per\nuniverse element, so for fixed $k$ Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential\ntime in the universe size, up to a polynomial input-size factor,''\nciting \\citet{Cygan2016}'s Observation~3.6. Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}\ntags this supported: the encoding is on paper, the counter is the\nverified one. What such a transfer inherits is the verified guarantee\non the SAT instance it builds, $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rho(n')}$ with $n'$ the\nnumber of SAT variables, and therefore the title's limitation with it:\nat $A = 1$ the exponential factor is above $2^{n'}$ for every\n$50 \\le n' < e^{5504}$ (Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}), with $C, A, d$\nunknown. In general terms: an algorithm obtained from the verified\nstatement through a variable-controlling reduction is a $2^{o(n')}$\nalgorithm whose verified guarantee is weaker, at every practical size,\nthan trying every assignment of the SAT instance it constructs. That is\nnot a defect of the transfer; it is the same sentence as the title,\nread through a reduction.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:consequences-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is\n  refuted}, for any problem, any parameter or any constant $c$. Such a\n  statement, proved from SETH, has lost its proof and kept its truth\n  value, which is unknown.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' is\n  refuted} either, unless $X$ has a reduction to fixed-width SAT that\n  controls the number of variables; the manuscript names one such\n  problem (Set Splitting) and we add none.\n\\item \\textbf{The unrestricted-width hypothesis is untouched}, and so is\n  the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, and so is every lower bound proved\n  from either; the verified statement is for each fixed $k$ and says\n  nothing about widths that grow with $n$.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about any specific problem's complexity} beyond\n  what the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 state and we quote. The\n  problems the manuscript lists at lines 213--225 and 233--235 are not\n  discussed here, and no example from any other parameterisation is\n  given, by the scope of this paper.\n\\item \\textbf{No re-reading of the sources.} Each hypothesis is stated\n  in the form the manuscript gives it, with the manuscript's citation;\n  we did not check that the cited sections and definitions state it in\n  the same words. If a source's form is stronger than the manuscript's,\n  the refutation of the manuscript's form may not reach it, and the\n  reader should check the source.\n\\item \\textbf{Asymptotic only.} Every refutation here is the negation of\n  an asymptotic statement and uses only $\\rhon = o(n)$. None says that\n  any machine decides or counts anything fast at any size; the constants\n  are as unknown here as in Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, and the\n  verified bound is above $2^n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the verified statement.} The manuscript's\n  sharper exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) are not used; where the\n  manuscript derives a consequence from them, it is not a consequence\n  of what is verified and is not claimed here.\n\\end{itemize}\n","userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SKfLBaUTtGymW22rhfj1WH","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
