in the common scale of the next subsection.\nFigure~\\ref{fig:practitioner} draws the same.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Random 3-CNF at $m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$, twenty seeds per\nsize, one machine, 60~s per call. Columns: $n$ variables; $m$ clauses;\n``sat'', the number of satisfiable instances among those CaDiCaL\nfinished; for each tool, ``fin'', the number of instances it finished\nwithin budget, and ``med'' and ``max'', its median and maximum seconds\nover all twenty seeds with a censored run counted as $> 60$. The last\ntwo columns are the two guarantees as shapes: $2^n$ (trying every\nassignment) and $2^{\\rhon}$ (the verified bound), both with constant\n$1$ and both multiplied by one common factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$ that puts\n$2^n$ through CaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$ ($t_{20} = 1.28 \\times\n10^{-4}$~s). They are shapes, not running times. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n\\label{tab:practitioner}\n\\footnotesize\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n\\input{tables/practitioner}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{practitioner.pdf}\n\\caption{The same data on a logarithmic time axis, where each grid line\nis ten times the one below. Solid lines with markers are medians, dotted\nlines maxima, over the twenty seeds; a tool whose median is censored\nstops at the budget line. The two guarantees are drawn as shapes with\nthe common factor of Table~\\ref{tab:practitioner}, so that $2^n$ passes\nthrough the $n = 20$ measurement and $2^{\\rhon}$ crosses it at $n = 50$,\nwhere the verified exponent overtakes $n$\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The left panel keeps the measured\nseconds in view and lets the guarantees leave the frame; the right\npanel is the same picture on the guarantees' own scale. From\n\\texttt{paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n\\label{fig:practitioner}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\paragraph{The checks.} On every instance where both finished, Ganak's\ncount equals the count by listing (40 instances at $n \\le 30$) and\nCaDiCaL's answer equals ``count $> 0$''; ApproxMC's estimate is within\nits factor $1 + \\varepsilon = 1.8$ of Ganak's on 178 of the 206\ninstances where both finished (it promises that with probability\n$1 - \\delta = 0.8$ per instance). The script exits non-zero if any of\nthese fails.\n\n\\paragraph{What the solvers did.} CaDiCaL's median is under a\nmillisecond up to $n = 80$, 2.5~ms at 100, 58~ms at 200, 1.5~s at 250\nand 3.7~s at 300 (maximum 20.5~s); at 400 it finishes 10 of 20 within\nthe budget and at 500 8 of 20, every finished one satisfiable, which is\nthe known shape of CDCL on random formulas at the threshold (the\nunsatisfiable half is the expensive half). Ganak finishes all 20 up to\n$n = 250$ (median 17.6~s there, maximum 45.7~s), 10 of 20 at 300 and\nnone at 400; its median is flat at 8.3~s and 6.2~s at $n = 100$ and\n150 and lower, 2.4~s, at 200, while its maximum rises throughout. The\nflat stretch looks like a fixed time budget inside the binding's\npreprocessing rather than counting difficulty; we report it as measured\nand tuned nothing (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06d). Where the count is\npositive it is small for the size: at $n = 200$ the fifteen satisfiable\ninstances have between 372 and $2.5 \\times 10^{12}$ solutions out of\n$2^{200}$ assignments. ApproxMC finishes all 20 up to $n = 200$ (median\n0.51~s there), 18 at 250, 11 at 300 and none at 400. The sizes at which\nthe three tools leave the budget, 300 for the counters and 400 for the\ndecision, are where this measurement ends; nothing is claimed beyond\nthem.\n\n\\subsection{The two guarantees beside them}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-guarantees}\n\nThe guarantees are drawn as shapes. Both are taken with constant $1$\n(the polynomial factors, $C$, $A$ and $d$ are all set aside), and both\nare multiplied by the same factor, chosen so that $2^n$ passes through\nCaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$. Because the factor is one number for both\ncurves, their ratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves,\n$2^{\\rhon - n}$: below $1$ for $n < 50$, equal to $1$ at $n = 50$ up to\nrounding, and above $1$ from there (it is $2.8 \\times 10^{3}$ at\n$n = 100$ and $4.0 \\times 10^{51}$ at $n = 500$). Scaling each curve to\nthe $n = 20$ point on its own would instead make the two touch at\n$n = 20$ and put the verified one above from there on; the common\nfactor is the honest choice because it keeps the crossing where the\nstatement puts it (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-scaling};\n\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a).\n\nIn that scale the brute-force guarantee reaches one second near\n$n = 33$ and the verified guarantee near $n = 35$. At $n = 60$, the\nmeasured CaDiCaL median is $3 \\times 10^{-4}$~s, against $1.4 \\times\n10^{8}$~s for $2^n$ and $5.4 \\times 10^{8}$~s for $2^{\\rhon}$: the\nmeasurement is below the two guarantees by factors of $10^{11.6}$ and\n$10^{12.2}$. At $n = 500$ the decision median is above the budget (8 of\n20 finished within 60~s), against $10^{140.6}$~s and $10^{192.2}$~s.\nNothing in those enormous factors is about the verified machine: the\ngap is between what a CDCL solver does on random formulas and what any\nexponential guarantee says, and it would be the same gap for the\nbrute-force guarantee alone. The one fact this section adds to the\ntitle is the order of the three objects: at every size in the table the\nmeasurement is far below $2^n$, and $2^{\\rhon}$ is above $2^n$ from\n$n = 50$ on, so the verified guarantee is the largest of the three at\nevery practical size and the measurement the smallest.\n\nOne remark on what the verified machine's \\emph{running time} would\nhave to be, were it ever run. Nothing in this section bears on it: the\nconstants $C, A, d$ are unknown (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), the\nmachine is a mathematical term, and a statement that something exists\nis not a program. The only values on record are the proof's own,\n$A \\ge 257$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$ (Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and\n\\ref{sec:proof}); a guarantee of $2^{257\\,\\rhon}$ steps is a guarantee\nand not a measurement, and a different proof of the same statement\ncould carry any positive constants.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Timings on one machine, one day, named versions.} The\n  seconds are from one AMD Ryzen 9 9950X shared with other work, under\n  \\texttt{nice -n 19} with eight calls in flight, through Python\n  bindings; they are reproducible in shape and not in value, and the\n  versions are those named in Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner-setup} and\n  in \\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}.\n\\item \\textbf{Random instances only.} Random 3-CNF at the threshold\n  ratio is one distribution; formulas with structure can be harder for\n  CDCL at far smaller $n$ and easier for counters. Nothing is claimed\n  about any other distribution, and nothing about worst-case behaviour.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the verified machine's running time.} It is\n  unknown, in both directions; no implementation exists to run; the\n  guarantees drawn are at constant $1$ and the constants are never\n  computed.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about $k > 3$.} The statement is for every fixed\n  $k$; the measurement is at $k = 3$.\n\\item \\textbf{The counters are reported, not tuned.} Ganak ran through\n  its binding at defaults, preprocessing included; ApproxMC at its\n  default tolerance; a different configuration gives different seconds.\n  The check against listing is at $n \\le 30$ only.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement about which tool a practitioner should use.}\n  The section shows that the verified guarantee is not the binding one;\n  it does not rank solvers.\n\\end{itemize}\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017PMzYZcaTEfLV67caF5A4b","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec7_practitioner.tex has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"b4a570b0-3d81-445b-8517-9ec3bf92acad","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:42:02.472Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec7_practitioner.tex","content":"% For the practitioner -- drafted by loop0015 item 06 (2026-11-05), rewritten in\n% plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05).\n% Every number of this section is produced by\n%   nice -n 19 python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py --regenerate   (the measurement)\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py                           (table, figure, stdout)\n% from random 3-CNF the script generates; no MOSP or pathwidth instance, nothing\n% under solutions/. Timings are from one machine on one day with named\n% versions (tables/practitioner_meta.json). The two guarantee curves are shapes\n% at constant 1, and nothing here is a running time of the verified machine.\n% The arithmetic of the scaling is in Appendix B.\n\\section{For the practitioner}\n\\label{sec:practitioner}\n\nThe title compares two guarantees. One is the verified bound,\n$C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, with $C$, $A$ and $d$ never computed (comparator\nfile lines 136--139 and 210--211, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The\nother is the trivial bound for trying every assignment, $2^n$ times a\npolynomial. Neither is what a practitioner runs. What runs on a real\nmachine, when the question is whether a formula can be satisfied, is a\n\\emph{SAT solver}; the standard kind is called CDCL, for\nconflict-driven clause learning, a search that learns from each dead\nend it reaches. When the question is how many satisfying assignments\nthere are, what runs is a \\emph{model counter}, exact or approximate.\nNo implementation of the verified machine exists to run beside them.\nThis section measures the tools a practitioner has, on random formulas,\nand draws the two guarantees next to the measurements as shapes, so\nthat the reader can see where each sits. The point is small and the\nsection says it once: the verified guarantee is not the one that\nmatters on any machine, because it is above $2^n$ for every $n \\ge 50$\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), while the solvers are many orders of\nmagnitude below $2^n$ at every size they were run at.\n\n\\subsection{Why the comparison is between guarantees and measurements}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-why}\n\nThe verified statement says a machine \\emph{exists} (comparator line 195\nopens with the existence quantifier, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The\nsolution's 3,977 files contain no command to run anything: no\n\\texttt{\\#eval}, no \\texttt{def main} and no input-output action. The\nscript of this section checks that, reading every file of the family\nwith \\texttt{open()} and counting those three patterns: 0, 0, 0. The\nlibrary proves that a machine with the stated bound exists and exhibits\none as a mathematical term, inside a development with 1,048\n\\texttt{noncomputable} declarations and no way to execute anything\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); nothing in the clone runs it on an input, and\nwe found no implementation elsewhere. So the verified algorithm's actual\nrunning time on any formula is unknown, in both directions: nothing says\nit is slow, and nothing says it is fast. What is known is its\n\\emph{guarantee}, and a guarantee is compared with other guarantees. The\nmeasurements below are of other programs entirely. They are in the\nsection because the question a practitioner asks is not ``which\nguarantee is smaller'' but ``what would I run'', and the answer to that\nhas not changed.\n\n\\subsection{What was run}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-setup}\n\n\\paragraph{Instances.} Random 3-CNF on $n$ variables with $m = \\lfloor\n4.26\\,n \\rceil$ clauses, each clause three distinct variables with\nrandom signs, generated by the script from $(n, \\mathrm{seed})$; twenty\nseeds per size; $n = 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300,\n400, 500$. The ratio $4.26$ clauses per variable is where random 3-SAT\nswitches from mostly satisfiable to mostly unsatisfiable\n\\citep{Selman1996Hard}; random formulas are hardest to decide there\n(the cavity method puts the threshold at $4.2667$,\n\\citealp{Mertens2006Threshold}). The sizes are chosen so that the\nsmallest ones are where the title's crossing is ($n = 50$) and the\nlargest are where a CDCL solver on random formulas begins to take\nseconds. These are random instances only; nothing is claimed about\nformulas with structure.\n\n\\paragraph{Tools.} CaDiCaL 1.9.5 \\citep{Biere2024CaDiCaL}, a CDCL solver,\nthrough python-sat 1.9.dev15 \\citep{Ignatiev2018PySAT}, one\n\\texttt{solve()} call, for the yes-or-no answer; Ganak 2.8.0\n\\citep{Sharma2019Ganak}, an exact model counter, through the pyganak\nbinding (which runs its Arjun preprocessing and then the counter), for\nthe exact count; ApproxMC 4.4.0 \\citep{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC,\nSoos2019BIRD}, an approximate counter, at its defaults $\\varepsilon =\n0.8$, $\\delta = 0.2$ (an answer within a factor $1.8$ of the truth with\nprobability $0.8$), with the projection set equal to all variables; and,\nat $n \\le 30$ only, exact counting by listing every solution with\nCaDiCaL, one at a time, which is not a counter at any larger size (its\ntime grows with the count, and the count grows exponentially in $n$)\nand is here as the independent check of the two counters where it can\nreach. Ganak and ApproxMC were installed from PyPI for this section; no\n\\texttt{sharpSAT}, \\texttt{d4} or other counter binary is on the host.\n\n\\paragraph{Protocol.} Each call runs in its own interpreter under\n\\texttt{nice -n 19}, at most eight at a time on the thirty-two cores of\nan AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (Linux 7.0.0, Python 3.12.13; the machine was\nshared with other work, load average at the start in\n\\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}), with a wall-clock budget of\n60~s per call. The time recorded is the wall clock around the\nconstruction of the solver object and the one call, inside the child\nprocess, with the interpreter's start-up and the import outside it. A\ncall over budget is killed and recorded as \\emph{censored}, that is, as\n``more than 60~s''. A size at which a tool finishes fewer than ten of\nits twenty instances ends that tool's climb to larger sizes. The whole\nrun took about twenty minutes (1,172~s).\n\n\\subsection{What came out}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-results}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:practitioner} gives, per size, the number of satisfiable\ninstances, CaDiCaL's median and maximum over the twenty seeds, the\nnumber of instances each counter finished with its median and maximum\n(a median over all twenty with censored runs counted as above the\nbudget, so a median is a number whenever more than ten finished), and\nthe two guarantees in the common scale of the next subsection.\nFigure~\\ref{fig:practitioner} draws the same.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Random 3-CNF at $m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$, twenty seeds per\nsize, one machine, 60~s per call. Columns: $n$ variables; $m$ clauses;\n``sat'', the number of satisfiable instances among those CaDiCaL\nfinished; for each tool, ``fin'', the number of instances it finished\nwithin budget, and ``med'' and ``max'', its median and maximum seconds\nover all twenty seeds with a censored run counted as $> 60$. The last\ntwo columns are the two guarantees as shapes: $2^n$ (trying every\nassignment) and $2^{\\rhon}$ (the verified bound), both with constant\n$1$ and both multiplied by one common factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$ that puts\n$2^n$ through CaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$ ($t_{20} = 1.28 \\times\n10^{-4}$~s). They are shapes, not running times. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n\\label{tab:practitioner}\n\\footnotesize\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n\\input{tables/practitioner}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{practitioner.pdf}\n\\caption{The same data on a logarithmic time axis, where each grid line\nis ten times the one below. Solid lines with markers are medians, dotted\nlines maxima, over the twenty seeds; a tool whose median is censored\nstops at the budget line. The two guarantees are drawn as shapes with\nthe common factor of Table~\\ref{tab:practitioner}, so that $2^n$ passes\nthrough the $n = 20$ measurement and $2^{\\rhon}$ crosses it at $n = 50$,\nwhere the verified exponent overtakes $n$\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The left panel keeps the measured\nseconds in view and lets the guarantees leave the frame; the right\npanel is the same picture on the guarantees' own scale. From\n\\texttt{paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n\\label{fig:practitioner}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\paragraph{The checks.} On every instance where both finished, Ganak's\ncount equals the count by listing (40 instances at $n \\le 30$) and\nCaDiCaL's answer equals ``count $> 0$''; ApproxMC's estimate is within\nits factor $1 + \\varepsilon = 1.8$ of Ganak's on 178 of the 206\ninstances where both finished (it promises that with probability\n$1 - \\delta = 0.8$ per instance). The script exits non-zero if any of\nthese fails.\n\n\\paragraph{What the solvers did.} CaDiCaL's median is under a\nmillisecond up to $n = 80$, 2.5~ms at 100, 58~ms at 200, 1.5~s at 250\nand 3.7~s at 300 (maximum 20.5~s); at 400 it finishes 10 of 20 within\nthe budget and at 500 8 of 20, every finished one satisfiable, which is\nthe known shape of CDCL on random formulas at the threshold (the\nunsatisfiable half is the expensive half). Ganak finishes all 20 up to\n$n = 250$ (median 17.6~s there, maximum 45.7~s), 10 of 20 at 300 and\nnone at 400; its median is flat at 8.3~s and 6.2~s at $n = 100$ and\n150 and lower, 2.4~s, at 200, while its maximum rises throughout. The\nflat stretch looks like a fixed time budget inside the binding's\npreprocessing rather than counting difficulty; we report it as measured\nand tuned nothing (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06d). Where the count is\npositive it is small for the size: at $n = 200$ the fifteen satisfiable\ninstances have between 372 and $2.5 \\times 10^{12}$ solutions out of\n$2^{200}$ assignments. ApproxMC finishes all 20 up to $n = 200$ (median\n0.51~s there), 18 at 250, 11 at 300 and none at 400. The sizes at which\nthe three tools leave the budget, 300 for the counters and 400 for the\ndecision, are where this measurement ends; nothing is claimed beyond\nthem.\n\n\\subsection{The two guarantees beside them}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-guarantees}\n\nThe guarantees are drawn as shapes. Both are taken with constant $1$\n(the polynomial factors, $C$, $A$ and $d$ are all set aside), and both\nare multiplied by the same factor, chosen so that $2^n$ passes through\nCaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$. Because the factor is one number for both\ncurves, their ratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves,\n$2^{\\rhon - n}$: below $1$ for $n < 50$, equal to $1$ at $n = 50$ up to\nrounding, and above $1$ from there (it is $2.8 \\times 10^{3}$ at\n$n = 100$ and $4.0 \\times 10^{51}$ at $n = 500$). Scaling each curve to\nthe $n = 20$ point on its own would instead make the two touch at\n$n = 20$ and put the verified one above from there on; the common\nfactor is the honest choice because it keeps the crossing where the\nstatement puts it (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-scaling};\n\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a).\n\nIn that scale the brute-force guarantee reaches one second near\n$n = 33$ and the verified guarantee near $n = 35$. At $n = 60$, the\nmeasured CaDiCaL median is $3 \\times 10^{-4}$~s, against $1.4 \\times\n10^{8}$~s for $2^n$ and $5.4 \\times 10^{8}$~s for $2^{\\rhon}$: the\nmeasurement is below the two guarantees by factors of $10^{11.6}$ and\n$10^{12.2}$. At $n = 500$ the decision median is above the budget (8 of\n20 finished within 60~s), against $10^{140.6}$~s and $10^{192.2}$~s.\nNothing in those enormous factors is about the verified machine: the\ngap is between what a CDCL solver does on random formulas and what any\nexponential guarantee says, and it would be the same gap for the\nbrute-force guarantee alone. The one fact this section adds to the\ntitle is the order of the three objects: at every size in the table the\nmeasurement is far below $2^n$, and $2^{\\rhon}$ is above $2^n$ from\n$n = 50$ on, so the verified guarantee is the largest of the three at\nevery practical size and the measurement the smallest.\n\nOne remark on what the verified machine's \\emph{running time} would\nhave to be, were it ever run. Nothing in this section bears on it: the\nconstants $C, A, d$ are unknown (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), the\nmachine is a mathematical term, and a statement that something exists\nis not a program. The only values on record are the proof's own,\n$A \\ge 257$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$ (Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and\n\\ref{sec:proof}); a guarantee of $2^{257\\,\\rhon}$ steps is a guarantee\nand not a measurement, and a different proof of the same statement\ncould carry any positive constants.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Timings on one machine, one day, named versions.} The\n  seconds are from one AMD Ryzen 9 9950X shared with other work, under\n  \\texttt{nice -n 19} with eight calls in flight, through Python\n  bindings; they are reproducible in shape and not in value, and the\n  versions are those named in Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner-setup} and\n  in \\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}.\n\\item \\textbf{Random instances only.} Random 3-CNF at the threshold\n  ratio is one distribution; formulas with structure can be harder for\n  CDCL at far smaller $n$ and easier for counters. Nothing is claimed\n  about any other distribution, and nothing about worst-case behaviour.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the verified machine's running time.} It is\n  unknown, in both directions; no implementation exists to run; the\n  guarantees drawn are at constant $1$ and the constants are never\n  computed.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about $k > 3$.} The statement is for every fixed\n  $k$; the measurement is at $k = 3$.\n\\item \\textbf{The counters are reported, not tuned.} Ganak ran through\n  its binding at defaults, preprocessing included; ApproxMC at its\n  default tolerance; a different configuration gives different seconds.\n  The check against listing is at $n \\le 30$ only.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement about which tool a practitioner should use.}\n  The section shows that the verified guarantee is not the binding one;\n  it does not rank solvers.\n\\end{itemize}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":4,"newStart":1,"newLines":5,"lines":["-% For the practitioner -- drafted by loop0015 item 06 (2026-11-05).","+% For the practitioner -- drafted by loop0015 item 06 (2026-11-05), rewritten in","+% plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05)."," % Every number of this section is produced by"," %   nice -n 19 python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py --regenerate   (the measurement)"," %   python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py                           (table, figure, stdout)"]},{"oldStart":6,"oldLines":42,"newStart":7,"newLines":47,"lines":[" % under solutions/. Timings are from one machine on one day with named"," % versions (tables/practitioner_meta.json). The two guarantee curves are shapes"," % at constant 1, and nothing here is a running time of the verified machine.","+% The arithmetic of the scaling is in Appendix B."," \\section{For the practitioner}"," \\label{sec:practitioner}"," ","-The title compares two guarantees: the verified one,","-$C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ with $C, A, d$ existential (comparator file lines","-136--139 and 210--211, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), and the trivial one,","-$2^n \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ for trying every assignment. Neither is what","-a practitioner runs. What runs on a machine, for the decision bit, is a","-conflict-driven clause-learning (CDCL) solver, and for the count an exact","-or approximate model counter; and no implementation of the verified","-machine exists to run beside them. This section measures the tools a","-practitioner has, on random 3-CNF, and draws the two guarantees next to","-the measurements as shapes, so that the reader can see where each sits.","-The point is small and the section says it once: the verified guarantee","-is not the binding one on any machine, because it is above $2^n$ for","-every $n \\ge 50$ (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}) while the solvers are many","-orders of magnitude below $2^n$ at every size they were run at.","+The title compares two guarantees. One is the verified bound,","+$C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, with $C$, $A$ and $d$ never computed (comparator","+file lines 136--139 and 210--211, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The","+other is the trivial bound for trying every assignment, $2^n$ times a","+polynomial. Neither is what a practitioner runs. What runs on a real","+machine, when the question is whether a formula can be satisfied, is a","+\\emph{SAT solver}; the standard kind is called CDCL, for","+conflict-driven clause learning, a search that learns from each dead","+end it reaches. When the question is how many satisfying assignments","+there are, what runs is a \\emph{model counter}, exact or approximate.","+No implementation of the verified machine exists to run beside them.","+This section measures the tools a practitioner has, on random formulas,","+and draws the two guarantees next to the measurements as shapes, so","+that the reader can see where each sits. The point is small and the","+section says it once: the verified guarantee is not the one that","+matters on any machine, because it is above $2^n$ for every $n \\ge 50$","+(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), while the solvers are many orders of","+magnitude below $2^n$ at every size they were run at."," "," \\subsection{Why the comparison is between guarantees and measurements}"," \\label{sec:practitioner-why}"," ","-The verified statement is existential in the machine (comparator line","-195 opens with \\texttt{$\\exists$ (machine : \\ldots)}, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}) and","-the solution's","-3,977 files contain no \\texttt{\\#eval}, no \\texttt{def main} and no","-\\texttt{IO} action (checked read-only by the script, which reads every","-file of the family with \\texttt{open()} and counts those three patterns:","-0, 0, 0). The library proves that a machine with the stated bound","-exists and exhibits one as a Lean term inside a development with 1,048","-\\texttt{noncomputable} declarations and no executable interface","+The verified statement says a machine \\emph{exists} (comparator line 195","+opens with the existence quantifier, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The","+solution's 3,977 files contain no command to run anything: no","+\\texttt{\\#eval}, no \\texttt{def main} and no input-output action. The","+script of this section checks that, reading every file of the family","+with \\texttt{open()} and counting those three patterns: 0, 0, 0. The","+library proves that a machine with the stated bound exists and exhibits","+one as a mathematical term, inside a development with 1,048","+\\texttt{noncomputable} declarations and no way to execute anything"," (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); nothing in the clone runs it on an input, and"," we found no implementation elsewhere. So the verified algorithm's actual"," running time on any formula is unknown, in both directions: nothing says"," it is slow, and nothing says it is fast. What is known is its"," \\emph{guarantee}, and a guarantee is compared with other guarantees. The","-measurements below are of other programs entirely; they are in the","+measurements below are of other programs entirely. They are in the"," section because the question a practitioner asks is not ``which"," guarantee is smaller'' but ``what would I run'', and the answer to that"," has not changed."]},{"oldStart":49,"oldLines":68,"newStart":55,"newLines":74,"lines":[" \\subsection{What was run}"," \\label{sec:practitioner-setup}"," ","-\\paragraph{Instances.} Random 3-CNF on $n$ variables with","-$m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$ clauses, each clause three distinct variables","-with uniform signs, generated by the script from $(n, \\mathrm{seed})$;","-twenty seeds per size; $n = 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250,","-300, 400, 500$. The ratio $4.26$ is the empirical satisfiability","-threshold of random 3-SAT \\citep{Selman1996Hard}, where decision is","-hardest for a given $n$ among random formulas (the cavity method puts the","-threshold at $4.2667$, \\citealp{Mertens2006Threshold}); the sizes are","-chosen so that the smallest ones are where the title's crossing is","-($n = 50$) and the largest are where a CDCL solver on random formulas","-begins to take seconds. These are random instances only; nothing is","-claimed about structured formulas.","+\\paragraph{Instances.} Random 3-CNF on $n$ variables with $m = \\lfloor","+4.26\\,n \\rceil$ clauses, each clause three distinct variables with","+random signs, generated by the script from $(n, \\mathrm{seed})$; twenty","+seeds per size; $n = 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300,","+400, 500$. The ratio $4.26$ clauses per variable is where random 3-SAT","+switches from mostly satisfiable to mostly unsatisfiable","+\\citep{Selman1996Hard}; random formulas are hardest to decide there","+(the cavity method puts the threshold at $4.2667$,","+\\citealp{Mertens2006Threshold}). The sizes are chosen so that the","+smallest ones are where the title's crossing is ($n = 50$) and the","+largest are where a CDCL solver on random formulas begins to take","+seconds. These are random instances only; nothing is claimed about","+formulas with structure."," ","-\\paragraph{Tools.} CaDiCaL 1.9.5 \\citep{Biere2024CaDiCaL} through","-python-sat 1.9.dev15 \\citep{Ignatiev2018PySAT}, one \\texttt{solve()} call,","-for the decision bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$; Ganak 2.8.0","-\\citep{Sharma2019Ganak}, through the pyganak binding (which runs its Arjun","-preprocessing and then the counter), for the exact count; ApproxMC 4.4.0","-\\citep{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC, Soos2019BIRD} at its defaults","-$\\varepsilon = 0.8$, $\\delta = 0.2$ with the projection set equal to all","-variables, for an approximate count; and, at $n \\le 30$ only, exact","-counting by enumeration over CaDiCaL with a blocking clause per model,","-which is not a counter at any larger size (it is linear in the count,","-and the count is exponential in $n$) and is here as the independent check","-of the two counters where it can reach. Ganak and ApproxMC were installed","-from PyPI for this section; no \\texttt{sharpSAT}, \\texttt{d4} or other","-counter binary is on the host.","+\\paragraph{Tools.} CaDiCaL 1.9.5 \\citep{Biere2024CaDiCaL}, a CDCL solver,","+through python-sat 1.9.dev15 \\citep{Ignatiev2018PySAT}, one","+\\texttt{solve()} call, for the yes-or-no answer; Ganak 2.8.0","+\\citep{Sharma2019Ganak}, an exact model counter, through the pyganak","+binding (which runs its Arjun preprocessing and then the counter), for","+the exact count; ApproxMC 4.4.0 \\citep{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC,","+Soos2019BIRD}, an approximate counter, at its defaults $\\varepsilon =","+0.8$, $\\delta = 0.2$ (an answer within a factor $1.8$ of the truth with","+probability $0.8$), with the projection set equal to all variables; and,","+at $n \\le 30$ only, exact counting by listing every solution with","+CaDiCaL, one at a time, which is not a counter at any larger size (its","+time grows with the count, and the count grows exponentially in $n$)","+and is here as the independent check of the two counters where it can","+reach. Ganak and ApproxMC were installed from PyPI for this section; no","+\\texttt{sharpSAT}, \\texttt{d4} or other counter binary is on the host."," "," \\paragraph{Protocol.} Each call runs in its own interpreter under"," \\texttt{nice -n 19}, at most eight at a time on the thirty-two cores of","-an AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (Linux 7.0.0, Python 3.12.13; the machine was shared","-with other work, load average at the start in","+an AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (Linux 7.0.0, Python 3.12.13; the machine was","+shared with other work, load average at the start in"," \\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}), with a wall-clock budget of","-60~s per call; the time recorded is \\texttt{perf\\_counter} around the","-construction of the solver object and the one call, inside the child,","-with the interpreter's start-up and the import outside it. A call over","-budget is killed and recorded as censored; a size at which a tool finishes","-fewer than ten of its twenty instances ends that tool's escalation. The","-whole run took about twenty minutes (1,172~s) minutes.","+60~s per call. The time recorded is the wall clock around the","+construction of the solver object and the one call, inside the child","+process, with the interpreter's start-up and the import outside it. A","+call over budget is killed and recorded as \\emph{censored}, that is, as","+``more than 60~s''. A size at which a tool finishes fewer than ten of","+its twenty instances ends that tool's climb to larger sizes. The whole","+run took about twenty minutes (1,172~s)."," "," \\subsection{What came out}"," \\label{sec:practitioner-results}"," "," Table~\\ref{tab:practitioner} gives, per size, the number of satisfiable","-instances, CaDiCaL's median and maximum over the twenty seeds, the number","-of instances each counter finished with its median and maximum (a median","-over all twenty with censored runs counted as above the budget, so a","-median is a number whenever more than ten finished), and the two","-guarantees in the common scale of the next subsection.","+instances, CaDiCaL's median and maximum over the twenty seeds, the","+number of instances each counter finished with its median and maximum","+(a median over all twenty with censored runs counted as above the","+budget, so a median is a number whenever more than ten finished), and","+the two guarantees in the common scale of the next subsection."," Figure~\\ref{fig:practitioner} draws the same."," "," \\begin{table}[htbp]"," \\centering"," \\caption{Random 3-CNF at $m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$, twenty seeds per","-size, one machine, 60~s per call. ``sat'' is the number of satisfiable","-instances among those CaDiCaL finished; ``fin'' the number a counter finished within budget; ``med''","-and ``max'' are seconds over all twenty seeds with a censored run counted","-as $> 60$. The last two columns are the brute-force and the verified","-guarantees at constant~1, $2^n$ and $2^{\\rhon}$, multiplied by the one","-common factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$ that puts $2^n$ through CaDiCaL's median at","-$n = 20$ ($t_{20} = 1.28 \\times 10^{-4}$~s); they are shapes, not running times.","-Regenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}","+size, one machine, 60~s per call. Columns: $n$ variables; $m$ clauses;","+``sat'', the number of satisfiable instances among those CaDiCaL","+finished; for each tool, ``fin'', the number of instances it finished","+within budget, and ``med'' and ``max'', its median and maximum seconds","+over all twenty seeds with a censored run counted as $> 60$. The last","+two columns are the two guarantees as shapes: $2^n$ (trying every","+assignment) and $2^{\\rhon}$ (the verified bound), both with constant","+$1$ and both multiplied by one common factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$ that puts","+$2^n$ through CaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$ ($t_{20} = 1.28 \\times","+10^{-4}$~s). They are shapes, not running times. Regenerate:","+\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}"," \\label{tab:practitioner}"," \\footnotesize\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}"," \\input{tables/practitioner}"]},{"oldStart":119,"oldLines":23,"newStart":131,"newLines":27,"lines":[" \\begin{figure}[htbp]"," \\centering"," \\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{practitioner.pdf}","-\\caption{The same data on a logarithmic time axis. Solid lines with","-markers are medians, dotted lines maxima, over the twenty seeds; a tool","-whose median is censored stops at the budget line. The two guarantees are","-drawn at constant~1 with the common factor of Table~\\ref{tab:practitioner},","-so that $2^n$ passes through the $n = 20$ measurement and $2^{\\rhon}$","-crosses it at $n = 50$, where $\\rhon \\ge n$ begins","-(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). From \\texttt{paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}","+\\caption{The same data on a logarithmic time axis, where each grid line","+is ten times the one below. Solid lines with markers are medians, dotted","+lines maxima, over the twenty seeds; a tool whose median is censored","+stops at the budget line. The two guarantees are drawn as shapes with","+the common factor of Table~\\ref{tab:practitioner}, so that $2^n$ passes","+through the $n = 20$ measurement and $2^{\\rhon}$ crosses it at $n = 50$,","+where the verified exponent overtakes $n$","+(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The left panel keeps the measured","+seconds in view and lets the guarantees leave the frame; the right","+panel is the same picture on the guarantees' own scale. From","+\\texttt{paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}"," \\label{fig:practitioner}"," \\end{figure}"," "," \\paragraph{The checks.} On every instance where both finished, Ganak's","-count equals the enumeration's (40 instances at $n \\le 30$) and","-CaDiCaL's bit equals $[\\mathrm{count} > 0]$; ApproxMC's estimate is","-within its factor $1 + \\varepsilon = 1.8$ of Ganak's on 178 of the","-206 instances where both finished (it promises that with","-probability $1 - \\delta = 0.8$ per instance). The script exits non-zero","-if any of these fails.","+count equals the count by listing (40 instances at $n \\le 30$) and","+CaDiCaL's answer equals ``count $> 0$''; ApproxMC's estimate is within","+its factor $1 + \\varepsilon = 1.8$ of Ganak's on 178 of the 206","+instances where both finished (it promises that with probability","+$1 - \\delta = 0.8$ per instance). The script exits non-zero if any of","+these fails."," "," \\paragraph{What the solvers did.} CaDiCaL's median is under a"," millisecond up to $n = 80$, 2.5~ms at 100, 58~ms at 200, 1.5~s at 250"]},{"oldStart":145,"oldLines":59,"newStart":161,"newLines":60,"lines":[" unsatisfiable half is the expensive half). Ganak finishes all 20 up to"," $n = 250$ (median 17.6~s there, maximum 45.7~s), 10 of 20 at 300 and"," none at 400; its median is flat at 8.3~s and 6.2~s at $n = 100$ and","-150 and lower, 2.4~s, at 200, while its maximum rises throughout ---","-the plateau looks like a fixed budget inside the binding's","-preprocessing rather than counting difficulty, and we report it as","-measured and tuned nothing (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06d). Where the","-count is positive it is small for the size: at $n = 200$ the fifteen","-satisfiable instances have between 372 and $2.5 \\times 10^{12}$ models","-out of $2^{200}$. ApproxMC finishes all 20 up to $n = 200$ (median","+150 and lower, 2.4~s, at 200, while its maximum rises throughout. The","+flat stretch looks like a fixed time budget inside the binding's","+preprocessing rather than counting difficulty; we report it as measured","+and tuned nothing (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06d). Where the count is","+positive it is small for the size: at $n = 200$ the fifteen satisfiable","+instances have between 372 and $2.5 \\times 10^{12}$ solutions out of","+$2^{200}$ assignments. ApproxMC finishes all 20 up to $n = 200$ (median"," 0.51~s there), 18 at 250, 11 at 300 and none at 400. The sizes at which","-the three tools leave the budget --- 300 for the counters, 400 for the","-decision --- are where this measurement ends; nothing is claimed beyond","+the three tools leave the budget, 300 for the counters and 400 for the","+decision, are where this measurement ends; nothing is claimed beyond"," them."," "," \\subsection{The two guarantees beside them}"," \\label{sec:practitioner-guarantees}"," ","-The guarantees are drawn as shapes. Both are taken at constant 1 --- the","-polynomial factors, $C$, $A$ and $d$ are all set aside --- and both are","-multiplied by the same factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$, where $t_{20}$ is","-CaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$. The factor is one number for both curves,","-so their ratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves,","-$2^{\\rhon - n}$: below 1 for $n < 50$, equal to 1 at $n = 50$ up to the","-rounding of $\\rho(50) = 50.13$, and above 1 from there (it is $2.8 \\times 10^{3}$ at","-$n = 100$ and $4.0 \\times 10^{51}$ at $n = 500$). A reader who prefers each curve","-scaled to the $n = 20$ point on its own would multiply $2^{\\rhon}$ by a","-further $2^{20 - \\rho(20)} = 2^{2.79}$ and see the two curves touch at","-$n = 20$ and the verified one above the trivial one from there on; the","-common factor is the honest choice because it keeps the crossing where","-the statement puts it (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a).","+The guarantees are drawn as shapes. Both are taken with constant $1$","+(the polynomial factors, $C$, $A$ and $d$ are all set aside), and both","+are multiplied by the same factor, chosen so that $2^n$ passes through","+CaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$. Because the factor is one number for both","+curves, their ratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves,","+$2^{\\rhon - n}$: below $1$ for $n < 50$, equal to $1$ at $n = 50$ up to","+rounding, and above $1$ from there (it is $2.8 \\times 10^{3}$ at","+$n = 100$ and $4.0 \\times 10^{51}$ at $n = 500$). Scaling each curve to","+the $n = 20$ point on its own would instead make the two touch at","+$n = 20$ and put the verified one above from there on; the common","+factor is the honest choice because it keeps the crossing where the","+statement puts it (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-scaling};","+\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a)."," "," In that scale the brute-force guarantee reaches one second near","-$n = 33$ and the verified guarantee near $n = 35$; at $n = 60$,","-the largest size of the title's practical range that this section's","-smallest tools were run at, the measured CaDiCaL median is $3 \\times 10^{-4}$ s against","-$2^n$ at $1.4 \\times 10^{8}$ s and $2^{\\rhon}$ at $5.4 \\times 10^{8}$ s, 11.6 and 12.2 decades","-respectively; at $n = 500$ the decision median is above the budget (8 of 20 finished within 60~s), against","-$10^{140.6}$ s and $10^{192.2}$ s. Nothing in those decades is about the","-verified machine: the gap is between what a CDCL solver does on random","-formulas and what any $2^{\\Theta(n)}$ guarantee says, and it would be","-the same gap for the brute-force guarantee alone. The one fact this","-section adds to the title is the order of the three objects: at every","-size in the table the measurement is far below $2^n$, and $2^{\\rhon}$ is","-above $2^n$ from $n = 50$ on, so the verified guarantee is the largest","-of the three at every practical size and the measurement the smallest.","+$n = 33$ and the verified guarantee near $n = 35$. At $n = 60$, the","+measured CaDiCaL median is $3 \\times 10^{-4}$~s, against $1.4 \\times","+10^{8}$~s for $2^n$ and $5.4 \\times 10^{8}$~s for $2^{\\rhon}$: the","+measurement is below the two guarantees by factors of $10^{11.6}$ and","+$10^{12.2}$. At $n = 500$ the decision median is above the budget (8 of","+20 finished within 60~s), against $10^{140.6}$~s and $10^{192.2}$~s.","+Nothing in those enormous factors is about the verified machine: the","+gap is between what a CDCL solver does on random formulas and what any","+exponential guarantee says, and it would be the same gap for the","+brute-force guarantee alone. The one fact this section adds to the","+title is the order of the three objects: at every size in the table the","+measurement is far below $2^n$, and $2^{\\rhon}$ is above $2^n$ from","+$n = 50$ on, so the verified guarantee is the largest of the three at","+every practical size and the measurement the smallest."," ","-One remark on what the verified machine's \\emph{running time} would have","-to be, were it ever run. Nothing in this section bears on it: the","+One remark on what the verified machine's \\emph{running time} would","+have to be, were it ever run. Nothing in this section bears on it: the"," constants $C, A, d$ are unknown (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), the","-machine is a Lean term, and an existential statement is not an","-implementation. The only values on record are the proof's own, $A \\ge","-257$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$ (Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and","+machine is a mathematical term, and a statement that something exists","+is not a program. The only values on record are the proof's own,","+$A \\ge 257$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$ (Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and"," \\ref{sec:proof}); a guarantee of $2^{257\\,\\rhon}$ steps is a guarantee","-and not a measurement, and a different proof of the same statement could","-carry any positive constants.","+and not a measurement, and a different proof of the same statement","+could carry any positive constants."," "," \\subsection{What this does not claim}"," \\label{sec:practitioner-not}"]},{"oldStart":209,"oldLines":20,"newStart":226,"newLines":21,"lines":["   bindings; they are reproducible in shape and not in value, and the","   versions are those named in Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner-setup} and","   in \\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}.","-\\item \\textbf{Random instances only.} Random 3-CNF at the threshold ratio","-  is one distribution; structured formulas can be harder for CDCL at","-  far smaller $n$ and easier for counters. Nothing is claimed about any","-  other distribution, and nothing about worst-case behaviour.","+\\item \\textbf{Random instances only.} Random 3-CNF at the threshold","+  ratio is one distribution; formulas with structure can be harder for","+  CDCL at far smaller $n$ and easier for counters. Nothing is claimed","+  about any other distribution, and nothing about worst-case behaviour."," \\item \\textbf{Nothing about the verified machine's running time.} It is","   unknown, in both directions; no implementation exists to run; the","-  guarantees drawn are at constant 1 and the constants are existential.","+  guarantees drawn are at constant $1$ and the constants are never","+  computed."," \\item \\textbf{Nothing about $k > 3$.} The statement is for every fixed","   $k$; the measurement is at $k = 3$."," \\item \\textbf{The counters are reported, not tuned.} Ganak ran through","   its binding at defaults, preprocessing included; ApproxMC at its","   default tolerance; a different configuration gives different seconds.","-  The check against enumeration is at $n \\le 30$ only.","+  The check against listing is at $n \\le 30$ only."," \\item \\textbf{No statement about which tool a practitioner should use.}","-  The section shows that the verified guarantee is not binding; it does","-  not rank solvers.","+  The section shows that the verified guarantee is not the binding one;","+  it does not rank solvers."," \\end{itemize}"]}],"originalFile":"% For the practitioner -- drafted by loop0015 item 06 (2026-11-05).\n% Every number of this section is produced by\n%   nice -n 19 python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py --regenerate   (the measurement)\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py                           (table, figure, stdout)\n% from random 3-CNF the script generates; no MOSP or pathwidth instance, nothing\n% under solutions/. Timings are from one machine on one day with named\n% versions (tables/practitioner_meta.json). The two guarantee curves are shapes\n% at constant 1, and nothing here is a running time of the verified machine.\n\\section{For the practitioner}\n\\label{sec:practitioner}\n\nThe title compares two guarantees: the verified one,\n$C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ with $C, A, d$ existential (comparator file lines\n136--139 and 210--211, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), and the trivial one,\n$2^n \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ for trying every assignment. Neither is what\na practitioner runs. What runs on a machine, for the decision bit, is a\nconflict-driven clause-learning (CDCL) solver, and for the count an exact\nor approximate model counter; and no implementation of the verified\nmachine exists to run beside them. This section measures the tools a\npractitioner has, on random 3-CNF, and draws the two guarantees next to\nthe measurements as shapes, so that the reader can see where each sits.\nThe point is small and the section says it once: the verified guarantee\nis not the binding one on any machine, because it is above $2^n$ for\nevery $n \\ge 50$ (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}) while the solvers are many\norders of magnitude below $2^n$ at every size they were run at.\n\n\\subsection{Why the comparison is between guarantees and measurements}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-why}\n\nThe verified statement is existential in the machine (comparator line\n195 opens with \\texttt{$\\exists$ (machine : \\ldots)}, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}) and\nthe solution's\n3,977 files contain no \\texttt{\\#eval}, no \\texttt{def main} and no\n\\texttt{IO} action (checked read-only by the script, which reads every\nfile of the family with \\texttt{open()} and counts those three patterns:\n0, 0, 0). The library proves that a machine with the stated bound\nexists and exhibits one as a Lean term inside a development with 1,048\n\\texttt{noncomputable} declarations and no executable interface\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); nothing in the clone runs it on an input, and\nwe found no implementation elsewhere. So the verified algorithm's actual\nrunning time on any formula is unknown, in both directions: nothing says\nit is slow, and nothing says it is fast. What is known is its\n\\emph{guarantee}, and a guarantee is compared with other guarantees. The\nmeasurements below are of other programs entirely; they are in the\nsection because the question a practitioner asks is not ``which\nguarantee is smaller'' but ``what would I run'', and the answer to that\nhas not changed.\n\n\\subsection{What was run}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-setup}\n\n\\paragraph{Instances.} Random 3-CNF on $n$ variables with\n$m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$ clauses, each clause three distinct variables\nwith uniform signs, generated by the script from $(n, \\mathrm{seed})$;\ntwenty seeds per size; $n = 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250,\n300, 400, 500$. The ratio $4.26$ is the empirical satisfiability\nthreshold of random 3-SAT \\citep{Selman1996Hard}, where decision is\nhardest for a given $n$ among random formulas (the cavity method puts the\nthreshold at $4.2667$, \\citealp{Mertens2006Threshold}); the sizes are\nchosen so that the smallest ones are where the title's crossing is\n($n = 50$) and the largest are where a CDCL solver on random formulas\nbegins to take seconds. These are random instances only; nothing is\nclaimed about structured formulas.\n\n\\paragraph{Tools.} CaDiCaL 1.9.5 \\citep{Biere2024CaDiCaL} through\npython-sat 1.9.dev15 \\citep{Ignatiev2018PySAT}, one \\texttt{solve()} call,\nfor the decision bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$; Ganak 2.8.0\n\\citep{Sharma2019Ganak}, through the pyganak binding (which runs its Arjun\npreprocessing and then the counter), for the exact count; ApproxMC 4.4.0\n\\citep{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC, Soos2019BIRD} at its defaults\n$\\varepsilon = 0.8$, $\\delta = 0.2$ with the projection set equal to all\nvariables, for an approximate count; and, at $n \\le 30$ only, exact\ncounting by enumeration over CaDiCaL with a blocking clause per model,\nwhich is not a counter at any larger size (it is linear in the count,\nand the count is exponential in $n$) and is here as the independent check\nof the two counters where it can reach. Ganak and ApproxMC were installed\nfrom PyPI for this section; no \\texttt{sharpSAT}, \\texttt{d4} or other\ncounter binary is on the host.\n\n\\paragraph{Protocol.} Each call runs in its own interpreter under\n\\texttt{nice -n 19}, at most eight at a time on the thirty-two cores of\nan AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (Linux 7.0.0, Python 3.12.13; the machine was shared\nwith other work, load average at the start in\n\\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}), with a wall-clock budget of\n60~s per call; the time recorded is \\texttt{perf\\_counter} around the\nconstruction of the solver object and the one call, inside the child,\nwith the interpreter's start-up and the import outside it. A call over\nbudget is killed and recorded as censored; a size at which a tool finishes\nfewer than ten of its twenty instances ends that tool's escalation. The\nwhole run took about twenty minutes (1,172~s) minutes.\n\n\\subsection{What came out}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-results}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:practitioner} gives, per size, the number of satisfiable\ninstances, CaDiCaL's median and maximum over the twenty seeds, the number\nof instances each counter finished with its median and maximum (a median\nover all twenty with censored runs counted as above the budget, so a\nmedian is a number whenever more than ten finished), and the two\nguarantees in the common scale of the next subsection.\nFigure~\\ref{fig:practitioner} draws the same.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Random 3-CNF at $m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$, twenty seeds per\nsize, one machine, 60~s per call. ``sat'' is the number of satisfiable\ninstances among those CaDiCaL finished; ``fin'' the number a counter finished within budget; ``med''\nand ``max'' are seconds over all twenty seeds with a censored run counted\nas $> 60$. The last two columns are the brute-force and the verified\nguarantees at constant~1, $2^n$ and $2^{\\rhon}$, multiplied by the one\ncommon factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$ that puts $2^n$ through CaDiCaL's median at\n$n = 20$ ($t_{20} = 1.28 \\times 10^{-4}$~s); they are shapes, not running times.\nRegenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n\\label{tab:practitioner}\n\\footnotesize\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n\\input{tables/practitioner}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{practitioner.pdf}\n\\caption{The same data on a logarithmic time axis. Solid lines with\nmarkers are medians, dotted lines maxima, over the twenty seeds; a tool\nwhose median is censored stops at the budget line. The two guarantees are\ndrawn at constant~1 with the common factor of Table~\\ref{tab:practitioner},\nso that $2^n$ passes through the $n = 20$ measurement and $2^{\\rhon}$\ncrosses it at $n = 50$, where $\\rhon \\ge n$ begins\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). From \\texttt{paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n\\label{fig:practitioner}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\paragraph{The checks.} On every instance where both finished, Ganak's\ncount equals the enumeration's (40 instances at $n \\le 30$) and\nCaDiCaL's bit equals $[\\mathrm{count} > 0]$; ApproxMC's estimate is\nwithin its factor $1 + \\varepsilon = 1.8$ of Ganak's on 178 of the\n206 instances where both finished (it promises that with\nprobability $1 - \\delta = 0.8$ per instance). The script exits non-zero\nif any of these fails.\n\n\\paragraph{What the solvers did.} CaDiCaL's median is under a\nmillisecond up to $n = 80$, 2.5~ms at 100, 58~ms at 200, 1.5~s at 250\nand 3.7~s at 300 (maximum 20.5~s); at 400 it finishes 10 of 20 within\nthe budget and at 500 8 of 20, every finished one satisfiable, which is\nthe known shape of CDCL on random formulas at the threshold (the\nunsatisfiable half is the expensive half). Ganak finishes all 20 up to\n$n = 250$ (median 17.6~s there, maximum 45.7~s), 10 of 20 at 300 and\nnone at 400; its median is flat at 8.3~s and 6.2~s at $n = 100$ and\n150 and lower, 2.4~s, at 200, while its maximum rises throughout ---\nthe plateau looks like a fixed budget inside the binding's\npreprocessing rather than counting difficulty, and we report it as\nmeasured and tuned nothing (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06d). Where the\ncount is positive it is small for the size: at $n = 200$ the fifteen\nsatisfiable instances have between 372 and $2.5 \\times 10^{12}$ models\nout of $2^{200}$. ApproxMC finishes all 20 up to $n = 200$ (median\n0.51~s there), 18 at 250, 11 at 300 and none at 400. The sizes at which\nthe three tools leave the budget --- 300 for the counters, 400 for the\ndecision --- are where this measurement ends; nothing is claimed beyond\nthem.\n\n\\subsection{The two guarantees beside them}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-guarantees}\n\nThe guarantees are drawn as shapes. Both are taken at constant 1 --- the\npolynomial factors, $C$, $A$ and $d$ are all set aside --- and both are\nmultiplied by the same factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$, where $t_{20}$ is\nCaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$. The factor is one number for both curves,\nso their ratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves,\n$2^{\\rhon - n}$: below 1 for $n < 50$, equal to 1 at $n = 50$ up to the\nrounding of $\\rho(50) = 50.13$, and above 1 from there (it is $2.8 \\times 10^{3}$ at\n$n = 100$ and $4.0 \\times 10^{51}$ at $n = 500$). A reader who prefers each curve\nscaled to the $n = 20$ point on its own would multiply $2^{\\rhon}$ by a\nfurther $2^{20 - \\rho(20)} = 2^{2.79}$ and see the two curves touch at\n$n = 20$ and the verified one above the trivial one from there on; the\ncommon factor is the honest choice because it keeps the crossing where\nthe statement puts it (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a).\n\nIn that scale the brute-force guarantee reaches one second near\n$n = 33$ and the verified guarantee near $n = 35$; at $n = 60$,\nthe largest size of the title's practical range that this section's\nsmallest tools were run at, the measured CaDiCaL median is $3 \\times 10^{-4}$ s against\n$2^n$ at $1.4 \\times 10^{8}$ s and $2^{\\rhon}$ at $5.4 \\times 10^{8}$ s, 11.6 and 12.2 decades\nrespectively; at $n = 500$ the decision median is above the budget (8 of 20 finished within 60~s), against\n$10^{140.6}$ s and $10^{192.2}$ s. Nothing in those decades is about the\nverified machine: the gap is between what a CDCL solver does on random\nformulas and what any $2^{\\Theta(n)}$ guarantee says, and it would be\nthe same gap for the brute-force guarantee alone. The one fact this\nsection adds to the title is the order of the three objects: at every\nsize in the table the measurement is far below $2^n$, and $2^{\\rhon}$ is\nabove $2^n$ from $n = 50$ on, so the verified guarantee is the largest\nof the three at every practical size and the measurement the smallest.\n\nOne remark on what the verified machine's \\emph{running time} would have\nto be, were it ever run. Nothing in this section bears on it: the\nconstants $C, A, d$ are unknown (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), the\nmachine is a Lean term, and an existential statement is not an\nimplementation. The only values on record are the proof's own, $A \\ge\n257$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$ (Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and\n\\ref{sec:proof}); a guarantee of $2^{257\\,\\rhon}$ steps is a guarantee\nand not a measurement, and a different proof of the same statement could\ncarry any positive constants.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Timings on one machine, one day, named versions.} The\n  seconds are from one AMD Ryzen 9 9950X shared with other work, under\n  \\texttt{nice -n 19} with eight calls in flight, through Python\n  bindings; they are reproducible in shape and not in value, and the\n  versions are those named in Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner-setup} and\n  in \\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}.\n\\item \\textbf{Random instances only.} Random 3-CNF at the threshold ratio\n  is one distribution; structured formulas can be harder for CDCL at\n  far smaller $n$ and easier for counters. Nothing is claimed about any\n  other distribution, and nothing about worst-case behaviour.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the verified machine's running time.} It is\n  unknown, in both directions; no implementation exists to run; the\n  guarantees drawn are at constant 1 and the constants are existential.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about $k > 3$.} The statement is for every fixed\n  $k$; the measurement is at $k = 3$.\n\\item \\textbf{The counters are reported, not tuned.} Ganak ran through\n  its binding at defaults, preprocessing included; ApproxMC at its\n  default tolerance; a different configuration gives different seconds.\n  The check against enumeration is at $n \\le 30$ only.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement about which tool a practitioner should use.}\n  The section shows that the verified guarantee is not binding; it does\n  not rank solvers.\n\\end{itemize}\n","userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_017PMzYZcaTEfLV67caF5A4b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
