{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgjKrNAwarpbG8fHrDVnL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LinYhBa6Nqpmtt9bU5o7Bk","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec8_lean.tex","content":"% The statement in our Lean -- drafted by loop0015 item 07 (2026-11-05),\n% rewritten in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05).\n% Every number here is printed by paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py (the copy, the\n% statement match, the sorry count; log paper3/lean/rediff.log) or by\n% `cd lean && lake env lean ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean` (the axiom audit;\n% log paper3/lean/axiom_check.log). Line numbers in our files are at the\n% repository commit the log names; line numbers in the clone are at 4eaee875.\n\\section{The statement in our Lean}\n\\label{sec:lean}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nSections~\\ref{sec:statement} and \\ref{sec:proof} read OpenAI's statement\nin OpenAI's files. This section describes the one thing we wrote in Lean\nourselves: a copy of the statement inside our own library, checked by\nthe same kernel, under our own name. The point is modest. Every sentence\nthis paper says about ``the verified statement'' is pinned to a named\nobject that Lean has accepted, and anyone who wants to use the statement\nas an assumption later can cite that one name. Nothing is proved about\nthe hypothesis here, and nothing is claimed beyond what the copy and its\naudit show.\n\nFour words first, each in one sentence. In Lean a \\emph{Prop} is a\nsentence that may be true or false. A \\emph{def} gives a sentence a name\nwithout asserting it. A \\emph{theorem} asserts a sentence and carries its\nproof. An \\emph{axiom} asserts a sentence without a proof. Our\nrestatement is a def: it names the sentence and asserts nothing.\n\nThe files are \\repofile{lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/PrimitiveMachine.lean}\nand \\repofile{lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/Frame.lean}, written\non 2026-11-04 for an earlier study of ours on a different question. The\ndirectory carries that study's name. The owner chose to leave the files\nwhere they are rather than move them (\\repofile{paper3/ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD},\nS3), and this paper cites nothing from that study: only the copied\nmodel, the restatement, and six small lemmas about them, all listed in\nAppendix~\\ref{app:lean}.\n\n\\subsection{The copy: definitions only}\n\\label{sec:lean-copy}\n\nThe comparator file of family~378,\n\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean}, has 220\nlines. Lines 1--190 define the objects the theorem speaks about: the\nformulas, their encoding as a string of bits, the multitape machine, its\nstep and its starting configuration, the exponent $\\runtimeScale$ and\nthe bound built from it, the count word, and the bookkeeping that\nrecords which tape cells a run has visited. Lines 191--219 state the\ntheorem \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} and end it with \\texttt{sorry},\nthe placeholder that a solution must replace. Line 220 closes the last\nnamespace.\n\nOur \\texttt{PrimitiveMachine.lean} is lines 1--190 and 220, and nothing\nelse. After a thirty-line header that records the source, the commit,\nthe file's hash and the Apache licence, the file is the copy\n\\leanref{copy-model}. The header says so, and a script checks it.\n\\repofile{paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py} reads both files with\n\\texttt{open()} only, takes everything in ours from the line\n\\texttt{import Mathlib} on, and compares it byte for byte with the\nclone's lines 1--190 followed by line 220. The diff is empty: 191 lines\non each side, identical. The script also checks the clone file's\nSHA-256 against the hash the header records, and that the clone is at\ncommit \\texttt{4eaee875}. The log is \\repofile{paper3/lean/rediff.log}.\n\nSo the copy holds the theorem's \\emph{vocabulary} and not the theorem.\nThe \\texttt{sorry} at line 218 is outside the copied range, and our file\nhas no \\texttt{sorry} and no axiom declaration anywhere (the same script\ncounts them: zero and zero in each of our two files). Everything in the\ncopy is a definition. A definition can be badly chosen, but it cannot be\nfalse; what the definitions mean is Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s\nsubject, and the copy adds nothing to it.\n\n\\subsection{The restatement}\n\\label{sec:lean-restatement}\n\n\\texttt{Frame.lean} defines a sentence named \\SubexpCount{}, with the\nwidth $k$ as its argument:\n\\[\n  \\SubexpCount\\ (k : \\mathbb{N}) : \\mathrm{Prop}.\n\\]\nIts body is the body of the comparator theorem for width $k$. The\ntheorem's two leading parts are handled thus: $k$ is the argument of the\ndef, and the condition $2 \\le k$ is carried by a second def,\n\\texttt{ExactCountAndSat}, which says ``for every $k \\ge 2$,\n\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\)'' (Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}, \\ref{lean:subexpcount}\nand \\ref{lean:exactcountandsat}). In words, \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) is the\nsentence of Section~\\ref{sec:statement}: one fixed machine, on every\nformula of width at most $k$, writes the exact number of satisfying\nassignments, answers whether there is one, runs within the bound built\nfrom $\\runtimeScale$, and visits only polynomially many cells.\n\nThe same script checks that the body is the comparator's. It takes the\n23 lines after \\texttt{def SubexpCount (k : }$\\mathbb{N}$\\texttt{) : Prop :=}\nin our file (lines 85--107) and the 23 lines after the theorem's leading\nparts in the clone (lines 195--217, with the closing \\texttt{:= by}\nremoved), and compares them in two ways: with every run of white space\ncollapsed to one space, and line by line with the indentation stripped.\nBoth comparisons find the two texts identical, 1,073 characters. The\nonly difference is that each of our lines starts two spaces further\nleft, because a def's body sits two columns inside a theorem's. We call\nthis ``character for character''; it is a textual match, by the same\nmethod as Section~\\ref{sec:proof}'s, and it rests on the file we copied\nfrom being the comparator file at the commit we name.\n\nNeither def is proved here, and neither is an axiom. The truth of\n\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) for $k \\ge 2$ rests on OpenAI's proof and on\nSections~\\ref{sec:proof} and \\ref{sec:replay}, not on anything in our\nlibrary. Our library would be unchanged if the proof were wrong.\n\n\\subsection{What is proved, and what it means in words}\n\\label{sec:lean-lemmas}\n\nSix small lemmas sit beside the defs, each proved in a few lines\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}, \\ref{lean:subexpcount-anti}--\\ref{lean:trace-projection}).\nNone of them says anything about any machine's running time or about\nwhether the hypothesis is true.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{A machine for width $k$ is a machine for every smaller\n  width.} \\texttt{SubexpCount.anti}: if \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) holds and\n  $k' \\le k$ then \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k'\\) holds, with the same machine and\n  the same constants, because a formula whose clauses have at most $k'$\n  members has at most $k$. \\texttt{ExactCountAndSat.subexpCount} unpacks\n  the ``for every $k \\ge 2$'' form.\n\\item \\textbf{The bound is what it is.} \\texttt{runtimeBound\\_eq}: the\n  wrapper \\texttt{runtimeBound} $C\\,A\\,d\\,L\\,n$ unfolds, by definition,\n  to $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\cdot\\rhon}$, the formula\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement} reads. The proof is \\texttt{rfl}: the two\n  sides are the same term.\n\\item \\textbf{The exponent grows more slowly than $n$.}\n  \\texttt{tendsto\\_log\\_sq\\_div\\_sqrt} says $(\\ln y)^2/\\sqrt{y} \\to 0$\n  as $y \\to \\infty$, and \\texttt{runtimeScale\\_isLittleO} says that\n  $\\rhon/n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$, which is written $\\rhon = o(n)$\n  \\leanref{rho-little-o}. This is the limit fact that\n  Sections~\\ref{sec:statement}, \\ref{sec:beyond} and\n  \\ref{sec:consequences} cite: in the limit the exponent is smaller than\n  $n$ by any factor one names. It is true at the same time as the\n  title's fact that $\\rhon > n$ for every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, and the\n  Lean says nothing about where the limit begins to bite.\n  \\texttt{isSubexpExponent\\_runtimeScale} is the same statement for\n  $A \\cdot \\rhon$, for every constant $A$ \\leanref{is-subexp-exponent}.\n\\item \\textbf{The time bound is a bound on the plain machine.}\n  \\texttt{Trace.config\\_of\\_locatedStep}: the comparator states its time\n  bound on the \\emph{located} step, which records visited cells beside\n  each configuration. A run of the located step projects to a run of the\n  plain step with the same number of steps, so the bound is a bound on\n  the machine itself \\leanref{trace-projection}. The one-line\n  \\texttt{LocatedCfg.monitor\\_config} is its helper.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{The axiom audit}\n\\label{sec:lean-audit}\n\nLean records, for every proved statement, the axioms its proof uses.\nThree are standard in Mathlib and in every proof of\nSections~\\ref{sec:proof} and \\ref{sec:replay}: \\texttt{propext},\n\\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{Quot.sound}. A fourth,\n\\texttt{sorryAx}, appears exactly when a proof was left out with\n\\texttt{sorry}. The audit \\repofile{paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean} prints\nthe axioms of 21 declarations: the ten copied definitions the statement\nrests on, the two defs, and the nine lemmas and helper definitions, in\nthe pattern of our first paper's audit. It is run from our library's\ndirectory as\n\\begin{verbatim}\ncd lean && lake env lean ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean \\\n  > ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.log\n\\end{verbatim}\nand the log is committed. The result: nineteen lines on exactly the\nthree standard axioms; \\texttt{Trace} on no axiom at all (it is an\ninductive definition); \\texttt{countWord} on two of the three;\n\\texttt{sorryAx} on none. The library it runs against is built with\nLean~4.34.0-rc2 and Mathlib at \\texttt{1192d624}, neither of which is\nwhat the clone pins (Lean~4.34.1, Mathlib \\texttt{d13f23b7},\nSection~\\ref{sec:proof}). That the copied model is accepted unchanged\nby a different Lean version and a different Mathlib revision is small\nevidence that it rests on nothing patched or private, and no more than\nthat. Our whole library builds with no \\texttt{sorry} and no axiom\ndeclaration, which the loop's gate checks after every session.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:lean-notclaim}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) is a def of a Prop, never proved\n  here.} Its truth is OpenAI's theorem, replayed in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:replay}; our library neither proves it nor assumes\n  it. Nothing in this section makes the refutation more or less certain.\n\\item \\textbf{The copy is definitions only.} The proof is not copied,\n  and could not be: it is 3,091 modules (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). What\n  is copied is the vocabulary of the statement, so that the restatement\n  reads as the comparator does.\n\\item \\textbf{No Lean computation evaluates the exponent.}\n  $\\runtimeScale$ is defined over the real numbers with real logarithms\n  and is marked \\texttt{noncomputable}; no \\texttt{\\#eval} can print\n  $\\rho(50)$ or $\\rho(50)/50$. Every number in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement} comes from a Python script, not from\n  Lean, and the Lean proves only the limit.\n\\item \\textbf{The match is textual.} ``Character for character'' means\n  the normalised texts are equal; it is not a check at the level of\n  \\texttt{lean4export}, and it rests on the clone being the repository\n  at the commit named.\n\\item \\textbf{The lemmas are trivial on purpose.} They fix the shape of\n  the bound and the limit of the exponent, and nothing else. No theorem\n  about any machine's running time, about the constants, or about the\n  manuscript's sharper exponents is stated, because none is verified.\n\\item \\textbf{The directory name is not a citation.} The files sit in a\n  directory named for another study of ours; nothing from that study\n  enters this paper, and the owner's decision to leave them in place is\n  recorded, not argued.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\endgroup\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5765,"cache_read_input_tokens":237787,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5765,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"fa0ae7eb-36f6-4ff5-83a8-c71b6b4a744d","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:42:47.030Z","request_id":"req_011CgjKrMS3FGLjknerzP8mK","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01LinYhBa6Nqpmtt9bU5o7Bk":{"file_path":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec8_lean.tex","content":"% The statement in our Lean -- drafted by loop0015 item 07 (2026-11-05),\n% rewritten in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05).\n% Every number here is printed by paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py (the copy, the\n% statement match, the sorry count; log paper3/lean/rediff.log) or by\n% `cd lean && lake env lean ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean` (the axiom audit;\n% log paper3/lean/axiom_check.log). Line numbers in our files are at the\n% repository commit the log names; line numbers in the clone are at 4eaee875.\n\\section{The statement in our Lean}\n\\label{sec:lean}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nSections~\\ref{sec:statement} and \\ref{sec:proof} read OpenAI's statement\nin OpenAI's files. This section describes the one thing we wrote in Lean\nourselves: a copy of the statement inside our own library, checked by\nthe same kernel, under our own name. The point is modest. Every sentence\nthis paper says about ``the verified statement'' is pinned to a named\nobject that Lean has accepted, and anyone who wants to use the statement\nas an assumption later can cite that one name. Nothing is proved about\nthe hypothesis here, and nothing is claimed beyond what the copy and its\naudit show.\n\nFour words first, each in one sentence. In Lean a \\emph{Prop} is a\nsentence that may be true or false. A \\emph{def} gives a sentence a name\nwithout asserting it. A \\emph{theorem} asserts a sentence and carries its\nproof. An \\emph{axiom} asserts a sentence without a proof. Our\nrestatement is a def: it names the sentence and asserts nothing.\n\nThe files are \\repofile{lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/PrimitiveMachine.lean}\nand \\repofile{lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/Frame.lean}, written\non 2026-11-04 for an earlier study of ours on a different question. The\ndirectory carries that study's name. The owner chose to leave the files\nwhere they are rather than move them (\\repofile{paper3/ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD},\nS3), and this paper cites nothing from that study: only the copied\nmodel, the restatement, and six small lemmas about them, all listed in\nAppendix~\\ref{app:lean}.\n\n\\subsection{The copy: definitions only}\n\\label{sec:lean-copy}\n\nThe comparator file of family~378,\n\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean}, has 220\nlines. Lines 1--190 define the objects the theorem speaks about: the\nformulas, their encoding as a string of bits, the multitape machine, its\nstep and its starting configuration, the exponent $\\runtimeScale$ and\nthe bound built from it, the count word, and the bookkeeping that\nrecords which tape cells a run has visited. Lines 191--219 state the\ntheorem \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} and end it with \\texttt{sorry},\nthe placeholder that a solution must replace. Line 220 closes the last\nnamespace.\n\nOur \\texttt{PrimitiveMachine.lean} is lines 1--190 and 220, and nothing\nelse. After a thirty-line header that records the source, the commit,\nthe file's hash and the Apache licence, the file is the copy\n\\leanref{copy-model}. The header says so, and a script checks it.\n\\repofile{paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py} reads both files with\n\\texttt{open()} only, takes everything in ours from the line\n\\texttt{import Mathlib} on, and compares it byte for byte with the\nclone's lines 1--190 followed by line 220. The diff is empty: 191 lines\non each side, identical. The script also checks the clone file's\nSHA-256 against the hash the header records, and that the clone is at\ncommit \\texttt{4eaee875}. The log is \\repofile{paper3/lean/rediff.log}.\n\nSo the copy holds the theorem's \\emph{vocabulary} and not the theorem.\nThe \\texttt{sorry} at line 218 is outside the copied range, and our file\nhas no \\texttt{sorry} and no axiom declaration anywhere (the same script\ncounts them: zero and zero in each of our two files). Everything in the\ncopy is a definition. A definition can be badly chosen, but it cannot be\nfalse; what the definitions mean is Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s\nsubject, and the copy adds nothing to it.\n\n\\subsection{The restatement}\n\\label{sec:lean-restatement}\n\n\\texttt{Frame.lean} defines a sentence named \\SubexpCount{}, with the\nwidth $k$ as its argument:\n\\[\n  \\SubexpCount\\ (k : \\mathbb{N}) : \\mathrm{Prop}.\n\\]\nIts body is the body of the comparator theorem for width $k$. The\ntheorem's two leading parts are handled thus: $k$ is the argument of the\ndef, and the condition $2 \\le k$ is carried by a second def,\n\\texttt{ExactCountAndSat}, which says ``for every $k \\ge 2$,\n\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\)'' (Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}, \\ref{lean:subexpcount}\nand \\ref{lean:exactcountandsat}). In words, \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) is the\nsentence of Section~\\ref{sec:statement}: one fixed machine, on every\nformula of width at most $k$, writes the exact number of satisfying\nassignments, answers whether there is one, runs within the bound built\nfrom $\\runtimeScale$, and visits only polynomially many cells.\n\nThe same script checks that the body is the comparator's. It takes the\n23 lines after \\texttt{def SubexpCount (k : }$\\mathbb{N}$\\texttt{) : Prop :=}\nin our file (lines 85--107) and the 23 lines after the theorem's leading\nparts in the clone (lines 195--217, with the closing \\texttt{:= by}\nremoved), and compares them in two ways: with every run of white space\ncollapsed to one space, and line by line with the indentation stripped.\nBoth comparisons find the two texts identical, 1,073 characters. The\nonly difference is that each of our lines starts two spaces further\nleft, because a def's body sits two columns inside a theorem's. We call\nthis ``character for character''; it is a textual match, by the same\nmethod as Section~\\ref{sec:proof}'s, and it rests on the file we copied\nfrom being the comparator file at the commit we name.\n\nNeither def is proved here, and neither is an axiom. The truth of\n\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) for $k \\ge 2$ rests on OpenAI's proof and on\nSections~\\ref{sec:proof} and \\ref{sec:replay}, not on anything in our\nlibrary. Our library would be unchanged if the proof were wrong.\n\n\\subsection{What is proved, and what it means in words}\n\\label{sec:lean-lemmas}\n\nSix small lemmas sit beside the defs, each proved in a few lines\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}, \\ref{lean:subexpcount-anti}--\\ref{lean:trace-projection}).\nNone of them says anything about any machine's running time or about\nwhether the hypothesis is true.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{A machine for width $k$ is a machine for every smaller\n  width.} \\texttt{SubexpCount.anti}: if \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) holds and\n  $k' \\le k$ then \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k'\\) holds, with the same machine and\n  the same constants, because a formula whose clauses have at most $k'$\n  members has at most $k$. \\texttt{ExactCountAndSat.subexpCount} unpacks\n  the ``for every $k \\ge 2$'' form.\n\\item \\textbf{The bound is what it is.} \\texttt{runtimeBound\\_eq}: the\n  wrapper \\texttt{runtimeBound} $C\\,A\\,d\\,L\\,n$ unfolds, by definition,\n  to $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\cdot\\rhon}$, the formula\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement} reads. The proof is \\texttt{rfl}: the two\n  sides are the same term.\n\\item \\textbf{The exponent grows more slowly than $n$.}\n  \\texttt{tendsto\\_log\\_sq\\_div\\_sqrt} says $(\\ln y)^2/\\sqrt{y} \\to 0$\n  as $y \\to \\infty$, and \\texttt{runtimeScale\\_isLittleO} says that\n  $\\rhon/n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$, which is written $\\rhon = o(n)$\n  \\leanref{rho-little-o}. This is the limit fact that\n  Sections~\\ref{sec:statement}, \\ref{sec:beyond} and\n  \\ref{sec:consequences} cite: in the limit the exponent is smaller than\n  $n$ by any factor one names. It is true at the same time as the\n  title's fact that $\\rhon > n$ for every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, and the\n  Lean says nothing about where the limit begins to bite.\n  \\texttt{isSubexpExponent\\_runtimeScale} is the same statement for\n  $A \\cdot \\rhon$, for every constant $A$ \\leanref{is-subexp-exponent}.\n\\item \\textbf{The time bound is a bound on the plain machine.}\n  \\texttt{Trace.config\\_of\\_locatedStep}: the comparator states its time\n  bound on the \\emph{located} step, which records visited cells beside\n  each configuration. A run of the located step projects to a run of the\n  plain step with the same number of steps, so the bound is a bound on\n  the machine itself \\leanref{trace-projection}. The one-line\n  \\texttt{LocatedCfg.monitor\\_config} is its helper.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{The axiom audit}\n\\label{sec:lean-audit}\n\nLean records, for every proved statement, the axioms its proof uses.\nThree are standard in Mathlib and in every proof of\nSections~\\ref{sec:proof} and \\ref{sec:replay}: \\texttt{propext},\n\\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{Quot.sound}. A fourth,\n\\texttt{sorryAx}, appears exactly when a proof was left out with\n\\texttt{sorry}. The audit \\repofile{paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean} prints\nthe axioms of 21 declarations: the ten copied definitions the statement\nrests on, the two defs, and the nine lemmas and helper definitions, in\nthe pattern of our first paper's audit. It is run from our library's\ndirectory as\n\\begin{verbatim}\ncd lean && lake env lean ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean \\\n  > ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.log\n\\end{verbatim}\nand the log is committed. The result: nineteen lines on exactly the\nthree standard axioms; \\texttt{Trace} on no axiom at all (it is an\ninductive definition); \\texttt{countWord} on two of the three;\n\\texttt{sorryAx} on none. The library it runs against is built with\nLean~4.34.0-rc2 and Mathlib at \\texttt{1192d624}, neither of which is\nwhat the clone pins (Lean~4.34.1, Mathlib \\texttt{d13f23b7},\nSection~\\ref{sec:proof}). That the copied model is accepted unchanged\nby a different Lean version and a different Mathlib revision is small\nevidence that it rests on nothing patched or private, and no more than\nthat. Our whole library builds with no \\texttt{sorry} and no axiom\ndeclaration, which the loop's gate checks after every session.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:lean-notclaim}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) is a def of a Prop, never proved\n  here.} Its truth is OpenAI's theorem, replayed in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:replay}; our library neither proves it nor assumes\n  it. Nothing in this section makes the refutation more or less certain.\n\\item \\textbf{The copy is definitions only.} The proof is not copied,\n  and could not be: it is 3,091 modules (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). What\n  is copied is the vocabulary of the statement, so that the restatement\n  reads as the comparator does.\n\\item \\textbf{No Lean computation evaluates the exponent.}\n  $\\runtimeScale$ is defined over the real numbers with real logarithms\n  and is marked \\texttt{noncomputable}; no \\texttt{\\#eval} can print\n  $\\rho(50)$ or $\\rho(50)/50$. Every number in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement} comes from a Python script, not from\n  Lean, and the Lean proves only the limit.\n\\item \\textbf{The match is textual.} ``Character for character'' means\n  the normalised texts are equal; it is not a check at the level of\n  \\texttt{lean4export}, and it rests on the clone being the repository\n  at the commit named.\n\\item \\textbf{The lemmas are trivial on purpose.} They fix the shape of\n  the bound and the limit of the exponent, and nothing else. No theorem\n  about any machine's running time, about the constants, or about the\n  manuscript's sharper exponents is stated, because none is verified.\n\\item \\textbf{The directory name is not a citation.} The files sit in a\n  directory named for another study of ours; nothing from that study\n  enters this paper, and the owner's decision to leave them in place is\n  recorded, not argued.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\endgroup\n"}}}
