ts identical, 1,073 characters. The\nonly difference is that each of our lines starts two spaces further\nleft, because a def's body sits two columns inside a theorem's. We call\nthis ``character for character''; it is a textual match, by the same\nmethod as Section~\\ref{sec:proof}'s, and it rests on the file we copied\nfrom being the comparator file at the commit we name.\n\nNeither def is proved here, and neither is an axiom. The truth of\n\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) for $k \\ge 2$ rests on OpenAI's proof and on\nSections~\\ref{sec:proof} and \\ref{sec:replay}, not on anything in our\nlibrary. Our library would be unchanged if the proof were wrong.\n\n\\subsection{What is proved, and what it means in words}\n\\label{sec:lean-lemmas}\n\nSix small lemmas sit beside the defs, each proved in a few lines\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}, \\ref{lean:subexpcount-anti}--\\ref{lean:trace-projection}).\nNone of them says anything about any machine's running time or about\nwhether the hypothesis is true.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{A machine for width $k$ is a machine for every smaller\n  width.} \\texttt{SubexpCount.anti}: if \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) holds and\n  $k' \\le k$ then \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k'\\) holds, with the same machine and\n  the same constants, because a formula whose clauses have at most $k'$\n  members has at most $k$. \\texttt{ExactCountAndSat.subexpCount} unpacks\n  the ``for every $k \\ge 2$'' form.\n\\item \\textbf{The bound is what it is.} \\texttt{runtimeBound\\_eq}: the\n  wrapper \\texttt{runtimeBound} $C\\,A\\,d\\,L\\,n$ unfolds, by definition,\n  to $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\cdot\\rhon}$, the formula\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement} reads. The proof is \\texttt{rfl}: the two\n  sides are the same term.\n\\item \\textbf{The exponent grows more slowly than $n$.}\n  \\texttt{tendsto\\_log\\_sq\\_div\\_sqrt} says $(\\ln y)^2/\\sqrt{y} \\to 0$\n  as $y \\to \\infty$, and \\texttt{runtimeScale\\_isLittleO} says that\n  $\\rhon/n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$, which is written $\\rhon = o(n)$\n  \\leanref{rho-little-o}. This is the limit fact that\n  Sections~\\ref{sec:statement}, \\ref{sec:beyond} and\n  \\ref{sec:consequences} cite: in the limit the exponent is smaller than\n  $n$ by any factor one names. It is true at the same time as the\n  title's fact that $\\rhon > n$ for every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, and the\n  Lean says nothing about where the limit begins to bite.\n  \\texttt{isSubexpExponent\\_runtimeScale} is the same statement for\n  $A \\cdot \\rhon$, for every constant $A$ \\leanref{is-subexp-exponent}.\n\\item \\textbf{The time bound is a bound on the plain machine.}\n  \\texttt{Trace.config\\_of\\_locatedStep}: the comparator states its time\n  bound on the \\emph{located} step, which records visited cells beside\n  each configuration. A run of the located step projects to a run of the\n  plain step with the same number of steps, so the bound is a bound on\n  the machine itself \\leanref{trace-projection}. The one-line\n  \\texttt{LocatedCfg.monitor\\_config} is its helper.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{The axiom audit}\n\\label{sec:lean-audit}\n\nLean records, for every proved statement, the axioms its proof uses.\nThree are standard in Mathlib and in every proof of\nSections~\\ref{sec:proof} and \\ref{sec:replay}: \\texttt{propext},\n\\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{Quot.sound}. A fourth,\n\\texttt{sorryAx}, appears exactly when a proof was left out with\n\\texttt{sorry}. The audit \\repofile{paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean} prints\nthe axioms of 21 declarations: the ten copied definitions the statement\nrests on, the two defs, and the nine lemmas and helper definitions, in\nthe pattern of our first paper's audit. It is run from our library's\ndirectory as\n\\begin{verbatim}\ncd lean && lake env lean ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean \\\n  > ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.log\n\\end{verbatim}\nand the log is committed. The result: nineteen lines on exactly the\nthree standard axioms; \\texttt{Trace} on no axiom at all (it is an\ninductive definition); \\texttt{countWord} on two of the three;\n\\texttt{sorryAx} on none. The library it runs against is built with\nLean~4.34.0-rc2 and Mathlib at \\texttt{1192d624}, neither of which is\nwhat the clone pins (Lean~4.34.1, Mathlib \\texttt{d13f23b7},\nSection~\\ref{sec:proof}). That the copied model is accepted unchanged\nby a different Lean version and a different Mathlib revision is small\nevidence that it rests on nothing patched or private, and no more than\nthat. Our whole library builds with no \\texttt{sorry} and no axiom\ndeclaration, which the loop's gate checks after every session.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:lean-notclaim}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) is a def of a Prop, never proved\n  here.} Its truth is OpenAI's theorem, replayed in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:replay}; our library neither proves it nor assumes\n  it. Nothing in this section makes the refutation more or less certain.\n\\item \\textbf{The copy is definitions only.} The proof is not copied,\n  and could not be: it is 3,091 modules (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). What\n  is copied is the vocabulary of the statement, so that the restatement\n  reads as the comparator does.\n\\item \\textbf{No Lean computation evaluates the exponent.}\n  $\\runtimeScale$ is defined over the real numbers with real logarithms\n  and is marked \\texttt{noncomputable}; no \\texttt{\\#eval} can print\n  $\\rho(50)$ or $\\rho(50)/50$. Every number in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement} comes from a Python script, not from\n  Lean, and the Lean proves only the limit.\n\\item \\textbf{The match is textual.} ``Character for character'' means\n  the normalised texts are equal; it is not a check at the level of\n  \\texttt{lean4export}, and it rests on the clone being the repository\n  at the commit named.\n\\item \\textbf{The lemmas are trivial on purpose.} They fix the shape of\n  the bound and the limit of the exponent, and nothing else. No theorem\n  about any machine's running time, about the constants, or about the\n  manuscript's sharper exponents is stated, because none is verified.\n\\item \\textbf{The directory name is not a citation.} The files sit in a\n  directory named for another study of ours; nothing from that study\n  enters this paper, and the owner's decision to leave them in place is\n  recorded, not argued.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\endgroup\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":4,"newStart":1,"newLines":5,"lines":["-% The statement in our Lean -- drafted by loop0015 item 07 (2026-11-05).","+% The statement in our Lean -- drafted by loop0015 item 07 (2026-11-05),","+% rewritten in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05)."," % Every number here is printed by paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py (the copy, the"," % statement match, the sorry count; log paper3/lean/rediff.log) or by"," % `cd lean && lake env lean ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean` (the axiom audit;"]},{"oldStart":10,"oldLines":27,"newStart":11,"newLines":27,"lines":[" "," Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and \\ref{sec:proof} read OpenAI's statement"," in OpenAI's files. This section describes the one thing we wrote in Lean","-ourselves: a copy of the statement inside our own library, checked by the","-same kernel, under our own name. The point is modest. Every sentence this","-paper says about ``the verified statement'' is pinned to a named object","-that the Lean kernel has elaborated, and anyone who wants to use the","-statement as a hypothesis later can cite that one name. Nothing is proved","-about the hypothesis here, and nothing is claimed beyond what the copy","-and its audit show.","+ourselves: a copy of the statement inside our own library, checked by","+the same kernel, under our own name. The point is modest. Every sentence","+this paper says about ``the verified statement'' is pinned to a named","+object that Lean has accepted, and anyone who wants to use the statement","+as an assumption later can cite that