ry number in","+  Section~\\ref{sec:statement} comes from a Python script, not from","+  Lean, and the Lean proves only the limit."," \\item \\textbf{The match is textual.} ``Character for character'' means","   the normalised texts are equal; it is not a check at the level of","-  \\texttt{lean4export}, and it rests on the clone being the repository at","-  the commit named.","+  \\texttt{lean4export}, and it rests on the clone being the repository","+  at the commit named."," \\item \\textbf{The lemmas are trivial on purpose.} They fix the shape of","   the bound and the limit of the exponent, and nothing else. No theorem","   about any machine's running time, about the constants, or about the"]}],"originalFile":"% The statement in our Lean -- drafted by loop0015 item 07 (2026-11-05).\n% Every number here is printed by paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py (the copy, the\n% statement match, the sorry count; log paper3/lean/rediff.log) or by\n% `cd lean && lake env lean ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean` (the axiom audit;\n% log paper3/lean/axiom_check.log). Line numbers in our files are at the\n% repository commit the log names; line numbers in the clone are at 4eaee875.\n\\section{The statement in our Lean}\n\\label{sec:lean}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nSections~\\ref{sec:statement} and \\ref{sec:proof} read OpenAI's statement\nin OpenAI's files. This section describes the one thing we wrote in Lean\nourselves: a copy of the statement inside our own library, checked by the\nsame kernel, under our own name. The point is modest. Every sentence this\npaper says about ``the verified statement'' is pinned to a named object\nthat the Lean kernel has elaborated, and anyone who wants to use the\nstatement as a hypothesis later can cite that one name. Nothing is proved\nabout the hypothesis here, and nothing is claimed beyond what the copy\nand its audit show.\n\nTwo words first. In Lean a \\emph{Prop} is a sentence that may be true or\nfalse; a \\emph{def} gives a sentence a name without asserting it; a\n\\emph{theorem} asserts a sentence and carries its proof; an \\emph{axiom}\nasserts one without a proof. Our restatement is a def. It names the\nsentence and asserts nothing.\n\nThe files are \\repofile{lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/PrimitiveMachine.lean}\nand \\repofile{lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/Frame.lean}, written\non 2026-11-04 for an earlier study of ours on a different question. The\ndirectory carries that study's name. The owner chose to leave the files\nwhere they are rather than move them (\\repofile{paper3/ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD},\nS3), and this paper cites nothing from that study: only the copied model,\nthe restatement, and six small lemmas about them, all listed in\nAppendix~\\ref{app:lean}.\n\n\\subsection{The copy: definitions only}\n\\label{sec:lean-copy}\n\nThe comparator file of family~378,\n\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean}, has 220\nlines. Lines 1--190 define the objects the theorem speaks about: the\nformulas, their canonical encoding as a bit string, the multitape machine,\nits step and its starting configuration, the running-time bound\n$\\runtimeScale$ and the wrapper around it, the count word, and the\nlocated configuration that records which tape cells a run has visited.\nLines 191--219 state the theorem \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} and end\nit with \\texttt{sorry}, the placeholder that a solution must replace.\nLine 220 closes the last namespace.\n\nOur \\texttt{PrimitiveMachine.lean} is lines 1--190 and 220, and nothing\nelse: after a thirty-line header that records the source, the commit, the\nfile's hash and the Apache licence, the file is the copy\n\\leanref{copy-model}. The header says so, and a script checks it.\n\\repofile{paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py} reads both files with\n\\texttt{open()} only, takes everything in ours from the line\n\\texttt{import Mathlib} on, and compares it byte for byte with the clone's\nlines 1--190 followed by line 220. The diff is empty: 191 lines on each\nside, identical. The script also checks the clone file's SHA-256 against\nthe hash the header records, and that the clone is at commit\n\\texttt{4eaee875}. The log is \\repofile{paper3/lean/rediff.log}.\n\nSo the copy holds the theorem's \\emph{vocabulary} and not the theorem.\nThe \\texttt{sorry} at line 218 is outside the copied range, and our file\nhas no \\texttt{sorry} and no axiom declaration anywhere (the same script\ncounts them: zero and zero in each of our two files). Everything in the\ncopy is a definition. A definition can be badly chosen, but it cannot be\nfalse; what the definitions mean is Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s\nsubject, and the copy adds nothing to it.\n\n\\subsection{The restatement}\n\\label{sec:lean-restatement}\n\n\\texttt{Frame.lean} defines\n\\[\n  \\SubexpCount\\ (k : \\mathbb{N}) : \\mathrm{Prop}\n\\]\nas the body of the comparator theorem for width $k$, with the theorem's\ntwo binders handled thus: $k$ is the argument of the def, and the\nhypothesis $2 \\le k$ is carried by a second def,\n\\texttt{ExactCountAndSat}, which says ``for every $k \\ge 2$,\n\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\)'' (Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}, \\ref{lean:subexpcount} and \\ref{lean:exactcountandsat}).\nIn words, \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) is the sentence of\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement}: one fixed machine, on every\nbounded-width formula, writes the exact number of satisfying assignments,\nanswers whether there is one, runs within the bound built from\n$\\runtimeScale$, and visits only polynomially many cells.\n\nThe same script checks that the body is the comparator's. It takes the\n23 lines after \\texttt{def SubexpCount (k : }$\\mathbb{N}$\\texttt{) : Prop :=} in our file\n(lines 85--107) and the 23 lines after the theorem's binders in the clone\n(lines 195--217, with the closing \\texttt{:= by} removed), and compares\nthem in two ways: with every run of white space collapsed to one space,\nand line by line with the indentation stripped. Both comparisons find the\ntwo texts identical, 1,073 characters. The only difference is that each\nof our lines starts two spaces further left, because a def's body sits\ntwo columns inside a theorem's type. We call this ``character for\ncharacter''; it is a textual match, by the same method as\nSection~\\ref{sec:proof}'s, and it rests on the file we copied from being\nthe comparator file at the commit we name.\n\nNeither def is proved here, and neither is an axiom. The truth of\n\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) for $k \\ge 2$ rests on OpenAI's proof and on\nSections~\\ref{sec:proof} and \\ref{sec:replay}, not on anything in our\nlibrary. Our library would be unchanged if the proof were wrong.\n\n\\subsection{What is proved, and what it means in words}\n\\label{sec:lean-lemmas}\n\nSix small lemmas sit beside the defs, each proved in a few lines\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}, \\ref{lean:subexpcount-anti}--\\ref{lean:trace-projection}). None of them says\nanything about any machine's running time or about whether the hypothesis\nis true.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{A machine for width $k$ is a machine for every smaller\n  width.} \\texttt{SubexpCount.anti}: if \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) holds and\n  $k' \\le k$ then \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k'\\) holds, with the same machine and\n  the same constants, because a formula whose clauses have at most $k'$\n  literals has at most $k$. \\texttt{ExactCountAndSat.subexpCount} unpacks\n  the ``for every $k \\ge 2$'' form.\n\\item \\textbf{The bound is what it is.} \\texttt{runtimeBound\\_eq}: the\n  wrapper \\texttt{runtimeBound} $C\\,A\\,d\\,L\\,n$ unfolds, by definition, to\n  $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\cdot\\rhon}$, the formula Section~\\ref{sec:statement}\n  reads. The proof is \\texttt{rfl}: the two sides are the same term.\n\\item \\textbf{The exponent is $o(n)$.} \\texttt{tendsto\\_log\\_sq\\_div\\_sqrt}\n  says $(\\ln y)^2/\\sqrt{y} \\to 0$ as $y \\to \\infty$, and\n  \\texttt{runtimeScale\\_isLittleO} says that $\\rhon/n \\to 0$ as\n  $n \\to \\infty$, that is, $\\rhon = o(n)$ \\leanref{rho-little-o}. This\n  is the limit fact that Sections~\\ref{sec:statement},\n  \\ref{sec:beyond} and \\ref{sec:consequences} cite: in the limit the\n  exponent is smaller than $n$ by any factor one names. It is true at the\n  same time as the title's fact that $\\rhon > n$ for every\n  $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, and the Lean says nothing about where the limit\n  begins to bite. \\texttt{isSubexpExponent\\_runtimeScale} is the same\n  statement for $A \\cdot \\rhon$, for every constant $A$\n  \\leanref{is-subexp-exponent}.\n\\item \\textbf{The time bound is a bound on the plain machine.}\n  \\texttt{Trace.config\\_of\\_locatedStep}: the comparator states its time\n  bound on the \\emph{located} step, which records visited cells beside\n  each configuration. A run of the located step projects to a run of the\n  plain step with the same number of steps, so the bound is a bound on\n  the machine itself \\leanref{trace-projection}. The one-line\n  \\texttt{LocatedCfg.monitor\\_config} is its helper.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{The axiom audit}\n\\label{sec:lean-audit}\n\nLean records, for every proved statement, the axioms its proof uses.\nThree are standard in Mathlib and in every proof of Sections~\\ref{sec:proof}\nand \\ref{sec:replay}: \\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Classical.choice} and\n\\texttt{Quot.sound}. A fourth, \\texttt{sorryAx}, appears exactly when a\nproof was left out with \\texttt{sorry}. The audit\n\\repofile{paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean} prints the axioms of 21\ndeclarations: the ten copied definitions the statement rests on, the two\ndefs, and the nine lemmas and helper definitions, in the pattern of our\nfirst paper's audit. It is run from our library's directory as\n\\begin{verbatim}\ncd lean && lake env lean ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean \\\n  > ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.log\n\\end{verbatim}\nand the log is committed. The result: nineteen lines on exactly the three\nstandard axioms; \\texttt{Trace} on no axiom at all (it is an inductive\ndefinition); \\texttt{countWord} on two of the three; \\texttt{sorryAx} on\nnone. The library it runs against is built with Lean~4.34.0-rc2 and\nMathlib at \\texttt{1192d624}, neither of which is what the clone pins\n(Lean~4.34.1, Mathlib \\texttt{d13f23b7}, Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). That\nthe copied model elaborates unchanged against a different toolchain and a\ndifferent Mathlib revision is small evidence that it rests on nothing\npatched or private, and no more than that. Our whole library builds with\nno \\texttt{sorry} and no axiom declaration, which the loop's gate checks\nafter every session.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:lean-notclaim}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) is a def of a Prop, never proved\n  here.} Its truth is OpenAI's theorem, replayed in Section~\\ref{sec:replay};\n  our library neither proves it nor assumes it. Nothing in this section\n  makes the refutation more or less certain.\n\\item \\textbf{The copy is definitions only.} The proof is not copied,\n  and could not be: it is 3,091 modules (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). What is\n  copied is the vocabulary of the statement, so that the restatement\n  reads as the comparator does.\n\\item \\textbf{No Lean computation evaluates the exponent.} $\\runtimeScale$\n  is defined over the real numbers with real logarithms and is marked\n  \\texttt{noncomputable}; no \\texttt{\\#eval} can print $\\rho(50)$ or\n  $\\rho(50)/50$. Every number in Section~\\ref{sec:statement} comes from a\n  Python script, not from Lean, and the Lean proves only the limit.\n\\item \\textbf{The match is textual.} ``Character for character'' means\n  the normalised texts are equal; it is not a check at the level of\n  \\texttt{lean4export}, and it rests on the clone being the repository at\n  the commit named.\n\\item \\textbf{The lemmas are trivial on purpose.} They fix the shape of\n  the bound and the limit of the exponent, and nothing else. No theorem\n  about any machine's running time, about the constants, or about the\n  manuscript's sharper exponents is stated, because none is verified.\n\\item \\textbf{The directory name is not a citation.} The files sit in a\n  directory named for another study of ours; nothing from that study\n  enters this paper, and the owner's decision to leave them in place is\n  recorded, not argued.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\endgroup\n","userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01LinYhBa6Nqpmtt9bU5o7Bk","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
