{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018X6Uew2sBRuqsjcM2RoeAh","type":"tool_result","content":"2     The verified statement, read exactly\n2.1   What we are reading, and the words we need\nOpenAI’s collection (OpenAI, 2026) holds, for the result it calls family 378, one machine-checked\ntheorem. It is named exact_count_and_sat. It is stated in the family’s challenge file, which\nis 220 lines long: lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean at commit\n4eaee875 of the collection. This section reads that file and says what the theorem asserts and\nwhat it does not. Line numbers are those of the file. We quote nothing from memory.\n    A few words first, each in one sentence. Lean is a proof assistant: a program that checks every\nstep of a proof and refuses a proof with a gap. The kernel is the small core of Lean that does the\nfinal checking; when we say a statement is verified, we mean the kernel accepted a proof of it. A\nformula here is a Boolean formula in conjunctive normal form: a list of clauses, each clause an “or”\n\n                                                      1\n\fof a few variables or negated variables, and the whole formula true when every clause is true.\nA k-CNF is such a formula whose clauses have at most k variables each; 3-SAT is the problem\nof deciding whether a 3-CNF can be made true. A satisfying assignment is a choice of true or\nfalse for every variable that makes the formula true; with n variables there are 2n assignments\nto try, and trying all 2n assignments is the method the title names. The comparator is OpenAI’s\nchecking tool. It takes a challenge file, which states a theorem and leaves the proof blank, and\na solution, which proves it, and checks that the two state the same theorem and that the proof\nuses only three standard axioms. The file we read is the challenge file. It carries the statement\nwith the placeholder sorry where the proof would go (line 218). The solution proves the same\nstatement in 3,091 files (Section 4). We rebuilt that proof ourselves and the kernel reported the\nthree standard axioms and nothing else (Section 5).\n\n2.2   The definitions, in words\nThe theorem speaks about five kinds of object. Each is defined in the file before the theorem.\nAppendix B.1 gives every definition with its line numbers; here is what each one means.\n\nFormulas and the count (lines 13–51). A formula is a finite set of clauses over n variables, and\na clause is a finite set of signed variables. The formula F has width at most k when every clause\nhas at most k members (line 38). The number count F is how many of the 2n assignments make\nthe formula true (line 47). Because clauses and formulas are sets, a repeated clause collapses into\none; the empty clause is allowed and can never be satisfied; and a clause holding both a variable\nand its negation is allowed and counts as two members. The manuscript says the same thing in\nits own words: “Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than k literals” (p. 1, line 44).\n\nThe input encoding (lines 53–81). The machine does not read a formula; it reads a string of\nbits. The file fixes how a formula becomes bits. Numbers are written in unary, that is, n is written\nas n ones and a zero. Each clause becomes a sorted list of such numbers, the formula becomes\na list of clauses, and the number of variables is written in front. Two things matter. First, the\nnumber of variables is part of the input, so a variable used in no clause still counts. Second, the\nstring is not long: a formula with m clauses of width k on n variables takes at most about 8 k n m\nbits. In symbols, with L the length of the string,\n                                                          \u0001\n                            L ≤ n + 2 + m 2k (4n + 1) + 4 = O(k n m).\nA formula with 125 variables, 500 clauses and width 4 becomes 1,006,223 bits; one with 1,000\nvariables, 4,000 clauses and width 3 becomes 47.9 million bits. Both numbers are from a script\nthat imitates the definitions, reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py. Nothing\nin the encoding grows exponentially.\n\nThe machine (lines 83–128). The machine is a Turing machine with several tapes: a finite table\nof states, a finite number of tapes, and a rule that, in each state, reads one cell of one tape and\nthen either stops with a yes-or-no answer, or writes one symbol, or moves one head by one cell.\nEvery part of it is finite. A step is one lookup in a table with three rows per state. No part\nof the machine can hide an unbounded computation inside a step. The tapes and the moves\nare Mathlib’s standard ones (Mathlib is the community library of mathematics for Lean, at the\nrevision the collection pins, d13f23b7).\n\n                                                 2\n\fRuns (lines 87–90). A trace of length t is a record that the machine, started in one configura-\ntion, reached another in exactly t successful steps. The step rule is a function, so each run is\ndetermined by its input; there is no randomness and no guessing.\n\nSpace monitoring (lines 147–189). Beside each configuration the file keeps, for every tape, the\nfurthest cells the head has reached to the left and to the right. The visited space is the total number\nof cells ever visited, summed over the tapes. The count word is a number written in binary, least\nsignificant bit first (line 147).\n\nThe exponent and the bound (lines 130–141). The theorem’s running-time bound is built from\none function of n, which the file calls runtimeScale and we write ρ(n):\n                                                              \u00012\n                                            n ln ln(n + 16)\n                                     ρ(n) =   p                    ,\n                                                ln(n + 16)\n\nwith ln the natural logarithm. In words: take n; multiply it by the square of the logarithm of the\nlogarithm of n + 16; divide by the square root of the logarithm of n + 16. The manuscript uses the\nsame logarithm: “Throughout, log denotes the natural logarithm” (p. 1, lines 48–49; again at p. 7,\nlines 326–327). The +16 keeps the double logarithm defined and positive at every n, including\nn = 0, so the theorem needs no condition on n. The bound itself, called runtimeBound (lines\n136–139), is\n                                        C ( L + 1) d 2 A ρ ( n ) ,\nthat is, a constant C, times a power d of the input length L, times two raised to A times ρ(n). We\ncall ρ(n) the exponent and C ( L + 1)d 2 Aρ(n) the bound throughout the paper. The constants C, A\nand d are the subject of the next subsection.\n\n2.3     What the theorem says, and in what order\nLines 194–217 state the theorem. Appendix B.2 displays it with every quantifier and the line of\nevery clause. In words, for every fixed width k of at least 2:\n\n        There exist a machine, a count tape, an answer tape, and five positive constants\n        C, A, S, d, e, such that for every number of variables n and every formula F on n vari-\n        ables of width at most k, the machine started on the encoding of F halts after some\n        number of steps, time, with the count of satisfying assignments written in binary on\n        the count tape, the bit “is the count positive” on the answer tape and in its halting\n        state, a count word of at most n + 1 bits, time at most C ( L + 1)d 2 Aρ(n) , and never\n        more than S ( L + 1)e cells visited.\n\n      Three features of the order of the words matter for everything that follows.\n\nThe machine and the constants come after k and before n. For each width k there is one\nmachine and one set of constants, and they serve every formula of every size. They may depend\non k and on nothing else. This is what complexity theorists write as Ok (1): a constant that\ndepends only on k.\n\n\n                                                  3\n\fThere is no lower limit on n. The manuscript’s Theorem 1.1 and its Proposition 6.4 are stated\nfor n ≥ 20 (p. 2, lines 56–57; p. 43, lines 2474–2475). The verified theorem has no such condition;\nthe +16 in the exponent is what makes that possible.\n\nThe constants exist, and that is all. The theorem says that values of C, A, S, d, e exist. It names\nnone of them. The proof obtains them from other existence lemmas and never writes a num-\nber down. We read the proof for the values it does pin: at the point where the main run is\nassembled, the exponent constant is set to A = 256 + At with At at least 1 (SqrtLogSpace/\nNativeLargeEntry.lean, line 51; SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean, lines 792–\n793). So the proof as written has A ≥ 257, and the theorem that the comparator exhibits adds\none more (Section 4, A ≥ 258). No closed value of any of the five constants exists anywhere.\nThe machine is explicit in one respect: it is compiled from a program with 80 one-bit registers\nand 1,220 tapes (SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean, lines 98–101). How many states it has is never\ncomputed.\n\n2.4   The exponent, in numbers\nEverything the title says rests on one comparison: the exponent ρ(n) against n itself. If ρ(n) is\nbelow n, the bound’s exponential part 2ρ(n) is below 2n , the cost of trying every assignment. If\nρ(n) is above n, it is not.\n                                                                              √\n    The ratio ρ(n)/n depends only on y = ln(n + 16): it equals (ln y)2 / y. That ratio starts\nat 0.64 for n = 1 and rises. It crosses 1 between n = 49 and n = 50: ρ(49)/49 = 0.9994\nand ρ(50)/50 = 1.0027. It keeps rising to a peak of 16/e2 = 2.165, reached when ln y = 4,\nwhich is at n ≈ 1023.7 , a number with 24 digits. Then it falls, and it is back below 1 only when\ny = 5503.7, which is at n ≈ e5504 ≈ 102390 , a number with 2,391 digits. Table 2.1 gives the values\nand Figures 2.1 and 2.2 draw them. Every number is printed by paper3/scripts/runtime_\nscale.py, run under python3 -I; it imports nothing from the clone and works in y, so that\nno n beyond the range of floating-point arithmetic is ever formed.\n    Two facts are true at once, and the title is about the second. In the limit, ρ(n)/n goes to 0:\nthe exponent grows more slowly than n, which is what “subexponential” means, and that is a\ntheorem in our companion Lean (Appendix A, A.6). And on every size anyone will ever write\ndown, from 50 to a number with 2,390 digits, the exponent is above n.\n\n2.5   The title’s claim, made precise\nThe title says the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2n assignments at every practical\nsize. Read against the theorem, that means the following four things, and nothing more.\n\n  1. What is verified is a guarantee, not a running time. The theorem says the machine halts\n     within C ( L + 1)d 2 Aρ(n) steps. It does not say how many steps the machine actually takes\n     on any input. No implementation of the machine exists to be timed. A bound above 2n\n     does not say the machine is slow; it says the proof promises nothing better than 2n there.\n\n  2. The constants are never computed. The theorem asserts that C, A, S, d, e exist. The proof\n     builds them from other existence lemmas and states no value, beyond the A ≥ 257 read\n     above. The machine’s number of states is likewise never computed.\n\n\n                                                4\n\f                                           peak 2.17 at n 1023.7\n\n                                   2.0              verified exponent, (n)/n with (n) = n(ln ln(n+16))2/ ln(n+16)\n\n\n\nexponent relative to brute force\n                                   1.5\n                                               above 2n from n = 50         back below 2n only at n e5504 102390\n\n\n\n\n   (bound = 2 ratio n at A = 1)\n                                                                                                                    brute force: 2n\n                                   1.0\n\n                                                    manuscript, general: nln ln n/ ln n (not verified)\n                                   0.5\n\n                                                    manuscript, fixed density: n/ ln n (not verified)\n                                   0.0\n                                                       500           1000            1500         2000         2500           3000\n                                               number of variables n (axis is log10 n: 1 means n = 10, 1000 means n = 101000)\n\n                                   2.00                                                                                           1.85\n                                   1.75                        verified exponent (n)/n\n\n\n\nexponent relative to brute force\n                                                                                                   1.62\n                                   1.50                                        1.42\n\n                                   1.25                       1.15\n                                                       1.00                                                         brute force: 2n\n\n\n\n   (bound = 2 ratio n at A = 1)\n                                   1.00\n                                                               manuscript, general (not verified)\n                                   0.75\n                                   0.50\n                                   0.25                        manuscript, fixed density (not verified)\n                                   0.00\n                                          10              102               103             104               105               106\n                                               number of variables n (the practical range; marks at n = 50, 125, 103, 104, 106)\n\nFigure 2.1: The verified exponent against trying every assignment. Each curve is an exponent divided\nby n; the dashed line at 1 is 2n , the cost of trying every assignment, and a curve above the line is a\nguarantee worse than that. Top: the whole range, the horizontal axis being the number of digits of n,\nfrom n = 10 to n = 103000 . The solid curve is the verified\n                                                        √ exponent ρ(n)/n; the two curves            √ beneath it\nare the manuscript’s two sharper exponents, n ln ln n/ ln n (Theorem 1.1, general) and n/ ln n (fixed\nclause density), which are not in the Lean library (Section 3). Bottom: the same three curves over the\npractical range n = 10 to 106 , with ρ(n)/n marked at n = 50, 125, 103 , 104 , 106 . All curves take the constant\nA to be 1; the verified A is unknown and at least 257 in the proof on record. Regenerate: python3 -I\npaper3/scripts/rho_over_n.py.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                      5\n\fTable 2.1: The verified exponent compared with n. The second column is the ratio ρ(n)/n; where it is\nabove 1, the bound’s exponential part 2ρ(n) (with A = 1) is        n\n                                                           pabove 2 , the cost of trying every assignment.\nThe third column is ρ(n) itself. ρ(n) = n(ln ln(n + 16))2 / ln(n + 16), the comparator’s runtimeScale\n(lines 132–134), natural logarithms. Regenerate: python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py.\n\n                                                                                 n    ρ(n)/n            ρ(n)\n                                                                                  1   0.6443           0.64\n                                                                                 20   0.8606          17.21\n                                                                                 49   0.9994          48.97\n                                                                                 50   1.0027          50.14\n                                                                                10