nts in it are not tracked. The manuscript itself calls ρ(n) “the weaker\nexponent” (p. 2, line 63), and its sharper exponents are not in the Lean library (Section 3). One\ndetail of bookkeeping: inside the solution the exponent is written with base-2 logarithms, and a\nlemma relates the two forms by a constant factor that A absorbs (Appendix B.2); nothing above\ndepends on it.\n\n2.6     What the statement does establish\nFor each fixed width k ≥ 2, there is a deterministic machine that, given any k-CNF on n vari-\nables with m clauses in the fixed encoding, halts with the exact number of satisfying assignments\nwritten in binary on a named tape, within C ( L + 1)d 2 Aρ(n) steps. Here L grows no faster than a\npolynomial in n + m, and ρ(n) grows more slowly than n. A formula of width k on n variables\ncannot have more than about nk distinct clauses (2,604,376 at n = 125, k = 3; Appendix B.2),\nso the whole bound is subexponential in n alone. In the standard vocabulary: a deterministic\n2o(n) · poly(n + m) algorithm for counting the solutions of k-SAT, and therefore for deciding it.\nThat refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis (Impagliazzo and Paturi, 2001), the conjecture that\n3-SAT needs time exponential in n, as stated. The verified theorem is the manuscript’s own\nverified form, the weaker exponent of its Proposition 6.4 (p. 43, lines 2474–2476), without the\nn ≥ 20 condition. We found no field, definition or quantifier through which the statement could\nbe accepted by the kernel and yet mean something weaker than this; the audit behind that sen-\ntence is reports/scratch/eth_audit/statement_audit.md. Our own restatement of the\ntheorem as a named Lean sentence, over a verbatim copy of the machine model, is (Appendix A,\nA.2); it is never proved and never assumed there (Section 8).\n\n2.7     What this does not claim\n      • No running time of any machine. Nothing here measures or estimates how long the\n        verified machine, or any program, takes on any input. The title compares two guarantees.\n        Section 7 is the only place in the paper where anything is timed, and it times ordinary\n        solvers, not this machine.\n\n                                                   7\n\f      • No statement about the proof. That the statement means what we say it means is a reading\n        of 220 lines. Whether the proof is sound is Section 4 and Section 5, which found no flaw.\n        The one proof-side fact quoted here, A = 256 + At , is a line of the solution read for the\n        value it assigns, not a claim about the argument.\n\n      • No value of the constants. A ≥ 257 is what the proof writes down, not what the machine\n        needs; a different proof of the same statement could have any A > 0. The statement itself\n        fixes nothing.\n\n      • Nothing about the manuscript’s sharper exponents, which are not in the Lean library and\n        are Section 3’s subject.\n\n      • No claim about sizes beyond e5504 . Past n ≈ 102390 the verified exponent is below n again\n        at A = 1; we say nothing about that range except that it is not a practical one.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HmjncNirMGedmcKgknwtRa","agent_id":"a37a7ff12e4b766a1","session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"524d1117-396e-43ea-a323-4cbe541ae5d7","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:44:41.364Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018X6Uew2sBRuqsjcM2RoeAh","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Blind lay reader of paper 3"}
