{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EcK3u5W8HE97hhfKZ14UvZ","type":"tool_result","content":"3      What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement\nThe manuscript (OpenAI, 2026) is 67 pages long. The verified statement is 220 lines long. Sec-\ntion 2 read the statement. This section reads the manuscript’s claims one by one. For each claim\nit says one of three things: this is what the Lean theorem asserts; this follows from the Lean\ntheorem by an argument that exists only on paper; or this rests on a bound that the Lean library\ndoes not contain at all.\n    The collection draws the line itself, in a short note on the family. We quote its two sentences\nin full (lean/docs/378.md, lines 9 and 11):\n        “For fixed k ≥ 2, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly counts satisfying √\n                                                                                             assign-\n                                                                                                     2\n        ments of a canonically encoded k-CNF on n variables in time ( L + 1)Ok (1) 2Ok (n(log log n) / log n) ,\n        where L is the encoding’s bit length. At every step, the machine has visited at most ( L + 1)Ok (1)\n        tape cells in total.”\n        “For m clauses,           √ October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound (n +\n                                 the\n        m)Ok (1) 2Ok (n log log n/\n                               √\n                                   log n)\n                                          . When m ≤ Dn for fixed D > 0, this improves to (n +\n        m)Ok,D (1) 2Ok,D (n/    log n)\n                                         .”\n\nIn words: the Lean proves the bound with the exponent of Section 2, the one with the squared\ndouble logarithm. The manuscript claims two better exponents, one with the double logarithm\nnot squared, and one, for formulas with few clauses, with no double logarithm at all. Everything\nbelow is an elaboration of that note against the manuscript’s text and the library’s files. Table 3.2\nis the summary.\n\n3.1     Three statuses, and what “paper-only” means\nWe tag every claim with one of three words.\n\n      • Verified. The claim is the comparator statement exact_count_and_sat, or one of its\n        parts, or a theorem of the solution tree that the replay of Section 5 compiled and whose\n        axioms it printed. Nothing else is called verified.\n\n\n\n                                                          8\n\f      • Supported. The claim follows from the verified statement by an argument that is on paper\n        only: a short reduction, or a citation. The argument is not in the Lean library, and we\n        have not checked it. What we have checked is narrower: the only property of the counter\n        the argument uses is one the verified statement provides, and the exponent it inherits is\n        therefore ρ(n) and not a sharper one.\n\n      • Paper-only. The claim rests on a bound that the Lean library at commit 4eaee875 does\n        not contain: Theorem 1.1’s two sharper exponents, and whatever is derived from them.\n\n    The brief for this section had two tags, verified and paper-only. The middle one is ours. The\nmanuscript’s corollaries are of two different kinds, and one word would have put Corollary 1.3,\nan encoding into the verified counter, beside Corollary 1.4, a bound whose proof calls the sharper\nexponent by name. In the brief’s two-way split, “supported” is a kind of “paper-only”: not in the\nlibrary. In no case does “paper-only” mean “wrong”. We did not read the manuscript’s proofs\nfor correctness. The tag records where a claim is established, not whether it is.\n\n3.2     The sharper exponents of Theorem 1.1\nTheorem 1.1 (p. 2, lines 56–62) states two running-time bounds for the same algorithm.\n                                                                                    √ The first\nholds for every k-CNF on n ≥ 20 variables: a polynomial in n + m times 2Ok (n log log n/ log n) . The\nsecond √holds when the number of clauses is at most a fixed multiple D of n: a polynomial times\n2Ok,D (n/ log n) . Both use a polynomial amount of space (line 60). The sentence that follows the\ntheorem is the one this paper’s title is about (p. 2, lines 63–67):\n        “The rational interpolation argument   in Section 6.4 gives the weaker exponent Ok (ρ(n)),\n        where ρ(n) = n(log log n) / log n = n/(log n)1/2+o(1) . Appendix D proves both bounds\n                                   2\n                                     p\n\n        in Theorem 1.1 by tensor contraction.”\n\nProposition 6.4 (p. 43, lines 2474–2476) states the weaker bound on its own: for each fixed k ≥ 2,\nthe interpolation algorithm counts in a polynomial times 2Ok (ρ(n)) bit operations and polynomial\nspace, for n ≥ 20.\n\nWhich of the three is in the Lean. The verified exponent is the weaker one.         p The compara-\n                                                                                  2\ntor’s runtimeScale (lines 132–134 of the comparator file) is n(ln ln(n + 16)) / ln(n + 16), the\nsquared form. The solution’s own exponent, called rho (SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean,\nlines 10–11), is the same function with base-2 logarithms, and a lemma, runtimeScale_le_\nfour_rho (lines 190–191), bounds the comparator’s exponent by four times it for n ≥ 20.\nThe proof is built around the parameters of the manuscript’s Section 6.4, to the letter: the\n                                                                  √\nmanuscript selects λn = ⌈log2 n⌉, νn = ⌈log2 λn ⌉ and s = ⌊ λn /νn2 ⌋, “the largest nonnegative\ninteger satisfying s2 νn4 ≤ λn ” (p. 43, lines 2480–2486), and Asymptotics.lean lines 14–20 de-\nfine sparsifyLambda, sparsifyNu and sparsifySchedule as exactly those three quantities.\nWhat Appendix D of the manuscript proves is p      not in the library in any form we could find\n                                                                                              p (Sec-\ntion 3.6). So the general exponent n log log n/ log n and the fixed-density exponent n/ log n\nare paper-only: proved at p. 57 (lines 3222–3246) and p. 54 (lines 3045–3064) respectively, by the\ncontraction theorem of Appendix D.\n\n\n\n\n                                                    9\n\fBoth bounds from one algorithm. The manuscript notes that a single algorithm attains both\npaper-only bounds, by switching between two methods according to the number of clauses (p. 57,\nlines 3247–3250). That algorithm is not the verified one either.\n\nThe three exponents, in numbers. Table 3.1 puts the three exponents side by side, each divided\nby n and each with its constant taken to be 1. A value above 1 means the exponent is above n,\nso the bound is worse than trying every\n                                      √ assignment. The verified ratio is the one of Table 2.1.\n                                                      √ 1: it peaks at 2/e = 0.736 at n ≈ 1,618\nThe general paper-only ratio, ln ln n/ ln n, never reaches\nand falls on both sides. The fixed-density ratio 1/ ln n falls from 0.578 at n = 20. So, at\nconstant 1, both paper-only guarantees sit below 2n at every size, and the verified one sits above\n2n from n = 50 to n ≈ 102390 . That is the whole content of the word “verified” in the title:\nthe manuscript’s exponents would make a different sentence, and they are not what the kernel\nchecked. Two cautions apply to the table equally. The constants in front of every exponent are\nnot computed anywhere, in the manuscript or in the Lean. And the manuscript’s exponents\nare undefined below n = e and are stated for n ≥ 20, while the verified one is stated for all n.\nFigure 2.1 draws the same three curves.\n\nTable 3.1: The three exponents of the manuscript, each divided by n, with every constant taken to be\n1. A value above 1 means the bound     p is worse than trying every assignment at that n. Columns: the\nverified ρ(n)√/n = (ln ln(n + 16))2 / ln(n + 16) (comparator lines√ 132–134); Theorem 1.1’s general expo-\n nent ln ln n/ ln n (p. 2, line 59); its fixed-density exponent 1/ ln n (p. 2, line 62). Natural logarithms\n                                              2\n throughout (p. 1, lines 48–49). The row ee is where the general ratio peaks (2/e). Regenerate: python3\n-I paper3/scripts/beyond_claims.py.\n                                                                 √                 √\n                 n            verified ρ(n)/n    general ln ln n/ ln n   density 1/ ln n\n                 20                      0.861                   0.634              0.578\n                 50                      1.003                   0.690              0.506\n                 125                     1.150                   0.717              0.455\n                 103                     1.423                   0.735              0.380\n                   2\n                 ee ≈ 1,618              1.472                   0.736              0.368\n                 106                     1.855                   0.706              0.269\n                 1023.7                  2.165                   0.541              0.135\n\n\n\nThe abstract’s form. The manuscript’s abstract states the running time as a polynomial times\n           1/2+o (1)\n2n/(log n)           (p. 1, lines 6–9). The small term o (1) in that exponent is a quantity that goes to\nzero as n grows. The verified exponent is of that form too, because (ln ln n)2 is (ln n)o(1) , and the\nmanuscript says so at lines 64–66. We tag the abstract’s form verified, with the remark that its\no (1) hides exactly the factor by which the three rows of Table 3.1 differ: at n = 106 that factor is\n6.9 for the verified exponent, 2.6 for the general one and 1 for the fixed-density one.\n\n3.3   The corollaries\nFinding and sampling a satisfying assignment (p. 2, lines 68–71). “When #F > 0, the standard\nself-reduction finds a satisfying assignment by fixing one variable at a time and checking that the\nremaining count is positive. Choosing each bit according to the two conditional counts instead\n\n\n                                                    10\n\fgives an exactly uniform random satisfying assignment,” with polynomial expected overhead\nand a citation. Supported. The method calls the verified counter at most n times on formulas of\nthe same width, and the verified statement provides the exact count it needs. The method is two\nsentences of prose and is not in the library.\n\nCorollary 1.2, Max-k-SAT within εm (p. 2, lines 77–94). A randomised algorithm finds an\nassignment that satisfies at least OPT( F ) − εm clauses, that is, within a small fraction of the best\npossible, with high probability, in time\n\n                                       (n + m)Ok,ε (1) 2Ok,ε (ρ(n))\n\n(line 80). The corollary is stated with ρ(n) itself, the verified exponent. Its proof samples O(n/ε2 )\nclauses, builds a small circuit, turns the circuit into a 3-CNF with Ok,ε (n) variables by a cited\nlemma, and calls Theorem 1.1 with binary search and self-reduction (lines 82–94). Supported:\nthe exponent matches the verified one because ρ(cn) = Oc (ρ(n)), and every call is to a fixed-\nwidth counter on O(n) variables; the sampling, the circuit and the reduction are on paper. Its\nrandomness is in the reduction, not in the counter.\n\nCorollary 1.3, independent sets, colourings, perfect matchings (p. 2–3, lines 100–120). The\nnumber of independent sets and, for fixed q, of proper q-colourings of a graph on v vertices and\ne edges, in time 2o(v) , and the number of perfect matchings in time 2o(v+e) , all in polynomial\nspace. The encodings are explicit: one variable per vertex and a clause of two literals per edge\n(lines 107–112); q variables per vertex with “exactly one colour” and “no equal colours” clauses, a\nq-CNF on qv variables (lines 112–115); one variable per edge and a linear-size circuit for “exactly\none selected edge meets each vertex”, reduced to 3-CNF with O(v + e) variables, with the extra\nvariables fixed by the edge bits so that the count is preserved (lines 116–119). Supported: the\nconclusion is 2o(·) , which needs only that ρ(n) grows more slowly than n (Appendix A, A.6), and\neach encoding preserves the count into a fixed-width CNF on O(v) or O(v + e) variables. None\nof the three encodings is in the library.\n\nCorollary 1.4, a circuit lower bound for ENP (p. 3, lines 121–127). “The fixed-density case of\nTheorem 1.1 gives a circuit lower bound for ENP ” (line 121): there\n                                                                 p is a language L in the class E\n                                                                                                  NP\n\nand a constant c > 0 such that L needs circuits of more than c n log n gates for infinitely many n\n(lines 123–127). The proof is Appendix C.1 (p. 51–52, lines 2925–2967), Williams’s witness-circuit\nargument p with a local reduction, and it invokes the paper-only exponent in so many words: “Put\na(t) = log2 t. Testing whether the count in the constant-density part of Theorem 1.1 is positive\ndecides 3-SAT with V variables and at most 5V clauses in time V b 2 AV/a(V ) for fixed A, b > 0”\n                           p threshold sn = ⌊ηn⌊ a(n)⌋⌋ is chosen against that a (line 2953), and\n(p. 52, lines 2949–2950); the                                                                 p the\nconstant c comes out as η/(d log 2) (line 2967). Paper-only: the quantitative bound c n log n\n                √\nis tied to 2 AV/ log V , which the library does not contain. What the same argument would give\nfrom the verified exponent is a question about the manuscript’s proof, which we do not examine\n(Section 3.8).\n\nCorollary C.1, ENP ̸⊆ SIZE(O(n)) (p. 51, lines 2912–2916). “Williams proved that if ETH is false,\nthen ENP 