ontracted in (s + L)O(1) 2O(s/ log s) bit operations\nand polynomial space. The fixed-density proof (p. 54, lines 3045–3064) encodes a formula as a\nnetwork with Ok (n + m) edges and applies Theorem D.1 with s = n + m; the arbitrary-density\nproof (p. 57, lines 3222–3246) combines the sparsification\n                                                        p     of Section 6 with Lemma D.2 (p. 54,\nline 3069) and reaches the exponent Ok (n log log n/ log n) at line 3243. Section D.4 changes\nthe logarithm convention for its own proof, “log denotes a base-two logarithm and ln denotes\na natural logarithm” (p. 57, lines 3251–3252). All of this is paper-only: no declaration about\ntensor networks, contraction, Hermite recovery or packets exists in the family’s 3,977 files, by the\nidentifier search of Section 3.6. We make no statement about Appendix D’s argument; we did\nnot read it for correctness.\n\n3.5   The hypotheses of §1.1.1 and the fine-grained remarks of §1.1.3\n§1.1.1 (p. 4, lines 187–191). “Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed,\ntesting whether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability and unsatisfiabil-\nity in deterministic 2o(n) (n + m)Ok (1) time for every fixed k; deciding satisfiability also solves\nthe Gap-ETH promise problem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\nrandomized versions of ETH [. . . ] and SETH [. . . ], and of Gap-ETH.” And §1.1.2 (p. 5, lines\n211–212): “Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1) solves #3-SAT deterministically in 2o(n) poly(n, m) time\nand therefore refutes #ETH.” These are the four named hypotheses about how hard satisfia-\nbility is, their randomised forms, and the counting hypothesis; Section 6 states each one in\nwords. Supported, and by the shortest argument in the manuscript: the verified statement\nis a deterministic machine whose answer tape says whether the count is positive and whose\n\n\n                                                 12\n\fcount tape carri