{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NHH5droZ6gYAmPeAJ7P7uB","type":"tool_result","content":"                                                                       15\n\f4     The proof, inspected\nSection 2 read the theorem. This section reads the proof, as far as reading can go. The question\nis not whether the mathematics is right. The kernel decides that, and Section 5 reports what\nthe kernel said when we ran it. The question is whether anything in the source could make the\nkernel’s acceptance mean less than it appears to mean. There are known ways for that to happen:\nan added axiom; a sorry, which is Lean’s word for a proof left out; a step proved by trusting a\ncompiled program rather than the kernel; a patched copy of the mathematics library; a statement\nin the solution that differs from the statement in the challenge; or a running-time bound proved\nagainst a cost model of the proof’s own choosing. We found none of these. What we did find is\nthat the repository offers no recorded evidence that the proof type-checks at all: no saved output\nof the axiom check, no automated build, and no second checker for this family. That is why\nSection 5 exists. The last subsection joins the two.\n\n4.1   Method\nThe clone of openai/math at commit 4eaee875 is treated as untrusted data. Every file was\nread with cat, sed, grep and python3 -I, and nothing in it was built, imported or executed\nfor this section. Two scripts from the audit of 2026-11-05 do the mechanical part. closure.py\nfollows the import lines from the solution’s main module, strips comments, and counts how\noften each keyword of interest occurs in what remains. defdiff.py compares each defini-\ntion in the challenge file with the library’s definition of the same name, ignoring differences\nin spacing. Both were re-run for this section, and every number below is from that fresh run;\npaper3/scripts/proof_inspection.py re-runs them, performs the remaining checks, and\nregenerates Table 4.1. The inspection reads the spine of the proof, that is, the final file, the\ncompiler, the entry lemmas and the constants, and sweeps the whole closure by keyword. The\narithmetic beneath the spine was not read (Section 4.9).\n\n4.2   The closure\nThe closure of a module is the set of all files it depends on, directly or through other files. The com-\nparator’s JSON names one solution module, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main,\na sixteen-line file that imports two others and declares nothing (Main.lean). Its closure inside\nthe repository is 3,091 modules, every one in the family’s own directory, totalling 550,551 lines;\nthe largest file is 2,873 lines (SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparsePriorityProgram.\nlean). Outside the family, the closure reaches 50 modules of Mathlib and one module of Lean’s\nown library, Lean.Elab.Tactic.Omega, imported by SqrtLogSpace/FiniteFallback.\nlean (line 14) and SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparseCharging.lean (line 5). That\none module is a proof tactic, a tool for writing proofs; the closure defines no tactic of its own\n(Section 4.3). Nothing is imported from any other family of the collection or from any of the\nrepository’s other dependencies.\n     The family’s directory holds 3,977 .lean files, so 886 are outside the closure. They are not\nreachable from the collection’s root module either: lean/OAI.lean imports the family only at\nits line 49, and the build’s default target is that root (lean/lakefile.lean, line 89). They are\nnever built by the default target and bear on nothing: dead files, not a hole. For completeness,\n\n\n\n                                                  16\n\fa raw search of the 886 for sorry, axiom, native_decide and admit finds nothing, with or\nwithout comments.\n\n4.3     The keyword sweep\nTable 4.1 gives the counts over the 3,091 files with comments stripped. The ones that matter are\nthe zeros, and each zero rules out one way of weakening a verified proof.\n\n      • No axiom, no sorry, no admit: nothing is assumed and no proof is left out.\n\n      • No native_decide and no ofReduceBool: nothing is proved by trusting a compiled\n        program’s answer instead of the kernel.\n\n      • No implemented_by, extern, unsafe or partial: no definition has a hidden imple-\n        mentation, and every recursion is one the kernel checks for termination.\n\n      • No elab, macro, syntax or simproc: no custom programming of Lean itself that could\n        rewrite a goal outside the kernel’s view.\n\n    The non-zero counts are ordinary. Nine declarations are opaque, a keyword that hides a\ndefinition’s body from the proof-writing layer; every one of the nine has a body, and the kernel\nchecks that body against its type, so nothing is assumed. The 1,048 noncomputable declara-\ntions, the eight open Classical and the four explicit uses each of Classical.choice and\npropext are the classical reasoning the comparator permits. The 148 instances and 40 local in-\nstances are standard typeclass instances; an instance cannot be “inconsistent” without an axiom,\nand there is none. The only options set in the closure switch off a pre-check on local notation in\ntwo files and have no bearing on soundness. Appendix B.4 lists every one of these with its file\nand line.\n\n4.4     The statement is the challenge’s\nThe solution does not import the challenge file. Instead the library declares again, under the same\nnames, every definition the statement uses, spread over seven files. So one has to check that the\ntwo copies agree. The definition diff compares 35 declarations: 33 are identical character for\ncharacter once spacing is ignored, and the remaining two, runtimeScale and runtimeBound,\ndiffer only in that the challenge marks each definition noncomputable by itself where the\nlibrary marks a whole section so (SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean, line 15), a difference the\nkernel does not see. The theorem statement itself, from its first word to the start of its proof, is\nidentical in the two files, 1,353 characters raw and 1,124 after normalisation, at line 194 of the\nchallenge and line 74 of SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean; the challenge’s proof is the single word\nsorry at its line 218. Appendix B.3 names the seven files.\n    This textual match is a sanity check and not the guarantee. The comparator (Lean FRO, 2026)\ncompares the two statements after exporting them from Lean, with every definition the statement\nmentions matched by name and content, so a library definition that differed from the challenge’s\nunder the same name would make the comparator fail rather than pass silently. What the text\nconfirms is that there is no such difference to find, and that the shape Section 2 read is the shape\nthe library proves.\n\n\n                                                17\n\fTable 4.1: The inspection in numbers. Each row is one count over the 3,091 files of the closure, comments\nstripped where a keyword is counted; the script column says which script produced it. Regenerate:\npython3 -I paper3/scripts/proof_inspection.py.\n\n quantity                                                value               script\n modules in the closure of Main.lean                     3,091               closure.py\n raw lines in those modules                              550,551             closure.py\n external imports: Mathlib modules / core modules        50 / 1              closure.py\n .lean files under the family / outside the closure      3,977 / 886         this script\n axiom,       sorry,    admit,      native_decide,       0, 0, 0, 0, 0       closure.py\n ofReduceBool\n implemented_by, extern, unsafe, partial                 0, 0, 0, 0          closure.py\n elab, macro, syntax, simproc                            0, 0, 0, 0          closure.py\n set_option (both quotPrecheck false)                    2                   closure.py\n opaque (all with bodies)                                9 (9)               closure.py, this script\n noncomputable                                           1,048               closure.py\n explicit Classical.choice / propext / open              4/4/8               closure.py\n Classical\n instance / local instance                               148 / 40            closure.py\n termination_by / decreasing_by                          9/5                 closure.py\n statement definitions compared / identical / identi-    35 / 33 / 2         defdiff.py, this script\n cal up to noncomputable\n theorem statement, characters (normalised / raw),       1,124 / 1,353       this script\n identical\n #print axioms commands: closure files / lines;          8 / 19; 17 / 35     this script\n family files / lines\n recorded depends on axioms outputs in the clone         0                   this script\n challenge JSONs: total / enable_nanoda false /          418 / 413 / 2 / 3   this script\n true / absent\n CI workflow files (lock-prs.yml only)                   1                   this script\n manifest packages / patch files / patches for Mathlib   42 / 23 / 0         this script\n\n\n4.5   The spine of the proof\nWe followed the proof from its last file down to the lemmas that connect it to the machine of the\nstatement. Appendix B.3 gives every name and line; here is what the spine shows.\n\nThe machine is a concrete table. The theorem’s machine is compiled from a program over 80\none-bit registers and 1,220 Boolean stacks, with twelve kinds of instruction (BitProgram.lean,\nlines 10–26). The compiler (SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean, line 131) builds the\nmachine’s transition table case by case from the program and then renames the states to numbers\nby a classical choice. The theorem only says such a machine exists, so this non-constructive\nrenaming costs it nothing; it is also the reason no running time can be read off the statement\n(Section 2).\n\nThe constants, as far as the proof pins them. For inputs above a cutoff, the main run supplies\na trace within the bound with 1 ≤ C and 1 ≤ A (SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean,\nline 12). Its exponent constant is 256 + At (line 51), where At is at least 1 and comes from\n\n\n                                                   18\n\fa contract on the leaf program (SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean, line 793). The\ncutoff itself is max 20 (max Nleaf Ns) (line 50), with Ns obtained from an “eventually” state-\nment and never computed. Below the cutoff, a finite scanner gives time ≤ 28700 ( L + 1)2\n(SqrtLogSpace/NativeEntry.lean, line 92). The two branches are folded together, and\nthe constants the final theorem exhibits are C + 28700, A + 1, S + 5022741, d + 2, e\n+ 2 (SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean, line 41). So the proof as written has an exponent constant\nof at least 258 in front of ρ(n), a polynomial degree that depends on k, and a leading constant of\nat least 28,701. None of these is in the statement, which asserts only that they are positive, and\nnone of the underlying quantities is computed anywhere. This confirms, from the proof side,\nwhat Section 2 said from the statement side: the constants are unknown, and the smallest they\ncould be is already far from 1.\n\nWhere a hidden cost model would live, and why it cannot. A proof of a running-time bound\nfor a machine that is compiled from a higher-level program could, in principle, prove the bound\nagainst a cost function of its own and leave the kernel to check only that function’s arithmetic.\nThat is not what happens here. The lemmas that connect the program to the machine conclude\nin the challenge’s own words: a source run of n steps becomes a run of the compiled machine of\n7n steps, with the overhead 7 proved as a theorem (SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean,\nlines 152–162), and the space lemma (SqrtLogSpace/CompileSpace.lean, lines 125–143)\nproduces a trace of the challenge’s own step function with its visited space bounded on every\nprefix. The time in the final theorem is literally the length of a trace of the challenge’s step\nfunction (its line 201). No cost function of the compiler’s appears in the statement; a wrong cost\nmodel would make these lemmas unprovable, not the theorem false. This is the structural point\non which the rest of the paper leans: whatever the proof’s internal accounting, the number the\ntheorem bounds is the number of steps of the comparator’s machine.\n\nCorrectness of the count. The outer lemma (SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean, lines 50–\n51) yields the count word and the answer bit from the canonical search, and a transport lemma\n(line 73) carries both to the machine’s tapes. These are theorems about the concrete step function.\nThe arithmetic that makes them true, the sparsification, the ring and moment computations, the\nsearch, is the content of the other three thousand files, and the only claim made about it here is\nthe one the kernel makes: it elaborates (Section 5).\n\n4.6   Toolchain, Mathlib, and the build hooks\nThe Lean version is fixed (lean/lean-toolchain: leanprover/lean4:v4.34.1; lean/\nlakefile.lean line 8: fixedToolchain := true). The manifest lists 42 packages; Math-\nlib is pinned to upstream revision d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612 at\nleanprover-community/mathlib4, and the audit of 2026-11-05 confirmed that revision on\nGitHub as the upstream commit that moves to v4.34.1. The repository carries 23 compatibility\npatches under lean/patches/, none for Mathlib; every one is for a third-party package that\nthis closure does not import. The build file has two hooks that run arbitrary code when the\nbuild tool configures or updates the package: one clones and patches eleven dependencies, the\nother verifies or applies the patches for the rest (Appendix B.4). They touch only the dependency\ndirectory, and only non-Mathlib packages. They are one reason the inspection did not build, and\n\n\n                                                19\n\fthe reason the replay of Section 5 runs inside a sandbox with the network on.\n\n4.7     What the repository does not record\nThree things one would expect beside a verified refutation of a forty-year-old hypothesis are\nabsent. Their absence is a fact about evidence, not about the proof.\n\n      • No saved axiom check. SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean ends with five #print axioms\n        commands (lines 197–201), and seven other files of the closure carry fourteen more (nine-\n        teen commands in eight files; the family’s directory holds seventeen such files, but nine of\n        them are among the 886 never built). Their output exists only while the build runs and is\n        recorded nowhere: a search of the whole clone for the string depends on axioms finds\n        nothing.\n\n      • No automated build. .github/workflows/ holds one file, lock-prs.yml, which locks\n        pull-request conversations. Nothing builds or checks the Lean.\n\n      • No second checker for this family.                  The comparator’s JSON (lean/\n        ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json) names the one theorem,\n        an empty list of definitions, the three permitted axioms propext, Quot.sound,\n        Classical.choice, and enable_nanoda: false.                   Nanoda is an independent\n        checker written in another language (Section 5); with it off, the collection’s own check of\n        this family rests on one implementation of the kernel. Of the collection’s 418 challenge\n        JSONs, 413 set enable_nanoda to false, two to true, and three omit the key. The\n        comparator’s README describes the check as: build challenge and solution in a sandbox,\n        export both, match each named theorem’s statement with every declaration it uses, confine\n        the proof to the permitted axioms, and have the Lean kernel accept it; with nanoda on,\n        the exported environment is replayed through the independent checker as well (ammkrn,\n        2026). Section 9 records the versions.\n\n    Two smaller consistency facts, for the record. The manuscript’s source is dated “17 Octo-\nber 2026, revised 25 October 2026” (preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-\nFalse-October-25-2026/build/main_full_version.tex, line 25); the collection’s his-\ntory file mentions the family once, in the sentence that names the working group at whose","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HmjncNirMGedmcKgknwtRa","agent_id":"a37a7ff12e4b766a1","session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"17dc883c-4908-4197-829f-4c5ef79ad163","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:44:46.532Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01NHH5droZ6gYAmPeAJ7P7uB","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Blind lay reader of paper 3"}
