{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SCLYmPdmQJKHopXzoKzM26","type":"tool_result","content":"tory file mentions the family once, in the sentence that names the working group at whose\nrequest the materials were released (history.md, line 18), and its only “Formalization Fix” en-\ntry (line 7) concerns another\n                           pfamily. And the scope note lean/docs/378.md states the verified\nexponent as n(log log n)2 / log n (line 9), which is what the library proves and what Section 3\ncompares with the manuscript’s.\n\n4.8     The trust boundary, joined with the replay\nBefore anything was run, the proof’s trust rested on five things: (i) OpenAI’s unpublished build,\nsince nothing showed the closure type-checks; (ii) the absence of any recorded axiom output; (iii)\nthe Lean kernel of v4.34.1 and Mathlib d13f23b7 being what upstream publishes; (iv) the\ncomparator run, not reproduced; (v) no second checker for this family. Table 4.2 says which of\nthe five Section 5 moved.\n    After the replay, then, the proof’s trust rests on four things: the Lean kernel of v4.34.1 and\nnanoda agreeing on the exported dependencies of the theorem; the export tool lean4export\n\n                                                  20\n\fTable 4.2: The trust boundary before and after the replay of Section 5. “Moved” means the item no longer\nhas to be taken on trust; “not moved” means it still does.\n\n item                               status after the replay\n (i) the build                      moved: the 3,091-module closure built from source in our sandbox\n                                    at the pinned toolchain, exit 0, no error, no sorry.\n (ii) the axiom output              moved: #print axioms of the comparator’s theorem printed\n                                    [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound], and the nine-\n                                    teen in-build axiom lines say the same; the output is on record.\n (iii) kernel and Mathlib           moved in part: Mathlib was fetched from upstream at the pinned\n                                    revision and not patched, so “as upstream” is now observed rather\n                                    than assumed; the Lean kernel of v4.34.1 is still trusted as up-\n                                    stream, now beside a second checker (v).\n (iv) the comparator run            not moved: the statement-level match through lean4export was\n                                    not performed; the match this paper relies on is the textual one of\n                                    Section 4.4.\n (v) a second checker               moved: nanoda_lib checked the exported dependencies of the\n                                    theorem, 95,176 declarations, with no errors and the three axioms;\n                                    what this adds and what it does not is stated in Section 5.\n\n\nwriting out what the environment holds; upstream Mathlib at the pinned revision; and the\ntextual, not export-level, match between the solution’s statement and the challenge’s. Everything\nthe inspection could find by reading, the build and the two checkers confirmed. Nothing the\ninspection could not settle by reading has been settled by anything else.\n\n4.9     What this does not claim\n      • The arithmetic was not read. The inspection followed the spine from SqrtLogSpace/\n        Final.lean down to the compile lemmas and swept the whole closure by keyword; the\n        content of the other three thousand files was not read. The argument above is that its\n        correctness is enforced by the kernel, not that we verified it.\n\n      • A text search is necessary, not sufficient. Zero sorry and zero axiom by text search\n        cannot show that a file type-checks; only the build does (Section 5). And a keyword count\n        is over source text at one commit; a proof can be weakened only through the mechanisms\n        searched for, and we searched for the ones we know.\n\n      • The textual statement match is not the comparator’s. The comparator matches exported\n        constants; we matched source text after normalising spacing and found it identical. A\n        difference the normalisation hides would have to be invisible to the kernel as well, but that\n        is an argument, not a run.\n\n      • Nothing about the constants’ values. The bounds A ≥ 258 and C ≥ 28,701 are what the\n        proof’s witnesses are built from; they are lower bounds on what this proof exhibits, not\n        statements about any machine’s running time, and a different proof of the same statement\n        could exhibit different ones.\n\n      • Nothing about the Lean kernel, Mathlib or the toolchain. All three are trusted as up-\n        stream at the pinned versions; the second checker of Section 5 is evidence about the first,\n\n                                                    21\n\f        not a proof of either.\n\n      • Nothing about what the theorem says. That is Section 2; a sound proof of a weak bound\n        is a sound proof of a weak bound.\n\n\n5      Independent replay\nThe repository holds no saved output of the axiom check for the family, no automated build of\nits Lean, and enable_nanoda: false in the comparator’s settings (Section 4). We therefore\nbuilt the proof ourselves, in isolation, at the Lean version the repository pins, and recorded what\nthe kernel reports. This section says exactly what was run, where, and what came out. The\nscripts are quoted verbatim in paper3/replay/README.md, and the logs are under paper3/\nreplay/logs/.\n\n5.1     What was run, and where\nA fresh copy of the clone (commit 4eaee875) was built inside a sandbox on the project’s ma-\nchine. The sandbox tool is bwrap (bubblewrap). Inside it, the system directories are read-\nonly; the home directory is a private, empty one, so nothing of the real home or of this\nrepository is visible; processes, inter-process communication and host names are in separate\nnamespaces; the whole sandbox is confined to eight cores (taskset -c 0-7) at the lowest\nscheduling priority; and the network is on, because the repository’s build file clones its 41 de-\npendencies at build time and the compiled Mathlib comes from a download cache. Inside the\nsandbox, the Lean installer elan was installed into the private home and fetched the pinned\nLean version from the repository’s own lean-toolchain file. Then, in the clone’s lean/\ndirectory, three commands ran: lake exe cache get, which downloads the compiled Math-\nlib; lake build OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main, which builds the solution’s\n3,091 files and not the collection’s default target, which would build all 721 families; and #print\naxioms of the one theorem the comparator names, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.\nSqrtLogSpace.exact_count_and_sat, through lake env lean. Only text left the sand-\nbox; nothing built inside it was executed outside it.\n     Two runs were needed. The first, started at 01:03 on 2026-11-05, failed at once at the build step\nbecause its command line carried -j 8, an option this version of the build tool does not have\n(the whole of its build log is the one line error: unknown short option ’-j’); nothing\nof the family was compiled. The second, started at 01:16 with that option removed and the core\ncap moved to taskset, is the run reported below. Both scripts, both versions, and the launch\ncommand are in the README; the first run’s logs are kept beside the second’s.\n\n5.2     What came out\nThe build finished with exit code 0 in 33 minutes of wall clock on eight cores (01:16:25 to 01:49:31).\nIt compiled 3,091 modules with the prefix OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT: exactly the\nset the inspection of Section 4 enumerated, the same 3,091 names with nothing missing on either\nside (paper3/replay/counts.sh). The build tool’s own job total, 12014, counts the cached\nMathlib and dependency modules as well, none of which was compiled. The log has no line\ncontaining error and none containing sorry. Its eleven warning lines are the build tool noting\n\n\n                                                 22\n\fthat the repository’s compatibility patches leave eleven dependencies with “local changes”, and\nnone of the eleven is Mathlib.\n    Eight source files of the closure carry nineteen #print axioms commands (seventeen files\nof the family’s directory do, but nine of those are never built), so the build log records nineteen\naxiom lines as it goes. Every one reads [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound], the\nthree standard axioms. Among them, SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean, lines 197–201, prints the\naxioms of compiled_exact_count_and_sat, exact_count_and_sat, exact_count_eth,\nsat_polynomial_space and original_sat_main. The separate #print axioms run after\nthe build gives, for the comparator’s theorem,\n      ’OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.exact_count_and_sat’\n      depends on axioms: [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]\n\n(paper3/replay/logs/run2/axioms.txt).                      The     versions    are   Lean    4.34.1\n(the       log       line      reads        Lean (version 4.34.1, commit 5045d005...,\nRelease)),          Lake      5.0.0-src+5045d00,             and       Mathlib      at     revision\nd13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612 from the clone’s own manifest, with\nits compiled files from the Mathlib cache (8,908 files). The replay copy is at commit 4eaee875,\nthe same as the pristine clone, and the comparator file and the solution’s files have the same\nhashes in both (paper3/replay/logs/run2/hashes.txt; the comparator statement’s\nSHA-256 begins 4183b205). The full build log is kept out of the repository by size; its SHA-256\n(d313f594...) and its last 200 lines are committed.\n    So the sentence every later section relies on reads: replayed. The solution builds from source\nat the pinned Lean version with unpatched Mathlib, and the kernel reports the three standard\naxioms and nothing else for the theorem the comparator names. This moves the first three items\nof the inspection’s trust boundary (Section 4): the build is no longer OpenAI’s unpublished one,\nthe axiom output is now on record, and the Mathlib revision was fetched from upstream and not\npatched.\n\n5.3   The second checker\nThe comparator’s settings for the family have enable_nanoda: false: OpenAI’s check of\nthis family ran the Lean kernel only. We ran a second checker, in the same sandbox, after the\nbuild (paper3/replay/nanoda.sh, recorded in the README before it ran). Two tools are\ninvolved. lean4export (tag v4.34.0, commit 076e8e57, built with the pinned v4.34.1\nLean so that it reads the compiled files the build produced) wrote out exact_count_and_sat\nand everything it depends on from the built environment, as a plain-text file: 2,355,666,449\nbytes, 43,633,742 lines, format version 3.1.0, in 68 seconds. Then nanoda_lib (ammkrn, 2026)\n(version 0.4.31, commit 8de7e0c4, 2026-10-29), a checker for Lean 4 proofs written in Rust\nthat shares no code with the Lean kernel, read that file and re-checked every declaration in\nit, with propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound as the permitted axioms. Its output\n(paper3/replay/logs/nanoda/nanoda_check.txt):\n\n      Checked 95176 declarations with no errors\n\nin 110 seconds on eight cores, exit code 0, after printing back the theorem’s statement and the\nthree axioms from its own reading of the file. The export contains exactly three axiom dec-\nlarations (grep -c ’\"axiom\"’ on the file), so nothing else could have been permitted. The\n\n                                                23\n\fexport file (SHA-256 bdbc9afe..., paper3/replay/logs/nanoda/export_sha256.txt)\nstays in the sandbox directory by size; the configuration, the build logs and the checker’s output\nare committed.\n\n5.4     What this does not claim\n      • The replay trusts the Lean kernel of the pinned version (v4.34.1) and upstream Math-\n        lib at the pinned revision. It moves the proof’s trust from “OpenAI’s unpublished build”\n        to “a build we ran”, not from the Lean kernel to anything else. The second checker re-\n        moves the Lean kernel from the list for the theorem’s dependencies as exported, but adds\n        lean4export (that it writes out what the environment holds) and nanoda_lib (that it\n        checks what it reads) to it. Two independent checkers agreeing is evidence, not a proof that\n        either is correct.\n\n      • It is not the comparator run. The comparator checks that the solution’s exported statement\n        matches the challenge’s; we did not run that tool chain, and the statement-level match the\n        paper relies on is the textual one of Section 4 (the 35 definitions and the theorem statement,\n        identical after normalising spacing, two of them up to the noncomputable keyword).\n\n      • The counts above are counts of lines in one build’s log on one machine with the named\n        versions; a different build tool would print different job totals (the 12,014 includes the\n        cached Mathlib modules), and a different machine a different time.\n\n      • Nothing here bears on what the theorem says (Section 2) or on the manuscript’s unverified\n        exponents (Section 3); a replayed proof of a weak bound is a replayed proof of a weak\n        bound.\n\n\n6      What follows, in general terms","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HmjncNirMGedmcKgknwtRa","agent_id":"a37a7ff12e4b766a1","session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"7d1a434d-7b9e-4595-8ccc-6c4521137ba6","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:44:48.203Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SCLYmPdmQJKHopXzoKzM26","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Blind lay reader of paper 3"}
