{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DxLGqwPqiJ5sZuSS9sC9No","type":"tool_result","content":"6      What follows, in general terms\nSections 2–5 read the verified statement, drew the line between it and the manuscript, inspected\nthe proof and replayed it. This section asks what follows from the verified statement alone. Two\nthings: which of the standard hardness hypotheses it refutes, and by what step; and what a lower\nbound that was derived from one of those hypotheses has lost. The manuscript answers both\nquestions itself, in its §1.1.1–§1.1.2 (p. 4–5) and §1.1.3 (p. 5), and we quote it. What we add is\nthe exact logical form of each hypothesis, the one line of the comparator statement that denies it,\nand the distinction, stated in general terms, between a theorem whose premise has become false\nand a statement that has been refuted. Nothing here names a problem the manuscript’s §1.1.1\nand §1.1.3 do not name, and nothing here is about any problem’s complexity beyond what those\ntwo sections state.\n    A hypothesis, in this section, is a conjecture about how hard satisfiability is. The Exponential\nTime Hypothesis, ETH, says that 3-SAT needs time exponential in the number of variables. Its\nstrong form, SETH, says that as the clause width k grows, no algorithm for k-SAT does much\nbetter than trying all 2n assignments. Several variants exist, and the manuscript lists them. A\nlower bound derived from a hypothesis is a theorem of the form “if the hypothesis holds, then\nproblem X has no fast algorithm”; hundreds of such theorems exist in the literature.\n\n\n\n\n                                                   24\n\f6.1   The one property every refutation uses\nEvery refutation below rests on the same parts of the verified theorem (comparator file lines 194–\n217, Section 2). For each fixed width k ≥ 2 there is one deterministic machine which, given any\nformula of width at most k on n variables, halts with the number of satisfying assignments on\nits count tape (lines 203–204), the answer “is that number positive” on its answer tape and in its\nhalting state (lines 205–207), within the bound C ( L + 1)d 2 Aρ(n) (lines 210–211). Four features of\nthat property do the work.\n\n  1. It is deterministic. The step rule is a function; there is no random choice and no guess.\n     A deterministic algorithm is also a randomised algorithm, one that happens never to err,\n     and also a nondeterministic algorithm, one that happens never to guess. So each refuta-\n     tion of a hypothesis about deterministic algorithms is a refutation of its randomised and\n     nondeterministic forms by the same machine.\n\n  2. The bound is subexponential in n alone. A formula of width k on n variables has at most\n     about nk distinct clauses (2,604,376 at n = 125, k = 3), so the input length L is at most a\n     polynomial in n, and the whole bound is a polynomial in n times 2 Aρ(n) . Since ρ(n)/n goes\n     to zero (Appendix A, A.6), for every rate δ > 0 there is a size beyond which the bound is\n     below 2δn ; below that size the bound is a constant depending on k and δ. In the standard\n     notation, the running time is Ok,δ (2δn ) for every δ > 0 (Appendix B.5). This is a fact about\n     the limit, and the hypotheses are statements about the limit. It coexists with Section 2’s\n     observation that ρ(n) > n on every 50 ≤ n < e5504 , which is about where the limit begins.\n\n  3. One machine per k, for every n. The machine and the constants are chosen after k and\n     before n (Section 2); there is no n ≥ 20 condition in the comparator statement, though the\n     manuscript’s Theorem 1.1 carries one (p. 2, lines 56–57). So the verified form is the strong,\n     uniform denial: a single algorithm that runs within 2δn times a polynomial for every δ at\n     once, where the denial of a hypothesis of the form “for every δ there is an algorithm” would\n     have allowed a different algorithm for each δ.\n\n  4. The machine model is the conservative one. The comparator’s machine is a multitape\n     Turing machine that moves one head one cell or writes one symbol per step. Every step of\n     such a machine is a bounded amount of work on any of the standard models in which the\n     hypotheses are stated, so the bound transfers to those models with at most a constant factor\n     per step, which the polynomial absorbs. The input is the manuscript’s own encoding, of\n     length polynomial in n + m; converting from any other reasonable encoding is polynomial\n     too.\n\n6.2   The hypotheses, one by one\nThe manuscript’s own sentence is (p. 4, lines 187–191):\n      “Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing whether the ex-\n      act count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability and unsatisfiability in determinis-\n      tic 2o(n) (n + m)Ok (1) time for every fixed k; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH\n      promise problem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error randomized\n      versions of ETH [DHM+ 14, Section 1] and SETH [CDL+ 16, Definition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH.”\n\n\n\n                                                    25\n\fand, for counting (p. 5, lines 211–212): “Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1) solves #3-SAT determinis-\ntically in 2o(n) poly(n, m) time and therefore refutes #ETH.” We take each hypothesis in the form\nthe manuscript states it, with the manuscript’s own source for that form, and say which part of\nthe verified theorem denies it. Table 6.1 is the summary, and Appendix B.5 gives each hypothesis\nand its denial with the quantifiers written out. In every case the only thing used is the property\nof Section 6.1; nothing is used from the manuscript’s sharper exponents, which are not verified\n(Section 3).\n\nETH. As stated (p. 4, lines 165–166): “The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH) asserted that\n3-SAT requires 2Ω(n) deterministic time in the worst case, and hence so does k-SAT for every fixed\nk ≥ 3 [IP01, Theorem 1],” citing Impagliazzo and Paturi (2001). In words: there is some fixed\nrate δ > 0 such that no deterministic algorithm decides 3-SAT within 2δn times a polynomial, on\nall inputs. Its denial: for every rate δ > 0 there is such an algorithm. The verified statement\nat k = 3 supplies, for every δ, the same machine, whose answer bit is the decision, within that\ntime (item 2 above). This holds on every input of every size, so it refutes the hypothesis whether\n“requires 2Ω(n) time” is read as a lower bound at infinitely many sizes or at all large sizes. In\nthe vocabulary of Impagliazzo and Paturi (2001), where sk is the best possible rate for k-SAT and\nETH is s3 > 0: the verified statement gives sk = 0 for every k ≥ 2, by one algorithm per k.\n\nSETH. As stated (p. 4, lines 166–169): “The Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis (SETH) made\nthe sharper prediction that k-SAT requires 2n(1−ok (1)) time as k grows. Precisely, for every ε ∈\n(0, 1), there is a fixed k for which no deterministic algorithm solves k-SAT in 2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1)\ntime,” with the motivation attributed to Impagliazzo and Paturi (2001)’s open problems (lines\n169–171). In words: however small a saving ε one asks for over trying all 2n assignments, some\nclause width k makes that saving impossible. Its denial: there is a saving ε that every fixed width\nk allows. The verified statement gives more: for every saving ε and every k ≥ 2, the machine for\nk runs within 2(1−ε)n times a polynomial for all large n, because ρ(n) grows more slowly than n;\nbelow that size the exponential factor is a constant. The polynomial’s degree depends on k, and\nthat is exactly what the hypothesis’s “for a fixed k” permits. The refutation does not pass through\nETH; it reads the statement at each k. It is also standard that SETH implies ETH, through the\nSparsification Lemma of Impagliazzo et al. (2001) (Appendix B.5); so the refutation of ETH alone\nwould already refute SETH. We note the implication for the record; the direct reading above is\nwhat the paper rests on.\n\nGap-ETH, and its randomised form. As stated (p. 4, lines 172–174): “The Gap Exponential\nTime Hypothesis (Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants δ, γ > 0 for which no\nO(2γn )-time algorithm can distinguish a satisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment\nviolates at least a δ fraction of the clauses, even when m = O(n) [Din16, MR17],” citing Dinur\n(2016) and Manurangsi and Raghavendra (2017); and (lines 175–176) “The randomized version\nrules out an algorithm with the same running time that distinguishes the two cases with proba-\nbility at least 2/3 on every promised input [CCK+ 20, Conjecture 2.5],” citing Chalermsook et al.\n(2020). In words: it should be hard even to tell a satisfiable formula from one that is far from\nsatisfiable. Its denial: for every rate γ there is an algorithm within O(2γn ) that tells the two\napart, already when the number of clauses is proportional to n. Exact decision tells them apart,\nsince a formula in which every assignment violates some clauses is unsatisfiable; and the verified\n\n\n                                                26\n\fmachine at k = 3 decides within O(2γn ) for every γ, at every number of clauses (item 2). The\npromise is never used: the machine answers correctly on every input, promised or not, with\nprobability 1, which refutes the randomised form by item 1.\n\nNSETH. As stated (p. 4, lines 181–183): “The Nondeterministic Strong Exponential Time Hy-\npothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every ε > 0, some fixed k admits no nondeterministic 2(1−ε)n\n(n + m)O(1) -time algorithm for deciding whether a k-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether\na k-DNF is a tautology [CGI+ 16],” citing Carmosino et al. (2016). In words: even an algorithm\nallowed to guess should not be able to certify unsatisfiability with a saving over 2n . Its denial:\nthere is a saving that every fixed k allows, for a guessing algorithm. The verified machine’s\nanswer bit is false exactly when the count is zero (lines 205–207), so it decides unsatisfiability\ndeterministically within the SETH bound of the previous paragraph for every ε and every k; and\na deterministic machine is a guessing machine that never guesses, so every convention for non-\ndeterministic time is met. The manuscript’s “decides both satisfiability and unsatisfiability” (line\n188) is this.\n\n#ETH. As stated (p. 4, lines 204–205): “The counting Exponential Time Hypothesis (#ETH)\nasserted that exactly counting the satisfying assignments of a 3-CNF requires 2Ω(n) deterministic\ntime [DHM+ 14, Section 1],” citing Dell et al. (2014). Its denial: for every rate δ > 0 there is\na deterministic algorithm within O(2δn ) that outputs the exact count. The verified machine at\nk = 3 halts with the count on its count tape, a binary number of at most n + 1 bits (lines 203–204,\n208–209), within the same bound. This is the one hypothesis for which the yes-or-no bit is not\nenough and the count tape is what refutes it. It is also the hypothesis the comparator statement\nwas written for: the count is the theorem’s first conclusion and the decision its corollary.\n\nThe bounded-error randomised ETH and SETH. The manuscript names them by citation only\n(p. 4, lines 190–191): “[DHM+ 14, Section 1]” for ETH (Dell et al., 2014) and “[CDL+ 16, Definition\n2.1]” for SETH (Cygan et al., 2016). In each, the algorithm may toss coins and must be right with\nprobability clearly above one half on every input. A deterministic algorithm is such an algorithm\nthat is right with probability 1 (item 1), so the refutations of ETH and SETH above refute these\nforms by the same machine, with no change to the bound. We have not re-read the two sources’\ndefinitions; we state the forms as the manuscript cites them, and a reader who needs the exact\nerror threshold or the exact model of the source should go to the cited section and definition.\n\nWhat the manuscript places beside the list. Two sentences follow the refutation paragraph\n(p. 4, lines 191–194): the result “does not conflict with unconditional exponential lower bounds\nfor restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13],” and “In 2016, Ryan Williams\nrefuted the Merlin–Arthur version of SETH (MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies k-CNF\nunsatisfiability in 2n/2 poly(n, m) time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3]” (Williams, 2016). Both are context.\nThe first says that a lower bound proved outright for one restricted way of writing proofs is not\na statement about algorithms in general and is not touched. The second says that one member\nof the family was already false before this result. Neither is used below.\n\n\n\n\n                                                27\n\fTable 6.1: The hypotheses the manuscript’s §1.1.1–§1.1.2 list, each in the form the manuscript states\nit (page of paper.pdf, lines of the committed extract), with the manuscript’s own source for that\nform, the logical form in symbols, and the part of the verified theorem that denies it (lines 194–217\nof the comparator file at 4eaee875). A, Ck , dk are the never-computed constants of Section 2. Gen-\nerated, with every page and fragment checked against the extract and the clone, by python3 -I\npaper3/scripts/consequences.py.\n\nhypothesis   stated      source           form                                    negated by\nETH          p. 4,       IP01, Theorem    ∃ δ > 0: no deterministic 2δn poly      the answer bit at k = 3: deterministic,\n             165–166     1                algorithm decides 3-SAT                 2 Aρ(n) poly, ρ(n) = o (n)\nSETH         p. 4,       IP01, Open       ∀ ε ∈ (0, 1) ∃ k: no deterministic      the answer bit at every k: for each k,\n             166–169     Problems, item   2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1) algorithm for       Ak ρ(n) ≤ (1 − ε)n for all large n\n                         2                k-SAT\nGap-ETH      p. 4,       Din16, MR17      ∃ δ, γ > 0: no O(2γn ) algorithm        exact decision separates the two cases;\n             172–174                      separates satisfiable 3-CNFs from       at m = O(n) the bound is\n                                          δ-far ones, even at m = O(n)            2 Aρ(n) poly(n) = O(2γn ) for every γ\nrandomised   p. 4,       CCK+20,          as Gap-ETH, for algorithms correct      a deterministic algorithm is correct\nGap-ETH      175–176     Conjecture 2.5   with probability ≥ 2/3 on every         with probability 1\n                                          promised input\nNSETH        p. 4,       CGI+16           ∀ ε > 0 ∃ k: no nondeterministic        the answer bit is false iff\n             181–183                      2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1) algorithm decides   count F = 0; a deterministic machine\n                                          k-UNSAT                                 is a nondeterministic one with no\n                                                                                  guess\nrandomised   p. 4, 190   DHM+14,          ETH for bounded-error randomised        a deterministic algorithm is a\nETH                      Section 1        algorithms                              bounded-error randomised one with\n                                                                                  error 0\nrandomised   p. 4,       CDL+16,          SETH for bounded-error randomised       as above\nSETH         190–191     Definition 2.1   algorithms\n#ETH         p. 4,       DHM+14,          ∃ δ > 0: no deterministic 2δn           the count tape at k = 3 holds\n             204–205     Section 1        algorithm counts the satisfying         countWord (count F ) within the same\n                                          assignments of a 3-CNF                  bound\n\n\n\n\n                                                      28\n\f6.3   What a derived lower bound loses\nThe manuscript’s §1.1.3 opens with a list of lower bounds that were proved from ETH or from\nSETH (p. 5, lines 213–225), for problems with exponential algorithms and for problems with\npolynomial ones, and then says what its result does to them (lines 226–227):\n      “Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness claims above. Some of\n      the same lower bounds follow from weaker hypotheses that may still hold.”\n\nWe say what that means in general terms, for a theorem of either of the two standard shapes,\n\n                      (E)   ETH ⇒ no 2o(n) algorithm for X,\n                      (S)   SETH ⇒ no (c − ε)k algorithm for Y for any ε > 0,\n\nwhere n and k are whatever measure of the input the theorem names and c is its constant. In\nwords: (E) says that if ETH holds, problem X has no subexponential algorithm; (S) says that if\nSETH holds, no algorithm for problem Y beats the base c. Nothing below depends on what X\nand Y are.\n\nThe theorem stands; its premise is false; it derives nothing. A theorem of shape (E) or (S) is\nan “if . . . then”. It is proved by a reduction: a recipe that turns a fast algorithm for X or Y into a\nfast algorithm for k-SAT. The recipe is unaffected by anything in the verified statement; the “if . . .\nthen” remains a theorem. What has changed is that its “if” is now known to be false (Section 6.2),\nso the theorem can no longer be applied: it derives nothing about X or Y. That is the whole of\nwhat “removes one basis” means.\n\nThe conclusion is not refuted. A false “if” does not make the “then” false. The statement “no\n2o(n) algorithm for X” and the statement “no (c − ε)k algorithm for Y” are untouched by anything\nin the comparator statement, which speaks about k-CNF formulas and nothing else. To refute\neither, one would need an algorithm for X or Y, and the verified statement supplies one only\nthrough a reduction in the opposite direction to the one the lower bound used. The manuscript\nsays exactly this (p. 5, lines 237–238): “To obtain a faster algorithm for a target problem from our\nSAT algorithm, we need a reduction in the other direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT,\nthat controls the number of variables.” And it places the general caveat on itself at p. 2, lines\n95–99:\n      “Note that runtime bounds of the form 2o(n) do not transfer under general polynomial-time\n                     o (1)\n      reductions (2n       would be needed for this), so our result does not imply that general NP\n      problems admit subexponential time algorithms.”\n\nSo for shape (E): a subexponential algorithm for X follows only from a reduction of X to fixed-\nwidth SAT in which the number of SAT variables is proportional to X’s own size measure, and\nthe manuscript gives one such transfer (below). For shape (S): an algorithm for Y with base\nbelow c would need the SAT instance’s exponent to be below k log2 c by a constant factor, and the\nverified statement’s exponent is A times ρ of the SAT instance’s variable count, with A unknown\nand no value below 257 on record (Appendix B.5). No statement of shape “no (c − ε)k algorithm”\nis refuted by anything in this paper or, so far as we can read, in the manuscript.\n\n\n                                                  29\n\fHardness as a classification. A statement of the form “Y is SETH-hard” (or ETH-hard) records\nthat a reduction exists from k-SAT to Y with the stated control of the size measure. The reduction\nstill exists, and the statement is still true as a statement about reductions. What it used to convey,\nthat a fast algorithm for Y is unlikely because it would refute SETH, it no longer conveys: a fast\nalgorithm for Y would refute SETH, and SETH is refuted. The classification is now a fact about\nreductions and no longer a fact about Y’s running time.\n\nThe weaker hypotheses the manuscript names are untouched. The manuscript gives two (p. 5,\nlines 229–236). The first is the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, which it states and then relates\nto SETH: “The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal Vectors shows that SETH implies OVC [Wil05,\nVW19]” (lines 231–232). That implication is a theorem with a false premise, like (E) and (S): it\nsays nothing about OVC either way, and nothing in the verified statement is about orthogonal\nvectors. The second is the form of SETH for formulas of unrestricted width, which we quote\nwith the manuscript’s list of the problems it covers elided (lines 233–236):\n        “The lower bounds for [. . . ] also follow from the hypothesis that unrestricted CNF-SAT admits\n        no 2(1−δ)n poly(n, m)-time algorithm for any constant δ > 0 [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem\n        1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each fixed clause width k, while this hypothesis allows\n        the width to grow with n.”\n\nThe verified statement cannot reach this hypothesis, and the reason is visible in the order of its\nwords (Section 2): the machine and the constants C, A, S, d, e are chosen after k, and nothing in\nthe statement bounds how they grow with k. For a formula whose width grows with n there\nis no single machine and no single bound in the statement at all; even the trivial observation\nthat every k-CNF is a CNF runs the wrong way, since the hypothesis speaks about all widths\nat once. So the unrestricted-width hypothesis, and every lower bound that follows from it, is\nexactly where it was.\n\nWhat a transferred algorithm inherits. The manuscript’s one positive transfer is Set Splitting\nwith sets of size at most k (p. 5, lines 239–243): one variable per element and two clauses per\nset give a k-CNF with “one variable per universe element, so for fixed k Theorem 1.1 gives\nsubexponential time in the universe size, up to a polynomial input-size factor,” citing Cygan et al.\n(2016)’s Observation 3.6. Section 3 tags this supported: the encoding is on paper, the counter is\nthe verified one. What such a transfer inherits is the verified guarantee on the SAT instance it\n                          ′\nbuilds, C ( L + 1)d 2 Aρ(n ) with n′ the number of SAT variables, and therefore the title’s limitation\n                                                        ′\nwith it: at A = 1 the exponential factor is above 2n for every 50 ≤ n′ < e5504 (Table 2.1), with\nC, A, d unknown. In general terms: an algorithm obtained from the verified statement through a\nvariable-controlling reduction is a subexponential algorithm whose verified guarantee is weaker,\nat every practical size, than trying every assignment of the SAT instance it constructs. That is not\na defect of the transfer; it is the same sentence as the title, read through a reduction.\n\n6.4     What this does not claim\n      • No statement “no (c − ε)k algorithm” is refuted, for any problem, any size measure or any\n        constant c. Such a statement, proved from SETH, has lost its proof and kept its truth value,\n        which is unknown.\n\n\n                                                      30\n\f      • No statement “no 2o(n) algorithm for X” is refuted either, unless X has a reduction to\n        fixed-width SAT that controls the number of variables; the manuscript names one such\n        problem (Set Splitting) and we add none.\n\n      • The unrestricted-width hypothesis is untouched, and so is the Orthogonal Vectors Con-\n        jecture, and so is every lower bound proved from either; the verified statement is for each\n        fixed k and says nothing about widths that grow with n.\n\n      • Nothing about any specific problem’s complexity beyond what the manuscript’s §1.1.1\n        and §1.1.3 state and we quote. The problems the manuscript lists at lines 213–225 and 233–\n        235 are not discussed here, and no example from any other setting is given, by the scope of\n        this paper.\n\n      • No re-reading of the sources. Each hypothesis is stated in the form the manuscript gives it,\n        with the manuscript’s citation; we did not check that the cited sections and definitions state\n        it in the same words. If a source’s form is stronger than the manuscript’s, the refutation of\n        the manuscript’s form may not reach it, and the reader should check the source.\n\n      • About the limit only. Every refutation here denies a statement about the limit and uses\n        only that ρ(n) grows more slowly than n. None says that any machine decides or counts\n        anything fast at any size; the constants are as unknown here as in Section 2, and the verified\n        bound is above 2n on every 50 ≤ n < e5504 .\n\n      • Nothing beyond the verified statement. The manuscript’s sharper exponents (Section 3)\n        are not used; where the manuscript derives a consequence from them, it is not a conse-\n        quence of what is verified and is not claimed here.\n\n\n7      For the practitioner\nThe title compares two guarantees. One is the verified bound, C ( L + 1)d 2 Aρ(n) , with C, A and d\nnever computed (comparator file lines 136–139 and 210–211, Section 2). The other is the trivial\nbound for trying every assignment, 2n times a polynomial. Neither is what a practitioner runs.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HmjncNirMGedmcKgknwtRa","agent_id":"a37a7ff12e4b766a1","session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"04310c29-5be2-4923-991f-6c8f89624138","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:44:50.835Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DxLGqwPqiJ5sZuSS9sC9No","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Blind lay reader of paper 3"}
