{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0143NBFCwhpmofUtDuXgJXfQ","type":"tool_result","content":"bound for trying every assignment, 2n times a polynomial. Neither is what a practitioner runs.\nWhat runs on a real machine, when the question is whether a formula can be satisfied, is a SAT\nsolver; the standard kind is called CDCL, for conflict-driven clause learning, a search that learns\nfrom each dead end it reaches. When the question is how many satisfying assignments there are,\nwhat runs is a model counter, exact or approximate. No implementation of the verified machine\nexists to run beside them. This section measures the tools a practitioner has, on random formulas,\nand draws the two guarantees next to the measurements as shapes, so that the reader can see\nwhere each sits. The point is small and the section says it once: the verified guarantee is not the\none that matters on any machine, because it is above 2n for every n ≥ 50 (Section 2), while the\nsolvers are many orders of magnitude below 2n at every size they were run at.\n\n7.1     Why the comparison is between guarantees and measurements\nThe verified statement says a machine exists (comparator line 195 opens with the existence quan-\ntifier, Section 2). The solution’s 3,977 files contain no command to run anything: no #eval, no\ndef main and no input-output action. The script of this section checks that, reading every file\n\n\n                                                   31\n\fof the family with open() and counting those three patterns: 0, 0, 0. The library proves that a\nmachine with the stated bound exists and exhibits one as a mathematical term, inside a devel-\nopment with 1,048 noncomputable declarations and no way to execute anything (Section 4);\nnothing in the clone runs it on an input, and we found no implementation elsewhere. So the\nverified algorithm’s actual running time on any formula is unknown, in both directions: nothing\nsays it is slow, and nothing says it is fast. What is known is its guarantee, and a guarantee is\ncompared with other guarantees. The measurements below are of other programs entirely. They\nare in the section because the question a practitioner asks is not “which guarantee is smaller”\nbut “what would I run”, and the answer to that has not changed.\n\n7.2   What was run\nInstances. Random 3-CNF on n variables with m = ⌊4.26 n⌉ clauses, each clause three dis-\ntinct variables with random signs, generated by the script from (n, seed); twenty seeds per size;\nn = 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 400, 500. The ratio 4.26 clauses per variable is where\nrandom 3-SAT switches from mostly satisfiable to mostly unsatisfiable (Selman et al., 1996); ran-\ndom formulas are hardest to decide there (the cavity method puts the threshold at 4.2667, Mertens\net al., 2006). The sizes are chosen so that the smallest ones are where the title’s crossing is (n = 50)\nand the largest are where a CDCL solver on random formulas begins to take seconds. These are\nrandom instances only; nothing is claimed about formulas with structure.\n\nTools. CaDiCaL 1.9.5 (Biere et al., 2024), a CDCL solver, through python-sat 1.9.dev15 (Ignatiev\net al., 2018), one solve() call, for the yes-or-no answer; Ganak 2.8.0 (Sharma et al., 2019), an\nexact model counter, through the pyganak binding (which runs its Arjun preprocessing and then\nthe counter), for the exact count; ApproxMC 4.4.0 (Chakraborty et al., 2013; Soos and Meel, 2019),\nan approximate counter, at its defaults ε = 0.8, δ = 0.2 (an answer within a factor 1.8 of the truth\nwith probability 0.8), with the projection set equal to all variables; and, at n ≤ 30 only, exact\ncounting by listing every solution with CaDiCaL, one at a time, which is not a counter at any\nlarger size (its time grows with the count, and the count grows exponentially in n) and is here\nas the independent check of the two counters where it can reach. Ganak and ApproxMC were\ninstalled from PyPI for this section; no sharpSAT, d4 or other counter binary is on the host.\n\nProtocol. Each call runs in its own interpreter under nice -n 19, at most eight at a time\non the thirty-two cores of an AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (Linux 7.0.0, Python 3.12.13; the machine was\nshared with other work, load average at the start in tables/practitioner_meta.json), with\na wall-clock budget of 60 s per call. The time recorded is the wall clock around the construction\nof the solver object and the one call, inside the child process, with the interpreter’s start-up and\nthe import outside it. A call over budget is killed and recorded as censored, that is, as “more than\n60 s”. A size at which a tool finishes fewer than ten of its twenty instances ends that tool’s climb\nto larger sizes. The whole run took about twenty minutes (1,172 s).\n\n7.3   What came out\nTable 7.1 gives, per size, the number of satisfiable instances, CaDiCaL’s median and maximum\nover the twenty seeds, the number of instances each counter finished with its median and maxi-\nmum (a median over all twenty with censored runs counted as above the budget, so a median is\n\n                                                    32\n\fa number whenever more than ten finished), and the two guarantees in the common scale of the\nnext subsection. Figure 7.1 draws the same.\n\nTable 7.1: Random 3-CNF at m = ⌊4.26 n⌉, twenty seeds per size, one machine, 60 s per call. Columns:\nn variables; m clauses; “sat”, the number of satisfiable instances among those CaDiCaL finished; for each\ntool, “fin”, the number of instances it finished within budget, and “med” and “max”, its median and\nmaximum seconds over all twenty seeds with a censored run counted as > 60. The last two columns\nare the two guarantees as shapes: 2n (trying every assignment) and 2ρ(n) (the verified bound), both\nwith constant 1 and both multiplied by one common factor t20 /220 that puts 2n through CaDiCaL’s me-\ndian at n = 20 (t20 = 1.28 × 10−4 s). They are shapes, not running times. Regenerate: python3 -I\npaper3/scripts/practitioner.py.\n\n                            CaDiCaL                Ganak             ApproxMC           guarantee, s\n        n    m    sat       med        max fin     med     max fin     med    max          2n          2ρ ( n )\n       20   85 12/20 1.3 × 10−4 1.6 × 10−4    20 0.0015 0.0047    20 0.0016 0.0073 1.3 × 10−4 1.9 × 10−5\n       30 128 12/20 1.6 × 10−4 1.9 × 10−4     20 0.0014 0.0295    20 0.0031 0.0074     0.1310     0.0254\n       40 170 11/20 2.0 × 10−4 2.6 × 10−4     20 0.0018 0.0466    20 0.0058 0.0117         134     53.18\n       50 213 16/20 2.2 × 10−4 3.6 × 10−4     20 0.0041    9.48   20 0.0044 0.0148       105.1      105.2\n       60 256 12/20 3.5 × 10−4 6.9 × 10−4     20 0.0288    9.60   20 0.0099 0.0151       108.1      108.7\n       80 341 12/20 6.4 × 10−4      0.0016    20 0.0518 11.76     20 0.0133 0.0245      1014.2     1016.1\n      100 426 9/20       0.0025     0.0038    20   8.34 10.97     20 0.0219 0.1987      1020.2     1023.6\n      150 639 10/20      0.0169     0.0443    20   6.16 16.11     20 0.0595    1.01     10 35.2    1043.3\n      200 852 15/20      0.0578     0.3906    20   2.37 29.05     20 0.5104 25.21       10 50.3    1063.5\n      250 1065 6/20         1.47       3.57   20 17.62 45.72      18   3.64 > 60        10 65.3    1084.3\n      300 1278 12/20        3.72     20.51    10 > 60 > 60        11 58.91 > 60         10 80.4   10105.4\n      400 1704 10/20       > 60       > 60     0 > 60 > 60         0 > 60 > 60         10 110.5   10148.4\n      500 2130 8/20        > 60       > 60    —      —       —    —      —       —     10140.6    10192.2\n\n\n\n\nThe checks. On every instance where both finished, Ganak’s count equals the count by listing\n(40 instances at n ≤ 30) and CaDiCaL’s answer equals “count > 0”; ApproxMC’s estimate is\nwithin its factor 1 + ε = 1.8 of Ganak’s on 178 of the 206 instances where both finished (it\npromises that with probability 1 − δ = 0.8 per instance). The script exits non-zero if any of these\nfails.\n\nWhat the solvers did. CaDiCaL’s median is under a millisecond up to n = 80, 2.5 ms at 100,\n58 ms at 200, 1.5 s at 250 and 3.7 s at 300 (maximum 20.5 s); at 400 it finishes 10 of 20 within\nthe budget and at 500 8 of 20, every finished one satisfiable, which is the known shape of CDCL\non random formulas at the threshold (the unsatisfiable half is the expensive half). Ganak fin-\nishes all 20 up to n = 250 (median 17.6 s there, maximum 45.7 s), 10 of 20 at 300 and none\nat 400; its median is flat at 8.3 s and 6.2 s at n = 100 and 150 and lower, 2.4 s, at 200, while\nits maximum rises throughout. The flat stretch looks like a fixed time budget inside the bind-\ning’s preprocessing rather than counting difficulty; we report it as measured and tuned nothing\n(ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD, 06d). Where the count is positive it is small for the size: at n = 200 the\nfifteen satisfiable instances have between 372 and 2.5 × 1012 solutions out of 2200 assignments.\nApproxMC finishes all 20 up to n = 200 (median 0.51 s there), 18 at 250, 11 at 300 and none at\n400. The sizes at which the three tools leave the budget, 300 for the counters and 400 for the\ndecision, are where this measurement ends; nothing is claimed beyond them.\n\n\n                                                     33\n\f                                                        measured, with the guarantees leaving the panel200 the same, on the guarantees' scale\n\n\n\n\nseconds (solid: median; dotted: max; 20 seeds)\n                                                                                                    10\n                                                  102                                         60 s budget                 n = 50:\n                                                                                                                            (n) n\n                                                                                                                          from here\n                                                  101                                                            10150\n                                                  100\n                                                 10 1                                                            10100\n\n                                                 10 2\n                                                                                                                 1050\n                                                 10 3\n                                                 10 4       n = 50                                                100\n                                                               100        200        300     400      500                   100        200      300       400         500\n\n                                                                          number of variables n (random 3-CNF, m = 4.26 n clauses)\n                                                        2n (brute force, guarantee)            CaDiCaL 1.9.5, decision            ApproxMC 4.4.0, approximate count\n                                                        2 (n) (verified, guarantee, A = 1)     Ganak 2.8.0, exact count\n\nFigure 7.1: The same data on a logarithmic time axis, where each grid line is ten times the one below.\nSolid lines with markers are medians, dotted lines maxima, over the twenty seeds; a tool whose median\nis censored stops at the budget line. The two guarantees are drawn as shapes with the common factor\nof Table 7.1, so that 2n passes through the n = 20 measurement and 2ρ(n) crosses it at n = 50, where the\nverified exponent overtakes n (Section 2). The left panel keeps the measured seconds in view and lets\nthe guarantees leave the frame; the right panel is the same picture on the guarantees’ own scale. From\npaper3/scripts/practitioner.py.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                            34\n\f7.4     The two guarantees beside them\nThe guarantees are drawn as shapes. Both are taken with constant 1 (the polynomial factors, C,\nA and d are all set aside), and both are multiplied by the same factor, chosen so that 2n passes\nthrough CaDiCaL’s median at n = 20. Because the factor is one number for both curves, their\nratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves, 2ρ(n)−n : below 1 for n < 50, equal to 1 at n = 50\nup to rounding, and above 1 from there (it is 2.8 × 103 at n = 100 and 4.0 × 1051 at n = 500).\nScaling each curve to the n = 20 point on its own would instead make the two touch at n = 20\nand put the verified one above from there on; the common factor is the honest choice because it\nkeeps the crossing where the statement puts it (Appendix B.6; ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD, 06a).\n    In that scale the brute-force guarantee reaches one second near n = 33 and the verified\nguarantee near n = 35. At n = 60, the measured CaDiCaL median is 3 × 10−4 s, against 1.4 × 108 s\nfor 2n and 5.4 × 108 s for 2ρ(n) : the measurement is below the two guarantees by factors of 1011.6\nand 1012.2 . At n = 500 the decision median is above the budget (8 of 20 finished within 60 s),\nagainst 10140.6 s and 10192.2 s. Nothing in those enormous factors is about the verified machine:\nthe gap is between what a CDCL solver does on random formulas and what any exponential\nguarantee says, and it would be the same gap for the brute-force guarantee alone. The one\nfact this section adds to the title is the order of the three objects: at every size in the table the\nmeasurement is far below 2n , and 2ρ(n) is above 2n from n = 50 on, so the verified guarantee is\nthe largest of the three at every practical size and the measurement the smallest.\n    One remark on what the verified machine’s running time would have to be, were it ever run.\nNothing in this section bears on it: the constants C, A, d are unknown (Section 2), the machine is\na mathematical term, and a statement that something exists is not a program. The only values\non record are the proof’s own, A ≥ 257 and C ≥ 28,701 (Sections 2 and 4); a guarantee of 2257 ρ(n)\nsteps is a guarantee and not a measurement, and a different proof of the same statement could\ncarry any positive constants.\n\n7.5     What this does not claim\n      • Timings on one machine, one day, named versions. The seconds are from one AMD Ryzen\n        9 9950X shared with other work, under nice -n 19 with eight calls in flight, through\n        Python bindings; they are reproducible in shape and not in value, and the versions are\n        those named in Section 7.2 and in tables/practitioner_meta.json.\n\n      • Random instances only. Random 3-CNF at the threshold ratio is one distribution; formulas\n        with structure can be harder for CDCL at far smaller n and easier for counters. Nothing is\n        claimed about any other distribution, and nothing about worst-case behaviour.\n\n      • Nothing about the verified machine’s running time. It is unknown, in both directions; no\n        implementation exists to run; the guarantees drawn are at constant 1 and the constants are\n        never computed.\n\n      • Nothing about k > 3. The statement is for every fixed k; the measurement is at k = 3.\n\n      • The counters are reported, not tuned. Ganak ran through its binding at defaults, pre-\n        processing included; ApproxMC at its default tolerance; a different configuration gives\n        different seconds. The check against listing is at n ≤ 30 only.\n\n\n\n                                                 35\n\f      • No statement about which tool a practitioner should use. The section shows that the\n        verified guarantee is not the binding one; it does not rank solvers.\n\n\n8      The statement in our Lean\nSections 2 and 4 read OpenAI’s statement in OpenAI’s files. This section describes the one thing\nwe wrote in Lean ourselves: a copy of the statement inside our own library, checked by the same\nkernel, under our own name. The point is modest. Every sentence this paper says about “the\nverified statement” is pinned to a named object that Lean has accepted, and anyone who wants\nto use the statement as an assumption later can cite that one name. Nothing is proved about the\nhypothesis here, and nothing is claimed beyond what the copy and its audit show.\n    Four words first, each in one sentence. In Lean a Prop is a sentence that may be true or false.\nA def gives a sentence a name without asserting it. A theorem asserts a sentence and carries\nits proof. An axiom asserts a sentence without a proof. Our restatement is a def: it names the\nsentence and asserts nothing.\n    The files are lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/PrimitiveMachine.\nlean and lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/Frame.lean, written on\n2026-11-04 for an earlier study of ours on a different question. The directory carries that\nstudy’s name. The owner chose to leave the files where they are rather than move them\n(paper3/ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD, S3), and this paper cites nothing from that study: only the\ncopied model, the restatement, and six small lemmas about them, all listed in Appendix A.\n\n8.1     The copy: definitions only\nThe comparator file of family 378, lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.\nlean, has 220 lines. Lines 1–190 define the objects the theorem speaks about: the formu-\nlas, their encoding as a string of bits, the multitape machine, its step and its starting config-\nuration, the exponent runtimeScale and the bound built from it, the count word, and the\nbookkeeping that records which tape cells a run has visited. Lines 191–219 state the theorem\nexact_count_and_sat and end it with sorry, the placeholder that a solution must replace.\nLine 220 closes the last namespace.\n    Our PrimitiveMachine.lean is lines 1–190 and 220, and nothing else. After a thirty-line\nheader that records the source, the commit, the file’s hash and the Apache licence, the file is the\ncopy (Appendix A, A.1). The header says so, and a script checks it. paper3/scripts/lean_\nrediff.py reads both files with open() only, takes everything in ours from the line import\nMathlib on, and compares it byte for byte with the clone’s lines 1–190 followed by line 220.\nThe diff is empty: 191 lines on each side, identical. The script also checks the clone file’s SHA-\n256 against the hash the header records, and that the clone is at commit 4eaee875. The log is\npaper3/lean/rediff.log.\n    So the copy holds the theorem’s vocabulary and not the theorem. The sorry at line 218 is\noutside the copied range, and our file has no sorry and no axiom declaration anywhere (the\nsame script counts them: zero and zero in each of our two files). Everything in the copy is a\ndefinition. A definition can be badly chosen, but it cannot be false; what the definitions mean is\nSection 2’s subject, and the copy adds nothing to it.\n\n\n\n                                               36\n\f8.2     The restatement\nFrame.lean defines a sentence named SubexpCount, with the width k as its argument:\n\n                                      SubexpCount (k : N) : Prop.\n\nIts body is the body of the comparator theorem for width k. The theorem’s two leading parts are\nhandled thus: k is the argument of the def, and the condition 2 ≤ k is carried by a second def,\nExactCountAndSat, which says “for every k ≥ 2, SubexpCount k” (Appendix A, A.2 and A.3).\nIn words, SubexpCount k is the sentence of Section 2: one fixed machine, on every formula of\nwidth at most k, writes the exact number of satisfying assignments, answers whether there is one,\nruns within the bound built from runtimeScale, and visits only polynomially many cells.\n    The same script checks that the body is the comparator’s. It takes the 23 lines after def\nSubexpCount (k : N) : Prop := in our file (lines 85–107) and the 23 lines after the the-\norem’s leading parts in the clone (lines 195–217, with the closing := by removed), and compares\nthem in two ways: with every run of white space collapsed to one space, and line by line with\nthe indentation stripped. Both comparisons find the two texts identical, 1,073 characters. The\nonly difference is that each of our lines starts two spaces further left, because a def’s body sits\ntwo columns inside a theorem’s. We call this “character for character”; it is a textual match, by\nthe same method as Section 4’s, and it rests on the file we copied from being the comparator file\nat the commit we name.\n    Neither def is proved here, and neither is an axiom. The truth of SubexpCount k for k ≥ 2\nrests on OpenAI’s proof and on Sections 4 and 5, not on anything in our library. Our library\nwould be unchanged if the proof were wrong.\n\n8.3     What is proved, and what it means in words\nSix small lemmas sit beside the defs, each proved in a few lines (Appendix A, A.4–A.8). None of\nthem says anything about any machine’s running time or about whether the hypothesis is true.\n\n      • A machine for width k is a machine for every smaller width. SubexpCount.anti: if\n        SubexpCount k holds and k′ ≤ k then SubexpCount k′ holds, with the same machine and\n        the same constants, because a formula whose clauses have at most k′ members has at most\n        k. ExactCountAndSat.subexpCount unpacks the “for every k ≥ 2” form.\n\n      • The bound is what it is. runtimeBound_eq: the wrapper runtimeBound C A d L n un-\n        folds, by definition, to C ( L + 1)d 2 A·ρ(n) , the formula Section 2 reads. The proof is rfl: the\n        two sides are the same term.\n\n      • The exponent grows more slowly than n.                    tendsto_log_sq_div_sqrt says\n                 √\n        (ln y) / y → 0 as y → ∞, and runtimeScale_isLittleO says that ρ(n)/n → 0 as\n              2\n\n        n → ∞, which is written ρ(n) = o (n) (Appendix A, A.6). This is the limit fact that Sec-\n        tions 2, 3 and 6 cite: in the limit the exponent is smaller than n by any factor one names. It is\n        true at the same time as the title’s fact that ρ(n) > n for every 50 ≤ n < e5504 , and the Lean\n        says nothing about where the limit begins to bite. isSubexpExponent_runtimeScale\n        is the same statement for A · ρ(n), for every constant A (Appendix A, A.7).\n\n      • The time bound is a bound on the plain machine. Trace.config_of_locatedStep:\n        the comparator states its time bound on the located step, which records visited cells beside\n\n                                                    37\n\f        each configuration. A run of the located step projects to a run of the plain step with the\n        same number of steps, so the bound is a bound on the machine itself (Appendix A, A.8).\n        The one-line LocatedCfg.monitor_config is its helper.\n\n8.4     The axiom audit\nLean records, for every proved statement, the axioms its proof uses. Three are standard in Math-\nlib and in every proof of Sections 4 and 5: propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound.\nA fourth, sorryAx, appears exactly when a proof was left out with sorry. The audit\npaper3/lean/axiom_check.lean prints the axioms of 21 declarations: the ten copied def-\ninitions the statement rests on, the two defs, and the nine lemmas and helper definitions, in the\npattern of our first paper’s audit. It is run from our library’s directory as\n\ncd lean && lake env lean ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean \\\n  > ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.log\n\nand the log is committed. The result: nineteen lines on exactly the three standard axioms; Trace\non no axiom at all (it is an inductive definition); countWord on two of the three; sorryAx on\nnone. The library it runs against is built with Lean 4.34.0-rc2 and Mathlib at 1192d624, neither\nof which is what the clone pins (Lean 4.34.1, Mathlib d13f23b7, Section 4). That the copied\nmodel is accepted unchanged by a different Lean version and a different Mathlib revision is\nsmall evidence that it rests on nothing patched or private, and no more than that. Our whole\nlibrary builds with no sorry and no axiom declaration, which the loop’s gate checks after every\nsession.\n\n8.5     What this does not claim\n      • SubexpCount k is a def of a Prop, never proved here. Its truth is OpenAI’s theorem,\n        replayed in Section 5; our library neither proves it nor assumes it. Nothing in this section\n        makes the refutation more or less certain.\n\n      • The copy is definitions only. The proof is not copied, and could not be: it is 3,091 modules\n        (Section 4). What is copied is the vocabulary of the statement, so that the restatement reads\n        as the comparator does.\n\n      • No Lean computation evaluates the exponent. runtimeScale is defined over the real\n        numbers with real logarithms and is marked noncomputable; no #eval can print ρ(50)\n        or ρ(50)/50. Every number in Section 2 comes from a Python script, not from Lean, and\n        the Lean proves only the limit.\n\n      • The match is textual. “Character for character” means the normalised texts are equal; it is\n        not a check at the level of lean4export, and it rests on the clone being the repository at\n        the commit named.\n\n      • The lemmas are trivial on purpose. They fix the shape of the bound and the limit of the\n        exponent, and nothing else. No theorem about any machine’s running time, about the\n        constants, or about the manuscript’s sharper exponents is stated, because none is verified.\n\n\n\n                                                  38\n\f    • The directory name is not a citation. The files sit in a directory named for another study\n      of ours; nothing from that study enters this paper, and the owner’s decision to leave them\n      in place is recorded, not argued.\n\n\n9    Related work and context","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HmjncNirMGedmcKgknwtRa","agent_id":"a37a7ff12e4b766a1","session_id":"6f557700-3e4f-4208-a06d-ff2061f5e975","uuid":"bd36baa9-8b87-4daa-8a2d-0eada9c24de8","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:44:51.654Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0143NBFCwhpmofUtDuXgJXfQ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Blind lay reader of paper 3"}
