rings of a graph on $v$ vertices and $e$ edges, in time $2^{o(v)}$, and the number of perfect matchings in time $2^{o(v+e)}$, all in polynomial space.\",\n \"For a graph on $v$ vertices and $e$ edges: the number of independent sets (sets of vertices no two of which are joined by an edge) and, for fixed $q$, of proper $q$-colourings (colourings with $q$ colours such that no edge joins two vertices of the same colour), in time $2^{o(v)}$, and the number of perfect matchings (sets of edges touching every vertex exactly once) in time $2^{o(v+e)}$, all in polynomial space.\"),\n('sec3_beyond.tex', \"(line 121): there is a language $L$ in the class $\\\\mathrm{E}^{\\\\mathrm{NP}}$ and a constant $c > 0$ such that $L$ needs circuits of more than $c\\\\,n\\\\sqrt{\\\\log n}$ gates for infinitely many $n$ (lines 123--127). The proof is Appendix~C.1 (p.~51--52, lines 2925--2967), Williams's witness-circuit argument with a local reduction,\",\n \"(line 121). A circuit lower bound is a proof that some problem needs large circuits; $\\\\mathrm{E}^{\\\\mathrm{NP}}$ is a class of problems whose definition is not needed here. The claim: there is a problem $L$ in that class and a constant $c > 0$ such that $L$ needs circuits of more than $c\\\\,n\\\\sqrt{\\\\log n}$ gates for infinitely many $n$ (lines 123--127). The proof is Appendix~C.1 (p.~51--52, lines 2925--2967), an argument of Williams's,\"),\n('sec3_beyond.tex', \"\\\\paragraph{Corollary C.1, $\\\\mathrm{E}^{\\\\mathrm{NP}} \\\\not\\\\subseteq \\\\mathrm{SIZE}(O(n))$ (p.~51, lines 2912--2916).}\",\n \"\\\\paragraph{Corollary C.1, the class $\\\\mathrm{E}^{\\\\mathrm{NP}}$ has no linear-size circuits (p.~51, lines 2912--2916).}\"),\n('sec3_beyond.tex', \"For a Boolean map whose every output bit reads at most $d$ input bits, the number of inputs mapping to a given output,\",\n \"For a Boolean map whose every output bit reads at most $d$ input bits (a ``constant-locality'' map), the number of inputs mapping to a given output,\"),\n('sec3_beyond.tex', \"The remark that follows, on Goldreich's candidate one-way functions (p.~53, lines 2994--2999),\",\n \"The remark that follows, on Goldreich's candidate one-way functions (functions easy to compute and conjectured hard to invert; p.~53, lines 2994--2999),\"),\n('sec3_beyond.tex', \"proves Theorem~1.1 ``by contracting Boolean tensor networks. The two running times reflect the number of wires in the networks'' (lines 3002--3003).\",\n \"proves Theorem~1.1 ``by contracting Boolean tensor networks. The two running times reflect the number of wires in the networks'' (lines 3002--3003). A tensor network is a way of writing one large sum as a product of small tables; the terms in the rest of this paragraph are the manuscript's, and we do not define them further.\"),\n('sec3_beyond.tex', \"proposition as a hypothesis and are not in the closure).\",\n \"proposition as a hypothesis and are not among the files the proof depends on, its closure, Section~\\\\ref{sec:proof}).\"),\n('sec3_beyond.tex', \"It names the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture (lines 229--232)\",\n \"It names the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, a hypothesis about a different problem (lines 229--232),\"),\n('sec3_beyond.tex', \"\\\\item \\\\texttt{original\\\\_sat\\\\_main} (line 184): the proposition \\\\texttt{SqrtLog.Statement.MainTheorem}\",\n \"\\\\item \\\\texttt{original\\\\_sat\\\\_main} (line 184): the sentence (a Lean \\\\texttt{Prop}, Section~\\\\ref{sec:lean}) \\\\texttt{SqrtLog.Statement.MainTheorem}\"),\n('sec3_beyond.tex', \"stated there as a \\\\texttt{Prop} ``no inhabitant is supplied here'' and proved in \\\\path{Final.lean}.\",\n \"stated there without proof (``no inhabitant is supplied here'') and proved in \\\\path{Final.lean}.\"),\n('sec3_beyond.tex', \"its proof takes $2d$, $2e$ and $4A$ from the comparator theorem's constants (line 132).\",\n \"its proof sets its own constants to twice, twice and four times the comparator theorem's $d$, $e$ and $A$ (line 132).\"),\n('sec3_beyond.tex', \"\\\\subsection{The table}\\n\", \"\\\\subsection{The table}\\n\\nTable~\\\\ref{tab:beyond-claims} lists every claim examined above with its\\nstatus and the one-line reason.\\n\"),\n# ---- sec4\n('sec4_proof.tex', \"no recorded evidence that the proof type-checks at all:\",\n \"no recorded evidence that the proof type-checks at all, that is, that Lean accepts it:\"),\n('sec4_proof.tex', \"The 148 instances and 40 local instances are standard typeclass instances; an instance cannot be ``inconsistent'' without an axiom, and there is none.\",\n \"The 148 instances and 40 local instances are standard bookkeeping declarations (``typeclass instances'') that tell Lean, for example, that a type is finite or a property is decidable; they are harmless because such a declaration can only make a false claim by way of an axiom, and there is none.\"),\n('sec4_proof.tex', \"The only options set in the closure switch off a pre-check on local notation in two files and have no bearing on soundness.\",\n \"The only options set in the closure switch off a check on notation in two files and have no bearing on soundness.\"),\n('sec4_proof.tex', \"and then renames the states to numbers by a classical choice. The theorem only says such a machine exists, so this non-constructive renaming costs it nothing;\",\n \"and then renames the states to numbers by an appeal to a choice principle that names no particular numbering. The theorem only says such a machine exists, so this non-constructive step (one that proves something exists without producing it) costs it nothing;\"),\n('sec4_proof.tex', \"with \\\\texttt{Ns} obtained from an ``eventually'' statement and never computed.\",\n \"with \\\\texttt{Ns} obtained from a statement that something holds for all large enough $n$, which yields no particular number, and never computed.\"),\n('sec4_proof.tex', \"The two branches are folded together, and the constants the final theorem exhibits are\",\n \"The two branches are folded together, and the final theorem's constants are those of the two branches added up:\"),\n('sec4_proof.tex', \"and the only claim made about it here is the one the kernel makes: it elaborates (Section~\\\\ref{sec:replay}).\",\n \"and the only claim made about it here is the one the kernel makes: Lean accepts it (Section~\\\\ref{sec:replay}).\"),\n('sec4_proof.tex', \"The manifest lists 42 packages;\", \"The manifest (the list of the packages the build depends on, each at an exact version) lists 42 packages;\"),\n('sec4_proof.tex', \"The build file has two hooks that run arbitrary code when the build tool configures or updates the package:\",\n \"The build file has two hooks, pieces of code that run automatically when the build tool configures or updates the package:\"),\n('sec4_proof.tex', \"the export tool \\\\texttt{lean4export} writing out what the environment holds;\",\n \"the export tool \\\\texttt{lean4export} writing out what the built environment (the set of all checked declarations) holds;\"),\n# ---- sec5\n('sec5_replay.tex', \"processes, inter-process communication and host names are in separate namespaces;\",\n \"processes, inter-process communication and host names are kept apart from the host's (separate ``namespaces'');\"),\n# ---- sec6\n('sec6_consequences.tex', \"So the verified form is the strong, uniform denial: a single algorithm\",\n \"So the verified form is the strong form of the denial, one algorithm for every rate at once: a single algorithm\"),\n('sec6_consequences.tex', \"Every step of such a machine is a bounded amount of work on any of the standard models in which the hypotheses are stated,\",\n \"Every step of such a machine is a bounded amount of work on any of the standard models of computation (random-access machines, other kinds of Turing machine) in which the hypotheses are stated,\"),\n('sec6_consequences.tex', \"where $s_k$ is the best possible rate for $k$-SAT and ETH is $s_3 > 0$:\",\n \"where $s_k$ is the smallest rate $\\\\delta$ such that $k$-SAT has an algorithm within $2^{\\\\delta n}$ and ETH is the statement that $s_3 > 0$:\"),\n('sec6_consequences.tex', \"through the Sparsification Lemma of \\\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001} (Appendix~\\\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses});\",\n \"through the Sparsification Lemma of \\\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}, a tool that trades one formula with many clauses for a few formulas with few clauses (Appendix~\\\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses});\"),\n('sec6_consequences.tex', \"citing \\\\citet{Carmosino2016}. In words: even an algorithm allowed to guess\",\n \"citing \\\\citet{Carmosino2016}. (A $k$-DNF is the mirror image of a $k$-CNF, an ``or'' of small ``and''s, and a tautology is a formula true under every assignment.) In words: even an algorithm allowed to guess\"),\n('sec6_consequences.tex', \"Both are context. The first says that a lower bound proved outright for one restricted way of writing proofs is not a statement about algorithms in general and is not touched. The second says that one member of the family was already false before this result.\",\n \"Both are context. The first says that a lower bound proved outright for one restricted way of writing proofs of unsatisfiability (``regular resolution'' is one such way) is not a statement about algorithms in general and is not touched. The second says that one member of the family, the version in which the proof of unsatisfiability may be checked with the help of randomness (a Merlin--Arthur protocol), was already false before this result.\"),\n('sec6_consequences.tex', \"For shape (S): an algorithm for $Y$ with base below $c$ would need the SAT instance's exponent to be below $k \\\\log_2 c$ by a constant factor, and the verified statement's exponent is $A$ times $\\\\rho$ of the SAT instance's variable count, with $A$ unknown and no value below $257$ on record (Appendix~\\\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses}).\",\n \"For shape (S): an algorithm for $Y$ with base below $c$ would need the SAT instance to be solved with an exponent a fixed fraction below $k \\\\log_2 c$; the verified exponent is $A$ times $\\\\rho$ of the SAT instance's variable count, $A$ is unknown with no value below $257$ on record, and nothing of the kind follows (Appendix~\\\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses}).\"),\n('sec6_consequences.tex', \"The manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of size at most $k$ (p.~5, lines 239--243):\",\n \"The manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of size at most $k$ (given small sets over a universe of elements, colour the elements with two colours so that no set is all one colour; p.~5, lines 239--243):\"),\n# ---- sec7\n('sec7_practitioner.tex', \"The point is small and the section says it once: the verified guarantee is not the one that matters on any machine,\",\n \"The point is small and the section says it once: the verified guarantee is not the binding one on any machine,\"),\n('sec7_practitioner.tex', \"(the cavity method puts the threshold at $4.2667$, \\\\citealp{Mertens2006Threshold})\",\n \"(a method from statistical physics puts the threshold at $4.2667$, \\\\citealp{Mertens2006Threshold})\"),\n('sec7_practitioner.tex', \"through the pyganak binding (which runs its Arjun preprocessing and then the counter), for the exact count;\",\n \"through the pyganak binding (which runs a simplification pass called Arjun and then the counter), for the exact count;\"),\n('sec7_practitioner.tex', \"with the projection set equal to all variables; and,\",\n \"with the count taken over all variables (its ``projection set''); and,\"),\n# ---- sec8\n('sec8_lean.tex', \"Line 220 closes the last namespace.\", \"Line 220 closes the last namespace (a grouping of names).\"),\n('sec8_lean.tex', \"the comparator states its time bound on the \\\\emph{located} step, which records visited cells beside each configuration.\",\n \"the comparator states its time bound on the \\\\emph{located} step, which is the plain step plus the bookkeeping of visited cells beside each configuration.\"),\n('sec8_lean.tex', \"\\\\texttt{Trace} on no axiom at all (it is an inductive definition); \\\\texttt{countWord} on two of the three; \\\\texttt{sorryAx} on none.\",\n \"\\\\texttt{Trace} on no axiom at all (it is defined by construction rules and carries no proof); \\\\texttt{countWord} on two of the three (a proof may use fewer than the three, and fewer is never a concern); \\\\texttt{sorryAx} on none.\"),\n]\nbad=0\nfor path,old,new in edits:\n    s=open(path).read()\n    pat=re.escape(old).replace('\\\\ ', r'\\s+').replace('\\\\\\n', r'\\s+')\n    n=len(re.findall(pat,s))\n    if n!=1:\n        print('MISMATCH',path,repr(old[:60]),n); bad+=1; continue\n    s=re.sub(pat, lambda m: new, s, count=1); open(path,'w').write(s)\nprint('applied', len(edits)-bad, 'of', len(edits))\nEOF\nmake check 2>&1 | tail -1; echo \"overfull: $(grep -c Overfull main.log)\"","description":"Apply the reader's glosses to sections 2-8 and rebuild","timeout":300000}}}
