ement about algorithms in general and is not touched. The second","-says that one member of the family was already false before this","-result. Neither is used below.","+\\citep{Williams2016}. Both are context. The first says that a lower bound proved outright for one restricted way of writing proofs of unsatisfiability (``regular resolution'' is one such way) is not a statement about algorithms in general and is not touched. The second says that one member of the family, the version in which the proof of unsatisfiability may be checked with the help of randomness (a Merlin--Arthur protocol), was already false before this result. Neither is used below."," "," \\subsection{What a derived lower bound loses}"," \\label{sec:consequences-lower-bounds}"]},{"oldStart":309,"oldLines":11,"newStart":299,"newLines":7,"lines":[" So for shape (E): a subexponential algorithm for $X$ follows only from a"," reduction of $X$ to fixed-width SAT in which the number of SAT variables"," is proportional to $X$'s own size measure, and the manuscript gives one","-such transfer (below). For shape (S): an algorithm for $Y$ with base","-below $c$ would need the SAT instance's exponent to be below $k \\log_2 c$","-by a constant factor, and the verified statement's exponent is $A$ times","-$\\rho$ of the SAT instance's variable count, with $A$ unknown and no","-value below $257$ on record (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses}). No","+such transfer (below). For shape (S): an algorithm for $Y$ with base below $c$ would need the SAT instance to be solved with an exponent a fixed fraction below $k \\log_2 c$; the verified exponent is $A$ times $\\rho$ of the SAT instance's variable count, $A$ is unknown with no value below $257$ on record, and nothing of the kind follows (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses}). No"," statement of shape ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is refuted by"," anything in this paper or, so far as we can read, in the manuscript."," "]},{"oldStart":355,"oldLines":8,"newStar